/*
 * AQA GCSE Geography (8035) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * Topic ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Real AQA GCSE Geography (8035) questions are built on
 * resources (described figures, tables, maps and photos) and named case studies,
 * with fieldwork questions on sampling, presentation and evaluation. Every item
 * here opens with a fully described resource or a located context.
 *
 * TARIFFS. Part tariffs follow the real mix taken from the site's own past-paper
 * data for this specification.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. Board style: point-marked items one point per line; extended
 * items give indicative content and the board's level ladder, including SPaG
 * where the board awards it.
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 * Drawing canvas flag (diagram: true) enables the sketch area in practice.html.
 */

const GEOGRAPHY_AQA_GCSE_PRACTICE = {
  '1.1': {
    name: 'Tectonic Hazards',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nWhich one of the following best describes a natural hazard?\n\nShade one circle only.\n\nA  Any natural event, such as a storm or an earthquake, whether or not people live nearby\nB  A natural event that threatens people's lives, property or the environment\nC  An event caused by people that damages the natural environment\nD  A natural event that happens only in low-income countries`, marks: 1, command: 'Shade', markScheme: `B (1)\nDo not credit more than one circle shaded.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 1: a cross-section through Iceland. Two plates are shown moving away from each other, with arrows pointing in opposite directions. Magma is rising from the mantle into the gap between the plates, and a ridge with a volcano and fissures runs along the centre of the cross-section. Labels show the North American plate to the west and the Eurasian plate to the east.\n\nUsing Figure 1, name the type of plate margin shown.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `constructive (plate margin) (1)\nAllow divergent.\nDo not credit destructive / conservative / collision.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 2: a table of five major earthquakes.\n\nEarthquake | Country | Magnitude\nL'Aquila, 2009 | Italy | 6.3\nHaiti, 2010 | Haiti | 7.0\nChristchurch, 2011 | New Zealand | 6.3\nTōhoku, 2011 | Japan | 9.0\nGorkha, 2015 | Nepal | 7.8\n\nUsing Figure 2, calculate the range in magnitude of the five earthquakes.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2.7 (1)\n(9.0 − 6.3 = 2.7)\nDo not credit 6.3–9.0 stated as a range without calculation.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 3: a photograph taken in a suburb of Christchurch, New Zealand, after the earthquake of 22 February 2011. Grey sand and water have spread across a road from cracks in the ground, and a car has sunk up to its wheel arches in the wet sediment. Houses in the background are tilted.\n\nUsing Figure 3, name the process that happens when shaking makes waterlogged sediment behave like a liquid.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `liquefaction (1)\nIgnore subsidence / flooding.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 4: a bar graph of the number of small earthquakes recorded each week beneath a volcano in the eight weeks before it erupted (data written for this practice question). Week 1: 4; week 2: 6; week 3: 5; week 4: 12; week 5: 25; week 6: 48; week 7: 90; week 8: 140.\n\nFigure 4 shows that scientists used seismometers to monitor the volcano.\n\nGive one other method scientists can use to monitor a volcano.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any one from:\n• tiltmeters / GPS to measure the ground swelling or bulging (1)\n• measuring gases given off, e.g. sulphur dioxide (1)\n• thermal imaging / satellites to detect rising temperature (1)\n• measuring changes in water temperature or levels in nearby springs (1)\n• drones / remote cameras to watch the crater (1)\nIgnore seismometers (given in the question).` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nOn 25 April 2015 a magnitude 7.8 earthquake struck Nepal, an LIC. Its epicentre was in the Gorkha district, about 80 km north-west of the capital, Kathmandu.\n\nGive one secondary effect of the 2015 Nepal earthquake.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any one from:\n• landslides / avalanches triggered, e.g. in the Langtang valley or on Mount Everest (1)\n• landslides blocked roads and rivers, cutting off remote villages (1)\n• people left homeless / living in tents or temporary shelters (1)\n• fall in tourism income / loss of jobs (1)\n• spread of disease / shortage of clean water after the earthquake (1)\n• crops / food stores lost, leading to food shortages (1)\nDo not credit primary effects such as buildings collapsing or people killed by falling buildings.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 5: a comparison of two earthquakes.\n\n | Christchurch, New Zealand (HIC) | Gorkha, Nepal (LIC)\nDate | 22 February 2011 | 25 April 2015\nMagnitude | 6.3 | 7.8\nDeaths | 185 | almost 9,000\n\nUsing Figure 5, suggest one reason why the number of deaths was lower in Christchurch than in Nepal.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any one from:\n• the Christchurch earthquake had a lower magnitude / released less energy (1)\n• buildings in New Zealand are built to stricter building codes / are earthquake resistant (1)\n• New Zealand has well-trained, well-equipped emergency services / faster rescue (1)\n• better hospitals / medical care for the injured in New Zealand (1)\n• Nepal is mountainous so many areas were hard to reach / landslides cut off villages (1)\nAllow any other valid reason linked to wealth or the size of the earthquake.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 6: a world map showing major earthquakes and plate margins. Most earthquakes form narrow lines that follow the plate margins, particularly in a ring around the edge of the Pacific Ocean (from the west coast of South America, along western North America, through Japan and Indonesia to New Zealand). A narrow line of earthquakes runs down the centre of the Atlantic Ocean. A wider band of earthquakes stretches across southern Europe and Asia, from Italy and Turkey through Iran to the Himalayas. Very few earthquakes are shown in the centre of continents such as Australia, Brazil or northern Russia.\n\nUsing Figure 6, describe the global distribution of earthquakes.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid descriptive point, or 2 marks for one developed point.\n• earthquakes are found in narrow bands / belts (1)\n• along plate margins / boundaries (1)\n• around the edge of the Pacific Ocean / the Pacific Ring of Fire (1)\n• e.g. along the west coast of South America / Japan / Indonesia (1)\n• a line down the middle of the Atlantic Ocean (1)\n• a wider band across southern Europe and Asia / the Himalayas (1)\n• few / none in the centre of continents, e.g. Australia (1)\nCredit use of named places or plates from the figure. Do not credit explanations of the pattern.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 7: a world map of active volcanoes and plate margins. Most volcanoes lie around the edge of the Pacific Ocean, along the Andes, western North America, Japan, the Philippines and Indonesia. There are volcanoes in Iceland and along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, in East Africa and in southern Italy. There are no volcanoes along the Himalayas or along the San Andreas Fault in California, although earthquakes occur in both places. A few isolated volcanoes are shown away from plate margins, such as Hawaii in the middle of the Pacific Ocean.\n\nUsing Figure 7, compare the distribution of volcanoes with the distribution of earthquakes.`, marks: 2, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid comparative point, or 2 marks for a developed comparison.\n• both are mainly found along plate margins / both found around the Pacific Ring of Fire (1)\n• volcanoes are absent from some areas that have earthquakes, e.g. the Himalayas / San Andreas Fault / conservative and collision margins (1)\n• some volcanoes are away from plate margins (hotspots), e.g. Hawaii (1)\n• earthquakes are more widespread / found in wider bands than volcanoes (1)\nAnswers must compare; one-sided descriptions max 1.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nKathmandu, the capital of Nepal, has grown rapidly as people have migrated from rural areas. Many new buildings in the city have been built quickly, several storeys high, on soft valley sediments.\n\nOutline one reason why rapid urbanisation can increase hazard risk.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid reason, 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• more people are concentrated in one area (1) so more people are exposed and could be killed or injured if an earthquake strikes (1)\n• buildings are built quickly / cheaply without following building codes (1) so they are more likely to collapse when the ground shakes (1)\n• people live in high-density informal housing (1) which makes rescue and evacuation more difficult (1)\n• building on soft sediments / steep slopes (1) increases the risk of shaking being amplified / landslides (1)\nMax 1 for an undeveloped reason.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 8: house damage in one mountain district after an earthquake (data written for this practice question).\n\nTotal number of houses in the district | 3,200\nNumber of houses destroyed | 2,400\n\nUsing Figure 8, calculate the percentage of houses in the district that were destroyed. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2,400 ÷ 3,200 (× 100) (1)\n= 75% (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 9: a map of the San Andreas Fault in California, USA. The fault runs north-west to south-east between San Francisco and Los Angeles. Arrows show the Pacific plate moving north-west relative to the North American plate, with both plates moving side by side.\n\nUsing Figure 9, explain why earthquakes happen along the San Andreas Fault.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid point, or 2 for a developed point.\n• it is a conservative plate margin where plates slide past each other (1)\n• the plates move in the same direction at different speeds / in opposite directions (1)\n• the plates get stuck / friction causes them to lock (1)\n• pressure / stress builds up until it is suddenly released as an earthquake (1)\nMax 1 for a description of the fault position without explanation.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nIceland lies on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, where the North American and Eurasian plates are moving apart by about 2 cm each year. Eruptions such as Eyjafjallajökull in 2010 and those on the Reykjanes peninsula since 2021 are common.\n\nExplain why volcanoes form at constructive plate margins such as Iceland.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid point, 1 further mark for development.\n• as the plates move apart, a gap forms (1) and magma rises from the mantle to fill it, erupting at the surface (1)\n• reduced pressure as plates move apart allows mantle rock to melt (1) creating magma that rises through fissures to form volcanoes (1)\nIgnore subduction. Max 1 if the plates are described as moving together.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nAfter the 2015 Nepal earthquake, thousands of people were trapped in collapsed buildings and many roads into the mountains were blocked by landslides.\n\nOutline one immediate response to the 2015 Nepal earthquake.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid response, 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• search and rescue teams came from countries such as India, China and the UK (1) to find people trapped under rubble (1)\n• helicopters were used to rescue people / deliver aid (1) to remote villages cut off by landslides / to climbers on Mount Everest (1)\n• tents / temporary shelters were provided (1) for people who had lost their homes (1)\n• field hospitals / medical teams treated the injured (1) as hospitals were overwhelmed / damaged (1)\n• food, water and water purification tablets were distributed (1) to reduce the risk of disease (1)\nDo not credit long-term responses such as rebuilding.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nThe earthquake that struck Christchurch, New Zealand, on 22 February 2011 killed 185 people. Much of the city centre, including the cathedral and the CTV building, was badly damaged, and parts of the eastern suburbs suffered severe liquefaction.\n\nOutline one long-term response to the 2011 Christchurch earthquake.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid response, 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• the government bought land in the worst-affected areas and declared a residential red zone (1) so people were moved away from land likely to liquefy again (1)\n• the city centre was rebuilt with stricter building codes (1) so new buildings are more likely to withstand future earthquakes (1)\n• insurance (e.g. the Earthquake Commission) paid out to homeowners (1) which funded repairs and rebuilding (1)\n• a temporary shopping mall built from shipping containers (Re:START) (1) helped businesses recover while the centre was rebuilt (1)\n• underground pipes, roads and sewers were replaced (1) restoring services to homes (1)\nDo not credit immediate responses such as search and rescue.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 10: a photograph of the lower slopes of Mount Etna, Sicily, Italy. Rows of vines and orange trees grow on dark soil in the foreground. A road leads uphill past cafés and a car park full of coaches to a cable car station. Smoke rises from the summit in the background.\n\nUsing Figure 10, outline one reason why people continue to live close to Mount Etna.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid reason, 1 further mark for development. Credit reasons visible in Figure 10.\ne.g.\n• volcanic soils are very fertile (1) so farmers can grow crops such as vines / oranges and earn a good income (1)\n• the volcano attracts tourists (1) which provides jobs in cafés, coach tours and the cable car (1)\n• eruptions are usually monitored closely (1) so people feel safe enough to stay / warnings can be given (1)\nMax 1 for an undeveloped reason.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nIceland is an HIC on a constructive plate margin. About 90% of homes in Iceland are heated using geothermal energy, and the Blue Lagoon geothermal spa is one of the country's most visited attractions.\n\nGive two economic advantages of living in a tectonically active area such as Iceland.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid advantage, up to 2 marks.\n• cheap / renewable geothermal energy for heating and electricity (1)\n• tourism, e.g. visitors to volcanoes, geysers and hot springs (1)\n• jobs in tourism or energy industries (1)\n• minerals / building materials from volcanic rock (1)\n• fertile volcanic soils for farming (1)\n• cheap energy attracts industry, e.g. aluminium smelting (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 11: a labelled diagram of an earthquake-resistant building in Tokyo, Japan. Labels show: steel cross-bracing in the walls; rubber shock absorbers (base isolators) between the foundations and the building; a heavy counterweight on the roof; automatic window shutters to stop glass falling into the street.\n\nUsing Figure 11, explain how one feature reduces the effects of an earthquake.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a feature, 1 further mark for explaining how it reduces effects.\ne.g.\n• rubber shock absorbers (1) absorb ground movement so less shaking passes into the building (1)\n• steel cross-bracing (1) strengthens the frame so the building can sway without collapsing (1)\n• counterweight (1) moves against the sway of the building, reducing movement (1)\n• window shutters (1) prevent broken glass falling and injuring people below (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nMount Vesuvius, near Naples in Italy, last erupted in 1944. Around 700,000 people live in the 'red zone' closest to the volcano.\n\nOutline one way that planning can reduce the effects of a volcanic eruption.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid planning strategy, 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• evacuation plans / routes are prepared (1) so people can be moved out of the red zone quickly before an eruption (1)\n• hazard maps show the areas most at risk (1) so new building can be restricted in those areas (1)\n• evacuation drills / education (1) so people know what to do when a warning is given (1)\n• emergency supplies and shelters are prepared in advance (1) so evacuated people have food and somewhere to stay (1)\n• paying people to move away from the red zone (1) so fewer are exposed to the hazard (1)\nDo not credit monitoring unless linked to a plan of action.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 12: the number of earthquakes of magnitude 7.0 or above recorded worldwide each year (data written for this practice question).\n\nYear | 2016 | 2017 | 2018 | 2019 | 2020\nNumber of earthquakes | 16 | 7 | 17 | 10 | 9\n\nUsing Figure 12, calculate the mean number of earthquakes of magnitude 7.0 or above per year. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `16 + 7 + 17 + 10 + 9 = 59 ÷ 5 (1)\n= 11.8 (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks. Allow 12 only if working is shown.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 13: causes of death in the Tōhoku earthquake and tsunami, Japan, 11 March 2011. A pie chart shows that about 90% of the deaths were from drowning, with the rest caused mainly by crushing, burns and other injuries.\n\nUsing Figure 13, suggest two reasons why most deaths were caused by the tsunami rather than the ground shaking.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid reason, up to 2 marks.\n• Japanese buildings are earthquake resistant so few collapsed in the shaking (1)\n• the tsunami waves were very high / overtopped sea walls (1)\n• the tsunami reached the coast within about 30 minutes, giving little time to evacuate (1)\n• many people lived in low-lying coastal towns (1)\n• some people did not evacuate / went back, believing the sea walls would protect them (1)\n• elderly people found it hard to reach higher ground quickly (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nNepal lies on the boundary between the Indo-Australian plate and the Eurasian plate. The Indo-Australian plate is moving north at about 4–5 cm each year.\n\nExplain why Nepal experiences powerful earthquakes.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid point, 1 further mark for development.\n• Nepal is on a collision (destructive) margin where two continental plates are pushed together (1) so pressure builds up and is suddenly released as an earthquake (1)\n• the plates are locked by friction as the Indo-Australian plate is forced under / into the Eurasian plate (1) and when the rocks give way a large amount of energy is released (1)\nAllow destructive or convergent margin. Ignore volcanoes.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 14: a cross-section through the Earth's crust and upper mantle. Arrows in the mantle form circular loops: hot rock rises beneath a mid-ocean ridge and spreads outwards, and cooler rock sinks beneath an ocean trench. An oceanic plate is shown sinking at the trench, labelled 'dense plate pulled down'.\n\nUsing Figure 14, explain how tectonic plates are moved.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation of at least two processes that move plates, with links between heat, density and movement. Uses Figure 14 appropriately.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic explanation with limited links; may describe a single process or list ideas.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• heat from the core / radioactive decay heats rock in the mantle\n• hot rock rises, spreads out under the plates and cools, then sinks — convection currents\n• friction between the moving mantle and the plates drags the plates along\n• at a trench the older, cooler, denser oceanic plate sinks into the mantle and pulls the rest of the plate with it (slab pull)\n• at a mid-ocean ridge, rising magma pushes the plates apart / plates slide away from the higher ridge (ridge push)\n• slab pull is now thought to be the main force` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 15: a cross-section of the west coast of South America. The oceanic Nazca plate moves east towards the continental South American plate and sinks beneath it at the Peru–Chile Trench. Inland, the Andes mountains contain a line of volcanoes. Earthquake foci are shown along the sinking plate, becoming deeper further inland.\n\nExplain the formation of the volcanoes shown in Figure 15. Use one or more diagrams to support your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true, markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation of the sequence of processes, showing understanding of subduction and melting. Diagram(s), if used, are accurate and annotated and add to the explanation.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic explanation; processes may be listed or the sequence unclear. Diagram(s) may be simple or unlabelled.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• this is a destructive plate margin where the plates move towards each other\n• the Nazca plate is oceanic and denser than the continental South American plate, so it is subducted\n• as it sinks into the mantle it heats up / friction and water released from the plate cause melting\n• magma forms and, being less dense, rises through cracks in the overlying crust\n• magma erupts at the surface, building volcanoes in the Andes\n• the volcanoes are explosive because the magma is thick / gas-rich and pressure builds up\nCredit diagrams showing subduction, trench, melting zone and rising magma.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nThe San Andreas Fault in California has experienced major earthquakes, including San Francisco in 1906 and Loma Prieta in 1989, but there are no volcanoes along it.\n\nExplain why conservative plate margins have earthquakes but no volcanoes.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation of both the earthquakes and the absence of volcanoes, linked to plate movement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic explanation of one part, or simple statements about both.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• at a conservative margin plates slide past each other, in the same direction at different speeds or in opposite directions\n• the plates do not move smoothly; friction causes them to stick\n• stress builds up over time until the rocks suddenly slip, releasing energy as an earthquake\n• earthquakes are often shallow and so can be very damaging, e.g. San Francisco\n• crust is neither created nor destroyed at a conservative margin\n• there is no subduction and no gap between plates, so no magma is formed or able to rise to the surface` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 16: two statements about hazard risk.\nStatement 1: 'The number of people affected by natural hazards has increased since 1970 because the world's population has grown.'\nStatement 2: 'Poor people suffer most from natural hazards because they have less ability to prepare and recover.'\n\nExplain how population growth and poverty can increase hazard risk. Use Figure 16 and your own understanding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation of how both population growth and poverty increase risk, with links to exposure, vulnerability or capacity to cope.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic explanation; may deal with only one factor or simply restate Figure 16.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• more people means more people are exposed to hazards, e.g. in fast-growing cities such as Kathmandu\n• population growth forces people to live on marginal land: steep slopes, floodplains, near volcanoes\n• rapid urbanisation leads to high-density, poorly built housing that is more likely to collapse\n• poor people cannot afford earthquake-resistant homes or insurance\n• poorer countries have less money for monitoring, warning systems and emergency services\n• poor people have fewer savings, so recovery takes longer and they may be more vulnerable to the next hazard\n• candidates may refer to named examples, e.g. Nepal 2015, Haiti 2010` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 17: a fact file on the Tōhoku earthquake, Japan, 11 March 2011.\n• Magnitude 9.0; epicentre about 70 km off the north-east coast of Japan\n• The tsunami that followed reached heights of up to 40 m and travelled up to 10 km inland\n• About 15,900 people died and over 2,500 were missing\n• Cooling systems failed at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power station, causing a meltdown and the evacuation of people living within 20 km\n• Estimated total cost: about US$235 billion\n\nUsing Figure 17, explain why the secondary effects of the Tōhoku earthquake were greater than the primary effects.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation, correctly distinguishing primary and secondary effects, using evidence from Figure 17.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic explanation; may list effects with limited explanation or confuse primary and secondary.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• primary effects (ground shaking) were limited because Japanese buildings are built to withstand earthquakes\n• the tsunami (a secondary effect) caused most deaths — about 90% drowned\n• the tsunami was up to 40 m high and went up to 10 km inland, destroying whole coastal towns\n• the tsunami overtopped sea walls that were designed for smaller waves\n• the Fukushima nuclear disaster forced long-term evacuation and contaminated land\n• much of the US$235 billion cost came from tsunami damage, lost power and the nuclear clean-up` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nMount Etna in Sicily is one of the most active volcanoes in Europe. Scientists at an observatory in Catania monitor it continuously using instruments placed on and around the volcano.\n\nExplain how monitoring a volcano can reduce the effects of an eruption.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation of how specific monitoring methods give warning, linked to actions that reduce effects.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic explanation; methods listed with little link to reducing effects.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• seismometers detect an increase in small earthquakes as magma rises\n• tiltmeters / GPS detect the ground swelling as magma collects beneath the volcano\n• gas sensors detect increases in sulphur dioxide being released\n• satellites / thermal imaging detect rising temperatures\n• these signs allow scientists to warn that an eruption is likely\n• people can be evacuated before the eruption, reducing deaths and injuries\n• authorities can close roads / airspace, or divert lava flows (e.g. Etna)\n• monitoring cannot give the exact time of an eruption, so warnings may be missed or false alarms may reduce trust` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nAround 3 million people live in and around Naples, Italy, close to Mount Vesuvius. In AD 79 an eruption of Vesuvius destroyed the Roman towns of Pompeii and Herculaneum.\n\nExplain why people continue to live in areas at risk from a tectonic hazard.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation of a range of reasons, developed with examples.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic explanation; reasons listed with little development.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• fertile volcanic soils support farming, e.g. vineyards around Vesuvius and Etna\n• tourism provides jobs and income, e.g. visitors to Pompeii and Vesuvius\n• geothermal energy provides cheap heat and electricity, e.g. Iceland\n• minerals and building materials are found in volcanic areas\n• people have lived there for generations; family, jobs and homes are there\n• poorer people may be unable to afford to move\n• eruptions or major earthquakes are infrequent, so people think the risk is low\n• monitoring and building design make people feel safe` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 18: a description of a map of Nepal. The country is about 800 km long. The south is a low plain; the north contains the Himalayas, including Mount Everest. Most villages in the north are reached only by narrow mountain roads or footpaths along steep-sided valleys. The 2015 earthquake happened in April, about six weeks before the start of the monsoon rains.\n\nUsing Figure 18, suggest why the physical geography of Nepal made the effects of the 2015 earthquake worse.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear suggestions linked to the physical geography in Figure 18, with developed effects.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic suggestions, not clearly linked to Figure 18 or poorly developed.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• steep mountain slopes meant the shaking triggered landslides and avalanches, e.g. Langtang valley, Everest Base Camp\n• landslides blocked the few narrow roads so rescue teams and aid could not reach remote villages\n• helicopters were needed, but there were too few, and bad weather limited flights\n• landslides blocked rivers, creating a risk of flooding if the dams burst\n• monsoon rains soon after the earthquake caused more landslides and made life in temporary shelters harder\n• cold at high altitude increased risk for people left without homes` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 19: three cross-sections of plate margins.\nA: two plates moving apart beneath the Atlantic Ocean, with magma rising to form a ridge.\nB: an oceanic plate sinking beneath a continental plate, with a trench offshore and a line of volcanoes inland.\nC: two plates sliding past each other with a fault line between them and no magma shown.\n\nUsing Figure 19, explain how the processes at different plate margins lead to different tectonic hazards.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed explanation of the processes at all three margins, clearly linked to the hazards that result. Accurate use of terminology and Figure 19.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation of processes at two or more margins with some links to hazards.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic explanation; may describe margins with little reference to processes or hazards.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• A (constructive): plates move apart, pressure falls and magma rises to fill the gap; gentle, frequent eruptions of runny lava, e.g. Iceland; small, shallow earthquakes as the crust cracks\n• B (destructive): denser oceanic plate is subducted; friction and melting create magma that rises to form explosive volcanoes, e.g. Andes; plates lock and release energy as powerful earthquakes, which may cause tsunamis if under the sea, e.g. Japan 2011\n• C (conservative): plates slide past each other and stick; stress is released as earthquakes, often shallow and destructive, e.g. San Andreas; no volcanoes because no magma is formed\n• destructive margins tend to produce the most dangerous hazards because both violent eruptions and very large earthquakes occur there` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 20: a comparison of two earthquakes.\n\n | Christchurch, New Zealand | Gorkha, Nepal\nLevel of development | HIC | LIC\nMagnitude | 6.3 | 7.8\nFocus depth | about 5 km | about 15 km\nDeaths | 185 | almost 9,000\nPeople made homeless | thousands (many later moved from the 'red zone') | about 2.8 million displaced\nEstimated cost | about NZ$40 billion | about US$7 billion (about one-third of Nepal's GDP)\n\nUsing Figure 20 and your own understanding, explain why the effects of the two earthquakes were different.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed explanation of physical and human reasons for the differences, using Figure 20 and own knowledge, including the different types of effect (social and economic).\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation of some reasons, with some use of Figure 20.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic explanation, or mainly describes the data.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Nepal's earthquake was more powerful (7.8 compared with 6.3), releasing far more energy and affecting a wider area\n• Christchurch was very shallow and close to the city, so the shaking was intense locally, explaining the high damage cost\n• New Zealand has strict building codes; most modern buildings stayed up, while many homes in Nepal were made of brick or stone and collapsed\n• New Zealand has well-equipped emergency services, so rescue was fast; Nepal relied on international aid\n• Nepal's mountains led to landslides and blocked access to remote villages\n• the cost was higher in Christchurch because property and infrastructure are worth more and are insured, but Nepal's cost was a much bigger share of its GDP\n• there are more deaths in LICs but higher economic losses in HICs` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 21: a description of Taipei 101, a 508 m tower in Taiwan, which lies close to a destructive plate margin. A 660-tonne steel ball (a tuned mass damper) hangs near the top of the building and swings to reduce movement. The building also has a strong steel frame and deep foundations.\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of protection in reducing the effects of earthquakes. Use Figure 21 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed evaluation of protection strategies with a well-supported judgement about effectiveness. Uses Figure 21 and examples.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear evaluation with some judgement; may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic description of protection with little or no evaluation.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• aseismic design (cross-bracing, base isolators, tuned mass dampers) reduces shaking and prevents collapse\n• buildings like Taipei 101 can withstand strong earthquakes and typhoon winds\n• retrofitting older buildings strengthens them; automatic gas shut-off valves reduce fires\n• in Japan and New Zealand, building codes have greatly reduced deaths from shaking\n• sea walls can protect against tsunamis but were overtopped in Japan 2011\n• protection is expensive, so it is less effective in LICs, e.g. Nepal, where many homes are self-built\n• cheaper methods can help, e.g. light roofs, wooden frames, reinforced concrete bands\n• protection does not prevent secondary hazards such as landslides or liquefaction\n• judgement: very effective for reducing deaths where it is affordable and enforced, but less so where it is not` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 22: a fact file on Mount Etna, Sicily, Italy.\n• Etna erupts regularly; lava flows have destroyed buildings, roads and orchards in the past\n• Volcanic soils on its lower slopes support vineyards, citrus groves and olive trees\n• Thousands of tourists visit each year to walk near the craters and use the cable car\n• The volcano is monitored continuously, and lava flows have sometimes been diverted using earth barriers\n\n'The advantages of living near a volcano are greater than the risks.' Do you agree? Use Figure 22 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed, balanced discussion of advantages and risks, using Figure 22 and own understanding, leading to a clear, supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear discussion of both sides, or one side in detail, with some use of Figure 22 and a judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic points, mostly copied from Figure 22, with little or no judgement.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\nAgree:\n• fertile soils give good crop yields and income\n• tourism provides jobs and money\n• monitoring gives warnings so people can be evacuated in time\n• Etna's eruptions are mostly lava flows, which move slowly enough for people to escape\n• lava diversion has protected some towns\nDisagree:\n• lava flows have destroyed homes, farmland and roads\n• ash falls can close Catania airport and damage crops\n• larger explosive eruptions could be very dangerous, e.g. Vesuvius threatens about 700,000 people in its red zone\n• earthquakes linked to the volcano damage buildings\nJudgement may depend on the type of volcano, the level of wealth and the quality of monitoring and planning.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 23: facts about earthquake planning in Japan.\n• Every year on 1 September (Disaster Prevention Day), millions of people take part in earthquake drills\n• Children practise taking cover under their desks at school\n• Local councils publish hazard maps showing areas at risk from tsunamis and liquefaction, with evacuation routes signposted\n• Many families keep emergency kits with food, water, a torch and a radio\n• An early warning system sends alerts to mobile phones seconds before strong shaking arrives\n\nAssess the importance of planning in reducing the effects of earthquakes. Use Figure 23 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed assessment of planning strategies, weighing their importance against other strategies or their limitations, with a supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear assessment with some weighing of importance; some use of Figure 23.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic description of planning with little assessment.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• drills mean people know how to react quickly, reducing injuries from falling objects\n• hazard maps stop building in high-risk areas and show safe evacuation routes\n• emergency kits help people survive until help arrives\n• early warnings give seconds to stop trains (bullet trains stopped in 2011), shut off gas and take cover\n• in 2011 many Japanese coastal residents evacuated, saving lives, but some ignored warnings or underestimated the wave\n• planning is relatively cheap, so it can also be used in LICs (e.g. school drills in Nepal)\n• planning cannot prevent buildings collapsing, so protection (building design) is also needed\n• earthquakes cannot be predicted, so planning is especially important\n• conclusion: planning is very important, but works best alongside protection and monitoring` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 24: a timeline of responses to the 2015 Nepal earthquake.\nDay 1: Nepalese army and local people begin searching the rubble by hand; hospitals in Kathmandu are overwhelmed.\nDays 2–3: International search and rescue teams arrive from countries including India, China and the UK; the single runway at Kathmandu airport becomes congested.\nWeek 1: Helicopters drop food and tents in remote areas; many villages are still unreached because of landslides.\nWeek 3: Aid agencies supply water purification tablets and shelters; a second earthquake (magnitude 7.3) strikes on 12 May.\n\nAssess the effectiveness of the immediate responses to the 2015 Nepal earthquake. Use Figure 24 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed assessment of the effectiveness of a range of immediate responses, using Figure 24 and own knowledge, with a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear assessment of some responses with some judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic description of responses with little assessment.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\nEffective:\n• local people and the army rescued many survivors in the first hours, when rescue matters most\n• international teams brought specialist equipment and sniffer dogs\n• helicopters reached some remote villages and rescued climbers from Everest\n• water purification tablets reduced the risk of disease outbreaks\nLess effective:\n• hospitals were overwhelmed so many injured people waited for treatment\n• congestion at Kathmandu airport delayed aid\n• landslides meant some villages waited weeks for help\n• the 7.3 aftershock caused more damage and fear, slowing the response\n• Nepal, as an LIC, depended on foreign aid and had few helicopters of its own\nJudgement: partly effective; good in Kathmandu but slow in remote mountain areas.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 25: a graph showing the number of deaths from earthquakes and the level of development of the countries affected (data written for this practice question). Eight earthquakes, all of magnitude 6.5–7.5, are plotted. The four earthquakes in LICs each caused between 2,000 and 9,000 deaths. The four earthquakes in HICs each caused fewer than 200 deaths.\n\n'The level of development of a country is the most important factor affecting hazard risk.' Do you agree? Use Figure 25 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed discussion of development and at least one other factor affecting hazard risk, using Figure 25 and examples, with a clear, supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear discussion with some use of Figure 25 and some judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic points with little use of Figure 25 or no judgement.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\nAgree:\n• Figure 25 shows LICs suffer many more deaths from earthquakes of similar magnitude\n• HICs can afford earthquake-resistant buildings, monitoring, warning systems and well-equipped emergency services\n• LICs have more poorly built housing and less capacity to respond, e.g. Nepal 2015 compared with Christchurch 2011\nDisagree / other factors:\n• magnitude and depth of the hazard: very large hazards cause high losses even in HICs, e.g. Japan 2011\n• population density and urbanisation: more people exposed in big cities\n• frequency of hazards and the time since the last one: people may be unprepared if hazards are rare\n• physical factors such as steep slopes or soft sediments (landslides, liquefaction)\n• time of day, e.g. night-time earthquakes catch people indoors\nJudgement: development is very important for deaths but other factors also matter, and HICs often suffer greater economic losses.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 26: a description of a photograph taken at Taro, a small town on the coast of north-east Japan, after the tsunami of 11 March 2011. A 10 m high concrete sea wall, once one of the largest in Japan, has been broken through in several places. Behind it, only the concrete foundations of houses remain. Signs on a hillside nearby show a tsunami evacuation route to higher ground.\n\nAssess how far protection and planning reduced the effects of the 2011 tsunami in Japan. Use Figure 26 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed assessment of both protection and planning, using Figure 26 and own knowledge, with a supported judgement on how far they reduced effects.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear assessment of protection and/or planning with some judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic points with little assessment.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• sea walls slowed some waves and gave people more time, but the tsunami was much higher than the walls were built for, so many were overtopped or broken, as at Taro\n• some people stayed behind because they trusted the sea walls\n• evacuation routes, signs and drills helped many people reach higher ground in time\n• tsunami warnings were issued within minutes, but early estimates of wave height were too low\n• earthquake-resistant buildings meant few deaths from the shaking itself\n• the nuclear power station's defences were too low, leading to the Fukushima meltdown\n• about 15,900 deaths shows the limits, but the death toll could have been far higher without planning\n• since 2011, Japan has built higher walls and moved some homes to higher ground` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 27: an extract from a news report.\n'On 6 April 2009 a magnitude 6.3 earthquake struck L'Aquila in central Italy, killing 309 people. In the weeks before, hundreds of small tremors had been felt. A week before the earthquake, a group of scientists met and said that a major earthquake was unlikely. In 2012, six of the scientists were convicted of manslaughter, although their convictions were later overturned.'\n\nUsing Figure 27 and your own understanding, explain why it is difficult to predict earthquakes.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed explanation of why earthquakes cannot be reliably predicted, using Figure 27 and own knowledge, with comparison to volcanic prediction or the consequences of failed predictions.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation with some use of Figure 27.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic points, often restating Figure 27.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• earthquakes happen deep underground where stress cannot be measured directly\n• there are no reliable warning signs; small tremors (foreshocks) occur in many places without a large earthquake following\n• at L'Aquila the tremors were not a clear sign, and scientists could not say when or if a big earthquake would come\n• scientists can only say which areas are at risk (hazard maps) and give probabilities over many years\n• frequent false alarms could make people ignore future warnings; evacuating a city is costly\n• early warning systems (e.g. Japan) can give only seconds of warning after an earthquake starts, which is not prediction\n• in contrast, volcanoes give warning signs (ground swelling, gases) so they are easier to predict\n• the L'Aquila trial shows the pressure on scientists and the difficulty of communicating risk` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 28: a diagram showing how volcanoes are monitored. A seismometer is placed in a borehole on the volcano's flank; a tiltmeter measures the slope of the ground; a gas sensor near the crater measures sulphur dioxide; a satellite overhead records ground temperature and surface height. A text box reads: 'In the weeks before the 1991 eruption of Mount Pinatubo, Philippines, monitoring led to the evacuation of about 60,000 people.'\n\nTo what extent can monitoring and prediction reduce the risks from volcanic eruptions? Use Figure 28 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed assessment of how monitoring and prediction reduce risk, including limitations, using Figure 28 and examples, with a supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear assessment with some limitations and some use of Figure 28.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic description of monitoring methods with little assessment.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• seismometers, tiltmeters, gas sensors and satellites detect signs that magma is rising\n• warnings allow evacuation, as at Pinatubo, saving many thousands of lives\n• airspace can be closed and roads shut, e.g. during the 2010 Eyjafjallajökull ash cloud\n• monitoring equipment is expensive, so many volcanoes in LICs are not well monitored\n• scientists cannot predict exactly when an eruption will happen or how big it will be\n• false alarms are costly and may make people ignore future warnings\n• monitoring does not stop damage to buildings, farmland or infrastructure\n• it works best alongside planning (evacuation routes, hazard maps)\nConclusion: monitoring greatly reduces deaths but cannot prevent economic losses.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\n'The effects of an earthquake depend more on the wealth of the country than on the magnitude of the earthquake.'\n\nDo you agree with this statement? Use one or more named examples in your answer.\n\n[9 marks]\n[+ 3 SPaG marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: Detailed evaluation of the roles of wealth and magnitude in the effects of earthquakes. Thorough use of named examples in contrasting areas of wealth with accurate, specific detail. Well-balanced argument and a clear, substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: Clear discussion of both factors with some use of examples; the judgement may be partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: Basic points, with limited use of examples and little or no judgement.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\nWealth:\n• Christchurch 2011 (HIC, magnitude 6.3): 185 deaths; strict building codes, quick rescue, insurance funded rebuilding\n• Nepal 2015 (LIC, magnitude 7.8): almost 9,000 deaths; poorly built homes, few resources, dependence on aid, slow access to remote areas\n• wealthier countries have lower death tolls but higher economic losses (Christchurch about NZ$40 billion)\n• poorer countries suffer greater long-term effects as losses are a larger share of GDP (Nepal about one-third of GDP)\nMagnitude:\n• Tōhoku 2011 (magnitude 9.0) caused about 15,900 deaths in one of the best-prepared countries, showing magnitude matters\n• the Nepal earthquake was far stronger than Christchurch, so the comparison is not only about wealth\nOther factors:\n• depth, distance from cities, population density, time of day, secondary hazards (tsunami, landslides, liquefaction)\nJudgement: e.g. wealth largely determines deaths and recovery, but very large magnitudes can overwhelm even wealthy countries.\n\nSPaG:\nHigh performance (3 marks): spells and punctuates with consistent accuracy and uses rules of grammar with effective control of meaning; wide range of specialist terms used precisely.\nIntermediate performance (2 marks): spells and punctuates with considerable accuracy and uses rules of grammar with general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance (1 mark): spells and punctuates with reasonable accuracy and uses rules of grammar with some control of meaning; limited range of specialist terms.\nNo marks awarded (0): writes nothing, or the response does not relate to the question, or achievement in SPaG does not reach the threshold.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nTo what extent do the responses to a tectonic hazard differ between areas of contrasting levels of wealth? Use named examples in your answer.\n\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: Detailed analysis of immediate and long-term responses in contrasting areas of wealth. Thorough, accurate use of named examples. Clear, substantiated judgement on the extent of difference, recognising similarities as well as differences.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: Clear discussion of responses in contrasting areas with some use of examples; may not cover both immediate and long-term responses; partial judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: Basic description of responses, perhaps from one example only, with little comparison.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\nHIC (e.g. Christchurch 2011, Japan 2011, L'Aquila 2009):\n• immediate: well-trained national rescue teams, functioning hospitals, army deployed, temporary accommodation\n• long-term: government and insurance funded rebuilding; red zone land purchase in Christchurch; stricter building codes; new sea walls and relocation in Japan\nLIC (e.g. Nepal 2015, Haiti 2010):\n• immediate: reliance on international search and rescue teams and aid agencies; field hospitals; tents; access slowed by landslides or damaged infrastructure\n• long-term: reconstruction relied on foreign aid and loans; rebuilding was slow, with many families in temporary shelters years later; efforts to 'build back better' with earthquake-resistant designs; restoring tourism\nSimilarities:\n• both rely on local people in the first hours; both may receive international help (Japan received foreign teams in 2011)\nJudgement: e.g. responses differ greatly in speed, funding and self-reliance, but the type of response is similar.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 29: information about Iceland and Nepal.\nIceland (HIC): lies on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge; about 90% of homes are heated by geothermal energy; volcanic landscapes attract large numbers of tourists; eruptions are closely monitored and people are evacuated when needed.\nNepal (LIC): lies on a collision margin; the Himalayas attract trekkers and climbers, providing many jobs; most people are farmers in mountain valleys; the 2015 earthquake killed almost 9,000 people and tourist numbers fell sharply afterwards.\n\n'The benefits of living in a tectonically active area outweigh the risks.' Do you agree? Use Figure 29 and your own understanding.\n\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: Detailed evaluation of benefits and risks, using Figure 29 and own knowledge of named places. Considers how the balance varies with wealth or hazard type. Clear, substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: Clear discussion of benefits and risks, with some use of Figure 29 and examples; partial judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: Basic points mostly from Figure 29, with little or no judgement.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\nBenefits:\n• geothermal energy (Iceland), fertile volcanic soils (Etna, Vesuvius), tourism (Iceland, Himalayas), minerals\n• jobs and income from tourism and farming\n• people have family and community ties to the area\nRisks:\n• earthquakes and eruptions can kill large numbers, e.g. Nepal 2015, Japan 2011\n• loss of homes, infrastructure and income; tourism can collapse after a disaster\n• secondary hazards: landslides, tsunamis, liquefaction, ash clouds (Eyjafjallajökull 2010 disrupted flights)\nBalance:\n• in HICs such as Iceland, monitoring, planning and protection reduce risk so benefits may outweigh risks\n• in LICs such as Nepal, people may have little choice and less protection, so risks are higher\n• depends on the frequency and type of hazard\nJudgement must be supported.` },
      { q: `Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\n\nFigure 30: four ways of reducing the risk from tectonic hazards.\nMonitoring: using scientific equipment to detect warning signs.\nPrediction: using monitoring and past events to forecast when and where a hazard will happen.\nProtection: designing buildings and structures to withstand a hazard.\nPlanning: identifying areas at risk and preparing people for a hazard.\n\nEvaluate the extent to which monitoring, prediction, protection and planning can reduce the risks from tectonic hazards. Use Figure 30 and named examples in your answer.\n\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: Detailed evaluation of all or most of the four strategies, for both earthquakes and volcanoes where relevant, with accurate use of named examples. Considers limitations and differences between areas of contrasting wealth. Clear, substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: Clear evaluation of some strategies with some examples; partial judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: Basic description of strategies with little evaluation or use of examples.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• monitoring: seismometers, tiltmeters, gas sensors and satellites detect signs of eruption; works well for volcanoes (Etna, Pinatubo 1991) but cannot detect when an earthquake will happen\n• prediction: volcanic eruptions can often be forecast, allowing evacuation; earthquakes cannot be predicted, as L'Aquila 2009 showed; hazard maps give long-term probabilities\n• protection: earthquake-resistant buildings (base isolators, cross-bracing, Taipei 101's damper) greatly reduce deaths from shaking in Japan and New Zealand; sea walls were overtopped in 2011; expensive for LICs such as Nepal\n• planning: hazard maps, land-use zoning, evacuation routes, drills (Japan's Disaster Prevention Day), emergency kits; cheap and effective, but relies on people following instructions\n• these strategies cannot stop hazards happening; they reduce deaths more than economic losses\n• effectiveness depends on wealth, governance, and the size of the hazard (very large events, such as Tōhoku 2011, can overwhelm even well-prepared countries)\nJudgement: e.g. combined, the strategies greatly reduce risk, with protection and planning most important for earthquakes and monitoring for volcanoes.` }
    ]
  },
  '1.2': {
    name: 'Weather Hazards',
    questions: [
      { q: `Figure 1: a cross-section of the global atmospheric circulation model from the North Pole to the Equator. Three cells are shown: the Polar cell (60°N to 90°N), the Ferrel cell (30°N to 60°N) and the Hadley cell (0° to 30°N). Arrows show air rising at the Equator and at 60°N, and air sinking at 30°N and at the North Pole.\n\nUsing Figure 1, identify the type of surface air pressure found at 30°N.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `High pressure (1).\nAccept: an area of high pressure / sinking air creates high pressure.\nDo not accept: sinking air on its own without reference to pressure.` },
      { q: `Figure 2: a table showing the category of tropical storms on the Saffir–Simpson scale by sustained wind speed.\nCategory 1: 119–153 km/h\nCategory 2: 154–177 km/h\nCategory 3: 178–208 km/h\nCategory 4: 209–251 km/h\nCategory 5: 252 km/h or more\n\nA hurricane approaching the Caribbean has sustained winds of 230 km/h. Using Figure 2, state the category of this hurricane.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Category 4 (1).` },
      { q: `Figure 3: a world map showing the tracks of tropical storms over a 20-year period. Tracks are concentrated over the warm oceans between about 5° and 30° north and south of the Equator: the Caribbean and Gulf of Mexico, the north-west Pacific near the Philippines and Japan, the Bay of Bengal, and the seas north of Australia. There are no tracks along the Equator itself and none in the South Atlantic.\n\nSuggest one reason why tropical storms do not form on the Equator.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid reason, e.g.\nThe Coriolis effect is too weak / absent at the Equator so the storm cannot start to spin (1).\nAccept: the Earth's rotation does not deflect winds at the Equator.` },
      { q: `Typhoon Haiyan struck the Philippines on 8 November 2013. Sustained winds reached about 315 km/h and a storm surge of up to 5 m flooded the coastal city of Tacloban on the island of Leyte.\n\nGive one primary effect of Typhoon Haiyan.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one valid primary effect, e.g.\nAbout 6,300 people were killed (1).\nAround 1 million homes were damaged or destroyed (1).\nTacloban airport terminal was badly damaged (1).\nPower lines were brought down / electricity was cut off (1).\nCrops / fishing boats were destroyed (1).\nDo not accept secondary effects such as disease, looting or long-term unemployment.` },
      { q: `Figure 4: monthly rainfall (mm) at a weather station in Somerset, December 2013 to March 2014, compared with the long-term average (data written for this practice question).\nDecember 2013: 165 mm (average 88 mm)\nJanuary 2014: 190 mm (average 82 mm)\nFebruary 2014: 140 mm (average 60 mm)\nMarch 2014: 52 mm (average 64 mm)\n\nUsing Figure 4, identify the month in which rainfall was below the long-term average.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `March (2014) (1).` },
      { q: `Figure 5: a table of UK weather records held by the Met Office.\nHighest temperature: 40.3 °C, Coningsby, Lincolnshire, 19 July 2022\nHighest 24-hour rainfall: 341.4 mm, Honister Pass, Cumbria, 4–5 December 2015\nLowest temperature: −27.2 °C, Braemar, Aberdeenshire (1895 and 1982) and Altnaharra, Highland (1995)\n\nUsing Figure 5, give the name of the storm that caused the record 24-hour rainfall at Honister Pass in December 2015.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Storm Desmond (1).` },
      { q: `Figure 1: a cross-section of the global atmospheric circulation model from the North Pole to the Equator. Three cells are shown: the Polar cell (60°N to 90°N), the Ferrel cell (30°N to 60°N) and the Hadley cell (0° to 30°N). At the Equator the Sun's rays are shown striking the surface at a high angle, with air rising and clouds forming.\n\nUsing Figure 1, explain why there is low pressure at the Equator.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for a developed explanation.\nThe Sun's rays are concentrated / intense at the Equator, heating the surface (1) so the air above is warmed and rises, reducing pressure at the surface (1).\nRising air cools and condenses, forming clouds and heavy rain (1) – credit as development only if linked to rising air.` },
      { q: `The UK lies at about 50°N to 60°N, within the Ferrel cell of the global atmospheric circulation. Surface winds in this cell blow from the high-pressure belt at 30°N towards the low-pressure belt at 60°N.\n\nOutline one reason why the prevailing wind in the UK is from the south-west.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `Surface air moves from high pressure at 30°N towards low pressure at 60°N, i.e. from the south (1); the Coriolis effect / the Earth's rotation deflects these winds to the right in the Northern Hemisphere, so they arrive from the south-west (1).\nAccept: south-westerly winds bring warm, moist air from the Atlantic (1) as a second mark only if linked to the source of the wind.` },
      { q: `Figure 6: a satellite image of a tropical storm in the Bay of Bengal. A spiral of thick white cloud about 500 km across surrounds a small, dark, circular cloud-free centre about 30 km wide. The cloud is thickest in a ring immediately around the centre, and curved bands of cloud spiral outwards with gaps of clear sky between them.\n\nUsing Figure 6, identify two features of the tropical storm.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark for each feature identified from the figure, up to 2 marks.\nA cloud-free eye at the centre (1).\nThe eye is small, about 30 km wide (1).\nThe storm is large / about 500 km across (1).\nThick cloud in a ring around the eye – the eyewall (1).\nSpiral bands of cloud / rain bands (1).\nRoughly circular / spiral shape (1).` },
      { q: `Figure 7: the number of named tropical storms in the Atlantic basin (data written for this practice question).\n2015: 11\n2016: 15\n2017: 17\n2018: 15\n2019: 18\n2020: 30\n\nUsing Figure 7, calculate the percentage increase in the number of named storms between 2015 and 2020. Show your working and give your answer to the nearest whole number.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Increase = 30 − 11 = 19 (1)\n19 ÷ 11 × 100 = 172.7… = 173% (1)\nCorrect answer of 173% with no working = 2 marks. Accept 172.7%.` },
      { q: `Figure 8: a map of the track of Typhoon Haiyan in November 2013 at a scale of 1 cm to 100 km. The track crosses the central Philippines from east to west, passing over Guiuan on Samar, Tacloban on Leyte, then across northern Cebu, Panay and out over the South China Sea. On the map the length of the track from Guiuan to the west coast of Panay measures 6.2 cm.\n\nUsing Figure 8, calculate the distance travelled by Typhoon Haiyan from Guiuan to the west coast of Panay. Give your answer in km.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `6.2 × 100 (1) = 620 km (1).\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Figure 3: a world map showing the tracks of tropical storms over a 20-year period. Tracks are concentrated over the warm oceans between about 5° and 30° north and south of the Equator: the Caribbean and Gulf of Mexico, the north-west Pacific near the Philippines and Japan, the Bay of Bengal, and the seas north of Australia. Most tracks start in the east of each ocean and curve westwards and then away from the Equator. There are no tracks in the South Atlantic.\n\nUsing Figure 3, describe the global distribution of tropical storms.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid descriptive point, up to 2 marks. Credit use of the figure (places, latitudes).\nFound between about 5° and 30° north and south of the Equator / in the tropics (1).\nOver oceans / seas, e.g. Caribbean, north-west Pacific, Bay of Bengal (1).\nNone on the Equator itself / none in the South Atlantic (1).\nTracks move westwards then curve away from the Equator (1).\nDo not credit explanation (e.g. warm water) on its own.` },
      { q: `Figure 9: a photograph of the Philippines after Typhoon Haiyan in November 2013. People queue for bottled water beside a road lined with collapsed wooden houses; a shop with a broken shutter has been emptied, and piles of debris surround a flooded rice field.\n\nUsing Figure 9, give two secondary effects of Typhoon Haiyan.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid secondary effect, up to 2 marks.\nShortage of clean drinking water (1).\nLooting / shops broken into (1).\nPeople made homeless / living in temporary shelters (1).\nLoss of farmland / crops leading to food shortages and loss of income (1).\nRisk of disease from contaminated water (1).\nDo not credit primary effects (e.g. houses collapsing in the wind) unless linked to a consequence.` },
      { q: `Hurricane forecasters in the USA use satellites, aircraft that fly into storms and computer models to track tropical storms and predict where they will reach land.\n\nOutline one way that monitoring and prediction can reduce the effects of tropical storms.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point, 2 for a developed point.\nSatellites track the path of the storm (1) so warnings can be issued days in advance, allowing people to evacuate and reducing deaths (1).\nPredicting the strength / storm surge height (1) means emergency services and supplies can be positioned before landfall (1).` },
      { q: `Bangladesh has built thousands of raised concrete cyclone shelters along its low-lying coast near the Bay of Bengal.\n\nOutline one way that protection can reduce the effects of tropical storms.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point, 2 for a developed point.\nCyclone shelters on stilts / raised ground (1) give people a safe place above the storm surge so fewer people drown (1).\nSea walls / embankments (1) hold back the storm surge and reduce flooding of homes and farmland (1).\nBuildings with reinforced roofs / strong foundations (1) are less likely to collapse in high winds (1).` },
      { q: `In January and February 2014 the Somerset Levels, a low-lying area of farmland in south-west England, flooded for several weeks. Villages such as Muchelney were cut off and residents of Thorney and Moorland were evacuated.\n\nOutline one social effect of the 2014 Somerset Levels flooding.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point, 2 for a developed point.\nAround 600 homes were flooded (1) so families had to move out into temporary accommodation for months (1).\nVillages such as Muchelney were cut off by floodwater (1) so residents had to use boats to reach shops, school and work (1).\nRoads / the railway line were closed (1) making it hard for people to travel to work or school (1).\nPeople suffered stress / anxiety from repeated flooding (1).` },
      { q: `Figure 10: daily rainfall (mm) recorded at a rain gauge in Cumbria during Storm Desmond, 3–7 December 2015 (data written for this practice question).\n3 Dec: 12 mm\n4 Dec: 96 mm\n5 Dec: 214 mm\n6 Dec: 31 mm\n7 Dec: 7 mm\n\nUsing Figure 10, calculate the mean daily rainfall for 3–7 December 2015. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `12 + 96 + 214 + 31 + 7 = 360 mm (1)\n360 ÷ 5 = 72 mm (1)\nCorrect answer of 72 mm with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Figure 11: mean annual temperature in the UK for each decade (Met Office data, rounded).\n1961–1970: 8.6 °C\n1971–1980: 8.7 °C\n1981–1990: 8.8 °C\n1991–2000: 9.3 °C\n2001–2010: 9.5 °C\n2011–2020: 9.6 °C\n\nUsing Figure 11, describe the trend in UK mean annual temperature between 1961 and 2020.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for the overall trend, 1 mark for use of data or detail of the pattern.\nTemperature has increased / risen steadily (1).\nIt rose by 1.0 °C, from 8.6 °C to 9.6 °C (1).\nThe largest rise was between the 1980s and the 1990s (0.5 °C) (1).\nEvery decade was warmer than the one before (1).` },
      { q: `Scientists predict that sea surface temperatures will continue to rise as a result of climate change, with water of 27 °C or more found further from the Equator.\n\nOutline one way that climate change may affect the distribution of tropical storms.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point, 2 for a developed point.\nWarmer oceans will extend further north and south of the tropics (1) so tropical storms could form and affect places at higher latitudes that are not affected now, e.g. southern Europe or further up the US coast (1).\nAccept: storms may travel further before weakening because they stay over warm water for longer (1).` },
      { q: `Figure 12: a diagram of the conditions needed for tropical storm formation. It shows an ocean surface labelled "sea temperature at least 27 °C to a depth of about 50 m", warm moist air rising above it, towering cumulonimbus clouds, and a label "location between 5° and 30° from the Equator".\n\nExplain how tropical storms form.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of the sequence of formation, with linked points, using appropriate terminology.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; isolated points or a list of conditions with little linkage.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Ocean temperatures of 27 °C or more heat the air above, causing warm, moist air to rise rapidly.\n• Rising air cools and water vapour condenses, releasing latent heat that powers the storm and makes air rise faster.\n• Rising air creates very low pressure at the surface, so more air rushes in, drawing more warm moist air into the system.\n• The Coriolis effect (stronger away from the Equator, so 5°–30°) makes the system spin.\n• Cool air sinks in the centre, forming the calm eye.\n• Low wind shear allows the storm to grow tall without being torn apart.\n• The storm strengthens while over warm water and weakens on reaching land or cooler water as the energy supply is cut off.` },
      { q: `Figure 13: a cross-section of a tropical storm. At the centre is a narrow column labelled "eye – descending air, clear skies". Either side of it, a wall of tall cumulonimbus cloud is labelled "eyewall". Further out, bands of cloud are labelled "rain bands". Arrows at the surface spiral inwards towards the centre, and arrows at the top of the storm spiral outwards.\n\nUsing Figure 13, explain the weather conditions experienced in different parts of a tropical storm.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of the weather in two or more parts of the storm, linked to air movement, with reference to Figure 13.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic description of the weather in parts of the storm with limited explanation.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Eye: air is sinking, so it warms and no clouds form – calm, dry, clear skies and very low pressure.\n• Eyewall: very rapid uplift of warm moist air forms towering cumulonimbus clouds – the strongest winds (over 119 km/h, often much more) and the heaviest rain.\n• Rain bands: further bands of rising air give heavy showers and gusty winds, separated by drier gaps.\n• Air spirals inwards at the surface because of low pressure and the Coriolis effect, and flows outwards at the top of the storm.\n• At the edges conditions are less extreme; pressure rises and winds ease.` },
      { q: `Figure 1: a cross-section of the global atmospheric circulation model, showing the Hadley, Ferrel and Polar cells in the Northern Hemisphere, high pressure at 30°N and at the North Pole, low pressure at the Equator and at 60°N, and surface winds labelled trade winds (0°–30°), westerlies (30°–60°) and polar easterlies (60°–90°).\n\nExplain how the global atmospheric circulation produces belts of high and low pressure and surface winds.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation linking heating, rising/sinking air, pressure belts and winds, using Figure 1.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; may describe pressure belts or winds without explaining them.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Intense heating at the Equator makes air rise, creating low pressure; the air moves towards the poles at high altitude.\n• The air cools and sinks at about 30°N/S, creating high pressure (hot deserts are found here).\n• At the surface, air moves from high to low pressure, forming winds: trade winds blow back to the Equator; westerlies blow towards 60°.\n• At 60° warmer air from the Ferrel cell meets cold polar air and rises, giving low pressure; air sinks at the poles giving high pressure.\n• Winds are curved by the Coriolis effect (to the right in the Northern Hemisphere), giving the north-east trades and the south-westerlies.` },
      { q: `Figure 14: a graph of global average sea surface temperature anomaly (compared with the 1951–1980 average), rising from about −0.1 °C in 1950 to about +0.7 °C in 2020. A note on the graph reads: "The proportion of the most intense (Category 4 and 5) tropical storms has increased since the 1980s."\n\nUsing Figure 14, explain how climate change might affect tropical storms.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of how climate change affects two or more of frequency, intensity and distribution, using Figure 14.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; simple statements with limited linkage.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Sea temperatures have risen by about 0.8 °C since 1950; warmer oceans provide more energy, so storms may become more intense (higher wind speeds, more Category 4 and 5 storms, as shown).\n• Warmer air holds more moisture, so storms bring heavier rainfall and more flooding.\n• More of the ocean may reach 27 °C, so storms could form further from the Equator and affect new areas (distribution).\n• Frequency: scientists are uncertain – the total number may stay similar or fall, even if more are intense.\n• Sea level rise from climate change makes storm surges more damaging.` },
      { q: `The UK is affected by a range of weather hazards, including heavy rainfall and flooding, strong winds, snow and ice, heatwaves and drought. Most of the UK's weather arrives from the Atlantic with the prevailing south-westerly winds, but at times air masses arrive from mainland Europe or the Arctic.\n\nExplain why the UK is affected by a range of weather hazards.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of why the UK experiences a range of hazards, linked to location and air masses / pressure systems.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; may list hazards with limited explanation.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• The UK lies at about 50°–60°N where warm tropical air meets cold polar air; low-pressure systems (depressions) form along this boundary and are carried towards the UK by the westerlies, bringing heavy rain and strong winds (e.g. Storm Desmond).\n• The jet stream steers depressions towards the UK; if it stays in one position, storms follow each other, causing prolonged rain (e.g. winter 2013–14).\n• Anticyclones (high pressure) that become "blocked" over the UK give heatwaves and drought in summer (e.g. 2022) and cold, frosty, foggy weather in winter.\n• Air masses from the Arctic or from Siberia bring snow and ice (e.g. the "Beast from the East", 2018).\n• The UK's island location means many different air masses can reach it, so weather is very variable.` },
      { q: `In winter 2013–14 a series of Atlantic storms brought persistent rain to southern England; January 2014 was the wettest January on record in southern England. The Somerset Levels are flat, low-lying land, much of it close to sea level, drained by the Rivers Parrett and Tone, which had not been dredged for about 20 years.\n\nUsing a named example of an extreme weather event in the UK, explain its causes.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of physical and/or human causes of a named UK extreme weather event, with accurate detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; generic causes with little detail of the example.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content (Somerset Levels 2014):\n• A strong jet stream steered a succession of depressions across southern England from December 2013 to February 2014, giving prolonged, heavy rainfall.\n• Ground became saturated, so further rain ran off quickly into rivers.\n• The Levels are flat and low-lying, so water drains slowly; high tides on the Bristol Channel prevented rivers draining to the sea at Bridgwater.\n• Rivers Parrett and Tone had not been dredged for about 20 years; silt reduced their capacity so they overflowed.\n• Other examples (e.g. Storm Desmond 2015: a deep depression with a warm, moist "atmospheric river" from the Atlantic; orographic rainfall over the Cumbrian fells; 341.4 mm in 24 h at Honister) should be credited.` },
      { q: `After Typhoon Haiyan, the government of the Philippines announced "no-build zones" within 40 m of the coast in some areas and began a programme to "build back better", including stronger schools and evacuation centres. Before the storm, about 800,000 people were evacuated using warnings from the national weather agency.\n\nExplain how planning can reduce the effects of tropical storms.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of two or more planning strategies, linked to how they reduce effects, using examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; strategies listed with little linkage to reduced effects.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Land-use planning / no-build zones keep homes away from areas at risk of storm surge, so fewer people and buildings are exposed.\n• Evacuation plans and marked evacuation routes allow people to move to safety before landfall, reducing deaths (e.g. 800,000 evacuated before Haiyan).\n• Emergency drills and education mean people know what to do when warnings are issued.\n• Building regulations (stronger roofs, raised floors) reduce damage.\n• Stockpiling food, water and medical supplies speeds up the response.\n• Limitation: plans only work if warnings reach people and are trusted; poorer people may be unable to leave.` },
      { q: `Figure 15: a fact file for Tacloban, Philippines, at the time of Typhoon Haiyan (November 2013).\nPopulation: about 220,000\nLocation: at the head of a narrow, funnel-shaped bay (San Pedro Bay) facing the Pacific Ocean\nHousing: many informal wooden houses close to the shore\nHeight of land: most of the city less than 5 m above sea level\nStorm surge: up to 5 m\n\nUsing Figure 15, explain why the effects of Typhoon Haiyan were severe in Tacloban.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of two or more reasons, linking physical and human factors to severity, using Figure 15.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; reasons identified from Figure 15 with limited linkage.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• The funnel-shaped bay concentrated the storm surge, increasing its height as it reached the city.\n• Most of the city is less than 5 m above sea level, so a surge of up to 5 m flooded most of it.\n• Many people lived in lightweight informal wooden houses near the shore, which were easily destroyed.\n• A large population (about 220,000) was exposed in a small area.\n• Many people did not understand the term "storm surge" in warnings, so not everyone evacuated.\n• The airport and roads were damaged, slowing aid.` },
      { q: `Figure 16: evidence about UK weather (Met Office data).\n• All ten of the warmest years in the UK series (since 1884) have occurred since 2003.\n• On 19 July 2022 the temperature reached 40.3 °C at Coningsby, Lincolnshire – the first time 40 °C had been recorded in the UK.\n• Winter 2013–14 was the wettest winter in the UK series.\n• In December 2015 Storm Desmond brought 341.4 mm of rain in 24 hours to Honister Pass, Cumbria – a UK record.\n• The coldest temperature on record in the UK, −27.2 °C, was first set in 1895.\n\n'There is clear evidence that the weather in the UK is becoming more extreme.' Do you agree? Use Figure 16 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough evaluation using Figure 16 and own understanding; considers evidence for and against; reaches a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear discussion of evidence, with some use of Figure 16; judgement may be partial or not fully supported.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points about UK weather; limited use of evidence; little or no judgement.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\nFor:\n• Ten warmest years since 2003 shows a warming trend; 40 °C first reached in 2022.\n• Record rainfall events (winter 2013–14, Storm Desmond 2015) suggest more intense rain.\n• Other examples: droughts in 2018 and 2022; more named storms such as Storms Dudley, Eunice and Franklin in one week in February 2022; Boscastle flash flood 2004.\nAgainst:\n• The UK weather record is relatively short, so extremes may be part of natural variability.\n• Cold extremes have not become more severe – the record low dates from 1895 (equalled 1982 and 1995).\n• Records improve with more weather stations, so more extremes are measured.\n• Some extreme events happened in the past, e.g. the 1953 North Sea flood, the 1987 Great Storm.\nJudgement: e.g. evidence for hotter extremes and heavier rainfall is strong, but evidence for more extreme cold or wind is weaker.` },
      { q: `Typhoon Haiyan hit the Philippines, a newly emerging economy (NEE), in November 2013, killing about 6,300 people and leaving around 4 million people displaced.\n\nUsing a named example of a tropical storm, assess the effectiveness of the immediate responses.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough assessment of a range of immediate responses with detailed, accurate knowledge of the named storm; reaches a supported judgement about effectiveness.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear description of immediate responses with some assessment; some place-specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic description of responses; little or no assessment; generic.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content (Typhoon Haiyan):\n• Before landfall: warnings from the weather agency and evacuation of about 800,000 people – saved many lives, but many did not understand "storm surge" and stayed.\n• Emergency aid from international donors, e.g. UK sent HMS Daring and HMS Illustrious and RAF aircraft; the US sent the aircraft carrier USS George Washington; field hospitals and water purification were set up.\n• The UN and charities provided food, water and shelter; the UN launched an emergency appeal.\n• Curfews to reduce looting.\n• Limitations: damage to Tacloban airport and roads delayed aid to remote islands for several days; looting and shortages occurred; the death toll still exceeded 6,000.\nJudgement: e.g. responses were effective once aid arrived, but the scale of damage and access problems limited effectiveness in the first week.\nCredit other named examples (e.g. Hurricane Katrina 2005, Cyclone Idai 2019).` },
      { q: `Figure 17: a photograph of a raised concrete cyclone shelter in coastal Bangladesh. The building stands on pillars about 3 m above the ground, with a ramp for people and livestock. A sign reads: "Cyclone Shelter – capacity 1,500 people". Fields and small houses stretch to the sea in the background. A text box states: "Cyclone Bhola (1970) killed around 300,000 people in Bangladesh; Cyclone Sidr (2007), of similar strength, killed about 3,500."\n\nAssess how far monitoring, prediction, protection and planning can reduce the effects of tropical storms. Use Figure 17 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough assessment of several strategies, using Figure 17 and own understanding, with a supported judgement about how far effects can be reduced.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of strategies with some assessment; some use of Figure 17.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic description of one or more strategies; little assessment.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Figure 17: deaths fell from around 300,000 to about 3,500 between storms of similar strength, suggesting shelters, warnings and evacuation plans are very effective at saving lives.\n• Raised shelters protect people and livestock from storm surge.\n• Monitoring by satellite and prediction allow warnings days in advance, giving time to evacuate.\n• Planning: evacuation routes, drills, land-use zoning.\n• Limitations: strategies reduce deaths more than economic damage – crops, houses and infrastructure are still destroyed; poorer countries cannot afford enough shelters or sea walls; warnings may not reach remote areas; very intense storms (e.g. Haiyan) can overwhelm defences.\nJudgement: e.g. strategies greatly reduce loss of life but cannot prevent damage.` },
      { q: `In 2014 the Somerset Levels and Moors Flood Action Plan was published, a 20-year plan costing about £20 million. Measures included dredging 8 km of the Rivers Parrett and Tone, raising roads such as the one into Muchelney, and plans for a tidal barrier at Bridgwater.\n\nUsing a named example of an extreme weather event in the UK, evaluate the management strategies used to reduce the risk in future.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough evaluation of a range of strategies with accurate detail of a named UK event; strengths and weaknesses weighed; supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of strategies with some evaluation and some specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic description of strategies; little evaluation.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content (Somerset Levels):\n• Dredging 8 km of the Parrett and Tone increased channel capacity – quick to carry out, but needs repeating as silt returns and may simply move water faster downstream.\n• Raising roads (e.g. to Muchelney) keeps communities connected during floods.\n• Tidal barrier at Bridgwater would stop high tides blocking river flow – expensive and long-term.\n• Upstream land management (tree planting, changing farming) slows run-off – cheap and sustainable but slow to take effect.\n• Improved pumping stations and flood defences for individual homes.\n• Judgement: e.g. a combination of measures is more effective than dredging alone; climate change may make future floods larger.\nCredit other UK events (e.g. Storm Desmond: Carlisle flood defences and the Cumbria Flood Action Plan).` },
      { q: `In December 2015 Storm Desmond brought record rainfall to Cumbria. The rivers Eden, Kent and Greta burst their banks, flooding parts of Carlisle, Kendal, Keswick and Appleby. The bridge at Pooley Bridge was washed away and a flooded electricity substation left about 60,000 homes in Lancaster without power.\n\nUsing a named example of an extreme weather event in the UK, explain its social, economic and environmental effects.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough explanation of social, economic and environmental effects, with detailed, accurate knowledge of the named event.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of effects in at least two categories with some specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic description of effects; generic; may cover one category only.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content (Storm Desmond):\n• Social: over 5,000 homes flooded in Cumbria and Lancashire; families displaced for months; Lancaster homes without power; communities cut off when bridges collapsed (e.g. Pooley Bridge); schools closed.\n• Economic: insurance claims estimated at around £500 million; businesses closed; roads such as the A591 closed, harming tourism in the Lake District; farmers lost livestock and land.\n• Environmental: river banks eroded and landslides; farmland covered in gravel and silt; habitats damaged; pollution from sewage overflow.\nSomerset Levels 2014 (e.g. about 600 homes flooded, farmland under water for weeks, cost to Somerset of over £10 million, contaminated fields) should be credited.` },
      { q: `Figure 18: weather recorded every 6 hours at a coastal weather station as a tropical storm passed directly over it (data written for this practice question).\n00:00 – pressure 1004 mb, wind 40 km/h, rainfall 2 mm\n06:00 – pressure 985 mb, wind 130 km/h, rainfall 25 mm\n12:00 – pressure 940 mb, wind 15 km/h, rainfall 0 mm\n18:00 – pressure 982 mb, wind 150 km/h, rainfall 40 mm\n24:00 – pressure 1002 mb, wind 45 km/h, rainfall 5 mm\n\nUsing Figure 18 and your own understanding, explain how the structure of a tropical storm affects the weather experienced as it passes over a place.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough explanation linking the changes in Figure 18 to the structure of a storm (rain bands, eyewall, eye), with accurate use of data.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of some changes, with some reference to structure and data.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic description of changes in Figure 18; little explanation.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• 00:00: outer edge / rain bands arriving – pressure falling, winds rising, light rain.\n• 06:00: first eyewall – pressure falls to 985 mb, winds 130 km/h, heavy rain (25 mm) from cumulonimbus clouds formed by rapidly rising air.\n• 12:00: the eye – lowest pressure (940 mb, a fall of 64 mb from 00:00) but calm winds (15 km/h) and no rain because air is sinking.\n• 18:00: second eyewall – winds from the opposite direction, strongest winds (150 km/h) and heaviest rain (40 mm); dangerous because people may think the storm has passed.\n• 24:00: storm moving away – pressure rising, winds and rain easing.` },
      { q: `Figure 19: a summary of the effects of Typhoon Haiyan, Philippines, November 2013.\nPrimary: about 6,300 deaths; around 1 million homes damaged or destroyed; Tacloban airport badly damaged; power and water supplies cut.\nSecondary: about 4 million people displaced; shortages of food and clean water; looting in Tacloban; outbreaks of disease; many fishing boats and coconut plantations destroyed, so people lost their incomes.\n\n'The secondary effects of tropical storms are more significant than the primary effects.' Do you agree? Use Figure 19 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough evaluation of primary and secondary effects, using Figure 19 and own understanding, reaching a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear discussion of primary and secondary effects with some use of Figure 19; judgement may be partial.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic identification of effects; limited discussion; little or no judgement.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Primary effects caused most of the deaths (about 6,300) and immediate destruction of homes and infrastructure – loss of life is the most serious effect.\n• Secondary effects affected far more people (about 4 million displaced) and lasted much longer.\n• Loss of fishing boats and coconut plantations (coconut trees take years to regrow) meant long-term poverty.\n• Damage to the airport and roads (primary) slowed aid, causing secondary effects such as food and water shortages and looting.\n• Significance depends on the timescale and on the wealth of the country – richer countries recover from secondary effects more quickly.\nJudgement: e.g. primary effects were most deadly, but secondary effects caused greater long-term hardship.` },
      { q: `Figure 20: a timeline of the Somerset Levels flooding, 2014.\nDecember 2013 – February 2014: persistent rainfall from a series of Atlantic storms.\nJanuary 2014: Muchelney cut off; residents rely on boats.\nFebruary 2014: villages of Thorney and Moorland evacuated; main railway line between Bridgwater and Taunton closed; Dutch pumps brought in to pump water off the Levels.\nMarch 2014: dredging of the Rivers Parrett and Tone begins.\n\n'Human actions were more important than physical factors in causing the effects of an extreme weather event in the UK.' Do you agree? Use Figure 20 and a named example.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough evaluation of physical and human factors with detailed knowledge of a named UK event, using Figure 20, and a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear discussion of physical and human factors with some specific detail; judgement may be partial.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic identification of causes; little or no judgement.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\nPhysical:\n• Persistent rainfall from a series of storms over three months; January 2014 the wettest on record in southern England.\n• Saturated ground; flat, low-lying land close to sea level; high tides preventing drainage.\nHuman:\n• Rivers Parrett and Tone not dredged for about 20 years, reducing capacity.\n• Villages and roads built on low land; the railway line crossing the Levels.\n• Upstream land use (e.g. maize farming leaving bare soil) increased run-off.\nJudgement: e.g. the extreme rainfall was the main cause, but human decisions increased the scale and length of flooding and its effects on people.\nCredit other named UK events.` },
      { q: `Figure 21: two opinions about tropical storms.\nScientist A: "As the oceans warm, a greater proportion of tropical storms will reach Category 4 and 5, and rising sea levels will make storm surges more dangerous."\nScientist B: "We cannot be sure that there will be more tropical storms. Better forecasting and evacuation plans mean fewer people now die in storms than in the past."\n\n'Climate change will increase the threat from tropical storms in the future.' Do you agree? Use Figure 21 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough evaluation using both opinions in Figure 21 and own understanding of climate change and tropical storms; substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear discussion of how climate change may affect storms with some use of Figure 21; judgement may be partial.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points; limited use of Figure 21; little or no judgement.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Intensity: warmer oceans provide more energy, so more Category 4 and 5 storms (Scientist A); heavier rainfall as warmer air holds more moisture.\n• Distribution: storms may affect places further from the Equator that are less prepared.\n• Sea-level rise increases storm surge height and flooding of coastal cities.\n• Frequency: uncertain – total numbers may not increase (Scientist B).\n• Threat also depends on vulnerability: growing coastal populations increase risk, but monitoring, prediction and planning reduce deaths (Scientist B).\nJudgement: e.g. the physical hazard is likely to increase, but the threat to people depends on how well countries prepare.` },
      { q: `Typhoon Haiyan struck the Philippines in November 2013. In the years that followed, the government's "Build Back Better" plan included rebuilding homes with stronger designs, new storm-surge warning systems, mangrove replanting along the coast, and "cash for work" schemes in which local people were paid to clear debris.\n\nUsing a named example of a tropical storm, evaluate the effectiveness of the immediate and long-term responses.\n[9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): Detailed evaluation of both immediate and long-term responses; thorough, accurate knowledge of a named tropical storm; well-supported judgement about effectiveness.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Clear explanation of immediate and/or long-term responses with some evaluation; some specific detail; judgement may be partial.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Basic description of responses; generic; little or no evaluation.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content (Typhoon Haiyan):\nImmediate:\n• About 800,000 people evacuated before landfall – saved lives, but many did not understand storm-surge warnings.\n• International aid: UK warships and aircraft, US aircraft carrier, UN appeal, field hospitals, food and water.\n• Problems: airport and road damage delayed aid for days; looting and curfews in Tacloban.\nLong-term:\n• Build Back Better: stronger homes and evacuation centres; no-build zones near the coast – reduces future risk but some families were moved far from their fishing livelihoods.\n• Cash for work: income and quicker clearance.\n• Mangrove replanting reduces future storm surge.\n• Replanting coconut trees takes years; recovery slow for the poorest.\nJudgement: e.g. long-term responses should reduce vulnerability, but immediate responses were limited by access problems.\nCredit other named storms.\n\nSPaG:\nHigh performance (3 marks): consistently accurate spelling, punctuation and grammar; wide range of specialist terms used precisely.\nIntermediate performance (2 marks): considerable accuracy; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance (1 mark): reasonable accuracy; limited range of specialist terms.\n0 marks: no response or response does not relate to the question.` },
      { q: `Figure 22: deaths from three tropical storms in the USA.\nGalveston hurricane, 1900: about 8,000 deaths\nHurricane Katrina, 2005: about 1,800 deaths\nHurricane Ian, 2022: about 150 deaths\nA note on the figure states that since the 1960s, tropical storms affecting the USA have been tracked by satellite, and that warnings for Hurricane Ian were issued several days before landfall.\n\n'Monitoring, prediction, protection and planning can prevent the effects of tropical storms.' To what extent do you agree? Use Figure 22 and your own understanding.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): Detailed evaluation of all four approaches using Figure 22 and own understanding; considers limitations; well-substantiated judgement on "to what extent".\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Clear explanation of some approaches with some evaluation and use of Figure 22; judgement may be partial.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Basic description of approaches; little use of Figure 22; little or no judgement.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Figure 22: deaths fell from about 8,000 to about 150, suggesting monitoring and prediction (satellites, the National Hurricane Center) and evacuation planning greatly reduce loss of life.\n• Protection: levees, sea walls and strong building codes – but Katrina's levees failed in New Orleans, flooding about 80% of the city.\n• Planning: evacuation routes and emergency plans – poorer residents without cars could not leave during Katrina.\n• The strategies reduce but do not prevent effects: Hurricane Ian still caused very large economic damage; homes and infrastructure are still destroyed.\n• Effectiveness is lower in poorer countries (e.g. limited shelters and communications), though Bangladesh shows low-cost shelters can work.\n• Very intense storms can exceed defences; climate change may increase intensity.\nJudgement: e.g. they greatly reduce deaths but cannot prevent effects, especially economic losses.` },
      { q: `During the winter of 2013–14 the Somerset Levels were flooded for several weeks. Around 600 homes were flooded, farmland was under water, the villages of Muchelney and Thorney were cut off or evacuated, and the cost to Somerset was estimated at over £10 million.\n\n'The economic effects of an extreme weather event in the UK are more important than the social and environmental effects.' To what extent do you agree? Use a named example in your answer.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): Detailed evaluation of economic, social and environmental effects with thorough, accurate knowledge of a named UK event; well-substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Clear explanation of effects in at least two categories with some evaluation and specific detail; judgement may be partial.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Basic description of effects; generic; little or no judgement.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content (Somerset Levels 2014):\n• Economic: cost to Somerset over £10 million; farms flooded for weeks, livestock moved and crops lost; businesses closed; the main railway line and roads closed, affecting trade and tourism.\n• Social: about 600 homes flooded; residents evacuated or cut off (boats to Muchelney); stress and health risks; disruption to schools.\n• Environmental: contaminated floodwater (sewage, oil); fields left covered in silt and slow to recover; damage to wildlife habitats, although wetlands are naturally adapted to some flooding.\n• Effects are linked – economic losses (farming) also cause social hardship.\n• Importance depends on who is affected and the timescale; a high-income country can absorb the economic cost through insurance and government funding.\nJudgement: e.g. economic effects were the largest in money terms, but social effects on people and communities were more serious.\nCredit other named UK events (e.g. Storm Desmond: about £500 million insurance cost, over 5,000 homes flooded).` },
      { q: `Figure 23: a world map of tropical storm tracks over a 20-year period, with sea surface temperatures shown. Tracks are found over oceans where the surface water is 27 °C or more, between about 5° and 30° north and south of the Equator. A note on the map states: "Tropical storms form in the Hadley cell, where surface trade winds converge and air rises."\n\nTo what extent does the global atmospheric circulation explain the distribution of tropical storms?\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): Detailed evaluation of the role of the global atmospheric circulation alongside other factors (ocean temperature, Coriolis effect, wind shear), using Figure 23; well-substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Clear explanation of how the circulation and other factors influence where storms form; some evaluation and use of Figure 23; judgement may be partial.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Basic description of distribution or of conditions; little explanation; little or no judgement.\n0 marks: Nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\nGlobal atmospheric circulation:\n• Intense heating near the Equator makes air rise in the Hadley cell, creating low pressure where trade winds converge – rising, moist air is the start of storm formation.\n• Trade winds steer storms westwards across oceans; westerlies then carry them polewards and eastwards (curving tracks in Figure 23).\n• Sinking air and high pressure at 30° suppress storms, helping to explain why storms are rarely found beyond about 30°.\nOther factors:\n• Sea temperatures of 27 °C or more are needed to supply heat and moisture – explains why storms form over oceans and not on land, and why none form in the cooler South Atlantic.\n• The Coriolis effect is too weak within about 5° of the Equator, so storms cannot spin there.\n• Low wind shear is needed.\n• Climate change may extend warm water further from the Equator, changing distribution in future.\nJudgement: e.g. circulation explains the pressure and wind patterns that steer storms, but ocean temperature and the Coriolis effect are needed to explain exactly where they form.` }
    ]
  },
  '1.3': {
    name: 'Climate Change',
    questions: [
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 1: a table of atmospheric carbon dioxide (CO₂) concentration measured at Mauna Loa Observatory, Hawaii (annual means, rounded to the nearest ppm).\n1960: 317 ppm; 1970: 326 ppm; 1980: 339 ppm; 1990: 354 ppm; 2000: 370 ppm; 2010: 390 ppm; 2020: 414 ppm.\n\nUsing Figure 1, state the CO₂ concentration in 1990.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `1 mark: 354 ppm.\nAllow "354" without units.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nClimate change can be caused by natural factors and by human factors.\n\nWhich one of the following is a natural factor that can cause climate change?\nA  Burning coal in power stations\nB  Changes in the shape of the Earth's orbit around the Sun\nC  Clearing tropical rainforest for cattle ranching\nD  Growing rice in flooded paddy fields\nShade one circle only.`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `1 mark: B (changes in the shape of the Earth's orbit around the Sun).\nA, C and D are human factors.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 2: a photograph of a cross-section through the trunk of an oak tree from northern England. The annual growth rings near the centre are wide and evenly spaced. A group of about ten rings close to the bark are much narrower and darker.\n\nSuggest one reason why some of the rings in Figure 2 are much narrower than others.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid reason, e.g.:\n• Those years were colder, so the growing season was shorter and the tree grew less.\n• Those years were drier / had drought, so growth was slower.\nCredit other valid reasons linked to climate conditions in the growing season (e.g. disease, competition) as long as a reason is given, not just "it grew less".` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 3: a photograph of a scientist in Antarctica holding a long, clear cylinder of ice drilled from about 3 km below the surface of the ice sheet. Small air bubbles can be seen trapped in the ice.\n\nGive one piece of information about past climate that scientists can obtain from the ice core in Figure 3.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one of:\n• The concentration of CO₂ / methane / greenhouse gases in the atmosphere at the time (from the trapped air bubbles).\n• Past temperature (from the ratio of oxygen isotopes in the ice / water molecules).\n• Evidence of past volcanic eruptions (layers of ash / sulphate).\n• The age of each layer / how climate changed over time (annual layers of snow).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nIn June 1991 Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines erupted, sending about 15 million tonnes of sulphur dioxide into the stratosphere. Average global temperatures fell by about 0.5 °C over the following year.\n\nGive one reason why a large volcanic eruption can cause global temperatures to fall.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one of:\n• Ash / sulphur dioxide / sulphate aerosols in the atmosphere reflect incoming solar radiation back into space.\n• Particles block out sunlight so less radiation reaches the Earth's surface.\nDo not credit "smoke makes it dark" without reference to blocking or reflecting the Sun's energy.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nIn December 2015, 196 parties signed the Paris Agreement at a United Nations climate conference.\n\nWhich one of the following statements describes the main aim of the Paris Agreement?\nA  To ban the use of coal in all countries by 2020\nB  To limit global temperature rise to well below 2 °C above pre-industrial levels\nC  To plant one trillion trees by 2030\nD  To make only high-income countries reduce their emissions\nShade one circle only.`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `1 mark: B (limit global temperature rise to well below 2 °C above pre-industrial levels, while pursuing efforts to limit it to 1.5 °C).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 1: a table of atmospheric carbon dioxide (CO₂) concentration measured at Mauna Loa Observatory, Hawaii (annual means, rounded to the nearest ppm).\n1960: 317 ppm; 1970: 326 ppm; 1980: 339 ppm; 1990: 354 ppm; 2000: 370 ppm; 2010: 390 ppm; 2020: 414 ppm.\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the percentage increase in CO₂ concentration between 1960 and 2020.\nGive your answer to one decimal place. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: (414 − 317) ÷ 317 × 100, or 97 ÷ 317 × 100.\n1 mark for the correct answer: 30.6%.\nCorrect answer with no working gains 2 marks. Allow 30.5% (truncated) for 1 mark only.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 1: a table of atmospheric carbon dioxide (CO₂) concentration measured at Mauna Loa Observatory, Hawaii (annual means, rounded to the nearest ppm).\n1960: 317 ppm; 1970: 326 ppm; 1980: 339 ppm; 1990: 354 ppm; 2000: 370 ppm; 2010: 390 ppm; 2020: 414 ppm.\n\nUsing Figure 1, describe the changes in CO₂ concentration between 1960 and 2020.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid descriptive point, up to 2 marks, e.g.:\n• CO₂ concentration increased in every decade / increased steadily (1).\n• It rose by 97 ppm overall, from 317 ppm to 414 ppm (1 for use of data).\n• The rate of increase got faster over time / accelerated (1), e.g. +9 ppm in 1960–1970 compared with +24 ppm in 2010–2020 (1 for supporting data).\nMaximum 1 mark if no data is used.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 4: a line graph of Arctic sea ice extent each September (the summer minimum), 1980–2020. The line falls with ups and downs from about 7.7 million km² in 1980 to a record low of about 3.4 million km² in 2012, then varies between about 4 and 4.5 million km² up to 2020.\n\nUsing Figure 4, calculate the percentage decrease in September Arctic sea ice extent between 1980 and 2012.\nGive your answer to the nearest whole number. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: (7.7 − 3.4) ÷ 7.7 × 100, or 4.3 ÷ 7.7 × 100.\n1 mark for the correct answer: 56%.\nCorrect answer with no working gains 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 5: a table of the global average temperature anomaly (difference from the 1951–1980 average) for ten years (data written for this practice question).\n2014: 0.73 °C; 2015: 0.89 °C; 2016: 1.00 °C; 2017: 0.88 °C; 2018: 0.81 °C; 2019: 0.97 °C; 2020: 0.84 °C; 2021: 0.86 °C; 2022: 0.89 °C; 2023: 1.17 °C.\n\nUsing Figure 5, calculate the mean temperature anomaly for 2014–2023.\nGive your answer to two decimal places. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: total of the ten values = 9.04, then 9.04 ÷ 10.\n1 mark for the correct answer: 0.90 °C.\nCorrect answer with no working gains 2 marks. Allow 0.9 °C.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 6: a line graph of evidence from the Vostok ice core, Antarctica, covering the last 400 000 years. One line shows temperature compared with today (from about −8 °C to +3 °C); the other shows CO₂ concentration (from about 180 ppm to about 300 ppm). Both lines rise and fall together in four large cycles, with short warm peaks about every 100 000 years separated by long cold periods.\n\nUsing Figure 6, describe the relationship between temperature and CO₂ concentration.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid point, up to 2 marks, e.g.:\n• There is a positive relationship / when CO₂ is high, temperature is high (1).\n• Both rise and fall together in cycles (1), roughly every 100 000 years (1).\n• Low CO₂ of about 180 ppm matches temperatures about 8 °C colder than today (1 for use of data).\nMaximum 1 mark if no data or pattern detail is given beyond "they are linked".` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nThe Central England Temperature record has measured monthly temperatures in a triangle of land between Lancashire, London and Bristol since 1659. It is the longest instrumental temperature record in the world.\n\nOutline one limitation of using the Central England Temperature record as evidence for global climate change.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid limitation, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• It only covers a small part of the world (1), so it may not show what is happening to global temperatures / UK weather is affected by local factors such as the North Atlantic Drift (1).\n• Early thermometers and recording methods were less accurate / were not standardised (1), so early readings may not be comparable with modern ones (1).\n• Places in the area have become more urban (1), so the heat island effect may exaggerate the warming shown (1).\n• It only goes back to 1659 (1), so it cannot show change over the whole Quaternary period / long-term natural cycles (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nBetween 1645 and 1715, a period called the Maunder Minimum, astronomers recorded very few sunspots. This was during the coldest part of the 'Little Ice Age', when the River Thames in London froze over in several winters and 'frost fairs' were held on the ice.\n\nOutline how changes in solar output can affect global climate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid point, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• Fewer sunspots means the Sun gives out slightly less energy (1), so less solar radiation reaches the Earth and global temperatures fall, as in the Little Ice Age (1).\n• More sunspots / higher solar activity increases the energy reaching Earth (1), which can cause warming (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 7: a photograph of part of the Great Barrier Reef, Australia, during the mass bleaching event of 2016. Large areas of coral are white instead of their normal brown, green and purple colours. A caption states that sea surface temperatures were up to 2 °C higher than normal for several weeks.\n\nUsing Figure 7, outline one effect of climate change on the environment.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid effect, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• Warmer seas cause coral bleaching (1); the coral expels the algae that give it colour and food, so it may die if high temperatures last (1).\n• Loss of coral reduces habitats (1), so fish and other species that depend on the reef decline / biodiversity falls (1).\nEffect must relate to the environment, not to people (e.g. tourism income is not credited).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nAbout two-thirds of Bangladesh is less than 5 metres above sea level. More than 100 million people live in the low-lying Ganges–Brahmaputra delta.\n\nOutline one effect of rising sea levels on people in Bangladesh.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid effect, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• Salt water gets into soils and wells (1), so rice harvests fall / drinking water becomes unsafe (1).\n• Coastal land and homes are lost / flooded more often (1), so people are forced to migrate to cities such as Dhaka (1).\n• Storm surges from tropical cyclones reach further inland (1), increasing deaths and damage (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nIn 2019 the UK government announced a plan to plant 30 000 hectares of new woodland each year across the UK.\n\nExplain how planting trees can help to manage climate change.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid point, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• Trees absorb CO₂ from the atmosphere during photosynthesis (1) and store the carbon in their wood, leaves and roots / act as a carbon sink (1).\n• This reduces the amount of greenhouse gas in the atmosphere (1), so less heat is trapped / the enhanced greenhouse effect is reduced (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nHornsea One, about 120 km off the Yorkshire coast, was the world's largest offshore wind farm when it was completed in 2020. Its 174 turbines can generate about 1.2 gigawatts of electricity.\n\nOutline one disadvantage of using wind power to manage climate change.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid disadvantage, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• Wind is not constant (1), so electricity is not generated on calm days and back-up / storage is needed (1).\n• Building offshore wind farms is very expensive (1), which may raise energy bills / be unaffordable for poorer countries (1).\n• Turbines can harm wildlife / have a visual impact (1), e.g. birds may be killed by the blades (1).\n• Making and installing turbines uses energy and materials (1), so they are not completely carbon-free (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 8: a pie chart of global greenhouse gas emissions by sector (approximate figures). Energy (electricity, heat, transport, buildings and industry) 73%; agriculture, forestry and land use 18%; industrial processes 5%; waste 3%; other 1%.\n\nUsing Figure 8, compare the emissions from energy with the emissions from agriculture, forestry and land use.`, marks: 2, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `1 mark for a comparative statement, +1 for use of data, e.g.:\n• Energy produces far more emissions than agriculture, forestry and land use (1).\n• Energy is 73% compared with 18% / it is 55 percentage points higher / about four times as much (1).\nData on its own without a comparison = 1 mark maximum.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nIn parts of the Sahel in West Africa, rainfall has become less reliable and droughts more frequent. Some farmers in Burkina Faso build lines of stones (bunds) across their fields and plant sorghum varieties that ripen quickly.\n\nOutline one way farmers can adapt to climate change.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid adaptation, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• Growing drought-resistant / fast-ripening crop varieties (1), so a harvest can still be produced in a short or dry rainy season (1).\n• Building stone lines / bunds or using water harvesting (1), which slows run-off so more water soaks into the soil (1).\n• Using irrigation / drip irrigation (1), so crops get water even when rainfall fails (1).\n• Changing planting dates or the type of crop grown (1) to match the new pattern of rainfall (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nThe EPICA Dome C ice core, drilled in Antarctica, is over 3 km long and contains a record of climate going back about 800 000 years, through much of the Quaternary period.\n\nExplain how ice cores provide evidence for climate change.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Point marked: 1 mark per valid point, with further marks for development (up to 4).\n• Snow falls each year and is compressed into layers of ice (1), so the deeper the ice the older it is / layers can be counted to date it (1).\n• Air bubbles trapped in the ice preserve samples of the past atmosphere (1); scientists measure the CO₂ / methane in them to show past greenhouse gas levels (1).\n• The ratio of oxygen isotopes in the ice shows the temperature when the snow fell (1), so warm interglacials and cold glacials can be identified (1).\n• The records show CO₂ and temperature rising and falling together (1), which allows past climate to be compared with today (1).\n• Ash or sulphate layers record past volcanic eruptions (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 9: a labelled diagram of the Earth's orbit. It shows three changes: the shape of the orbit varies between nearly circular and more elliptical (about every 100 000 years); the tilt of the Earth's axis varies between about 22° and 24.5° (about every 41 000 years); and the Earth wobbles on its axis (about every 26 000 years).\n\nUsing Figure 9, explain how changes in the Earth's orbit can cause climate change.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Point marked: 1 mark per valid point, with further marks for development (up to 4). Allow reference to Milankovitch cycles.\n• When the orbit is more elliptical (eccentricity), the Earth is further from the Sun at some times of year (1), so the amount of solar energy received changes, which can trigger glacial or interglacial periods (1).\n• A greater tilt makes seasons more extreme (1); smaller tilt gives cooler summers so snow does not melt and ice sheets grow (1).\n• The wobble (precession) changes which hemisphere faces the Sun when the Earth is closest to it (1), changing the strength of the seasons (1).\n• The cycles combine to explain the regular glacial–interglacial pattern in the Quaternary period (1), about every 100 000 years (1 for data from Figure 9).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 10: a diagram of the greenhouse effect. Short-wave radiation from the Sun passes through the atmosphere and heats the Earth's surface. The surface gives off long-wave radiation. Some of this escapes to space, but some is absorbed by a layer labelled 'greenhouse gases (CO₂, methane, nitrous oxide)' and re-radiated back towards the surface. A coal-fired power station and cars on a road are shown beneath the layer.\n\nUsing Figure 10, explain how burning fossil fuels contributes to climate change.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Point marked: 1 mark per valid point, with further marks for development (up to 4).\n• Burning coal, oil and gas in power stations, vehicles and factories releases CO₂ (1), increasing the concentration of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere (1).\n• A thicker layer of greenhouse gases absorbs more long-wave radiation from the Earth (1) and re-radiates more of it back towards the surface, so less heat escapes to space (1).\n• This is the enhanced greenhouse effect (1), which raises global average temperatures (1).\n• CO₂ stays in the atmosphere for a long time / demand for energy is rising (1), so the effect builds up over time (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nGlobal average sea level has risen by about 20 cm since 1900, and the rate of rise has increased in recent decades.\n\nExplain why global sea levels are rising.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Point marked: 1 mark per valid point, with further marks for development (up to 4).\n• Higher global temperatures warm the oceans (1), and water expands as it warms (thermal expansion) so it takes up more space (1).\n• Glaciers and ice sheets on land, e.g. Greenland and Antarctica, are melting (1); the meltwater flows into the sea and adds to its volume (1).\n• Warming is caused by the enhanced greenhouse effect / rising CO₂ (1), and as warming speeds up so does the rate of melting and expansion (1).\nDo not credit melting sea ice as a cause of sea-level rise (it is already floating), but allow it as a feedback that speeds warming.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nSince 1996 the Sleipner gas field in the Norwegian North Sea has separated CO₂ from natural gas and injected about 1 million tonnes of it each year into a layer of porous sandstone about 1 km below the sea bed.\n\nExplain how carbon capture and storage can help to reduce climate change.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Point marked: 1 mark per valid point, with further marks for development (up to 4).\n• CO₂ is captured from power stations or industrial sites before it is released (1), so it does not enter the atmosphere (1).\n• It is compressed and transported by pipeline (1) and stored deep underground in porous rock / old oil and gas fields (1).\n• This reduces the amount of greenhouse gas in the atmosphere (1), reducing the enhanced greenhouse effect while fossil fuels are still being used (1).\n• Sleipner shows it can store around 1 million tonnes a year (1 for use of the resource).\nAllow a limitation as development, e.g. it is expensive / uses energy / risk of leakage (max 1).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 11: a satellite photograph of part of Rondônia state, Brazil. A 'fishbone' pattern of straight roads cuts through dark green rainforest, with pale rectangles of cleared land on either side. Smoke from fires is visible over some of the cleared areas.\n\nUsing Figure 11, explain how deforestation contributes to climate change.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Point marked: 1 mark per valid point, with further marks for development (up to 4).\n• Burning trees to clear land releases the carbon stored in them as CO₂ (1), increasing greenhouse gases in the atmosphere (1).\n• Fewer trees are left to absorb CO₂ through photosynthesis (1), so the rainforest stores less carbon / is a smaller carbon sink (1).\n• Cleared land is often used for cattle ranching (1), and cattle give off methane, a powerful greenhouse gas (1).\n• Use of the resource, e.g. smoke shows burning / large rectangles show large areas cleared (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nThe Kyoto Protocol (1997) set legally binding emission targets for high-income countries, but the USA never ratified it. Under the Paris Agreement (2015) every country sets its own target. The USA withdrew from the Paris Agreement in 2020 and rejoined in 2021.\n\nExplain why it can be difficult to reach international agreements to reduce climate change.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Point marked: 1 mark per valid point, with further marks for development (up to 4).\n• Countries are at different levels of development (1); LICs and NEEs argue that they need fossil fuels to develop, and that HICs caused most past emissions (1).\n• Reducing emissions can be costly and affect jobs, e.g. in coal mining (1), so governments may not want to harm their economy (1).\n• Changes of government can change policy (1), e.g. the USA withdrawing from the Paris Agreement in 2020 (1 for use of the resource).\n• Targets under Paris are voluntary / there are no penalties (1), so some countries do not meet them (1).\n• Some countries rely on exporting oil, gas or coal (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nThe Maldives is a group of about 1200 coral islands in the Indian Ocean. About 80% of its land is less than 1 metre above sea level. Around the capital, Malé, a 3 m high sea wall has been built, and an artificial island, Hulhumalé, has been raised about 2 m above sea level for new housing.\n\nExplain how adaptation can reduce the risks from rising sea levels.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Point marked: 1 mark per valid point, with further marks for development (up to 4).\n• Sea walls are built along the coast (1), which stop high tides and storm waves flooding homes and businesses (1).\n• Land can be raised / new homes built on higher land, e.g. Hulhumalé (1), so people can live safely above predicted sea levels (1).\n• Coastal habitats such as mangroves or coral reefs can be protected / replanted (1), which absorb wave energy (1).\n• Buildings can be put on stilts / flood-resistant designs used (1), reducing damage when flooding happens (1).\n• People may be relocated away from the most at-risk areas (1).\nAllow examples from other places, e.g. the Thames Barrier protecting London.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 12: three pieces of evidence.\n• A line graph of global average temperature anomaly since 1850: the line stays close to −0.3 °C until about 1910, rises slowly to about 0 °C by 1940, levels off until about 1975, then rises steeply to about +1.1 °C by 2020.\n• A table of CO₂ at Mauna Loa, Hawaii: 317 ppm in 1960 rising to 414 ppm in 2020.\n• A line graph of September Arctic sea ice extent: about 7.7 million km² in 1980 falling to about 4 million km² in 2020.\n\n'There is clear evidence that the climate is changing.' Do you agree? Use Figure 12 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO3 evaluation using AO2 and AO4 skills).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough use of all parts of Figure 12 with data; reaches a clear, supported judgement; considers the strength and limitations of the evidence.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear use of Figure 12 with some data; some evaluation; a judgement is given but may be partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: limited use of Figure 12; mainly description; little or no judgement.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Temperature has risen by about 1.1–1.4 °C since 1850 and the rate has been fastest since about 1975.\n• CO₂ has increased by 97 ppm (about 31%) in 60 years, showing a cause of warming.\n• Sea ice has roughly halved since 1980, showing the effect of warming.\n• Three independent types of evidence all point in the same direction, which makes the case stronger.\n• Own understanding: shrinking glaciers, sea-level rise of about 20 cm since 1900, earlier flowering / bird migration, ice cores showing CO₂ is higher now than at any point in 800 000 years.\n• Limitations: temperature records only begin in 1850; early records were less accurate and covered fewer places; there are ups and downs from year to year (e.g. levelling off 1940–1975) and natural variation; one graph alone could be misleading.\n• Judgement: likely to agree strongly because several sources of evidence agree, though short-term variation exists.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 13: a timeline bar chart showing how far back different types of climate evidence reach. Temperature records (thermometers): about 170 years. Historical records (diaries, paintings, harvest dates): about 1000 years. Tree rings: about 10 000 years. Ice cores: about 800 000 years.\n\nAssess the usefulness of different types of evidence for climate change from the Quaternary period to the present day. Use Figure 13 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO3 with AO4 use of the figure).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough assessment of at least two types of evidence, weighing strengths and limitations; uses Figure 13 effectively; clear supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear assessment of at least two types with some strengths and limitations; some use of Figure 13.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points about one or more types; little assessment.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Ice cores: the longest record (about 800 000 years), covering most glacial and interglacial cycles of the Quaternary; give both CO₂ and temperature; but only from polar ice sheets and glaciers, and dating very deep ice is less precise.\n• Tree rings: accurate to the year and can be cross-dated; show local conditions (temperature, rainfall); but only about 10 000 years and only where trees grow; growth affected by other factors such as disease.\n• Temperature records: the most accurate and global since about 1850; but very short, early instruments and coverage (few ocean records) less reliable; urban heat islands.\n• Historical records (e.g. Thames frost fairs, grape harvest dates): useful for the Little Ice Age; but subjective and local.\n• Judgement: no single type is enough; ice cores most useful for the long Quaternary timescale, instrumental records most reliable for recent change; evidence is strongest when types agree.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 14: a line graph of global average temperature anomaly from 1980 to 2000 (data written for this practice question). The line rises gradually from about +0.2 °C in 1980 to +0.4 °C in 1990, drops sharply to about 0 °C in 1992 after the eruption of Mount Pinatubo, then recovers to +0.4 °C by 1995 and continues rising to +0.6 °C by 2000.\n\nAssess the importance of natural factors in causing climate change. Use Figure 14 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO2 understanding and AO3 assessment).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough understanding of several natural factors with clear explanation; uses Figure 14 well; balanced assessment with a supported conclusion about their importance.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of at least two natural factors; some use of Figure 14; some assessment.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points about natural factors; little use of Figure 14 or assessment.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Volcanic activity: ash and sulphate aerosols reflect solar radiation; Figure 14 shows a fall of about 0.4 °C after Pinatubo, but temperatures recovered within about three years, so the effect is short-term.\n• Orbital changes (Milankovitch cycles): eccentricity, tilt and wobble explain glacial–interglacial cycles of the Quaternary over tens of thousands of years — very important in the long term.\n• Solar output: sunspot cycles; the Maunder Minimum coincided with the Little Ice Age; but solar output has not increased since the 1950s while temperatures have risen.\n• Figure 14: the long-term trend continues upwards despite the eruption, suggesting another cause.\n• Judgement: natural factors are very important over the Quaternary period and for short-term variation, but cannot explain the rapid warming of recent decades, which is mainly due to human factors.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 15: a pie chart of global greenhouse gas emissions by sector (approximate figures). Energy (electricity, heat, transport, buildings and industry) 73%; agriculture, forestry and land use 18%; industrial processes 5%; waste 3%; other 1%. Beside it are two photographs: a herd of cattle on a feedlot and rice growing in flooded paddy fields.\n\nDiscuss how human activities cause climate change. Use Figure 15 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO2 understanding, with AO3 and AO4 use of the figure).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough discussion of a range of human factors with clear links to the enhanced greenhouse effect; effective use of Figure 15 with data.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear discussion of at least two human factors with some links to greenhouse gases; some use of Figure 15.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple statements about human activities; little explanation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Fossil fuels: the largest source (73% from energy); burning coal, oil and gas for power, transport and industry releases CO₂.\n• Agriculture: cattle and other livestock release methane from digestion; flooded rice paddies release methane; fertilisers release nitrous oxide; methane traps much more heat per molecule than CO₂.\n• Deforestation: burning releases stored carbon; fewer trees absorb CO₂, e.g. the Amazon.\n• Waste: landfill sites release methane (3%).\n• Link to enhanced greenhouse effect: more greenhouse gases trap more long-wave radiation, raising global temperatures.\n• Discussion: emissions are increasing with population growth and economic development, e.g. in China and India; HICs have the highest emissions per person.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 16: three photographs with captions.\n• A polar bear on a small ice floe: "Arctic summer sea ice has roughly halved since 1980."\n• Bleached white coral on the Great Barrier Reef: "Mass bleaching events in 2016, 2017, 2020 and 2022."\n• A retreating glacier in the Alps with bare rock below the ice: "Most Alpine glaciers are shrinking."\n\nAssess the effects of climate change on the environment. Use Figure 16 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO2 understanding and AO3 assessment).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough assessment of a range of environmental effects, with explanation of the links to warming; effective use of Figure 16; judgement on scale or seriousness.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of at least two effects; some use of Figure 16; some assessment.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple descriptions of effects; little explanation or assessment.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Melting sea ice reduces the habitat and hunting grounds of polar bears and seals; less ice reflects less sunlight, causing further warming (positive feedback).\n• Warmer, more acidic seas cause coral bleaching, reducing biodiversity on reefs.\n• Retreating glaciers reduce meltwater for rivers; ice sheet melt raises sea level, flooding coastal habitats such as salt marshes and mangroves.\n• Species move towards the poles or to higher altitudes; some cannot move and may become extinct.\n• Changes in rainfall cause more droughts and wildfires, e.g. in Australia; seasons change, e.g. earlier flowering.\n• Assessment: some effects are irreversible (extinction); the most serious effects are in polar and coral reef environments; some species may benefit (longer growing seasons).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 17: two quotations.\nA rice farmer in Bangladesh: "Every year the salt water comes further inland. My rice will not grow in salty soil and the cyclones are getting stronger."\nA farmer in south-east England: "Our summers are warmer now. We have planted vines and the vineyard is doing well, but the droughts mean we have to pay more for water."\n\nDiscuss the effects of climate change on people. Use Figure 17 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO2 understanding and AO3 application).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough discussion of a range of effects on people, including positive and negative effects and contrasts between places of different wealth; effective use of Figure 17.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear discussion of at least two effects; some use of Figure 17; some contrast.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple statements of effects; little use of Figure 17.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Negative: rising sea levels and salt water flood farmland and homes in low-lying areas (Bangladesh); stronger tropical storms; lower crop yields leading to food shortages; water shortages and drought; more heatwaves causing deaths; spread of diseases such as malaria to new areas; climate refugees / migration.\n• Positive: longer growing seasons and new crops in some HICs (vineyards in England); fewer cold-related deaths; new shipping routes in the Arctic.\n• Cost of adaptation, e.g. higher water costs, flood defences.\n• Contrast: people in LICs are often more vulnerable as they depend on farming and have less money to adapt; HICs can afford defences such as the Thames Barrier.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 18: a table of sources of UK electricity generation (approximate percentages).\n2010 — gas 46%, coal 28%, nuclear 16%, renewables 7%, other 3%.\n2023 — gas 34%, coal 1%, nuclear 14%, renewables 47%, other 4%.\n\nEvaluate the use of alternative energy as a way of managing climate change. Use Figure 18 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO3 evaluation with AO4 use of the figure).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough evaluation of advantages and disadvantages of alternative energy with a supported judgement; effective use of Figure 18 with data.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear consideration of advantages and some disadvantages; some use of Figure 18.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points about alternative energy; little evaluation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Figure 18: renewables rose from 7% to 47% while coal fell from 28% to 1%, so UK electricity produces much less CO₂.\n• Wind, solar, hydro-electric, tidal and nuclear produce little or no CO₂ when generating electricity, reducing the enhanced greenhouse effect.\n• Limitations: wind and solar are intermittent and need back-up (gas still 34%); high set-up costs; visual and wildlife impacts; nuclear produces radioactive waste; HEP dams flood land.\n• Electricity is only part of emissions — transport, heating and farming still use fossil fuels.\n• Many LICs and NEEs cannot afford renewables and still build coal power stations; global effect depends on all countries.\n• Judgement: very effective where adopted, but must be combined with other strategies (carbon capture, tree planting, agreements).` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 19: two views about tree planting.\nForestry officer: "New forests take carbon out of the air for decades. They also reduce flooding and provide habitats for wildlife."\nClimate scientist: "Planting trees is helpful, but young trees absorb little carbon and forests can be cut down or burned. We cannot plant our way out of climate change while we keep burning fossil fuels."\n\nAssess the effectiveness of planting trees as a way of managing climate change. Use Figure 19 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO3 assessment with AO2 understanding).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough, balanced assessment using both views in Figure 19 and own understanding; clear supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear assessment of strengths and some weaknesses; some use of Figure 19.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points; little assessment.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Trees absorb CO₂ in photosynthesis and store carbon, acting as a carbon sink; relatively cheap and possible in most countries.\n• Extra benefits: reduce flood risk, prevent soil erosion, create habitats, provide jobs; examples such as the UK target of 30 000 ha a year or Africa's Great Green Wall.\n• Limitations: young trees absorb little carbon so benefits take decades; carbon is released again if forests are cleared, burned or die in drought; needs large areas of land, which may compete with farming; tree planting does not stop emissions from fossil fuels.\n• Protecting existing forests (e.g. stopping Amazon deforestation) may be more effective than planting new ones.\n• Judgement: useful as part of a wider strategy but not effective enough on its own.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 20: a timeline of international climate agreements.\n1997 — Kyoto Protocol: HICs agree legally binding cuts in emissions; the USA does not ratify it.\n2015 — Paris Agreement: almost all countries agree to keep warming well below 2 °C and to set their own targets, reviewed every five years.\n2021 — Glasgow Climate Pact (COP26): countries agree to 'phase down' coal power.\n2023 — Global temperatures reach about 1.4 °C above pre-industrial levels.\n\n'International agreements are the most effective way of managing climate change.' Do you agree? Use Figure 20 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO3 evaluation with AO4 use of the figure).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough, balanced evaluation of international agreements compared with other strategies; effective use of Figure 20; clear supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear evaluation with some balance; some use of Figure 20.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points; little evaluation or judgement.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• For: climate change is a global problem so needs global action; Paris includes almost all countries, unlike Kyoto; agreements set targets that drive national policies (e.g. the UK's net zero by 2050 target); regular reviews raise ambition; funding helps LICs.\n• Against: targets are voluntary with no penalties; countries may withdraw (USA left Paris in 2020); emissions and temperatures have continued to rise (1.4 °C in 2023); 'phase down' rather than 'phase out' of coal shows compromise.\n• Other strategies may be more effective in practice: alternative energy, carbon capture, planting trees; adaptation is still needed as some change is already happening.\n• Judgement: agreements are essential to coordinate action but are only effective if countries carry out their pledges through other strategies.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 21: three examples of adaptation to climate change.\n• Bangladesh: floating gardens made of water hyacinth allow vegetables to be grown during floods.\n• Kenya: farmers switch from maize to drought-tolerant crops such as sorghum and millet.\n• London: the Thames Barrier, opened in 1982, protects the city from tidal flooding and is planned to work until at least 2070.\n\nAssess how far adaptation strategies can reduce the effects of climate change on people. Use Figure 21 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO3 assessment with AO2 understanding).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough assessment of several adaptation strategies, considering how effective they are and their limits; effective use of Figure 21; clear supported conclusion on 'how far'.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of at least two strategies with some assessment; some use of Figure 21.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple descriptions of strategies; little assessment.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Agriculture: floating gardens and drought-tolerant crops protect food supply and incomes; low-cost and suitable for LICs.\n• Water supply: reservoirs, water transfers, desalination, water metering reduce shortages in droughts.\n• Sea-level rise: sea walls, raised land, the Thames Barrier protect people and property; managed retreat.\n• Other: heatwave plans, mosquito control, early warning for storms.\n• Limits: expensive engineering is unaffordable for many LICs; defences may be overwhelmed if sea levels rise more than planned; adaptation treats effects but not causes, so emissions keep rising; some places (e.g. low-lying islands) may not be able to adapt fully.\n• Conclusion: adaptation can greatly reduce effects in the short to medium term, especially in wealthy places, but has limits without mitigation.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nClimate change can be caused by natural factors, such as orbital changes, solar output and volcanic activity, and by human factors, such as burning fossil fuels, agriculture and deforestation.\n\n'Human factors are now more important than natural factors in causing climate change.' Do you agree?\n[9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO2 3 marks, AO3 6 marks).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: thorough understanding of both natural and human factors and how they affect climate; detailed, balanced evaluation of their relative importance over different timescales; well-supported conclusion; uses accurate evidence and examples.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear understanding of natural and human factors; some evaluation; a conclusion that is partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: limited understanding; mainly descriptive; one-sided or no conclusion.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Natural: Milankovitch cycles (eccentricity about 100 000 years, tilt about 41 000 years, wobble about 26 000 years) caused glacial and interglacial periods during the Quaternary; solar output (Maunder Minimum and Little Ice Age); volcanic eruptions cause short-term cooling (Pinatubo 1991 about 0.5 °C).\n• Human: fossil fuels release CO₂ (CO₂ now above 410 ppm compared with about 280 ppm before the Industrial Revolution and about 180–300 ppm in ice-core records); agriculture releases methane and nitrous oxide; deforestation releases carbon and removes a sink.\n• Evaluation: natural factors operate over long timescales or are short-lived; orbital changes are too slow to explain warming since 1850; solar output has not risen since the 1950s; the rate of recent warming is much faster than past natural changes; scientists (IPCC) conclude humans are the main cause.\n• Counter-view: natural factors still cause year-to-year variation and could become important again (a very large eruption); over the whole Quaternary natural factors have been dominant.\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance 3 marks: spelling and punctuation with consistent accuracy; grammar rules used with effective control of meaning; wide range of specialist terms used with precision.\nIntermediate performance 2 marks: spelling and punctuation with considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance 1 mark: spelling and punctuation with reasonable accuracy; reasonable control of meaning; limited range of specialist terms.\n0 marks: no response, or the response does not relate to the question, or errors severely hinder meaning.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nFigure 22: two approaches to managing climate change.\nMitigation — reducing the causes of climate change: alternative energy, carbon capture and storage, planting trees, international agreements.\nAdaptation — responding to the effects of climate change: changing agricultural systems, managing water supply, reducing the risk from rising sea levels.\n\nTo what extent is mitigation more effective than adaptation in managing climate change? Use Figure 22 and your own understanding.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO2 3 marks, AO3 6 marks).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: thorough understanding of both mitigation and adaptation with examples; detailed evaluation of effectiveness; well-supported judgement on 'to what extent'.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear understanding of both approaches; some evaluation and examples; a partly supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: limited understanding; simple points about one or both approaches; little or no judgement.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Mitigation: tackles the cause by reducing greenhouse gases, so long-term benefits for everyone; examples: UK renewables rising to almost half of electricity; Sleipner CCS storing about 1 million tonnes of CO₂ a year; afforestation; Paris Agreement aim of well below 2 °C.\n• Limits of mitigation: slow to take effect because CO₂ stays in the atmosphere; needs global cooperation (USA leaving Paris in 2020); costly; emissions are still rising in many NEEs.\n• Adaptation: helps people cope now, e.g. Thames Barrier, sea walls and raised islands in the Maldives, drought-tolerant crops in Kenya, floating gardens in Bangladesh, reservoirs and water transfers.\n• Limits of adaptation: does not tackle the cause; costs rise as warming increases; unaffordable for many LICs; some places cannot adapt (low-lying islands).\n• Judgement: mitigation is more effective in the long term, but adaptation is essential because some warming (about 1.1 °C already) is unavoidable; the two are needed together.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nThe Quaternary period began about 2.6 million years ago. Scientists use ice cores, tree rings, historical records and temperature records to reconstruct how the climate has changed since then.\n\nEvaluate the evidence that the climate has changed from the beginning of the Quaternary period to the present day.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO2 3 marks, AO3 6 marks).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: thorough understanding of a range of evidence across different timescales; detailed evaluation of the reliability and limitations of each type; well-supported overall judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear understanding of at least two types of evidence; some evaluation of strengths and limitations; partly supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: limited understanding; describes evidence with little evaluation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• The Quaternary has had repeated glacial and interglacial periods; the last glacial ended about 11 700 years ago; the Little Ice Age (about 1300–1870) and warming since 1850.\n• Ice cores (EPICA Dome C about 800 000 years; Vostok about 400 000 years): CO₂ in air bubbles and oxygen isotopes show glacial cycles; strengths — long, continuous record; limitations — only polar areas; deep layers compressed and harder to date; do not cover the first part of the Quaternary.\n• Tree rings: precise annual dating; about 10 000 years; but only show local conditions and are affected by factors other than climate.\n• Historical records: frost fairs on the Thames, harvest dates, paintings; useful for the Little Ice Age but subjective and patchy.\n• Temperature records since about 1850: accurate, global coverage more recently; show about 1.1 °C warming; but short and early readings less reliable.\n• Other evidence: glacial landforms in the UK, retreating glaciers, sea-level rise, pollen in sediments.\n• Judgement: individual types have weaknesses, but they cover different timescales and agree with each other, so the overall evidence for climate change is very strong.` },
      { q: `Paper 1, Section A — The challenge of natural hazards.\nClimate change is affecting both people and the environment, from coral reefs and the Arctic to low-lying countries such as Bangladesh and the Maldives.\n\n'The effects of climate change on people are more serious than its effects on the environment.' Do you agree? Use examples in your answer.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO2 3 marks, AO3 6 marks).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: thorough understanding of a range of effects on people and on the environment, supported by located examples; detailed, balanced evaluation of seriousness; well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear understanding of effects on both people and the environment with some examples; some evaluation; partly supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: limited understanding; may focus on only people or only the environment; little evaluation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• People: sea-level rise threatens homes and farmland in Bangladesh and the Maldives; salt water damages crops and drinking water; more intense tropical storms; droughts reduce food production in the Sahel; heatwaves cause deaths; spread of disease; migration; costs of adaptation; some positive effects (longer growing seasons in the UK, Arctic shipping routes).\n• Environment: Arctic sea ice loss and polar bear habitat; coral bleaching on the Great Barrier Reef; melting glaciers; species shift polewards or become extinct; more wildfires; changing seasons.\n• Evaluation: effects on people are often more serious in LICs with less money to adapt, while HICs can protect themselves; environmental effects such as extinction are irreversible and also harm people (loss of fisheries, tourism, ecosystem services); people and environment are linked.\n• Judgement: depends on the place and timescale; credit any well-supported conclusion.` }
    ]
  },
  '1.4': {
    name: 'Ecosystems and Tropical Rainforests',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 1 shows a food chain in a freshwater pond in the UK:\nphytoplankton (algae) → water flea → stickleback → heron\n\nUsing Figure 1, name the primary consumer.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `water flea (1)\ndo not accept phytoplankton / algae (producer)\ndo not accept stickleback / heron` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 2 is a world map of major ecosystems. One ecosystem is shown as a wide belt between about 50°N and 70°N across Canada, Scandinavia and Russia, made up of evergreen trees such as pine, spruce and fir.\n\nWhich ecosystem is shown by this belt?\nShade one circle only.\nA  Temperate deciduous forest\nB  Coniferous (boreal) forest\nC  Tundra\nD  Temperate grassland`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `B — Coniferous (boreal) forest (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 3 shows mean monthly temperatures (°C) for Manaus, Brazil (data written for this practice question):\nJan 26.6, Feb 26.5, Mar 26.6, Apr 26.7, May 26.9, Jun 26.8, Jul 26.9, Aug 27.5, Sep 27.9, Oct 27.8, Nov 27.4, Dec 26.9\n\nUsing Figure 3, calculate the annual temperature range. Give your answer in °C.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1.4 (°C) (1)\n(27.9 − 26.5 = 1.4)` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 4 is a cross-section through the layers of a tropical rainforest. Layer X is made up of a few very tall, scattered trees, up to 50 m high, that stand above the main tree cover and are exposed to strong sunlight and wind.\n\nUsing Figure 4, name layer X.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `emergent (layer) (1)\nallow emergents\ndo not accept canopy` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 5: a photograph of a soil profile in the Amazon rainforest. Beneath a very thin layer of dark leaf litter and humus, the soil is a deep, reddish colour and up to several metres deep.\n\nGive one reason why the soil in Figure 5 is infertile.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any one from (1):\n• heavy rainfall leaches (washes) nutrients out of the soil / downwards\n• most nutrients are stored in the biomass (vegetation) not the soil\n• nutrients released by decomposition are quickly taken up by plants\n• thin humus layer / little leaf litter remains on the surface\nignore 'it is red' unqualified` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 6: a pie chart showing the estimated causes of deforestation in the Amazon rainforest (approximate figures written for this practice question): cattle ranching 70%; small-scale subsistence farming 15%; commercial crops such as soya 8%; logging 3%; mining, roads, dams and settlement 4%.\n\nUsing Figure 6, identify the main cause of deforestation in the Amazon rainforest.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `cattle ranching (1)\nallow commercial farming of cattle / beef farming\ndo not accept farming unqualified` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nIn a tropical rainforest, leaves that fall to the forest floor are broken down within a few weeks, so there is very little leaf litter.\n\nSuggest one reason why leaf litter is broken down quickly in a tropical rainforest.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any one from (1):\n• hot / warm temperatures all year (about 27 °C)\n• humid / wet conditions all year\n• conditions are ideal for decomposers / fungi / bacteria / insects to work quickly\n• no cold or dry season to slow decomposition\nignore 'lots of animals eat it' unqualified` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 7 describes part of a food web in a small pond in a UK park:\n• pondweed and algae are eaten by snails, tadpoles and water fleas\n• water fleas are eaten by sticklebacks and dragonfly nymphs\n• tadpoles are eaten by dragonfly nymphs and sticklebacks\n• sticklebacks are eaten by herons and pike\n\nUsing Figure 7, suggest how the ecosystem might change if the number of sticklebacks fell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.\n• herons / pike would have less food (1) so their numbers might fall or they may move elsewhere / eat more of other prey (1)\n• fewer sticklebacks eating water fleas / tadpoles (1) so the numbers of water fleas / tadpoles may increase, which could reduce the amount of algae / pondweed (1)\nCredit any valid chain of effects that uses the food web.\nMax 2` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nEpping Forest, on the north-east edge of London, is a small-scale UK woodland ecosystem with oak, beech and hornbeam trees. Each autumn the trees lose their leaves, which form a layer of leaf litter on the woodland floor.\n\nOutline the role of decomposers in a small-scale UK ecosystem such as Epping Forest.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.\n• decomposers such as fungi / bacteria / earthworms break down dead leaves / organisms (1) releasing nutrients into the soil which are taken up again by plant roots (1)\n• they recycle nutrients (1) so the soil stays fertile / plants can keep growing (1)\nMax 2` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 8 is a world map of major ecosystems. Tropical rainforest is shown in the Amazon Basin of South America, the Congo Basin of central Africa, and South-East Asia (including Malaysia and Indonesia), with smaller areas in Central America, eastern Madagascar and north-east Australia.\n\nUsing Figure 8, describe the distribution of tropical rainforests.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point, e.g.\n• found close to / either side of the Equator (1)\n• mostly between the Tropic of Cancer and the Tropic of Capricorn (1)\n• largest area in South America / the Amazon Basin (1)\n• found on several continents, e.g. South America, Africa and Asia (1)\n• use of named place or data, e.g. Congo Basin / Malaysia (1)\nMax 2` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 9 shows mean monthly rainfall (mm) for a location in the Amazon rainforest (data written for this practice question):\nJan 260, Feb 290, Mar 310, Apr 300, May 250, Jun 115, Jul 90, Aug 60, Sep 85, Oct 125, Nov 175, Dec 220\n\nUsing Figure 9, calculate the mean monthly rainfall. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `190 mm (2)\nTotal = 2280 mm (1)\n2280 ÷ 12 = 190 (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 10: a photograph of leaves on a tropical rainforest tree. The leaves are thick and waxy, and each narrows to a long, pointed tip. Water droplets are running off the tips.\n\nUsing Figure 10, explain how the leaves are adapted to the climate of the tropical rainforest.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying the adaptation, 1 mark for explaining it, e.g.\n• drip tips (1) allow heavy rain to run off quickly so the leaf does not rot / grow algae / fungi, and is not damaged by the weight of water (1)\n• waxy surface (1) helps water run off the leaf (1)\nMax 2` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nThe three-toed sloth lives in the canopy of the Amazon rainforest. It moves very slowly, hangs upside down from branches using long, hooked claws, and has green algae growing in its fur.\n\nOutline one way the sloth is adapted to life in the tropical rainforest.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for the adaptation, 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• algae in its fur gives it a green colour (1) which camouflages it from predators such as harpy eagles / jaguars (1)\n• long, hooked claws (1) let it grip branches and hang in the canopy where its food (leaves) is found (1)\n• slow movement / slow metabolism (1) so it needs little energy from its low-nutrient diet of leaves / is less noticeable to predators (1)\nMax 2` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 11 shows two nutrient cycle diagrams (Gersmehl models). Each has three circles for the stores — biomass, litter and soil — drawn in proportion to the amount of nutrients held. In the tropical rainforest the biomass circle is very large and the litter and soil circles are small, with thick arrows between stores. In the temperate deciduous forest the soil circle is the largest and the arrows are thinner.\n\nUsing Figure 11, compare the nutrient cycle in a tropical rainforest with the nutrient cycle in a temperate deciduous forest.`, marks: 2, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `1 mark per valid comparative point, e.g.\n• the tropical rainforest stores most nutrients in the biomass, whereas the temperate deciduous forest stores most in the soil (1)\n• nutrients are transferred more quickly / in larger amounts in the tropical rainforest (thicker arrows) (1)\n• the litter store is smaller in the tropical rainforest than in the temperate deciduous forest (1)\nPoints must be comparative.\nMax 2` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 12 shows annual deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon (km², rounded, based on Brazilian government satellite monitoring):\n2004: 27,800\n2008: 12,900\n2012: 4,600\n2016: 7,900\n2019: 10,100\n2021: 13,000\n\nUsing Figure 12, calculate the percentage change in annual deforestation between 2004 and 2012. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `−83.5% (2)\n(4,600 − 27,800) = −23,200 (1)\n−23,200 ÷ 27,800 × 100 = −83.5% (1)\nallow a decrease of 83.5% / 83% / 83.45%\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nThe Trans-Amazonian Highway (BR-230) was started in Brazil in the 1970s and runs for about 4,000 km through the Amazon rainforest from east to west.\n\nOutline one way road building leads to deforestation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.\n• trees are cleared to build the road itself (1) and a strip of forest is lost along its length (1)\n• roads open up remote areas of forest (1) so loggers / farmers / settlers / miners can reach new areas and clear more forest (1)\n• roads make it cheaper to transport timber / beef / soya to market (1) which encourages more land to be cleared for commercial activity (1)\nMax 2` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nMalaysia is one of the world's largest producers of palm oil. Large areas of rainforest in Sabah and Sarawak, on the island of Borneo, have been cleared for oil palm plantations.\n\nOutline one economic impact of deforestation in a tropical rainforest you have studied.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.\n• creates jobs, e.g. on palm oil plantations / in logging / mining (1) giving people a regular income / helping reduce poverty (1)\n• exports of palm oil / timber / beef / minerals earn money for the country (1) which the government can spend on development, e.g. roads, schools (1)\n• negative: loss of future income from tourism / sustainable forest products (1) because the forest resource is destroyed (1)\nCredit positive or negative economic impacts. Max 2` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 13: a photograph of a burnt area of the Amazon rainforest in Rondônia, Brazil, being cleared for cattle pasture. Smoke rises from burning tree trunks across a wide area.\n\nUsing Figure 13, outline how deforestation contributes to climate change.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.\n• burning trees releases carbon dioxide (1) which is a greenhouse gas that increases the greenhouse effect / global warming (1)\n• fewer trees are left to absorb carbon dioxide by photosynthesis (1) so more CO₂ stays in the atmosphere / the rainforest becomes a weaker carbon sink (1)\nMax 2` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nTropical rainforests cover a small proportion of the Earth's land surface but are thought to contain more than half of the world's plant and animal species.\n\nOutline one reason why tropical rainforests have high biodiversity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.\n• hot, wet climate all year (1) gives a long / continuous growing season so plants grow quickly and provide plenty of food and habitats (1)\n• several layers of vegetation (1) provide many different habitats / niches for different species (1)\n• rainforests are very old / have been undisturbed for a long time (1) allowing many species to evolve (1)\nMax 2` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 14: a description of a logging scheme in a Malaysian rainforest. Only mature trees above a set size are cut down; trees are removed by helicopter or along narrow tracks; the area is left to recover for 30 or more years before it is logged again.\n\nUsing Figure 14, explain how selective logging can be a sustainable management strategy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.\n• only mature trees are felled (1) so younger trees are left to grow and replace them / the forest can regenerate (1)\n• removal by helicopter / narrow tracks (1) avoids damaging surrounding trees and soil (1)\n• the canopy is largely kept (1) so the soil is protected from heavy rain / habitats are kept (1)\n• timber can still be sold / provide income (1) without destroying the forest for future generations (1)\nMax 2` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nIn a 'debt-for-nature swap', part of a country's international debt is cancelled or reduced in return for that country agreeing to protect an area of its rainforest. Costa Rica took part in several of these swaps from the late 1980s.\n\nOutline how debt reduction can help to manage tropical rainforests sustainably.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.\n• countries are under less pressure to clear forest for exports (timber, beef, minerals) (1) because they no longer need to earn as much to repay debt (1)\n• money that would have repaid debt is spent on conservation (1) e.g. national parks / forest rangers / monitoring (1)\nMax 2` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 15: a description of the Brazil nut tree in the Amazon rainforest. Its flowers can only be pollinated by large orchid bees. The hard seed pods can only be opened by the agouti, a rodent, which eats some seeds and buries others, some of which grow into new trees.\n\nUsing Figure 15, outline one example of interdependence in the tropical rainforest.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.\n• the Brazil nut tree depends on orchid bees for pollination (1) while the bees gain nectar / food from the flowers (1)\n• the agouti gets food from the seeds (1) and the tree depends on the agouti to spread / bury its seeds so new trees grow (1)\n• if one species is lost, the others are affected, e.g. without agoutis few new Brazil nut trees grow (1)\nMax 2` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 16: a description of a pond on farmland in Shropshire. After heavy rain, fertiliser washes off nearby fields into the pond. During the following summer the water turns green with algae, and dead fish are found at the surface.\n\nExplain how changing one component can affect other parts of a small-scale UK ecosystem. Use Figure 16 and your own understanding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of a chain of effects linking the change to other components; good use of the resource and / or own understanding.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic explanation, possibly a list of effects with little linkage.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• fertiliser adds nutrients (nitrates / phosphates) to the water\n• algae grow rapidly (algal bloom), covering the surface and blocking sunlight\n• pondweed / plants below cannot photosynthesise and die\n• bacteria decompose dead plants and algae, using up oxygen in the water\n• fish and invertebrates die from lack of oxygen\n• consumers higher in the food web, e.g. herons / kingfishers, lose their food source\n• other changes may be credited, e.g. removal of hedgerows, draining, introducing a new species (pike, signal crayfish)` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nEpping Forest is a deciduous woodland of about 2,400 hectares on the north-east edge of London. Beech, oak and hornbeam trees shed their leaves each autumn onto the woodland floor.\n\nExplain how nutrients are cycled in a small-scale UK ecosystem you have studied.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of how nutrients move between stores, with linked processes and some reference to a named example.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of parts of the cycle; limited linkage.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• three stores: biomass, litter and soil\n• leaves / dead organisms fall to form leaf litter\n• decomposers (fungi, bacteria, earthworms, woodlice) break down the litter, releasing nutrients into the soil\n• nutrients are also added from weathering of rock and from rainfall\n• plant roots take up nutrients from the soil, which are stored in the biomass\n• animals eat plants, and nutrients return to the litter when they die / through their waste\n• in a deciduous woodland the soil store is relatively large because decomposition is slower in cooler temperatures\n• nutrients can be lost by leaching or run-off` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 17: a climate graph for Kuching, Malaysia (data written for this practice question). The temperature line stays between 26 °C and 28 °C all year. Monthly rainfall bars are all above 150 mm, with the highest in January (about 680 mm) and the total for the year is about 4,000 mm.\n\nExplain the physical characteristics of the tropical rainforest. Use Figure 17 and your own understanding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of physical characteristics (climate and / or soils, biodiversity) with reasons; uses the resource.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of characteristics with little explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• high temperatures all year (26–28 °C) because the Sun is high in the sky near the Equator\n• small annual temperature range, no distinct seasons\n• very high rainfall (about 4,000 mm) from daily convectional rainstorms as warm, moist air rises\n• high humidity\n• soils (latosols) are deep, as weathering is rapid, but infertile because nutrients are leached and held in the biomass\n• red colour from iron oxides\n• dense, layered vegetation and very high biodiversity because of the continuous growing season` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 18: photographs of three animals from the Amazon rainforest — a spider monkey with a long tail wrapped around a branch, a toucan with a large, lightweight bill, and a poison dart frog with bright red and blue skin.\n\nExplain how animals are adapted to the physical conditions in tropical rainforests. Use Figure 18 and your own understanding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of how adaptations help animals survive, linked to the conditions of the rainforest; uses the resource.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic identification of adaptations with little explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• spider monkey: prehensile tail acts as an extra limb to grip branches and move through the canopy, where most food is found\n• toucan: large, light bill reaches fruit on thin branches and helps it lose heat in the hot climate\n• poison dart frog: bright colours warn predators that it is poisonous\n• sloth: camouflage, slow metabolism to cope with low-energy diet\n• flying frogs / gliding animals move between trees without going to the forest floor\n• many animals are nocturnal to avoid the heat of the day\n• many animals live in the canopy because that is where light, fruit and flowers are concentrated` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nThe Carajás mine in the Brazilian Amazon is one of the largest iron ore mines in the world. The Belo Monte dam on the Xingu River, completed in 2019, is one of the largest hydro-electric power stations in Brazil.\n\nExplain how mineral extraction and energy development cause deforestation in a tropical rainforest you have studied.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of both mineral extraction and energy development as causes, with developed links to forest loss and use of named detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic explanation; may only address one cause or lack linkage.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• open-pit mines require the forest to be cleared completely to reach the minerals\n• mines need roads / railways (e.g. the Carajás railway) and settlements for workers, causing further clearance\n• iron ore smelting may use charcoal from trees\n• hydro-electric dams flood large areas of rainforest behind the dam (reservoir)\n• power lines and access roads cut through the forest\n• the cheap energy attracts industry and settlement, increasing pressure on the forest\n• credit Malaysian examples, e.g. the Bakun dam in Sarawak` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nAbout one quarter of modern medicines contain ingredients that originally came from tropical rainforest plants. The rosy periwinkle, from the rainforests of Madagascar, is used to make drugs that treat childhood leukaemia.\n\nExplain why tropical rainforests are valuable to people and the environment.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of the value of rainforests to both people and the environment, with developed points.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic list of uses / values; may cover only people or only the environment.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\nPeople:\n• source of medicines (e.g. rosy periwinkle, quinine)\n• food products (e.g. Brazil nuts, cocoa, bananas) and raw materials (rubber, timber)\n• home and livelihood for indigenous people, e.g. the Yanomami\n• income from ecotourism and sustainable products\nEnvironment:\n• absorb and store carbon dioxide (carbon sink), reducing climate change\n• produce oxygen and recycle water through transpiration, influencing rainfall\n• protect soils from erosion and reduce flooding downstream\n• very high biodiversity / habitat for millions of species` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nThe Forest Stewardship Council (FSC) is an international organisation that labels timber and wood products that come from forests that are managed sustainably. Some international agreements also ban the trade in endangered hardwoods, such as some species of mahogany.\n\nExplain how international agreements about the use of tropical hardwoods can help to manage tropical rainforests sustainably.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of how agreements reduce unsustainable logging, with linked points; may refer to limitations.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic explanation with little development.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• FSC labelling lets consumers choose timber from sustainably managed forests, reducing demand for illegally logged wood\n• certified forests must replant / log selectively so the forest can regenerate\n• banning trade in endangered species (e.g. mahogany) reduces the incentive to cut them down\n• agreements between countries (e.g. on tropical timber) set rules for the timber trade\n• limitations: illegal logging is difficult to police; agreements rely on countries enforcing them; certification can be expensive for small producers` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nIn a tropical rainforest the climate, soils, plants, animals and people are closely linked. For example, indigenous people in the Amazon clear small plots, grow crops for a few years and then move on, allowing the forest to grow back.\n\nExplain the interdependence of the components of a tropical rainforest ecosystem.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of how several components depend on each other, with linked chains of reasoning.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic statements about links between components; little explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• hot, wet climate allows rapid plant growth all year\n• plants transpire, returning water to the atmosphere and helping to produce rainfall\n• plants provide food and habitats for animals; animals pollinate flowers and disperse seeds\n• rapid decomposition returns nutrients to the soil, which are quickly taken up by plants\n• the dense canopy protects the infertile soil from heavy rain and leaching\n• people depend on the forest for food, shelter and medicine; shifting cultivation allows recovery\n• if one component is changed (e.g. trees removed), others are affected (e.g. soil erosion, loss of animals, drier climate)` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 19: a cross-section through a tropical rainforest. The emergent layer (up to 50 m) receives full sunlight and wind; the canopy (about 30 m) forms a continuous roof that absorbs most light; the under-canopy has young trees and lianas climbing towards the light; the shrub layer and forest floor are dark, humid and receive about 2% of the sunlight. Large buttress roots spread out from the base of the tallest trees, and epiphytes grow on branches high in the canopy.\n\nExplain how plants are adapted to the physical conditions in different layers of the tropical rainforest. Use Figure 19 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of a range of plant adaptations clearly linked to the conditions in different layers; effective use of the resource.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some adaptations with some links to conditions; some use of the resource.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic identification of adaptations with limited explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• emergent trees have buttress roots to support their great height in thin soils and strong winds\n• tall, straight trunks with branches only at the top to reach the sunlight\n• smooth, thin bark because there is no need for protection from cold; water runs off easily\n• canopy leaves have drip tips and waxy surfaces to shed heavy rain\n• lianas root in the ground and climb trees to reach light in the canopy\n• epiphytes (orchids, bromeliads) grow on branches to reach light and take moisture and nutrients from the air and rain\n• forest-floor plants have large, dark green leaves to absorb as much of the limited light as possible\n• shallow roots take up nutrients quickly from the thin, nutrient-rich surface layer` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 20: a world map of major ecosystems together with three climate graphs. Tropical rainforest (Manaus, 3°S): hot all year and very wet. Hot desert (Riyadh, about 25°N): very hot summers and almost no rain. Tundra (Barrow, Alaska, 71°N): below freezing for most of the year with little precipitation.\n\nExplain why large-scale ecosystems are found in different parts of the world. Use Figure 20 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of the global distribution of several ecosystems, clearly linked to climate (temperature and rainfall) and latitude; effective use of the resource.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of the distribution of some ecosystems with some links to climate.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of where ecosystems are found; little explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• climate is the main factor controlling the type of ecosystem\n• near the Equator, high sun angle gives high temperatures; rising air gives high rainfall → tropical rainforest\n• about 30°N and S, sinking air (high pressure) gives clear skies and little rain → hot deserts (e.g. Sahara, Arabian)\n• savanna lies between rainforest and desert, with a wet and a dry season\n• mid-latitudes (UK) have mild, wet climate → temperate deciduous forest\n• 50–70°N cold winters → coniferous forest; beyond this, very cold, short summers → tundra\n• other factors: altitude, distance from the sea, soil type` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 21: a description of a lake in a UK nature reserve. Signal crayfish, a non-native species from North America, were introduced to the lake about 20 years ago. They eat water plants, insect larvae and fish eggs, and dig burrows in the banks. Native white-clawed crayfish have almost disappeared from the lake, and the banks have begun to collapse.\n\nAssess the impact of changing one component of a small-scale UK ecosystem. Use Figure 21 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of the impacts of a change on a range of components, with a judgement about the scale or seriousness of the impact; effective use of the resource.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some impacts on other components; some assessment.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of impacts; little assessment.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• signal crayfish compete with and spread disease to native white-clawed crayfish, causing their decline\n• they eat water plants, so fewer producers and less habitat / oxygen for other species\n• they eat insect larvae and fish eggs, so fish numbers fall; predators such as herons and otters lose food\n• burrowing collapses banks, increasing sediment and making water cloudy, reducing photosynthesis\n• the food web becomes unbalanced; impacts spread through several trophic levels\n• judgement: impact is large and long-term because invasive species are hard to remove; some species (e.g. otters, which eat crayfish) may benefit\n• credit other changes, e.g. fertiliser run-off, drought, hedgerow removal` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nUsing a case study of a tropical rainforest, explain the causes of deforestation.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of a range of causes, with well-developed links to forest loss and accurate, specific case-study detail.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some causes with some case-study detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic list of causes; little or no case-study detail.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content (Amazon or Malaysia):\n• commercial farming: cattle ranching (the largest cause in the Brazilian Amazon), soya; palm oil plantations in Malaysia\n• subsistence farming: slash-and-burn by small farmers / migrants\n• logging: hardwoods such as mahogany for export\n• road building: Trans-Amazonian Highway opened up remote areas\n• mineral extraction: Carajás iron ore mine, gold mining\n• energy development: Belo Monte dam (Brazil), Bakun dam (Malaysia) flooding forest\n• settlement and population growth: government schemes encouraging people to move into the Amazon\n• underlying causes: national debt, demand for exports, government policy` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 22 shows annual deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon (km², rounded, based on Brazilian government satellite monitoring):\n2004: 27,800\n2008: 12,900\n2012: 4,600\n2016: 7,900\n2019: 10,100\n2021: 13,000\n\nUsing Figure 22 and your own understanding, explain why the rate of deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon has changed.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of the changes in rate, clearly linked to the pattern in the data and to specific reasons; effective use of data.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some changes with some use of data.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of the trend or basic reasons; limited use of data.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• rate fell sharply from 27,800 km² (2004) to 4,600 km² (2012), a fall of about 83%\n• reasons for the fall: satellite monitoring and enforcement, more protected areas and indigenous reserves, soya moratorium / beef industry agreements, international pressure and funding (e.g. the Amazon Fund)\n• rate rose again after 2012, to about 13,000 km² by 2021 (nearly three times the 2012 figure)\n• reasons for the rise: weaker enforcement and cuts to environmental agencies, government support for farming / mining, high world demand for beef and soya, illegal land clearance and fires\n• economic conditions and changes of government policy affect the rate\n• credit reference to more recent falls since 2022` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 23: a photograph of a steep hillside in Sabah, Malaysia, where rainforest has been cleared for an oil palm plantation. Deep gullies have formed in the bare soil between rows of young palms, and the river at the bottom of the slope is brown with sediment.\n\nExplain the environmental impacts of deforestation. Use Figure 23 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of a range of environmental impacts with well-developed chains of reasoning; effective use of the resource.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some impacts; some use of the resource.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic list of impacts; limited explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• without the canopy and roots, heavy rain hits bare soil directly, washing it away (soil erosion, gullies)\n• eroded soil enters rivers, making them muddy, harming fish and increasing flood risk downstream\n• nutrients are lost quickly because the main store (biomass) is removed; soils become infertile\n• loss of habitat and biodiversity, e.g. orang-utans in Borneo\n• less transpiration, so drier local climate and less rainfall\n• burning and loss of trees release CO₂, contributing to climate change; fewer trees absorb CO₂\n• monoculture plantations support far fewer species than the original forest` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 24: two opinions about deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon.\nCattle rancher: "Clearing the forest has given my family a good income. Brazil earns billions of dollars from exporting beef and soya, and that pays for new roads and schools."\nIndigenous leader: "Our people have lived here for thousands of years. When the forest goes, we lose our food, our medicines and our way of life — and the whole world loses a store of carbon."\n\n'The economic benefits of deforestation are greater than the costs.' Do you agree? Use Figure 24 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed evaluation of both benefits and costs, with a well-supported judgement; effective use of the resource.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear discussion of benefits and costs; judgement may be partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points, may be one-sided; little or no judgement.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\nBenefits:\n• jobs and income for farmers, loggers, miners\n• export earnings from beef, soya, timber, minerals help pay off debt and fund development\n• new roads, energy (e.g. HEP) and settlements improve infrastructure\nCosts:\n• loss of land, culture and livelihoods for indigenous people; conflict over land\n• loss of medicines, future resources and tourism income\n• soil erosion and infertile soils mean farmland may only be productive for a few years\n• contribution to climate change, which has global economic costs\n• judgement: benefits often go to large companies / short term; costs are long-term and global` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 25: a photograph and leaflet for an ecolodge in Costa Rica. The lodge is built from local materials and uses solar power; guests are limited to 40 at a time; guides are trained local people; part of each guest's fee goes to protect the surrounding forest.\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of ecotourism as a strategy for managing tropical rainforests sustainably. Use Figure 25 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed evaluation of the strengths and limitations of ecotourism, with a well-supported judgement about its effectiveness; effective use of the resource.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of how ecotourism helps, with some evaluation.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points about ecotourism; little evaluation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• local people earn income as guides / staff, so they have a reason to protect the forest rather than clear it\n• fees fund conservation, e.g. rangers, research\n• small numbers of visitors and low-impact buildings limit environmental damage\n• raises awareness / educates visitors about the value of rainforests\n• Costa Rica: ecotourism is a major part of the economy and forest cover has increased since the 1980s\n• limitations: only protects small areas; flights to reach the lodge produce CO₂; income may go to outside companies; visitor numbers can grow beyond what is sustainable\n• judgement: effective locally, but cannot on its own stop large-scale deforestation` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nTumucumaque Mountains National Park in Amapá, northern Brazil, covers about 38,000 km² of rainforest, making it one of the largest rainforest national parks in the world. Logging, farming and mining are not allowed inside the park.\n\nAssess the effectiveness of conservation and education in managing tropical rainforests sustainably.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of conservation and education, with a well-supported judgement about effectiveness and use of examples.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of how conservation and / or education help; some assessment.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points; little assessment.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• national parks / reserves legally protect large areas from logging, farming and mining\n• indigenous reserves give land rights to people who manage the forest sustainably\n• conservation protects biodiversity and carbon stores\n• education teaches local people and consumers about the value of rainforests and sustainable alternatives, reducing demand for unsustainable products\n• education in schools / farmer training (e.g. agroforestry) can change long-term behaviour\n• limitations: protected areas are large and difficult to patrol; illegal logging and mining continue; governments may reduce protection; education is slow to have an effect\n• judgement on how effective these strategies are, ideally comparing them` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 28 shows the area of oil palm plantations in Malaysia (data written for this practice question):\n1990: 2.0 million hectares\n2000: 3.4 million hectares\n2010: 4.9 million hectares\n2020: 5.9 million hectares\nA text box notes that logging roads often reach an area of forest first, and that the Bakun dam in Sarawak flooded about 700 km² of land.\n\nAssess the extent to which commercial farming is the main cause of deforestation in a tropical rainforest you have studied. Use Figure 28 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of commercial farming compared with other causes, with a well-supported judgement; effective use of data and case-study detail.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of commercial farming and some other causes; some assessment.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic list of causes; little assessment or use of data.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Figure 28: plantation area almost tripled, from 2.0 to 5.9 million hectares (an increase of 3.9 million hectares, about 195%), suggesting commercial farming is a major cause\n• palm oil is a valuable export; world demand for food, cosmetics and biofuels drives clearance\n• in the Amazon, cattle ranching and soya are the largest causes\n• logging often comes first, removing valuable hardwoods and building roads that open the forest to farmers\n• energy development (Bakun dam, Belo Monte dam), mineral extraction, settlement and population growth also cause clearance\n• subsistence farming by small farmers / migrants\n• judgement: commercial farming is the largest single cause, but causes are linked and other causes enable it` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\n'The economic development of tropical rainforest areas is more important than protecting the environment.'\n\nTo what extent do you agree with this statement? Use a case study of a tropical rainforest in your answer.\n[9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `AO1 / AO2 / AO3. Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed knowledge of the case study; thorough evaluation of both economic development and environmental impacts; a substantiated judgement about the extent of agreement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear knowledge with some case-study detail; reasonable evaluation; a partly supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic knowledge; limited or one-sided discussion; little or no judgement.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content (Amazon or Malaysia):\nFor economic development:\n• jobs and income from cattle ranching, soya, palm oil, logging, mining (Carajás), HEP (Belo Monte, Bakun)\n• export earnings, repaying debt and funding schools, healthcare and infrastructure\n• roads and energy supplies improve quality of life and attract investment\nFor protecting the environment:\n• soil erosion and infertile soils make cleared land unproductive in the long term\n• loss of biodiversity, medicines and the livelihoods of indigenous people\n• contribution to climate change; loss of carbon sink; changes to rainfall\n• sustainable strategies (selective logging, ecotourism, sustainable products) can give economic benefits without destroying the forest\nJudgement: e.g. development is needed but should be sustainable; the long-term and global costs outweigh short-term gains.\n\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance 3: spelling and punctuation with consistent accuracy; rules of grammar used with effective control of meaning; wide range of specialist terms used precisely.\nIntermediate performance 2: spelling and punctuation with considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance 1: spelling and punctuation with reasonable accuracy; meaning not significantly hindered; limited range of specialist terms.\n0: no relevant content or errors severely hinder meaning.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 26: a description of sustainable management in tropical rainforests. A map of Malaysia shows areas of FSC-certified forest in Sabah, Taman Negara National Park in Peninsular Malaysia, and areas of replanting after selective logging. A text box states that Malaysia's forest cover fell from about 68% of its land area in 1990 to about 58% in 2020 (approximate figures written for this practice question).\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of strategies used to manage tropical rainforests sustainably. Use Figure 26 and your own understanding.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO2 / AO3. Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed understanding of a range of strategies; thorough evaluation of their strengths and weaknesses; a substantiated judgement about effectiveness; effective use of the resource and examples.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear understanding of some strategies; some evaluation; a partly supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic description of strategies; little evaluation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\n• selective logging and replanting: forest can regenerate, but logging still damages other trees; replanted areas have lower biodiversity\n• conservation: national parks such as Taman Negara protect large areas, but enforcement is difficult\n• education: raises awareness, reduces demand for unsustainable products; slow to have an effect\n• ecotourism: income for local people, but small scale\n• international agreements (FSC, bans on endangered hardwoods): reduce demand for illegal timber, but hard to police\n• debt reduction (debt-for-nature swaps): reduces pressure to clear forest; relatively few examples\n• use of the resource: forest cover is still falling (about 68% to about 58%), suggesting strategies have slowed but not stopped deforestation\n• judgement: a combination of strategies is most effective; success depends on government enforcement and global demand` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\n'Tropical rainforests are more valuable to the world as a whole than to the people who live in or near them.'\n\nDo you agree with this statement? Use examples in your answer.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `AO1 / AO2 / AO3. Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed knowledge of the value of rainforests at different scales; thorough evaluation; a substantiated judgement; well-used examples.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear knowledge of the value of rainforests; some evaluation; a partly supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic list of values; little evaluation or judgement.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\nValue to the world:\n• carbon sink, regulating global climate; producing oxygen\n• biodiversity and gene pool; source of medicines (e.g. rosy periwinkle) and food products used worldwide\n• influence on rainfall and atmospheric circulation beyond the region\nValue to local and national people:\n• home, food, shelter and medicines for indigenous people (e.g. Yanomami, Penan in Sarawak)\n• jobs and income from timber, farming, mining, ecotourism; export earnings for countries such as Brazil and Malaysia\n• local water supply, flood control, soil protection\nEvaluation:\n• local people may gain most from using the forest, whereas the world gains most from conserving it — a conflict of interest\n• global value can be turned into local income (e.g. carbon credits, ecotourism, debt-for-nature swaps)\nJudgement: e.g. rainforests are valuable at every scale; the global value is greater in the long term, but local needs must be met for protection to work.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world.\n\nFigure 27: a photograph of an area of the Amazon rainforest in Pará, Brazil, ten years after clearance. Patches of thin grass and scrub cover red, compacted soil, with a few cattle grazing. A sign shows that the land has been abandoned. The surrounding rainforest edge is visible in the distance.\n\nTo what extent is the loss of biodiversity the most serious impact of deforestation? Use Figure 27 and a case study of a tropical rainforest in your answer.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `AO1 / AO2 / AO3. Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed knowledge of a range of impacts; thorough evaluation of their relative seriousness; a substantiated judgement; effective use of the resource and case study.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear knowledge of impacts with some case-study detail; some evaluation; a partly supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic knowledge of impacts; little evaluation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\n\nIndicative content:\nBiodiversity:\n• habitat loss threatens species with extinction, e.g. jaguar, harpy eagle in the Amazon; orang-utan in Borneo\n• interdependence means the loss of one species affects others (e.g. Brazil nut tree, agouti, orchid bees)\n• loss of potential medicines and food sources; loss is permanent\nOther impacts:\n• soil erosion and loss of fertility — Figure 27 shows land abandoned within ten years\n• climate change: release of CO₂ and loss of carbon sink; reduced rainfall locally\n• impacts on indigenous people: loss of land, culture and livelihoods\n• economic development: jobs and exports (a positive impact)\nJudgement: e.g. biodiversity loss is irreversible and global, so is arguably the most serious; but climate change affects more people worldwide; impacts are linked (soil erosion and climate change also reduce biodiversity).` }
    ]
  },
  '1.5': {
    name: 'Hot Deserts',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 1: climate data for a weather station in the central Sahara, Algeria (data written for this practice question).\nMean monthly temperature (°C): Jan 13, Feb 15, Mar 19, Apr 24, May 29, Jun 34, Jul 36, Aug 35, Sep 32, Oct 26, Nov 19, Dec 14.\nMonthly rainfall (mm): Jan 3, Feb 2, Mar 2, Apr 1, May 1, Jun 0, Jul 0, Aug 1, Sep 1, Oct 1, Nov 2, Dec 3.\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the annual range of temperature.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for 23 °C (36 − 13).\nAccept 23 without units.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 1: climate data for a weather station in the central Sahara, Algeria (data written for this practice question).\nMonthly rainfall (mm): Jan 3, Feb 2, Mar 2, Apr 1, May 1, Jun 0, Jul 0, Aug 1, Sep 1, Oct 1, Nov 2, Dec 3.\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the total annual rainfall.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for 17 mm.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 2: a photograph of a hot desert landscape in southern Morocco. Bare, stony ground with patches of loose orange sand; a few small, widely spaced grey-green shrubs; no trees; a cloudless sky.\n\nWhich one of the following statements about hot desert soils is correct?\nShade one circle only.\nA  Hot desert soils are deep and rich in humus.\nB  Hot desert soils are thin, sandy or stony and low in organic matter.\nC  Hot desert soils are waterlogged for most of the year.\nD  Hot desert soils are acidic because of heavy leaching.`, marks: 1, command: 'Shade', markScheme: `B (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 2: a photograph of a hot desert landscape in southern Morocco. Bare, stony ground with patches of loose orange sand; a few small, widely spaced grey-green shrubs; no trees; a cloudless sky.\n\nUsing Figure 2, give one piece of evidence that the area receives very little rainfall.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for any one of:\n• vegetation is sparse / widely spaced shrubs\n• no trees\n• bare ground / exposed rock or sand\n• cloudless sky.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nThe fennec fox lives in the Sahara. It has very large ears, thick fur on the soles of its feet and is active mainly at night.\n\nGive one way in which the fennec fox is adapted to the hot desert environment.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for any one of:\n• large ears give off (radiate) heat to keep it cool\n• furry soles protect its feet from hot sand / help it walk on sand\n• nocturnal – avoids the highest daytime temperatures\n• can get most of its water from its food.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 3: a world map of hot deserts. The deserts form two belts, one centred on about 30°N (Sahara, Arabian, Thar, Sonoran) and one centred on about 30°S (Namib, Kalahari, Atacama, Great Victoria / Australian deserts).\n\nSuggest one reason why hot deserts are found at about 30° north and south of the Equator.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for any one of:\n• air sinks / descends here (high pressure)\n• sinking air warms so clouds do not form / little rain\n• this is where the Hadley cell air descends.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 3: a world map of hot deserts. The deserts form two belts, one centred on about 30°N (Sahara, Arabian, Thar, Sonoran) and one centred on about 30°S (Namib, Kalahari, Atacama, Great Victoria / Australian deserts). Most lie on the western sides or interiors of continents.\n\nUsing Figure 3, describe the distribution of hot deserts.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point:\n• found in two belts around 30°N and 30°S / along the Tropics of Cancer and Capricorn (1)\n• found on the western side of continents / in continental interiors (1)\n• use of a named example with location, e.g. the Sahara across northern Africa / the Atacama on the west coast of South America (1)\n• the largest area is in northern Africa and the Middle East (1).\nNo credit for explanation.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 1: climate data for a weather station in the central Sahara, Algeria (data written for this practice question).\nMean monthly temperature (°C): Jan 13, Feb 15, Mar 19, Apr 24, May 29, Jun 34, Jul 36, Aug 35, Sep 32, Oct 26, Nov 19, Dec 14.\n\nUsing Figure 1, describe the pattern of temperature during the year.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point:\n• temperatures rise from January to a peak in July (36 °C) (1)\n• then fall to December / lowest in January (13 °C) (1)\n• temperatures above 30 °C from June to September (1)\n• annual range is large – 23 °C (1).\nMax 1 mark if no data used.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 1: climate data for a weather station in the central Sahara, Algeria (data written for this practice question).\nMean monthly temperature (°C): Jan 13, Feb 15, Mar 19, Apr 24, May 29, Jun 34, Jul 36, Aug 35, Sep 32, Oct 26, Nov 19, Dec 14.\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the mean annual temperature. Give your answer to one decimal place. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2 marks for 24.7 °C.\n1 mark for working: 296 ÷ 12 (or 24.67 / 24.666… without rounding).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 4: climate data for a weather station in the Thar Desert, Rajasthan, India (data written for this practice question).\nMonthly rainfall (mm): Jan 2, Feb 3, Mar 3, Apr 2, May 6, Jun 18, Jul 70, Aug 65, Sep 20, Oct 2, Nov 1, Dec 1. Total 193 mm.\n\nUsing Figure 4, calculate the percentage of the annual rainfall that falls in July and August. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2 marks for 70% (accept 69.9%).\n1 mark for working: (70 + 65) = 135; 135 ÷ 193 × 100.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 5: a photograph of a saguaro cactus in the Sonoran Desert, USA. A tall green stem with folds (pleats) along its length, covered in sharp spines, with a waxy shine; the ground around it is bare and stony.\n\nUsing Figure 5, outline one way the saguaro cactus is adapted to the hot desert climate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for an adaptation, +1 for development (how it helps):\n• spines instead of leaves (1) reduce the surface area so less water is lost by transpiration / deter animals from taking its stored water (1)\n• pleated / folded stem (1) can expand to store water after rain (1)\n• thick waxy skin (1) reduces water loss by transpiration (1)\n• shallow, widespread roots (not visible, own knowledge accepted) (1) collect water quickly over a wide area after rain (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nThe dromedary camel is used by people to cross the Sahara. It can go several days without drinking and can drink about 100 litres of water in around ten minutes.\n\nOutline one way the camel is adapted to survive in a hot desert.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for an adaptation, +1 for development:\n• hump stores fat (1) which can be broken down for energy when food is scarce / fat is concentrated in one place so the rest of the body loses heat more easily (1)\n• can drink large amounts quickly (1) so it can survive long periods between water sources (1)\n• long eyelashes / nostrils that can close (1) keep out blowing sand (1)\n• wide, padded feet (1) spread its weight so it does not sink into sand (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 6: woodland cover within 10 km of a village in the Sahel, northern Burkina Faso (data written for this practice question).\n1990: 38 km². 2020: 23 km².\n\nUsing Figure 6, calculate the percentage decrease in woodland cover between 1990 and 2020. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2 marks for 39.5% (accept 39% or 39.47%).\n1 mark for working: (38 − 23) = 15; 15 ÷ 38 × 100.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nIn the Sahel, on the southern fringe of the Sahara, most rural households cook on open fires.\n\nOutline one way that the removal of fuelwood can lead to desertification.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, +1 for development:\n• trees and shrubs are cut down for firewood (1) so roots no longer bind the soil and it is eroded by wind and rain (1)\n• loss of tree cover exposes the soil to the sun (1) so it dries out and loses organic matter / fertility (1)\n• fewer trees means less shade and leaf litter (1) so the land becomes unable to support vegetation (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 7: a photograph of farmland at the edge of the Sahara in Niger. A field of millet with no fallow land nearby; the soil between rows is pale and powdery, and a dust cloud is being blown across the field.\n\nUsing Figure 7, outline how over-cultivation can cause desertification.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, +1 for development (reference to Figure 7 for full marks):\n• the land is farmed continuously without fallow (1) so nutrients are used up and the soil loses its structure (1)\n• the soil becomes dry and powdery (1) so it is easily blown away by the wind, as shown by the dust cloud (1)\n• crops are not replaced quickly enough to cover the soil (1) so it is exposed to erosion (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nThe Thar Desert in north-west India and eastern Pakistan has large deposits of minerals and fuels, including the Mangala oil field near Barmer, lignite (brown coal), and marble from Makrana.\n\nOutline one development opportunity that mineral extraction brings to a hot desert area.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, +1 for development:\n• creates jobs in mining / oil extraction (1), giving local people a regular income (1)\n• raises money for the government through exports / royalties (1), which can be spent on roads, schools and water supply (1)\n• supplies raw materials such as marble and gypsum (1) for construction and fertiliser industries elsewhere in India (1)\n• attracts investment in infrastructure, e.g. pipelines and roads (1), improving access to the region (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 8: a photograph of the Indira Gandhi Canal in Rajasthan, India. A wide, straight concrete-lined channel carries water across flat sandy land; green irrigated fields of wheat lie on one side and bare sand on the other.\n\nUsing Figure 8, outline one way the canal has created an opportunity for farming in the Thar Desert.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, +1 for development:\n• provides water for irrigation (1) so crops such as wheat, cotton and mustard can be grown where they could not before (1)\n• green fields next to bare sand show land has been brought into cultivation (1), increasing farm incomes / food supply (1)\n• a reliable water supply (1) allows more than one crop a year / commercial farming (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nIn Burkina Faso, farmers build lines of stones (about 25 cm high) along the contours of their fields. Many also dig small planting pits (zaï pits) which they fill with manure.\n\nOutline one way these appropriate technology methods help to reduce desertification.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, +1 for development:\n• stone lines slow down rainwater running off the land (1) so more water soaks into the soil and less soil is washed away (1)\n• stone lines trap soil and seeds (1) so vegetation can re-establish (1)\n• zaï pits collect and hold rainwater around the plant (1) so crops can grow even in dry years (1)\n• manure in the pits adds nutrients / organic matter (1) improving soil fertility (1)\n• cheap, uses local materials and labour (1) so local farmers can maintain them (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nThe Great Green Wall is a project, launched in 2007, to plant a belt of trees and restore land about 8000 km long across the Sahel, from Senegal in the west to Djibouti in the east.\n\nOutline one way that tree planting can reduce the risk of desertification.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, +1 for development:\n• tree roots bind the soil (1) so it is less likely to be eroded by wind or rain (1)\n• trees act as windbreaks (1) reducing wind speed and so soil erosion (1)\n• leaf fall adds organic matter (1) improving soil fertility and water retention (1)\n• trees provide shade (1) reducing evaporation from the soil (1)\n• trees provide fuelwood / fodder (1) so people do not cut down remaining natural vegetation (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 9: daily temperatures for a site in the Namib Desert on one day in January (data written for this practice question). Maximum 44 °C at 2 pm; minimum 9 °C at 5 am. The sky was clear throughout.\n\nExplain why hot deserts have a large daily (diurnal) range of temperature. Use Figure 9 and your own understanding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of both high day and low night temperatures, linked to cloud cover / humidity; uses Figure 9 (e.g. a range of 35 °C).\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic explanation of one part, or description of the range only.\n\nIndicative content:\n• the range is 35 °C (44 − 9)\n• clear skies allow intense insolation during the day, so the ground heats rapidly\n• the sun is high in the sky in the tropics, concentrating energy\n• bare, dry sand and rock heat up quickly because there is little vegetation or moisture\n• at night there is no cloud to trap the heat, so it is lost (radiated) rapidly to space\n• low humidity – little water vapour to absorb and hold heat.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 3: a world map of hot deserts. The deserts form two belts, one centred on about 30°N and one centred on about 30°S of the Equator.\n\nExplain why most hot deserts receive less than 250 mm of rainfall a year.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear, developed explanation linking atmospheric circulation and other factors to low rainfall.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points, e.g. "it is high pressure", with little explanation.\n\nIndicative content:\n• at about 30° N/S air in the Hadley cell descends, creating high pressure\n• sinking air warms and its relative humidity falls, so condensation and cloud formation do not happen\n• winds blow outwards from these areas, so moist air is not brought in\n• continental interiors (e.g. central Sahara) are far from the sea, so air has lost moisture\n• cold ocean currents off western coasts (e.g. Benguela off the Namib) cool the air, so it holds little moisture and fog rather than rain forms\n• rain shadow effect on the leeward side of mountains.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 2: a photograph of a hot desert landscape in southern Morocco. Bare, stony ground with patches of loose orange sand; a few small, widely spaced grey-green shrubs; no trees.\n\nExplain how the climate of hot deserts affects the characteristics of the soil.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of two or more soil characteristics linked to climate.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic description of soil characteristics or limited links to climate.\n\nIndicative content:\n• low rainfall means sparse vegetation, so there is little leaf litter and the soil is low in organic matter / humus\n• dry conditions slow decomposition and chemical weathering, so soils are thin\n• high temperatures cause evaporation which draws water and dissolved salts upwards, leaving a salty crust (salinisation)\n• lack of moisture means the soil is loose, sandy or stony and easily eroded by wind\n• occasional heavy downpours wash away the thin topsoil (flash floods).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 10: a sketch of a hot desert food web (written description). Plants (cacti, acacia shrubs, ephemeral grasses) are eaten by insects, gerbils and camels. Insects are eaten by lizards and fennec foxes. Gerbils are eaten by fennec foxes and snakes. People keep camels and collect acacia for firewood.\n\nExplain the interdependence between plants, animals and people in a hot desert ecosystem. Use Figure 10.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of two or more links in both directions, using Figure 10.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic description of feeding links, little explanation.\n\nIndicative content:\n• plants are producers – they provide food (and water from their tissues) for insects, gerbils and camels\n• if plants are lost, herbivores such as gerbils decline, and so do fennec foxes and snakes that eat them\n• animals help plants, e.g. by spreading seeds in their droppings and adding nutrients to the soil\n• people depend on camels for transport, milk and meat, and on acacia for fuel\n• if people overgraze camels or cut too much acacia, the whole food web is affected and the soil is exposed to erosion\n• the harsh climate limits the number of producers, so the food web is simple and easily disrupted.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nIn the Sahel, the number of cattle, goats and sheep has increased as the human population has grown. Herds are often kept close to wells.\n\nExplain how overgrazing can lead to desertification.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of a sequence of processes leading to desertification.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points, e.g. "animals eat the grass".\n\nIndicative content:\n• too many animals for the carrying capacity of the land eat the grass and shrubs faster than they regrow\n• plant roots no longer bind the soil, so it is exposed to wind and heavy rain and is eroded\n• trampling by hooves compacts the soil near wells, so rain runs off rather than soaking in\n• less infiltration means drier soil and more soil erosion by run-off\n• with less vegetation and topsoil the land can no longer support plants or animals – it becomes desert\n• herders move to new areas, spreading the problem.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nThe Thar Desert, India: summer temperatures often exceed 45 °C, and Phalodi in Rajasthan recorded 51 °C in May 2016. Much of the western Thar is far from large cities and has few paved roads.\n\nExplain how extreme temperatures and inaccessibility are challenges to development in a hot desert you have studied.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of both challenges, with some specific detail from a named desert.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points; may cover only one challenge or be generic.\n\nIndicative content (Thar or another named desert, e.g. Sahara):\n• extreme heat makes outdoor work (farming, construction, mining) difficult and dangerous – heatstroke, dehydration\n• high temperatures increase evaporation from reservoirs and canals, so water is lost\n• machinery and solar panels may overheat; crops and animals suffer heat stress\n• the area is vast and sparsely served by roads, so it is expensive to transport goods, workers and raw materials\n• many areas lack electricity and water pipelines, so services are hard to provide\n• shifting sand dunes can cover roads and railways; tourists may find areas hard to reach.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 11: a photograph of the Indira Gandhi Canal in Rajasthan. Water in the open channel is shimmering in strong sunshine; beside the canal some fields have a white crust on the surface and crops are stunted.\n\nExplain why water supply is a challenge to development in the Thar Desert. Use Figure 11 and your own understanding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of two or more challenges, with reference to Figure 11 and specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points about lack of water.\n\nIndicative content:\n• annual rainfall is low and unreliable (often under 250 mm), mostly in the monsoon months\n• few permanent rivers; much water must come from groundwater or the canal from Punjab\n• water table is falling because wells are over-pumped for farming and growing towns\n• open canals lose a large amount of water to evaporation in high temperatures (Figure 11)\n• over-irrigation and evaporation draw salts to the surface – the white crust in Figure 11 is salinisation, reducing crop yields\n• waterlogging near the canal and seepage; groundwater may be salty.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 12: the creosote bush grows in the Mojave and Sonoran Deserts. It has small leaves with a waxy coating, a deep and widespread root system, and is evenly spaced, with bare ground between each bush. Its seeds can lie dormant for years until rain falls.\n\nExplain how plants are adapted to the physical conditions of hot deserts. Use Figure 12 and your own understanding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of two or more adaptations linked to specific conditions.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): adaptations listed with little explanation.\n\nIndicative content:\n• small, waxy leaves reduce transpiration in high temperatures and low humidity\n• deep roots reach groundwater; widespread shallow roots collect rain quickly before it evaporates\n• even spacing reduces competition for scarce water\n• dormant seeds survive drought and germinate only after rain\n• other examples: ephemerals complete their life cycle in weeks after rain; cacti store water in stems and have spines instead of leaves; some plants have tolerance of salty soils.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 1: climate data for a weather station in the central Sahara, Algeria (data written for this practice question). Temperature ranges from 13 °C in January to 36 °C in July; total annual rainfall is 17 mm, with no month above 3 mm.\nA photograph shows a date palm oasis: tall palms with long roots near a pool, surrounded by bare sand with scattered low thorny shrubs.\n\nSuggest how plants are adapted to the climate in hot deserts. Use Figure 1 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed, well-developed suggestions of several adaptations, each clearly linked to specific features of the climate in Figure 1.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear suggestions with some links to the climate and some use of Figure 1.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements of adaptations with little link to the climate.\n\nIndicative content:\n• very low rainfall (17 mm) – plants have long tap roots to reach groundwater, e.g. date palms near the oasis, acacia, mesquite\n• widespread shallow roots absorb water quickly after rare rain\n• water storage in stems (cacti, succulents)\n• high temperatures (36 °C in July) – small or spiny leaves, waxy cuticles and fewer stomata reduce transpiration; some leaves turn edge-on to the sun\n• stomata open at night when it is cooler\n• thorns protect water stores from animals\n• ephemerals and dormant seeds survive long dry periods and grow rapidly after rain\n• wide spacing reduces competition for water.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 13: three desert animals (written description).\n• Dromedary camel – hump of fat, long eyelashes, closable nostrils, wide padded feet.\n• Kangaroo rat – lives in burrows during the day, gets almost all its water from seeds, produces very concentrated urine.\n• Thorny devil lizard (Australia) – grooves in its skin channel dew and water to its mouth; it is pale in colour.\n\nExplain how animals are adapted to the physical conditions in hot deserts. Use Figure 13 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation of a range of adaptations, both physical and behavioural, clearly linked to heat, lack of water and/or sandy ground, with good use of Figure 13.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of some adaptations with some links to conditions.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): adaptations listed with little or no explanation.\n\nIndicative content:\n• lack of water: camels can go days without drinking and drink large amounts quickly; kangaroo rats get water from food and lose little in urine; the thorny devil collects dew\n• heat: burrowing / nocturnal behaviour avoids the hottest part of the day; fat stored in one place (hump) allows heat to be lost from the rest of the body; pale colours reflect heat; large ears (fennec fox) radiate heat\n• sand: long eyelashes and closing nostrils protect against sandstorms; wide feet stop sinking into sand\n• food scarcity: hump fat used as an energy store.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 14: renewable energy in a hot desert. The Noor Ouarzazate solar complex in Morocco, on the edge of the Sahara, has a capacity of about 580 MW. It uses mirrors to concentrate sunlight and also photovoltaic panels. A photograph shows rows of curved mirrors across flat, stony desert with a tall solar tower in the background.\n\nAssess the opportunities that hot deserts offer for the development of energy resources. Use Figure 14 and an example you have studied.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): thorough assessment of opportunities, with a balanced judgement (e.g. noting limitations) and good use of Figure 14 and specific example detail.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of opportunities with some assessment and use of figure / example.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points about sunshine or oil with little assessment.\n\nIndicative content:\n• high insolation and clear skies all year make solar power reliable (Figure 14)\n• large areas of flat, cheap, sparsely populated land for solar farms, e.g. Bhadla Solar Park in the Thar (one of the world's largest)\n• strong, steady winds support wind farms, e.g. Jaisalmer Wind Park\n• fossil fuels: oil at Barmer (Mangala field) in the Thar; oil and gas in the Algerian Sahara\n• benefits: jobs, export earnings, energy security, reducing carbon emissions\n• limitations: high construction costs; dust and sand reduce panel efficiency and need water for cleaning; distance from cities means long transmission lines; heat reduces efficiency of PV panels; limited local jobs once built.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 15: visitors to Jaisalmer, Rajasthan, on the edge of the Thar Desert. Tourists stay in the sandstone fort city, take camel safaris into the sand dunes at Sam and attend the annual Desert Festival. A photograph shows tourist camps with tents in the dunes and litter beside a sand track.\n\nAssess the extent to which tourism provides opportunities for development in a hot desert. Use Figure 15 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): thorough assessment weighing opportunities against problems/limitations, reaching a judgement; good use of Figure 15.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of opportunities with some assessment.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points about tourism, limited assessment.\n\nIndicative content:\n• attractions: dunes, clear skies, heritage (Jaisalmer Fort), culture and festivals\n• jobs for camel handlers, guides, hotel staff and craft sellers; multiplier effect on local businesses\n• income spent on roads and services, improving accessibility\n• preserves traditional culture as a tourist attraction\n• limitations: seasonal – few tourists in the extreme heat of May–June; leakage of profits to outside companies\n• environmental costs: litter (Figure 15), damage to dunes and fragile vegetation, extra water demand from hotels in a water-scarce area\n• judgement on how far tourism benefits local people.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 16: causes of desertification in the Sahel (written description of a diagram). Boxes labelled "Population growth", "Overgrazing", "Over-cultivation", "Removal of fuelwood" and "Climate change – lower, less reliable rainfall" all have arrows pointing to "Soil erosion", which has an arrow pointing to "Desertification".\n\nExplain how human activities can cause desertification. Use Figure 16 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation of several human causes, showing clear chains of reasoning and links between them (e.g. population growth driving the others), with use of Figure 16.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of some human causes.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): human causes listed with little explanation.\n\nIndicative content:\n• population growth increases demand for food, fuel and water, putting pressure on fragile land\n• overgrazing removes vegetation and trampling compacts soil\n• over-cultivation without fallow exhausts nutrients and breaks down soil structure\n• removal of fuelwood strips trees and shrubs, so roots no longer bind the soil\n• over-abstraction of groundwater lowers the water table so plants die\n• all lead to exposed soil being eroded by wind and flash floods – soil erosion leads to desertification\n• credit links to climate change only where they are connected to human activity (e.g. human-enhanced greenhouse effect).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 17: strategies to reduce desertification in the Sahel (written description of three photographs).\nA – lines of stones laid along the contours of a field in Burkina Faso.\nB – young trees growing in a fenced plot as part of the Great Green Wall in Senegal.\nC – a farmer cooking on a solar cooker made of reflective panels.\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of strategies used to reduce the risk of desertification. Use Figure 17 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): thorough evaluation of a range of strategies, weighing strengths and weaknesses, with a supported judgement and good use of Figure 17.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of how strategies work, with some evaluation.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic description of strategies.\n\nIndicative content:\n• stone lines (A) / zaï pits: cheap, use local materials and labour, slow run-off and trap soil – highly effective at a local scale but labour-intensive\n• tree planting (B): binds soil, acts as windbreaks, adds organic matter; but many seedlings die in drought, large schemes need long-term funding; the Great Green Wall has restored less land than planned\n• solar cookers (C) reduce the need for fuelwood, so fewer trees are cut; but cannot be used at night or on cloudy days and some people prefer traditional cooking\n• water management: drip irrigation, small dams, rainwater harvesting\n• soil management: crop rotation, fallow periods, controlling herd sizes\n• appropriate technology is often more sustainable than large schemes because local people can maintain it\n• strategies do little against climate change, which needs global action.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 18: annual rainfall at a weather station in the Sahel, northern Nigeria (data written for this practice question).\n1960s average 610 mm; 1970s 470 mm; 1980s 420 mm; 1990s 490 mm; 2000s 520 mm; 2010s 500 mm.\nThe population of the surrounding district more than doubled between 1960 and 2010.\n\n'Climate change is the main cause of desertification in the Sahel.' Do you agree? Use Figure 18 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): thorough, balanced discussion of physical and human causes with a clear, supported conclusion; detailed use of Figure 18.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear discussion with some balance and some use of Figure 18.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points, one-sided, limited use of data.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Figure 18: rainfall fell from 610 mm (1960s) to 420 mm (1980s), a fall of 190 mm, then partly recovered to about 500 mm\n• lower and less reliable rainfall means plants die, rivers dry up and soil is exposed\n• higher temperatures increase evaporation, drying out soils\n• but rainfall has partly recovered while population has doubled – suggests human pressure remains important\n• overgrazing, over-cultivation and fuelwood removal all increase with population growth\n• the causes interact – human pressure makes land more vulnerable during droughts\n• a judgement is needed; either view can gain full marks if supported.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nThe Thar Desert covers about 200 000 km² of north-west India and eastern Pakistan. It is one of the most densely populated deserts in the world. Rainfall is low and most falls during the monsoon (July–September).\n\nExplain how farming has created opportunities for development in a hot desert you have studied.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation of farming opportunities, well-developed, with accurate case-study detail.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation with some case-study detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic, generic points.\n\nIndicative content (Thar):\n• the Indira Gandhi Canal (about 650 km long) brings water from the Punjab rivers, allowing irrigation\n• crops such as wheat, cotton, mustard and citrus fruits grown commercially\n• traditional subsistence farming of millet and pulses during the monsoon\n• livestock – goats, sheep, camels – for milk, wool and meat; nomadic herding\n• jobs and income for rural people, reduces migration to cities\n• new settlements along the canal; increased food security for India\n• credit Sahara examples (e.g. date palms at oases, irrigated farms in southern Algeria / Egypt) if named.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 19: a photograph of a sandstorm sweeping across a road in the Sahara in southern Algeria. Sand covers part of the road; a truck has stopped with its lights on; there are no buildings in sight.\n\nExplain the challenges of developing a hot desert environment. Use Figure 19 and a hot desert you have studied.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation of a range of challenges (extreme temperatures, water supply, inaccessibility), with specific case-study detail and use of Figure 19.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of some challenges with some detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic, generic points.\n\nIndicative content:\n• inaccessibility: vast distances, few roads; sand blows over roads and railways (Figure 19); high cost of transport and supplying services\n• extreme temperatures: over 45 °C in summer in the Thar / Sahara; dangerous for workers; increases evaporation; equipment overheats\n• water supply: low unreliable rainfall; falling water tables; evaporation from canals; salinisation of irrigated land; competing demands from farming, industry and towns\n• sandstorms reduce visibility, damage equipment and solar panels\n• sparse population makes it hard to find a workforce and to provide schools and health care.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 20: two photographs of the Thar Desert, India. Photograph A shows rows of solar panels at Bhadla Solar Park stretching to the horizon. Photograph B shows a woman carrying water pots across sand dunes to a village, in strong sunshine.\n\n'The opportunities for development in hot deserts are greater than the challenges.' Do you agree? Use Figure 20 and a case study of a hot desert.\n[9 marks + 3 SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): AO2 thorough application of knowledge to evaluate the statement; AO3 thorough use of Figure 20; AO1 detailed case-study detail; well-balanced argument with a clear, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear discussion with some balance; some case-study detail and some use of the figure.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic points, mostly generic or one-sided; limited use of the figure.\n\nIndicative content (Thar; credit Sahara or other named desert):\nOpportunities –\n• energy: high insolation and flat land – Bhadla Solar Park (Photograph A); wind farms near Jaisalmer; oil at Barmer\n• minerals: gypsum, feldspar, limestone, marble (Makrana), lignite – jobs and export earnings\n• farming: irrigation from the Indira Gandhi Canal allows wheat and cotton\n• tourism: Jaisalmer, camel safaris, Desert Festival\nChallenges –\n• water supply: low unreliable rainfall, women walk long distances to collect water (Photograph B), falling water tables, salinisation\n• extreme temperatures: over 45 °C, limit outdoor work\n• inaccessibility: poor roads, sand, high costs\nEvaluation –\n• opportunities often benefit companies and the national economy more than local people (Photograph B shows rural poverty alongside large energy projects)\n• technology and investment can overcome some challenges (canals, solar power)\n• conclusion may depend on scale, time and who benefits.\n\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance (3): spelling and punctuation with consistent accuracy; rules of grammar used with effective control of meaning; wide range of specialist terms used adeptly and with precision.\nIntermediate performance (2): considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance (1): reasonable accuracy; no serious obstruction of meaning; limited range of specialist terms.\n0: no answer, response not related to the question, or below threshold.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 21: a written description of a hot desert ecosystem diagram. Arrows link "Climate (hot, dry, unreliable rain)" to "Soils (thin, sandy, salty)" and "Plants (sparse, drought-adapted)"; "Plants" to "Animals (camels, gerbils, lizards)"; "Animals" and "Plants" to "People (herders, farmers)". An arrow from "People" leads back to "Soils" labelled "overgrazing and fuelwood collection".\n\nTo what extent are the climate, soils, plants, animals and people of a hot desert interdependent? Use Figure 21 and your own understanding.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): AO2 thorough evaluation of a range of links in both directions, recognising that some links are stronger than others; detailed use of Figure 21; clear, supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear explanation of several links with some evaluation and use of Figure 21.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic description of some links; little evaluation.\n\nIndicative content:\n• climate → soils: low rainfall and high evaporation give thin, salty soils low in humus\n• climate → plants: drought-adapted plants are sparse, so little leaf litter returns to the soil\n• soils → plants: low fertility limits growth; salt-tolerant species dominate\n• plants → animals: food and water source; animals adapted to scarce food (camel hump, kangaroo rat)\n• animals → plants: seed dispersal, dung adds nutrients\n• people depend on plants and animals for food, fuel, transport and income\n• people → soils / plants: overgrazing and fuelwood removal remove vegetation and cause soil erosion and desertification\n• evaluation: climate is the dominant control – changes in it affect every other component; human activities can upset the balance quickly; the simple food web makes the ecosystem fragile, so the components are highly interdependent.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 22: a map of the Sahel, the semi-arid belt along the southern edge of the Sahara from Senegal and Mauritania in the west to Sudan and Eritrea in the east. Shading shows areas "at high risk of desertification" covering much of Mali, Niger, Burkina Faso and Chad. Labels note: "Population of Niger more than doubled 2000–2020"; "Rainfall lower and less reliable since the 1970s".\n\nTo what extent are human activities responsible for desertification on the fringe of hot deserts? Use Figure 22 and your own understanding.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): AO2 thorough evaluation of human and physical causes and the links between them; thorough use of Figure 22; clear, substantiated judgement on "to what extent".\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear explanation of human and physical causes with some evaluation and use of Figure 22.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic causes listed; little evaluation.\n\nIndicative content:\nHuman –\n• population growth (Figure 22: Niger's population more than doubled) increases demand for food, water and fuel\n• overgrazing, over-cultivation, removal of fuelwood and over-abstraction of groundwater remove vegetation and expose soil to erosion\nPhysical –\n• climate change: lower and less reliable rainfall since the 1970s (Figure 22), droughts, higher temperatures increasing evaporation\n• natural variations in rainfall; strong winds and flash floods erode soil\nEvaluation –\n• the two interact: drought reduces vegetation, and human pressure then prevents recovery\n• climate change itself may be partly human-caused (enhanced greenhouse effect), strengthening the human role\n• strategies that manage human activities (stone lines, zaï pits, tree planting, herd control) have restored land even during dry periods, suggesting human factors are important\n• judgement: either view credited if supported.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (hot deserts).\nFigure 23: farmer-managed natural regeneration in Niger. Farmers protect and prune tree stumps and shoots that regrow naturally in their fields instead of clearing them. Since the 1980s, tree cover has been restored on about 5 million hectares. A photograph shows millet growing between scattered young trees.\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of strategies used to reduce desertification. Use Figure 23 and one or more examples you have studied.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): AO2 thorough evaluation of a range of strategies (water management, soil management, tree planting, appropriate technology), weighing successes and limitations; good use of Figure 23 and specific examples; clear, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear explanation of strategies with some evaluation and use of examples.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic description of strategies; little evaluation.\n\nIndicative content:\n• farmer-managed regeneration (Figure 23): very cheap, uses existing root systems so trees regrow quickly; trees bind soil, act as windbreaks and add organic matter; provides fuelwood and fodder; large scale (about 5 million ha); depends on farmers having rights to the trees\n• tree planting – the Great Green Wall: ambitious scale, but many seedlings die and progress has been slower than planned; needs funding and political stability\n• water management: rainwater harvesting, small earth dams, drip irrigation, careful use of groundwater\n• soil management: stone lines and zaï pits in Burkina Faso, crop rotation, fallow, controlling herd sizes\n• appropriate technology: solar cookers, stone lines – cheap, sustainable, maintained by local people\n• limitations: strategies cannot control rainfall; conflict, poverty and population growth undermine them\n• conclusion: small-scale, community-led strategies are often more effective than large top-down schemes.` }
    ]
  },
  '1.6': {
    name: 'Cold Environments',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 1: climate data for a tundra weather station on the north coast of Alaska (data written for this practice question).\nMean monthly temperature (°C), Jan–Dec: −26, −28, −26, −18, −6, 1, 4, 3, −1, −9, −18, −23.\nMean monthly precipitation (mm), Jan–Dec: 4, 4, 3, 3, 3, 8, 26, 24, 16, 11, 6, 4.\n\nUsing Figure 1, identify the number of months with a mean temperature above 0 °C.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark: 3 (June, July and August).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 2: a world map of cold environments. Polar ice (shaded white) covers almost the whole of one continent centred on the South Pole and most of Greenland. Tundra (shaded pale green) forms a band across northern Alaska, northern Canada, the coasts of Greenland, northern Scandinavia and northern Russia, mostly between about 60°N and 75°N.\n\nUsing Figure 2, name the continent that is almost entirely covered by polar ice.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `1 mark: Antarctica.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 3: a photograph of tundra vegetation in northern Norway in July. Low cushions of moss, lichen and small flowering plants grow no more than about 10 cm high; there are no trees, and the ground between the plants is wet and stony.\n\nUsing Figure 3, give one reason why tundra plants grow close to the ground.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one of:\n• to shelter from strong, cold winds\n• to stay within the warmer air just above the ground surface\n• roots cannot grow deep because of permafrost, so plants cannot support tall growth\n• the short growing season does not allow time for tall growth\n• to be covered and insulated by snow in winter.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 4: a photograph of a polar bear walking across sea ice in Svalbard. It has a thick white coat and very large, wide paws.\n\nUsing Figure 4, give one way the polar bear is adapted to its environment.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one of:\n• white fur for camouflage against snow and ice when hunting seals\n• thick fur to insulate it from the cold\n• large, wide paws spread its weight on snow and thin ice / help it swim.\nAccept thick layer of blubber / black skin to absorb heat as own understanding.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nSvalbard is a group of Norwegian islands between about 74°N and 81°N. Around 2,500 people live there, most of them in the town of Longyearbyen. There are no roads linking the settlements.\n\nSuggest one reason why so few people live in Svalbard.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for one of:\n• extremely cold temperatures / long, dark winters (polar night)\n• inaccessible — no roads, so travel only by air, sea or snowmobile\n• permafrost and ice make building difficult and expensive\n• very short growing season so little farming is possible\n• risk from polar bears / avalanches.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nThe Svalbard Global Seed Vault was opened in 2008 inside a mountain near Longyearbyen. It stores copies of seeds of food crops from around the world.\n\nSuggest one reason why Svalbard was chosen as the location for the seed vault.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for one of:\n• permafrost keeps the vault naturally frozen, even if power fails\n• remote / politically stable and safe location\n• the vault is above sea level so protected from flooding\n• low tectonic activity.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 1: climate data for a tundra weather station on the north coast of Alaska (data written for this practice question).\nMean monthly temperature (°C), Jan–Dec: −26, −28, −26, −18, −6, 1, 4, 3, −1, −9, −18, −23.\nMean monthly precipitation (mm), Jan–Dec: 4, 4, 3, 3, 3, 8, 26, 24, 16, 11, 6, 4.\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the annual temperature range. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark: correct values identified — highest 4 °C (July) and lowest −28 °C (February) / 4 − (−28).\n1 mark: 32 °C.\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 1: climate data for a tundra weather station on the north coast of Alaska (data written for this practice question).\nMean monthly temperature (°C), Jan–Dec: −26, −28, −26, −18, −6, 1, 4, 3, −1, −9, −18, −23.\nMean monthly precipitation (mm), Jan–Dec: 4, 4, 3, 3, 3, 8, 26, 24, 16, 11, 6, 4.\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the percentage of the annual precipitation that falls in the four months June to September. Give your answer to the nearest whole number.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark: total annual precipitation 112 mm and June–September total 74 mm (8 + 26 + 24 + 16) / 74 ÷ 112 × 100.\n1 mark: 66%.\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 1: climate data for a tundra weather station on the north coast of Alaska (data written for this practice question).\nMean monthly temperature (°C), Jan–Dec: −26, −28, −26, −18, −6, 1, 4, 3, −1, −9, −18, −23.\nMean monthly precipitation (mm), Jan–Dec: 4, 4, 3, 3, 3, 8, 26, 24, 16, 11, 6, 4.\n\nUsing Figure 1, describe the pattern of precipitation during the year.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point, max 2:\n• very low precipitation all year / total only 112 mm\n• lowest (3–4 mm) from January to May / in winter and spring\n• peaks in July at 26 mm / most falls in summer and early autumn (July–September)\n• decreases again from October to December.\nCredit use of data for the second mark.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 2: a world map of cold environments. Polar ice (shaded white) covers almost the whole of one continent centred on the South Pole and most of Greenland. Tundra (shaded pale green) forms a band across northern Alaska, northern Canada, the coasts of Greenland, northern Scandinavia and northern Russia, mostly between about 60°N and 75°N.\n\nUsing Figure 2, describe the distribution of tundra environments.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point, max 2:\n• mainly in the Northern Hemisphere / almost none in the Southern Hemisphere\n• found in a band between about 60°N and 75°N / just south of the polar ice\n• across North America (northern Alaska and Canada), and northern Europe and Asia (Scandinavia, Russia)\n• along the coasts of Greenland.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nThe arctic fox lives in the tundra of northern Canada, Alaska and Scandinavia. In winter its fur is thick and white; in summer its coat turns brown-grey.\n\nOutline one way the arctic fox is adapted to the tundra environment.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for the adaptation, 1 mark for development:\n• its fur changes colour (1) so it is camouflaged against snow in winter and bare rock/vegetation in summer, helping it hunt and avoid predators (1)\n• thick fur / fur on the soles of its feet (1) insulates it against temperatures below −30 °C (1)\n• small, rounded ears and short legs/muzzle (1) reduce the surface area from which heat is lost (1)\n• keen hearing (1) lets it locate lemmings moving under the snow (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nIn the Alaskan tundra the growing season, when the mean temperature is above about 6 °C, lasts only around 50–60 days.\n\nOutline one way that tundra plants are adapted to the short growing season.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for the adaptation, 1 mark for development:\n• many plants are perennials / keep their leaves (1) so they can photosynthesise as soon as conditions allow without regrowing leaves each year (1)\n• plants flower and set seed very quickly (1) so they complete their life cycle within the few weeks of summer (1)\n• some flowers (e.g. arctic poppy) follow the sun (1) to absorb as much heat and light as possible (1)\n• dark/red leaves (1) absorb more solar energy (1)\n• many reproduce by runners/rhizomes rather than seed (1) which is quicker and more reliable (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nIn the tundra of Alaska the ground is permanently frozen (permafrost) a short distance below the surface. Each summer the top 0.5–1 m (the active layer) thaws.\n\nExplain why tundra soils are often waterlogged in summer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point, or 1 + 1 for a developed point:\n• the permafrost below is impermeable (1) so meltwater from snow and the active layer cannot drain downwards (1)\n• low temperatures mean low evaporation rates (1) so water stays at the surface / forms bogs and pools (1)\n• flat land / gentle relief (1) slows surface run-off (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nLongyearbyen, Svalbard, is built on permafrost. Many buildings stand on wooden or steel piles driven several metres into the ground, with a gap of about 1 m between the floor and the ground.\n\nOutline one challenge that permafrost causes for buildings in cold environments.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for the challenge, 1 mark for development:\n• heat from buildings can thaw the permafrost (1) causing the ground to subside so buildings tilt, crack or collapse (1)\n• the active layer freezes and thaws each year (1) causing the ground to heave and move, damaging foundations (1)\n• pipes cannot be buried in frozen ground (1) so water and sewage must be carried in insulated pipes above ground, which is expensive (1)\n• climate change is warming permafrost (1) so foundations designed for frozen ground become unstable (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nThe Barents Sea around Svalbard and the Bering Sea off western Alaska are among the most productive fishing areas in the world.\n\nUsing a named example of a cold environment, outline one opportunity for development from fishing.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for the opportunity, 1 mark for development/located detail:\n• Alaska: commercial salmon fishing, e.g. Bristol Bay sockeye (1), provides jobs in fishing and fish processing and export income (1)\n• Svalbard / Barents Sea: cod and shrimp stocks (1) support Norwegian fishing fleets and processing jobs (1)\n• cold, nutrient-rich waters support large fish stocks / plankton (1) so there is a reliable resource to sell (1)\n• sport fishing for tourists (1) brings income to lodges and guides (1).\nNo named example: max 1.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 5: cruise ship passengers landing in Svalbard (data written for this practice question). 2010: 30,900; 2013: 34,200; 2016: 38,600; 2019: 52,300.\n\nUsing Figure 5, calculate the percentage increase in cruise passengers between 2016 and 2019. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark: correct method — (52,300 − 38,600) ÷ 38,600 × 100 / 13,700 ÷ 38,600 × 100.\n1 mark: 35.5%.\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 6: a photograph of the Trans-Alaska Pipeline crossing the tundra. The pipe is raised about 2–3 m above the ground on vertical supports, follows a zigzag path rather than a straight line, and a herd of caribou is walking underneath it.\n\nUsing Figure 6, identify two ways the design of the pipeline reduces its impact on the environment.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark for each of two:\n• raised above the ground so the hot oil does not melt the permafrost\n• raised high enough for caribou/animals to pass underneath, so migration routes are not blocked\n• zigzag shape allows the pipe to move/expand (with temperature or earthquakes) without breaking, reducing the risk of oil spills\n• supports take up only a small area of ground, so less vegetation is damaged.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nThe Arctic National Wildlife Refuge in north-east Alaska covers about 78,000 km² and has no roads or permanent settlements inside most of it.\n\nGive two reasons why cold environments such as this are valued as wilderness areas.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for each of two:\n• they are among the last places largely untouched by human activity\n• they are home to unique / endangered species (e.g. polar bears, caribou, musk oxen)\n• they are important for scientific research (e.g. ice cores, climate change)\n• they are fragile and take a very long time to recover from damage\n• they help regulate the global climate (ice reflects sunlight; permafrost stores carbon)\n• they provide traditional homes and food for indigenous peoples (e.g. Gwich'in, Iñupiat)\n• they offer opportunities for low-impact recreation and solitude.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nThe Antarctic Treaty was signed by 12 countries in 1959. In 1991 the Protocol on Environmental Protection (Madrid Protocol) was added to it.\n\nOutline one way international agreements help to protect Antarctica.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for the strategy, 1 mark for how it protects:\n• the Madrid Protocol bans all mining / mineral extraction (1) so there is no large-scale industrial damage to ice and ecosystems (1)\n• Antarctica may be used only for peaceful purposes and scientific research (1) so military activity and nuclear waste are prohibited (1)\n• the Protocol requires environmental impact assessments for all activities (1) so damaging projects can be stopped (1)\n• waste must be removed from the continent / non-native species controlled (1) to reduce pollution of the fragile environment (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 7: a simple tundra food web (written for this practice question). Lichen, mosses, sedges and arctic willow are eaten by lemmings, arctic hares and caribou. Lemmings are eaten by arctic foxes and snowy owls; caribou are hunted by wolves and by local Iñupiat people. Dead plants and animals decompose very slowly in the cold, wet soil.\n\nUsing Figure 7, explain how the plants, animals and soils of the tundra are interdependent.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of more than one link between components, using Figure 7 and own understanding; links are developed (cause and effect).\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic explanation; links are simple or listed.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• plants are the producers; lemmings, hares and caribou depend on them for food\n• predators (arctic fox, snowy owl, wolves) depend on the herbivores; lemming numbers rise and fall and fox/owl numbers follow\n• people depend on caribou for food, clothing and income\n• slow decomposition means nutrients are released slowly, so soils are thin and infertile and plant growth is slow\n• vegetation insulates the permafrost; if it is removed (e.g. by overgrazing or vehicles) the permafrost thaws, soils become waterlogged/erode and fewer plants can grow\n• a change to one part (e.g. a fall in lichen) affects the whole food web.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nIn Svalbard winter temperatures can fall below −30 °C, the sun does not rise for about four months, and food is very scarce in winter.\n\nExplain how animals are adapted to the physical conditions of cold environments.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of how adaptations help animals survive the named conditions; reference to more than one adaptation/condition.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: adaptations mainly described or listed with little explanation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• thick fur / blubber (polar bear, seals, walrus) insulates against extreme cold\n• small ears and compact bodies (arctic fox, musk ox) reduce heat loss\n• white winter coats (arctic fox, arctic hare, ptarmigan) give camouflage on snow\n• Svalbard reindeer build up fat in summer to survive the winter food shortage and have short legs to conserve heat\n• migration (caribou, many birds such as the arctic tern) avoids the harshest winter conditions\n• wide hooves/paws spread weight on snow and help dig for lichen under the snow\n• hibernation (e.g. pregnant female polar bears in dens) saves energy in the dark winter.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nTyre tracks made by vehicles driven across the Alaskan tundra in the 1960s can still be seen today.\n\nExplain why cold environments are fragile and slow to recover from damage.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation linking physical characteristics to slow recovery; more than one developed point.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points, little explanation of why recovery is slow.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• the growing season is very short and temperatures low, so plants grow slowly (some only a few mm a year)\n• low biodiversity and simple food webs mean that the loss of one species affects many others\n• removing vegetation exposes the permafrost, which then thaws, causing subsidence and waterlogging (thermokarst) so vehicle tracks deepen over time\n• decomposition is very slow, so pollutants (e.g. oil) and waste remain for decades\n• thin, infertile soils take a very long time to form and are easily eroded.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nThe Trans-Alaska Pipeline carries oil about 1,287 km from Prudhoe Bay on the north coast of Alaska to the port of Valdez in the south. It was completed in 1977.\n\nExplain how technology can be used to reduce the impact of economic development in cold environments.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of how specific technologies reduce specific impacts; use of a located example.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: technologies named or described with little explanation of their effect.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• the pipeline is raised on supports over about half its length so the warm oil does not melt the permafrost\n• heat pipes / refrigerated supports keep the ground frozen\n• zigzag design and sliding supports allow movement during earthquakes, reducing the risk of spills\n• raised and buried sections let caribou migrate\n• ice roads and ice drilling pads are used in winter and melt away in summer, leaving less damage to the tundra\n• directional drilling allows several wells from one small drilling pad, reducing land disturbance\n• buildings on piles with a gap beneath stop heat thawing permafrost (e.g. Longyearbyen, Svalbard).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nThere is no road or rail link between Longyearbyen and mainland Norway, about 850 km away. The fjords around Svalbard are frozen for several months each year.\n\nUsing a named example, explain how inaccessibility is a challenge to development in cold environments.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of how inaccessibility affects development, with located detail from a named example.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points; generic or little explanation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• goods, fuel and building materials must arrive by air or by sea in the ice-free season, raising costs\n• sea ice and storms can close ports for months, limiting exports such as coal (Svalbard) or the season for tourism\n• few or no roads between settlements, so people use snowmobiles, boats or light aircraft (Svalbard, much of Alaska) — expensive and weather-dependent\n• remote mines and oilfields (e.g. Prudhoe Bay) need workers to be flown in and housed in camps\n• building transport links is itself difficult and costly because of permafrost and extreme cold (e.g. the Dalton Highway to Prudhoe Bay is gravel and often closed by snow).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nIn the tundra the mean annual temperature is below 0 °C, precipitation is less than about 250 mm a year and the ground beneath the surface is permanently frozen.\n\nExplain how the climate of the tundra affects the characteristics of its soils.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation linking climate to more than one soil characteristic.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: soil characteristics described with little link to climate.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• low temperatures slow decomposition, so organic matter builds up as a peaty layer and few nutrients are released — soils are infertile\n• weathering is slow so soils are thin\n• permafrost below the active layer is impermeable, so soils are waterlogged in summer (gley soils)\n• repeated freezing and thawing of the active layer causes frost heave, sorting stones into patterned ground and disrupting roots\n• the active layer is thin, so plants can only have shallow roots.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 8: tourism in Svalbard (written for this practice question, based on real activities). Activities include dog-sledding, snowmobile safaris, glacier hikes, Northern Lights tours and cruise ship visits. A local hotel manager says: "Tourism now provides more jobs in Longyearbyen than coal mining."\n\nUsing Figure 8, explain the opportunities that tourism brings to a cold environment.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of more than one opportunity, using Figure 8 and own understanding.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic list of opportunities with little explanation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• creates jobs (guides, hotels, restaurants, transport) replacing declining coal mining jobs\n• tourists spend money in shops and hotels, which supports local services (multiplier effect)\n• tourism can be all-year: snow activities and Northern Lights in winter, midnight sun, glaciers and wildlife in summer\n• taxes and fees can be used to fund conservation and infrastructure (e.g. airport, harbour)\n• tourism uses the landscape and wildlife as a resource without removing them, so it may be more sustainable than mining if managed.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 9: climate data for two cold environment weather stations (data written for this practice question).\nStation A (tundra, northern Alaska) — mean monthly temperature (°C), Jan–Dec: −26, −28, −26, −18, −6, 1, 4, 3, −1, −9, −18, −23; total annual precipitation 112 mm.\nStation B (polar, interior of Antarctica) — mean monthly temperature (°C), Jan–Dec: −32, −44, −57, −65, −66, −66, −68, −68, −66, −57, −43, −32; total annual precipitation 20 mm.\n\nUsing Figure 9, compare the climates of Station A and Station B.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear comparison of temperature and precipitation using comparative terms and data from both stations.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: separate descriptions of each station or limited use of data.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Station B is much colder all year — warmest months −32 °C compared with 4 °C at Station A\n• Station A has three months above 0 °C; Station B has none\n• coldest month: −28 °C at A compared with −68 °C at B, 40 °C colder\n• temperature range is similar (32 °C at A, 36 °C at B)\n• the seasons are reversed: A is warmest in July, B is warmest in December/January (Southern Hemisphere)\n• both are dry, but B receives much less precipitation (20 mm compared with 112 mm).` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 1: climate data for a tundra weather station on the north coast of Alaska (data written for this practice question).\nMean monthly temperature (°C), Jan–Dec: −26, −28, −26, −18, −6, 1, 4, 3, −1, −9, −18, −23.\nMean monthly precipitation (mm), Jan–Dec: 4, 4, 3, 3, 3, 8, 26, 24, 16, 11, 6, 4.\nFigure 3: a photograph of tundra vegetation in July. Low cushions of moss, lichen and small flowering plants grow no more than about 10 cm high; there are no trees, and the ground between the plants is wet and stony.\n\nSuggest how plants are adapted to the climate of the tundra. Use Figure 1, Figure 3 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed suggestions that link specific plant adaptations to specific climatic conditions; effective use of both figures (e.g. data on temperature, growing season, low precipitation).\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear suggestions with some links to climate; some use of figures.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of plants or climate with limited links; little use of figures.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• only three months above 0 °C (Figure 1) — plants grow, flower and set seed quickly in the short summer\n• very low temperatures and strong winds — plants are low-growing/cushion-shaped (Figure 3) to stay in the warmer, sheltered air near the ground and be insulated by snow in winter\n• small leaves and hairy stems reduce water loss and trap heat\n• low precipitation (112 mm) and frozen water in winter — some plants have waxy leaves; but waterlogged ground in summer means water is available when growing\n• permafrost — shallow roots that spread sideways in the active layer\n• no trees (Figure 3) because roots cannot penetrate the permafrost and the growing season is too short\n• lichens and mosses survive long periods of freezing and need few nutrients, suiting infertile soils.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nAntarctica contains about 90% of the world's ice. Scientists have drilled ice cores there that record climate conditions over the last 800,000 years.\n\nAssess the importance of cold environments as wilderness areas.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of a range of reasons, with a reasoned judgement about which are most important; use of located examples.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some reasons; some attempt at judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic list of reasons; no judgement.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• scientific value: ice cores reveal past climates; Antarctica and Svalbard research stations monitor climate change\n• climate regulation: ice and snow reflect solar radiation (high albedo); permafrost stores large amounts of carbon that could be released as it thaws\n• biodiversity: unique species (polar bears, penguins, caribou) adapted to extreme conditions; important breeding grounds for migratory birds and whales\n• fragile ecosystems that recover very slowly, so they need protection\n• cultural value for indigenous peoples (e.g. Iñupiat, Gwich'in, Sámi) who depend on them\n• recreation and the value of having places largely untouched by humans\n• judgement, e.g. their role in regulating global climate affects everyone, so it is arguably the most important.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nAlaska is the largest US state. Its economy depends on oil from Prudhoe Bay, fishing, mining (e.g. the Red Dog zinc mine) and tourism.\n\nUsing a named example of a cold environment, explain the opportunities for economic development.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of a range of opportunities with accurate, located case study detail.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some opportunities with some case study detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic, generic points; little or no case study detail.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content (Alaska):\n• energy — oil and gas from Prudhoe Bay transported by the Trans-Alaska Pipeline; oil revenue funds the Alaska Permanent Fund, which pays an annual dividend to residents\n• minerals — gold (e.g. Fort Knox mine near Fairbanks), zinc and lead (Red Dog mine) provide jobs and exports\n• fishing — salmon, pollock and crab; Bristol Bay sockeye salmon is one of the world's largest salmon fisheries; fish processing jobs\n• tourism — cruise ships, Denali National Park, wildlife viewing, Northern Lights\nIndicative content (Svalbard): coal mining (Longyearbyen, Barentsburg); fishing (cod, shrimp); tourism (cruises, dog-sledding); scientific research (Ny-Ålesund) and the Global Seed Vault.\nCredit any valid named cold environment.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 10: two views about drilling for oil in the coastal plain of the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge (ANWR), Alaska.\nOil company spokesperson: "There could be billions of barrels of oil here. Drilling would create thousands of jobs, bring money to Alaska and reduce US reliance on imported oil. Modern technology means we can drill with very little damage."\nEnvironmental campaigner: "The coastal plain is the calving ground of the Porcupine caribou herd, which the Gwich'in people depend on. Spills, roads and noise would damage one of the last true wildernesses, and burning more oil adds to climate change."\n\nAssess which view you think is more convincing. Use Figure 10 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of both views, using Figure 10 and own understanding, leading to a well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear consideration of both views with some own understanding; judgement may be partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points, mostly lifted from Figure 10; one-sided; little or no judgement.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• for drilling: jobs and income; taxes and Permanent Fund dividends for Alaskans; energy security; technology such as ice roads, small drilling pads and directional drilling reduces the footprint\n• against drilling: caribou calving and migration disrupted; Gwich'in food supply and culture threatened; risk of spills (e.g. Exxon Valdez, 1989, Prince William Sound) — oil breaks down very slowly in cold conditions; permafrost damage from roads and buildings; contributes to climate change which is already thawing the Arctic\n• evaluation: the oil company may underestimate risks; jobs may be short-term; the value of wilderness cannot easily be replaced; judgement either way credited if supported.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nAbout 65% of the land area of Svalbard is protected in national parks and nature reserves. The Norwegian government has banned heavy fuel oil in ships sailing in most of Svalbard's waters, and environmental groups such as WWF campaign for further protection of the Arctic.\n\nExplain how governments and conservation groups can help to protect cold environments.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of the roles of both governments and conservation groups, with located examples.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some roles; may focus mainly on one group.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic list of strategies with little explanation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• governments: create national parks and nature reserves (Svalbard; Arctic National Wildlife Refuge, Alaska); pass laws (Svalbard Environmental Protection Act 2001) controlling vehicles off-road, hunting and building; require environmental impact assessments; ban heavy fuel oil; set fishing quotas\n• international agreements: Antarctic Treaty and Madrid Protocol ban mining in Antarctica\n• conservation groups (WWF, Greenpeace): raise awareness, campaign against drilling, fund research on polar bears and sea ice, monitor and report pollution, put pressure on governments and companies\n• Greenpeace campaigned for Antarctica to become a 'World Park', helping lead to the mining ban\n• both: work with indigenous peoples to protect traditional ways of life.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nIn Longyearbyen, Svalbard, temperatures in February average around −15 °C, the town has four months of polar night, and in December 2015 an avalanche destroyed eleven homes.\n\nUsing a named example of a cold environment, explain the challenges that extreme temperatures and inaccessibility cause for development.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of challenges from both extreme temperatures and inaccessibility with accurate, located case study detail.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some challenges; may be unbalanced between the two.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic, generic points; little case study detail.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• extreme temperatures: frostbite and hypothermia risk for outdoor workers; machinery and vehicles fail or need heating; high heating costs; water pipes freeze; limited working season for construction; polar night makes outdoor work and travel harder\n• Alaska: temperatures below −40 °C at Prudhoe Bay in winter; oil kept warm in the pipeline\n• inaccessibility: Svalbard has no roads between settlements — travel by snowmobile, boat or plane; supplies arrive by ship or air so costs are high; frozen fjords; Alaska's North Slope reached only by the gravel Dalton Highway or air\n• other hazards: polar bears (people must carry protection outside Longyearbyen), avalanches and landslides as snow and permafrost change.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 11: a photograph of a street in Longyearbyen, Svalbard. Brightly painted wooden houses stand on wooden piles about 1 m above the ground. Insulated water and sewage pipes run above ground in boxes alongside the road, and a snowmobile is parked outside each house.\n\nExplain how buildings and infrastructure are adapted to the challenges of a cold environment. Use Figure 11 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation linking adaptations to specific challenges; effective use of Figure 11 and own understanding/located detail.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some adaptations with some links to challenges; some use of the figure.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of features from the figure with little explanation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• houses on piles with an air gap so heat from the building does not thaw the permafrost and cause subsidence\n• piles driven deep into permanently frozen ground to give stable foundations below the active layer\n• insulated pipes above ground (utilidors) because pipes cannot be buried in frozen ground and would freeze\n• wooden, well-insulated buildings with triple glazing to reduce heat loss\n• snowmobiles because there are few roads and snow cover for much of the year\n• Alaska: the Trans-Alaska Pipeline raised on supports; gravel pads beneath roads and airstrips insulate the permafrost; ice roads used in winter\n• climate change: warming permafrost means some Longyearbyen buildings have had to be re-piled or demolished.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nThe Arctic is warming about three to four times faster than the global average. In northern Alaska the active layer of the permafrost is becoming deeper and shrubs are spreading north into the tundra.\n\nExplain how climate change could affect the interdependence of the tundra ecosystem.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of chains of effects through several components (climate, permafrost, soils, plants, animals, people).\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some effects with some links between components.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic, separate effects with few links.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• warmer temperatures thaw permafrost, so the ground subsides and becomes waterlogged; drainage changes affect which plants can grow\n• thawing permafrost releases methane and carbon dioxide, increasing warming further (positive feedback)\n• shrubs spread and shade out lichens and mosses, reducing winter food for caribou/reindeer\n• more rain-on-snow events form ice layers that stop reindeer reaching lichen (e.g. Svalbard reindeer starvation)\n• loss of sea ice reduces polar bear hunting time on the ice\n• species from further south (e.g. red fox) move north and compete with the arctic fox\n• indigenous people who depend on caribou or sea ice for hunting are affected.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 12: visitors to Antarctica (data written for this practice question, based on real trends). 2000: 14,000; 2010: 33,000; 2020: 74,000. Nearly all visitors arrive on cruise ships in the short summer season (November–March), and most landings are on the Antarctic Peninsula.\n\nAssess the effectiveness of strategies used to manage tourism in Antarctica. Use Figure 12 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of the strengths and limitations of several strategies, using Figure 12, leading to a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of strategies with some evaluation; some use of the figure.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of strategies; little or no evaluation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• IAATO rules: ships with more than 500 passengers cannot land people; no more than 100 people ashore at once; set distances from wildlife; cleaning boots to avoid bringing in seeds\n• Antarctic Treaty / Madrid Protocol: environmental impact assessments; waste must be removed\n• site guidelines limit landings to certain places and paths\n• strengths: most operators follow the rules; impacts at individual sites have been kept low\n• limitations: visitor numbers have increased more than fivefold since 2000 (Figure 12), concentrated in the same small, ice-free areas of the Peninsula, so pressure is rising; IAATO membership is voluntary; enforcement is difficult in such a remote area; cruise ships emit greenhouse gases and risk fuel spills\n• judgement: effective so far at a local scale, but may not cope with continued growth.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 13: two photographs of Alaska. Photograph A shows a wide area of flat, treeless tundra with snow-capped mountains in the distance. Photograph B shows an oil drilling rig on a gravel pad on the North Slope, with a road and pipelines leading away from it across the frozen ground.\n\nDiscuss the challenges and opportunities for development in a cold environment. Use Figure 13 and a case study.\n[9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `AO2 (knowledge and understanding), AO3 (application) and AO4 (use of the figure).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed knowledge and understanding of both challenges and opportunities; well-developed discussion; effective use of Figure 13 and accurate, detailed case study evidence; balanced and coherent.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear knowledge of challenges and opportunities, though may be unbalanced; some case study detail; some use of the figure.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic points, generic or list-like; little case study detail; little discussion.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• opportunities — energy: oil from Prudhoe Bay (Photograph B) via the Trans-Alaska Pipeline to Valdez; revenue funds the Alaska Permanent Fund; minerals: gold and zinc (Red Dog mine); fishing: salmon in Bristol Bay; tourism: wilderness landscapes (Photograph A), Denali, cruises, Northern Lights\n• challenges — extreme temperatures (below −40 °C in winter) affect workers and machinery; inaccessibility — vast distances, few roads (Dalton Highway), supplies flown in; buildings and infrastructure on permafrost — gravel pads and raised pipelines needed to stop thaw; environmental risks — oil spills (Exxon Valdez 1989), damage to caribou migration\n• discussion: opportunities bring jobs and wealth, but challenges raise costs; technology helps overcome challenges; climate change is changing both (longer ice-free seasons, but more permafrost thaw).\nCredit Svalbard or another valid case study.\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance (3): spelling and punctuation consistently accurate; grammar used to control meaning effectively; wide range of specialist terms used precisely.\nIntermediate (2): spelling and punctuation considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold (1): spelling and punctuation reasonable accuracy; no serious obstruction to meaning; limited range of specialist terms.\n0: no rewardable SPaG, or response does not relate to the question.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nIn 2017 Store Norske closed its Svea coal mine in Svalbard and spent several years removing the buildings and restoring the land. Meanwhile, the number of tourists visiting Svalbard has continued to grow.\n\n'Cold environments should be left as wilderness and not developed.' Do you agree with this statement? Use examples to support your answer.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed understanding of the value of wilderness and the benefits of development; well-developed arguments on both sides; accurate, located examples; a clear, well-supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear arguments with some examples; may be unbalanced; conclusion partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic, generic points; little use of examples; no real conclusion.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• agree — fragile ecosystems recover very slowly; unique wildlife (polar bears, caribou, penguins); scientific value (ice cores, climate monitoring); climate regulation (albedo, permafrost carbon); rights of indigenous peoples; risk of spills (Exxon Valdez); Antarctica is already protected from mining by the Madrid Protocol\n• disagree — people already live there and need jobs and income (Longyearbyen, Alaska); oil, minerals and fish are valuable resources; Alaska Permanent Fund benefits all residents; well-managed tourism and research bring income while conserving the landscape; technology (raised pipelines, ice roads, directional drilling) reduces impacts\n• evaluation — the Svea mine closure shows land can be restored, but at high cost; some areas (Antarctica, ANWR) may be best left as wilderness while others allow limited, controlled development; a balanced or 'it depends' conclusion is valid if supported.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nFigure 14: strategies used in cold environments (written for this practice question).\n• Antarctica: the Madrid Protocol (1991) bans mining; tourism is controlled by IAATO rules.\n• Svalbard: about 65% of the land is protected; off-road driving on bare ground is banned; visitors must follow rules on approaching wildlife.\n• Alaska: the Trans-Alaska Pipeline is raised over permafrost; ice roads are used for winter drilling; parts of the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge are protected from development.\n\nTo what extent is it possible to balance the needs of economic development and conservation in cold environments? Use Figure 14 and your own understanding.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed evaluation of how far strategies achieve a balance, using Figure 14 and own understanding with located detail; reaches a substantiated judgement on 'to what extent'.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear explanation of strategies with some evaluation; some use of the figure and examples; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic description of strategies; little evaluation; no judgement.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• use of technology: raised pipelines, ice roads, buildings on piles, directional drilling allow development with a smaller footprint\n• role of governments: protected areas and laws in Svalbard; national parks and wildlife refuges in Alaska; fishing quotas\n• international agreements: Antarctic Treaty and Madrid Protocol have largely kept Antarctica free from industrial development\n• conservation groups (WWF, Greenpeace) monitor and campaign\n• evidence of balance: Svalbard is moving from coal to tourism and research; Antarctic tourism regulated\n• evidence against: oil spills still happen; political decisions change (debate over drilling in ANWR; approval of the Willow oil project in 2023); tourism numbers rising fast; climate change caused by global emissions cannot be controlled locally\n• judgement: balance is possible in places with strong laws and low populations, but harder where resources are very valuable.` },
      { q: `Section B — The living world (cold environments).\nLongyearbyen and Prudhoe Bay both lie well north of the Arctic Circle, where winter temperatures fall far below −20 °C and the ground is permanently frozen.\n\n'The physical characteristics of cold environments are the biggest challenge to their development.' To what extent do you agree? Use a case study of a cold environment in your answer.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed understanding of physical and other (economic, environmental, political) challenges; well-developed evaluation with accurate case study detail; clear, substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear explanation of challenges with some case study detail; some evaluation; judgement may be partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic, generic list of challenges; little evaluation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• physical challenges: extreme temperatures (risk to workers, machines failing, high heating costs); permafrost (subsidence, buildings on piles, raised pipelines); polar night; inaccessibility caused by sea ice, mountains and snow; hazards such as avalanches (Longyearbyen 2015)\n• other challenges: high costs of transport and supplies; small local labour force and workers flown in; environmental laws and protected areas restrict development (e.g. Svalbard protected areas, ANWR); environmental opposition and indigenous land rights; changing world energy prices and the shift away from coal (Svea mine closure 2017)\n• evaluation: technology has overcome many physical challenges (e.g. Trans-Alaska Pipeline), so economic and political factors may now be bigger; climate change is making some physical challenges worse (thawing permafrost) and others easier (longer ice-free shipping season)\n• a judgement either way is credited if supported.` }
    ]
  },
  '1.7': {
    name: 'Coastal Landscapes in the UK',
    questions: [
      { q: `Figure 1 shows the average annual rate of cliff erosion at six sites along the Holderness coast, from north to south (data written for this practice question).\n\nSite 1: 1.2 m per year\nSite 2: 1.8 m per year\nSite 3: 2.1 m per year\nSite 4: 2.6 m per year\nSite 5: 3.4 m per year\nSite 6: 2.0 m per year\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the range of the erosion rates. Give your answer in metres per year.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2.2 (m per year) (1)\n(3.4 − 1.2 = 2.2.) Units not required for the mark.` },
      { q: `Figure 2 describes part of a 1:25 000 Ordnance Survey (OS) map extract of a chalk coastline (map described for this practice question). Easting line 03 runs north–south on the western side of the square and easting line 04 on the eastern side; northing line 80 runs along the southern side and northing line 81 along the northern side. A natural arch is shown seven-tenths of the way east from easting 03 and four-tenths of the way north from northing 80.\n\nUsing Figure 2, give the six-figure grid reference of the natural arch.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `037804 (1)\nNo other answer. Eastings must come before northings.` },
      { q: `Figure 3 is a description of a photograph of a cliff on the Holderness coast after a wet winter. A large block of boulder clay has slipped down the cliff along a curved surface and has rotated backwards, so that the grass on its top now tilts towards the land. A crescent-shaped scar marks the top of the cliff.\n\nUsing Figure 3, name the type of mass movement shown.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Slumping / rotational slip / rotational slumping (1)\nDo not accept rockfall or landslide on its own.` },
      { q: `Coastal transportation moves sediment in several ways.\n\nWhich one of the following describes saltation?\nShade one circle only.\n\nA  Large boulders are rolled along the sea bed.\nB  Small pebbles and sand grains are bounced along the sea bed.\nC  Fine sediment is carried within the water.\nD  Minerals are dissolved in the water and carried in solution.`, marks: 1, command: 'Shade', markScheme: `B (1)` },
      { q: `On a 1:50 000 OS map of the East Riding of Yorkshire, Spurn Head spit measures 11 cm from where it joins the mainland near Kilnsea to its tip at Spurn Point (map measurement written for this practice question).\n\nUsing this information, calculate the length of the spit in kilometres.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `5.5 (km) (1)\n(At 1:50 000, 2 cm = 1 km, so 11 ÷ 2 = 5.5 km.)` },
      { q: `Figure 4 shows the results of a vegetation survey along a transect through the sand dunes at Studland Bay, Dorset, from the beach inland (data written for this practice question).\n\nZone A (embryo dunes, 20 m from the strandline): 8% plant cover\nZone B (fore dunes, 60 m): 35% plant cover\nZone C (yellow dunes, 120 m): 70% plant cover\nZone D (grey dunes, 250 m): 95% plant cover\n\nUsing Figure 4, identify the zone with the most plant cover.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Zone D / grey dunes (1)` },
      { q: `Figure 5 is a description of a photograph of the shingle storm beach at Chesil Beach, Dorset, taken after a winter storm. The beach is steep, with a high ridge of pebbles at the top and several narrower ridges (berms) lower down.\n\nSuggest one reason why the beach in Figure 5 is steep.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for one of:\n• Pebbles/shingle are large, so backwash soaks into the beach and cannot drag them back down (1)\n• Strong swash of storm waves has thrown material high up the beach to form a ridge (1)\n• Powerful/destructive waves have removed finer sediment from the lower beach (1)\nAccept any other valid reason.` },
      { q: `Figure 6 is a description of a photograph of the chalk cliffs at Old Harry Rocks, Dorset. The cliff face is cut by many vertical cracks (joints), and there are piles of angular chalk fragments at the cliff foot.\n\nOutline how freeze–thaw weathering could have produced the angular fragments shown in Figure 6.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point.\n• Water enters cracks/joints in the chalk (1) and when it freezes it expands (by about 9%), putting pressure on the rock (1)\n• Repeated freezing and thawing widens the crack (1) until angular pieces break off and fall to the cliff foot (1)` },
      { q: `Figure 7 is a description of a cross-section of a wave approaching a beach. The wave is tall and steep and breaks almost vertically. A short arrow up the beach is labelled 'weak swash' and a long arrow down the beach is labelled 'strong backwash'. Waves arrive at about 13 per minute.\n\nUsing Figure 7, describe two characteristics of this type of wave.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each characteristic, max 2:\n• It is a destructive wave (credit once only with a characteristic) \n• Tall/steep/high wave (1)\n• Plunges/breaks vertically downwards (1)\n• Backwash is stronger than swash (1)\n• High frequency, e.g. more than 10 waves per minute (1)\n• Removes/erodes material from the beach (1)\nNaming the wave type alone is not credited.` },
      { q: `A group of students counted the number of waves breaking on the beach at Hornsea in five one-minute periods (data written for this practice question).\n\nCount 1: 13 waves\nCount 2: 11 waves\nCount 3: 14 waves\nCount 4: 12 waves\nCount 5: 10 waves\n\nCalculate the mean number of waves per minute. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `13 + 11 + 14 + 12 + 10 = 60 (1)\n60 ÷ 5 = 12 waves per minute (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Figure 8 is a description of a photograph of a well-jointed chalk headland at Flamborough Head, East Yorkshire. Large waves are breaking against the foot of the cliff, and the cracks in the rock have been widened into small openings.\n\nUsing Figure 8, outline how hydraulic action erodes the cliff.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point.\n• The force of waves hitting the cliff forces water and air into cracks/joints (1)\n• The air is compressed, then expands as the wave retreats, which weakens and breaks off the rock / widens the crack (1)\nAccept the sheer force of water hitting the cliff dislodging rock for 1 mark.` },
      { q: `Figure 9 is a description of a photograph of a rock groyne on the beach at Mappleton, East Yorkshire. The beach level is about 1.5 m higher on the north side of the groyne than on the south side. The prevailing wind blows from the north-east.\n\nUsing Figure 9, explain why the beach is higher on the north side of the groyne.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point.\n• Longshore drift moves sediment from north to south along this coast (1)\n• The groyne traps the sediment on its updrift (north) side so it builds up there (1)\n• Swash moves material up the beach at an angle with the prevailing wind; backwash returns it at right angles, giving a zigzag movement southwards (1)` },
      { q: `Figure 10 describes the features shown in grid square 0480 of a 1:25 000 OS map extract of a Dorset coastline (map described for this practice question). It contains a black cliff symbol (a line with short hachures pointing seaward) along the whole northern edge of the coast, a stippled (dotted) area of sand at the foot of the cliff in the west of the square, a small isolated cliff-outlined rock in the sea labelled 'The Pinnacle', and the blue mean low water line close to the cliff foot in the east.\n\nUsing Figure 10, describe the coastline in grid square 0480.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid point, max 2:\n• Cliffs along the coast / high, steep coastline (1)\n• Small beach/sandy area in the west of the square (1)\n• A stack (The Pinnacle) offshore (1)\n• Little or no beach in the east / sea reaches the cliff foot at low tide (1)\nAccept use of map evidence (symbols, names).` },
      { q: `Before a rock groyne scheme was built at Mappleton, the average rate of cliff erosion just south of Mappleton, at Cowden, was 1.6 m per year. After the scheme was completed, it was 3.5 m per year (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the rate of erosion at Cowden. Give your answer to the nearest whole number. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(3.5 − 1.6) ÷ 1.6 × 100 or 1.9 ÷ 1.6 × 100 (1)\n= 119% (1)\n(118.75 rounded.) Correct answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `At Medmerry, West Sussex, the Environment Agency completed a managed retreat (managed realignment) scheme in 2013. A new 7 km clay embankment was built inland and the old shingle bank was allowed to breach, letting the sea flood farmland to create new intertidal habitat.\n\nOutline one advantage of managed retreat.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point.\n• Creates new salt marsh/intertidal habitat (1) which supports wildlife such as wading birds / increases biodiversity (1)\n• The salt marsh absorbs wave energy (1) reducing the flood risk to settlements inland, e.g. Selsey (1)\n• Cheaper than maintaining hard defences (1) as the old shingle bank no longer needs constant repair (1)\n• Natural/sustainable (1) as it works with natural processes rather than against them (1)` },
      { q: `Figure 11 is a description of a photograph of cliffs at Aldbrough on the Holderness coast. The cliffs are low and made of soft boulder clay. After heavy rain the surface is wet and cracked, and there are piles of clay at the cliff foot that are being removed by the sea.\n\nUsing Figure 11, explain why these cliffs are affected by mass movement.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point.\n• Boulder clay is soft/weak/unconsolidated (1) so it is easily eroded and loses strength (1)\n• Rainwater saturates the clay, making it heavier and acting as a lubricant (1) so material slumps down a curved slip surface (1)\n• The sea removes the slumped material from the cliff foot (1) leaving the cliff unsupported so it slumps again (1)` },
      { q: `Figure 12 is a description of a photograph of Old Harry Rocks, Dorset. A tall chalk stack stands just off the end of a headland. Next to it there is a much lower rock that is only visible at low tide.\n\nSuggest how the stack in Figure 12 is likely to change in the future.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point.\n• The stack will be eroded at its base by abrasion/hydraulic action, forming a notch (1)\n• The stack will collapse/become unstable (1) to form a stump like the lower rock next to it (1)\n• Weathering (e.g. freeze–thaw) will weaken the top (1)` },
      { q: `Figure 13 is a description of an OS map extract of Hurst Castle Spit, Hampshire. A long, narrow ridge of shingle extends eastwards from the mainland into the Solent. Its end curves sharply towards the north-east. Behind the spit, the map shows the symbols for marsh and mud, crossed by small channels.\n\nUsing Figure 13, identify two features of the spit.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark for each feature, max 2:\n• Long/narrow ridge of sediment (1)\n• Joined to the land at one end / extends out to sea (1)\n• Recurved/hooked end (1)\n• Salt marsh/mudflats behind the spit (1)\n• Made of shingle (1)` },
      { q: `The rock groynes at Mappleton cost about £2 million and were built in 1991 to protect the village and the B1242 coast road.\n\nOutline one disadvantage of building groynes on the Holderness coast.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point.\n• Groynes trap sediment moved by longshore drift (1) so beaches further down the coast (south, e.g. Cowden) are starved of sediment and erode more quickly (1)\n• Expensive to build (1) and need maintenance (1)\n• Can look unattractive/spoil the natural landscape (1) which may put off tourists (1)` },
      { q: `You have carried out a fieldwork enquiry to investigate how a beach changes along a coastline.\n\nOutline one method you could use to measure the size of beach sediment.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point.\n• Measure the longest axis of pebbles with a ruler/callipers (1) for a set number of pebbles, e.g. 10, selected at fixed points along a transect (1)\n• Use systematic/random sampling, e.g. a quadrat every 5 m down the beach (1) and measure each pebble touching a point on the quadrat (1)\n• Use a Powers' roundness chart (1) to classify shape at each sample site (1) — credit only if linked to size/shape enquiry` },
      { q: `On a 1:25 000 OS map of a chalk headland, a spot height of 85 m is marked at the cliff top. The cliff foot at sea level (0 m) is 1 cm away on the map, measured horizontally (map measurement written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the average gradient between the cliff top and the cliff foot. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Horizontal distance: 1 cm at 1:25 000 = 250 m (1)\nGradient = 85 ÷ 250 = 1 in 2.9 (accept 1:3 or 0.34) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Figure 14 is a description of a photograph of a cliff at Flamborough Head at low tide. A deep notch has been cut along the foot of the cliff, and the rock above the notch overhangs the beach.\n\nOutline how the notch in Figure 14 has formed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point.\n• Waves erode the cliff between the high and low tide marks (1) by abrasion/hydraulic action, concentrating erosion at the cliff foot (1)\n• The notch is cut back over time (1) until the overhang becomes unsupported and collapses (1)` },
      { q: `Figure 15 is a description of a photograph of Handfast Point, Dorset. A chalk headland ends in a cliff with a small cave at its base. Just offshore is a tall stack (Old Harry) and a low stump. An arch further along the headland collapsed in the past.\n\nExplain how caves, arches and stacks are formed. Use one or more diagrams (described in words) to support your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true, markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of the sequence of formation, with accurate use of processes (hydraulic action, abrasion, weathering); diagrams, if used, are labelled and support the sequence.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic explanation, e.g. a description of the landforms or a sequence with little reference to processes.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Headlands are made of resistant rock (e.g. chalk) but have lines of weakness such as joints and faults\n• Hydraulic action and abrasion widen a crack into a cave\n• Erosion continues through the headland (or caves on both sides meet) to form an arch\n• The base of the arch is eroded and weathering (e.g. freeze–thaw) weakens the roof until it collapses, leaving a stack\n• The stack is undercut at its base and collapses to form a stump\n• Diagrams showing the stages, labelled with processes, can gain full credit.` },
      { q: `Figure 16 is a description of a simplified geology map of the Isle of Purbeck, Dorset. The rocks run at right angles to the coast. From north to south they are: chalk (resistant), forming the Foreland/Handfast Point; Wealden clays and sands (less resistant), forming Swanage Bay; Purbeck and Portland limestone (resistant), forming Durlston Head.\n\nUsing Figure 16, explain how headlands and bays are formed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of differential erosion on a discordant coast, using Figure 16.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic explanation or description, e.g. 'soft rock erodes to make a bay'.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Alternating bands of resistant and less resistant rock meet the coast at right angles (a discordant coastline)\n• The less resistant clays and sands of the Wealden beds are eroded more quickly by hydraulic action and abrasion, forming Swanage Bay\n• The resistant chalk and limestone erode more slowly and are left jutting out as headlands (Handfast Point, Durlston Head)\n• Once formed, headlands are more exposed to wave energy as waves refract around them, while bays are sheltered, so deposition forms a beach in the bay.` },
      { q: `Spurn Head is a spit at the southern end of the Holderness coast, East Yorkshire. Longshore drift moves sediment eroded from the boulder clay cliffs southwards. At Kilnsea the coast changes direction and the Humber estuary begins.\n\nExplain how a spit such as Spurn Head is formed. Use one or more diagrams (described in words) to support your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true, markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of the formation of a spit, with processes in sequence.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic explanation, may describe the spit with limited reference to processes.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Longshore drift transports sediment along the coast (swash at an angle, backwash at right angles)\n• Where the coast changes direction (e.g. at the mouth of the Humber estuary), sediment continues to be carried and is deposited in the calmer/sheltered water, forming a ridge that extends out to sea\n• Changes in wind/wave direction curve the end to form a recurved/hooked end\n• The river current/tidal flow stops the spit crossing the estuary\n• Sheltered water behind the spit allows mud to be deposited, forming salt marsh.` },
      { q: `Figure 17 is a description of a cross-section through the sand dunes at Studland Bay, Dorset. Near the beach, small embryo dunes about 1 m high are colonised by sea couch grass. Behind them are taller yellow dunes, up to 10 m high, covered by marram grass. Further inland, grey dunes have a darker soil and heather. Low wet areas between some of the dunes are labelled 'dune slacks'.\n\nUsing Figure 17, explain how sand dunes develop.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of dune formation and development (succession), using Figure 17.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic explanation or description of the dune zones.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• A large, wide beach exposed at low tide dries out and onshore winds blow sand inland\n• Sand is trapped by an obstacle (e.g. driftwood, strandline debris) and builds up to form embryo dunes\n• Pioneer plants such as sea couch grass colonise and stabilise the sand\n• Marram grass grows as sand accumulates, binding the sand with long roots, so yellow dunes grow taller\n• As plants die and decay, humus is added, the soil darkens and the dunes become grey dunes with more varied plants such as heather\n• Dune slacks form where hollows reach the water table.` },
      { q: `Slapton Sands in south Devon is a ridge of shingle that stretches across the mouth of a bay. Behind it is Slapton Ley, a freshwater lake.\n\nExplain how a bar such as Slapton Sands is formed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of bar formation linked to the lagoon/lake.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic explanation, e.g. 'longshore drift deposits sand across a bay'.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Longshore drift moves sediment along the coast\n• A spit grows across a bay where there is no strong river current to stop it\n• It continues to grow until it joins the headland on the other side, forming a bar\n• Alternatively, rising sea levels after the last ice age pushed offshore sediment onshore (credit this explanation for Slapton)\n• Water is trapped behind the bar to form a lagoon (Slapton Ley), which is fed by streams.` },
      { q: `Figure 18 is a description of a sequence of three photographs of the same cliff at Skipsea on the Holderness coast. In Photograph A the cliff is steep, with waves reaching its foot at high tide. In Photograph B, taken after a wet winter, a curved section of the cliff top has slumped. In Photograph C the slumped clay has been removed from the beach and the cliff is steep again but further inland.\n\nUsing Figure 18, explain how weathering and mass movement contribute to the retreat of the cliff.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation linking weathering, mass movement and marine erosion in a cycle of retreat, using Figure 18.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic explanation, may describe the changes in the photographs.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Mechanical/chemical weathering and heavy rainfall weaken the boulder clay\n• Rainwater saturates the clay, adding weight and lubricating it, so it slumps along a curved slip plane (rotational slip)\n• Material also moves by sliding or rockfall\n• Waves remove slumped material from the cliff foot (transportation)\n• Waves undercut the cliff foot, making it unstable again, so the cycle repeats and the cliff retreats inland.` },
      { q: `Figure 19 shows beach profiles measured at Hornsea, East Yorkshire, in two months (data written for this practice question).\n\nJuly: beach gradient 6°; wave frequency 7 per minute; beach width 45 m; a sandy ridge (berm) present at the top of the beach.\nJanuary: beach gradient 14°; wave frequency 14 per minute; beach width 25 m; no berm, much of the sand removed, exposing pebbles.\n\nUsing Figure 19, explain why the beach changes between July and January.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of both seasons using wave types, with use of data.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic explanation or description of the changes.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• In July, constructive waves (low frequency, 7 per minute) have a strong swash and weak backwash, so sand is deposited, building a wide beach (45 m) with a berm and a gentle gradient (6°)\n• In January, storms bring destructive waves (high frequency, 14 per minute) with a strong backwash, so sand is removed offshore\n• The beach becomes narrower (25 m) and steeper (14°), exposing coarser pebbles\n• Stronger winds over a longer fetch in winter make waves larger.` },
      { q: `As part of the Lyme Regis Environmental Improvement Scheme, Dorset, the main beach was nourished with sand and shingle and the beach was reprofiled. Beach nourishment is a type of soft engineering.\n\nExplain how beach nourishment and reprofiling help to protect a coastline from erosion.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of how both strategies reduce erosion, with use of the example.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic explanation or description of what the strategies are.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Beach nourishment adds sand/shingle (often dredged offshore or imported) to the beach, making it wider and higher\n• A wider beach absorbs/dissipates wave energy before waves reach the cliffs or sea wall, so less erosion\n• Reprofiling redistributes sediment from the lower beach to the upper beach, changing the gradient to reduce wave energy\n• At Lyme Regis the new sandy beach also protects the sea wall and attracts tourists\n• Both need regular maintenance because longshore drift and storms remove material.` },
      { q: `Figure 20 is a description of an annotated sketch of the coast at Mappleton, East Yorkshire. Two rock groynes extend out across the beach. A revetment of rock armour runs along the foot of the cliff between the groynes, and the cliff behind has been regraded to a gentler slope. The village and the B1242 road lie close to the cliff top. To the south of the scheme, at Cowden, the cliff is steep and freshly collapsed and a farm building stands right at the cliff edge.\n\nAssess the effectiveness of the coastal management strategies used at Mappleton. Use Figure 20 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO3 detailed, balanced assessment of effectiveness, using Figure 20 and own knowledge; AO2 thorough understanding of how strategies work.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear assessment with some balance; some use of Figure 20.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points, mainly descriptive; little or no assessment.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Groynes trap sediment moved southwards by longshore drift, building a wide beach that absorbs wave energy, so the village and B1242 are protected\n• Rock armour/revetment absorbs wave energy at the cliff foot; regrading reduces slumping\n• The scheme (about £2 million, 1991) has been effective at Mappleton itself\n• However, beaches south of the scheme are starved of sediment (terminal groyne effect), so erosion has increased at Cowden, threatening farmland and buildings\n• Rock armour is costly to maintain and visually intrusive\n• Judgement: effective locally, but moves the problem along the coast; effectiveness depends on whose viewpoint is taken.` },
      { q: `Figure 21 describes the coastline between Lulworth Cove and Durdle Door in Dorset. Here, the resistant Portland limestone forms a narrow line of rock along the shore. Behind it are softer clays and sands, and then chalk. At Lulworth Cove, the sea has broken through the limestone and eroded a rounded bay in the softer rock behind. At Durdle Door, the limestone has been eroded into a natural arch.\n\nExplain how geology has influenced the landforms of the Dorset coast. Use Figure 21 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of the influence of rock type and structure on several landforms, with good use of Figure 21 and accurate processes.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation, some links between geology and landforms.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points, mainly descriptive.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Rock type: resistant Portland limestone and chalk erode slowly; clays and sands erode quickly\n• Rock structure: at Lulworth the rocks are parallel to the coast (concordant) — once the limestone is breached, the soft rock behind is eroded rapidly into a cove, with the chalk forming the back wall\n• At Durdle Door, joints and lines of weakness in the limestone were eroded by hydraulic action/abrasion to form a cave and then an arch\n• Elsewhere in Purbeck, rocks are at right angles to the coast (discordant), forming headlands (Handfast Point) and bays (Swanage Bay)\n• Joints/faults in chalk lead to caves, arches, stacks and stumps at Old Harry Rocks.` },
      { q: `Figure 22 shows two opinions about how to manage a stretch of eroding coastline in the UK.\n\nCouncil engineer: "Hard engineering such as sea walls and rock armour gives the most reliable protection for homes and roads."\nLocal conservation officer: "Soft engineering such as beach nourishment and dune regeneration works with nature and is better for the environment and for tourism."\n\n'Soft engineering is a better way to manage coastlines than hard engineering.' Do you agree? Use Figure 22 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed evaluation of both approaches, with a well-supported judgement and use of Figure 22 and examples.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear evaluation, some balance; judgement may be partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points about one or both approaches; little evaluation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Hard engineering (sea walls, groynes, rock armour, gabions) gives strong, immediate protection to high-value property, e.g. sea wall at Lyme Regis; but is expensive (sea wall several thousand pounds per metre), unsightly and can increase erosion elsewhere (Mappleton/Cowden)\n• Soft engineering (beach nourishment, reprofiling, dune regeneration) is cheaper, looks natural and keeps beaches for tourism; but must be repeated, may not cope with severe storms\n• Managed retreat (e.g. Medmerry) creates habitat but loses land\n• Credit a judgement that the best choice depends on the value of land at risk, cost–benefit analysis and location; many schemes combine both.` },
      { q: `You have carried out a physical geography fieldwork enquiry at a coast to investigate whether longshore drift moves sediment along the beach. You measured the height of the beach on both sides of five groynes and measured the size of 10 pebbles at each sample point.\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of your data collection methods in this enquiry.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed evaluation of methods, with strengths and limitations, and reasoned judgement on how far they allowed the question to be answered.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear evaluation, some strengths and limitations.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points about methods, mainly descriptive.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Measuring beach height on each side of groynes (e.g. with a tape/metre rule) gives direct evidence of sediment build-up on the updrift side\n• Five groynes give a reasonable sample, but more sites would improve reliability\n• Pebble size measured with a ruler/callipers along the long axis is quick but may involve human error; random or systematic sampling reduces bias\n• Data collected on one day only — tides, recent storms or weather could affect results\n• Safety: tides and slippery rocks limited time/sites\n• Judgement on reliability and accuracy, with suggested improvements (repeat visits, more groynes, a tracer experiment).` },
      { q: `Figure 23 describes the Medmerry managed realignment scheme in West Sussex, completed in 2013. A new 7 km embankment was built up to 2 km inland, costing about £28 million. The old shingle bank was allowed to breach, and about 180 hectares of new salt marsh and mudflat habitat formed. Around 350 homes, a water treatment works and holiday parks behind the new embankment are now better protected. Some farmland has been permanently lost to the sea.\n\nAssess the advantages and disadvantages of managed retreat as a coastal management strategy. Use Figure 23 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of both advantages and disadvantages, with good use of Figure 23 and a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear assessment, some balance and use of Figure 23.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points, little use of Figure 23.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Advantages: new salt marsh/mudflats absorb wave energy and reduce flood risk; homes, a treatment works and holiday parks are better protected; new habitat for birds and wildlife (RSPB reserve), increasing biodiversity; sustainable and cheaper long-term than repairing the shingle bank; attracts visitors\n• Disadvantages: high initial cost (about £28 million); farmland lost and farmers may need compensation; some people dislike losing land to the sea; may change footpaths/access\n• Judgement: effective where land value is low compared with the cost of hard defences, and with rising sea levels it is more sustainable.` },
      { q: `Figure 24 is a description of a map of the Holderness coast, East Yorkshire. The coast runs about 61 km from Flamborough Head (chalk) to Spurn Head. The cliffs are made of boulder clay (till). The coast faces the North Sea, with a long fetch from the north-east. Hard engineering protects Bridlington, Hornsea, Mappleton and Withernsea; between these towns the coast is not protected. The beaches are narrow.\n\nExplain why the Holderness coast is one of the fastest-eroding coastlines in Europe. Use Figure 24 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of several physical and human factors, well linked, using Figure 24.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some factors.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points, mainly descriptive.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Geology: boulder clay is soft and unconsolidated, easily eroded by hydraulic action and abrasion\n• Mass movement: clay saturated by rain slumps, and the sea removes it\n• Long fetch from the north-east gives powerful destructive waves, especially in winter storms\n• Narrow beaches offer little protection; boulder clay contains little sand, so material is carried away in suspension\n• Longshore drift moves sediment south towards Spurn Head\n• Human factors: groynes at towns (e.g. Mappleton, Hornsea) trap sediment, starving beaches further south\n• Average erosion around 2 m per year, locally much more.` },
      { q: `Figure 25 is a description of weather records for the Holderness coast during one winter (data written for this practice question). There were 9 storms with winds over 60 km/h from the north-east, rainfall was 40% above average, and there were 22 nights with air frost. Cliff retreat at one site was 4.5 m, compared with a long-term average of 2 m per year.\n\nExplain how weather and climate affect coastal processes. Use Figure 25 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of how several aspects of weather and climate affect erosion, weathering and mass movement, using Figure 25.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some links.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Storms and strong winds create powerful destructive waves, increasing hydraulic action and abrasion; prevailing wind direction controls the direction of longshore drift\n• Heavy rainfall saturates cliffs, causing slumping and other mass movement\n• Frosts lead to freeze–thaw weathering, loosening rock\n• Warm, wet climates encourage chemical weathering (carbonation of chalk/limestone)\n• Seasonal changes: more destructive waves in winter, constructive in summer\n• Use of data: 9 storms, 40% above average rain and 22 frosts coincide with 4.5 m retreat, more than double the 2 m average.` },
      { q: `Lyme Regis, Dorset, is built on unstable cliffs of clay and limestone. Since the 1990s the Lyme Regis Environmental Improvement Scheme has included a new sea wall and promenade, rock armour, beach nourishment, and cliff stabilisation using drainage and soil nails. The town relies heavily on tourism.\n\nFor a named example of a coastal management scheme in the UK, assess the social, economic and environmental effects of the scheme. You may use Lyme Regis or another example you have studied.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of a range of effects across social, economic and environmental themes, with accurate place-specific detail.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear assessment, some place-specific detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points, little place-specific detail.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content (Lyme Regis):\n• Social: homes and roads protected from landslips and erosion; reduced fear and insurance problems; new promenade improves access and quality of life\n• Economic: new sandy beach and promenade boost tourism and local businesses; property values protected; but schemes cost tens of millions of pounds of public money\n• Environmental: rock armour and sea wall change the natural landscape of a World Heritage coast; cliff stabilisation may reduce exposure of fossils; nourished beach changes habitats; erosion may be displaced along the coast\n• Credit equivalent points for another scheme (e.g. Mappleton, Swanage).\n• A judgement on the overall balance of effects is needed for Level 3.` },
      { q: `Figure 26 describes part of a 1:25 000 OS map extract of the Studland area of Dorset. In the south, a chalk ridge ends at the coast in steep cliffs, with an offshore stack labelled 'Old Harry' and a stump. North of this, a wide sandy beach curves along Studland Bay, backed by ridges shown with sand symbols and rough grassland. In the far north the land narrows to a point (South Haven Point) at the entrance to Poole Harbour, with mud and marsh symbols on the harbour side.\n\nUsing Figure 26, explain the formation of the landforms shown.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of both erosional and depositional landforms, accurately linked to map evidence and processes.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some landforms, some map evidence.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic identification/description of landforms.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Erosional: resistant chalk forms a headland; joints eroded by hydraulic action and abrasion into caves and arches, which collapse to leave the stack (Old Harry) and stump\n• Depositional: in sheltered Studland Bay, constructive waves deposit sand to form a wide beach\n• Onshore winds blow dry sand inland; it is trapped and colonised by marram grass to form sand dunes (the ridges)\n• Longshore drift moves sediment northwards, forming a spit at South Haven Point\n• Sheltered water behind the spit/in the harbour allows mud deposition and salt marsh.` },
      { q: `Figure 27 describes the coast at Happisburgh, Norfolk. The soft sand and clay cliffs here have retreated by over 100 m since the 1990s. The old wooden revetments have collapsed and the Shoreline Management Plan has chosen a policy of 'no active intervention' for this stretch. Over 30 properties have been lost. Some homeowners were helped to move inland.\n\nExplain why some stretches of the UK coastline are not protected. Use Figure 27 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of several reasons, linked to cost–benefit, sustainability and Figure 27.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some reasons.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Cost–benefit analysis: the cost of defences may be greater than the value of the land/property protected (low-value farmland, a few homes)\n• Shoreline Management Plans choose between hold the line, advance the line, managed retreat and no active intervention\n• Protecting one stretch can increase erosion downdrift by trapping sediment\n• Eroding cliffs supply sediment to beaches further along the coast, protecting other places\n• Sea level rise and storms make long-term protection unsustainable\n• Allowing natural processes conserves habitats and geology\n• Use of Figure 27: collapsed revetments too costly to replace; residents relocated rather than defended.` },
      { q: `'Hard engineering is the most effective way to protect the UK coastline from erosion.'\n\nTo what extent do you agree with this statement? Use a named example of a coastal management scheme in the UK in your answer.\n[9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO2 thorough understanding of hard and soft engineering and managed retreat; AO3 detailed, balanced evaluation with a well-substantiated judgement; detailed place-specific knowledge of a named scheme.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear understanding; some evaluation and a partly supported judgement; some place-specific detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic understanding; limited evaluation; little or no place-specific detail.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Hard engineering: sea walls reflect wave energy, rock armour/gabions absorb it, groynes trap longshore drift sediment — strong, long-lasting protection for high-value land\n• Example: Mappleton (1991, about £2 million): two rock groynes and rock armour protect the village and B1242, but erosion increased at Cowden downdrift\n• Example: Lyme Regis — sea wall and rock armour combined with beach nourishment and cliff stabilisation\n• Disadvantages of hard engineering: high cost, maintenance, visual impact, can displace erosion\n• Soft engineering (nourishment, reprofiling, dune regeneration) and managed retreat (Medmerry) can be more sustainable and cheaper\n• Judgement: hard engineering is most effective locally for valuable assets, but not for the whole coastline; integrated schemes often work best.\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance 3: spelling and punctuation consistently accurate; grammar used to control meaning; wide range of specialist terms used accurately.\nIntermediate performance 2: considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance 1: reasonable accuracy; does not hinder meaning; limited range of specialist terms.\n0: no answer, or not related to the question, or errors severely hinder meaning.` },
      { q: `Figure 28 describes the coastline of the Isle of Purbeck, Dorset. North of Swanage, a chalk headland ends in Old Harry Rocks (stacks and stumps). Swanage Bay is cut into clays and sands. Further south, limestone forms Durlston Head. To the west, Lulworth Cove is a rounded bay behind a narrow gap in limestone, and Durdle Door is a limestone arch. Studland Bay has a sandy beach and sand dunes.\n\nTo what extent is the landscape of a named UK coastline the result of geology rather than physical processes? You may use Figure 28 and your own understanding.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO2 thorough understanding of the interaction of geology and processes; AO3 detailed evaluation with a substantiated judgement; detailed place-specific detail.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear understanding; some evaluation; some place-specific detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic understanding; limited evaluation; little place-specific detail.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Geology: discordant coast (chalk, Wealden beds, limestone) gives headlands and bays; concordant section gives Lulworth Cove; joints and faults are lines of weakness for caves and arches\n• Processes: erosion (hydraulic action, abrasion, attrition, solution), weathering (freeze–thaw, carbonation of chalk/limestone), mass movement (slumping in clays), transportation (longshore drift) and deposition (beaches, dunes at Studland, spit at South Haven Point)\n• Depositional landforms depend more on processes and sediment supply than geology\n• Climate and weather (storms, prevailing south-westerly winds) control the energy available\n• Judgement: geology sets the pattern and the rate, but landforms are only produced by processes acting upon it; both interact.` },
      { q: `You have carried out a physical geography fieldwork enquiry at a coast. Your enquiry question was: 'How effective are the coastal defences at protecting the beach and cliffs?' You collected beach profiles, measured sediment size, and completed an environmental quality survey of the defences.\n\n'The conclusions of my fieldwork enquiry were reliable.' To what extent do you agree with this statement?\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO3 detailed evaluation of the reliability of conclusions, linked to specific methods, data and the enquiry question; a well-substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear evaluation with some links to the methods and data; a partly supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic points about the enquiry; little evaluation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Reliability depends on sample size, sampling strategy (random/systematic/stratified), and whether the data were repeated\n• Beach profiles with ranging poles and a clinometer: possible human error in reading angles; limited number of transects\n• Sediment measurement: subjective judgement of roundness; sample size of pebbles\n• Environmental quality survey: subjective scores, different group members may score differently\n• Data collected on one day; recent storms, tides or seasons could change beach shape\n• Secondary data (e.g. erosion rates, Shoreline Management Plan) can support or contradict conclusions\n• Judgement: partly reliable; suggestions for improvement (more sites, repeat visits, compare protected and unprotected stretches).` },
      { q: `Figure 29 is a description of a table about two contrasting coastal management approaches (figures rounded).\n\nMappleton, East Yorkshire (hold the line, 1991): about £2 million; two rock groynes and rock armour; protects about 50 homes and the B1242 road; erosion has increased downdrift at Cowden.\nMedmerry, West Sussex (managed realignment, 2013): about £28 million; 7 km inland embankment; about 180 ha of new intertidal habitat; protects about 350 homes; some farmland lost.\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of a coastal management scheme in the UK. Use Figure 29 and a named example you have studied.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO2 thorough understanding of the management strategies; AO3 detailed evaluation of effectiveness against clear criteria (social, economic, environmental, sustainability), with a well-substantiated judgement; good use of Figure 29 and detailed place-specific knowledge.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear understanding; some evaluation and use of Figure 29; some place-specific detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic understanding; limited evaluation; little place-specific detail.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Reasons for management: rapid erosion of boulder clay at Holderness (about 2 m per year); flood risk at Medmerry from a low shingle bank\n• Mappleton: cheap relative to Medmerry; effective at protecting homes and road; but terminal groyne effect at Cowden; not sustainable in the long term with rising sea levels\n• Medmerry: expensive, but protects more homes; creates habitat and recreation; works with natural processes; loss of farmland\n• Criteria: cost per home protected, environmental effects, impacts on other places, sustainability\n• Judgement: effectiveness depends on the criteria used and the timescale; credit any reasoned conclusion.` }
    ]
  },
  '1.8': {
    name: 'River Landscapes in the UK',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 1 (data written for this practice question): a long profile of a river in northern England. The source is at 620 m above sea level. The height falls to 300 m in the first 10 km, to 100 m by 30 km, and to 20 m by 60 km. The river reaches the sea at 75 km from the source.\n\nUsing Figure 1, describe how the gradient of the river changes downstream from the source.`, marks: 1, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for: the gradient becomes gentler / less steep downstream (allow: steep near the source, gentle near the mouth; it falls 320 m in the first 10 km but only 80 m between 30 km and 60 km).\nDo not accept a list of heights with no description of change.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 2 (data written for this practice question): a storm hydrograph for a river in Cumbria. Rainfall peaks at 14 mm in the hour ending 09:00 on 3 December. Discharge rises from a base flow of 8 cumecs to a peak of 46 cumecs at 21:00 on 3 December, then falls back to 12 cumecs by 21:00 on 4 December.\n\nUsing Figure 2, state the peak discharge of the river.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `1 mark: 46 cumecs (m³/s). Units required.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 3: a description of a photograph of the upper course of the River Tees near its source on Cross Fell in the North Pennines. A narrow stream about 2 m wide flows between steep valley sides. Large, angular boulders lie in the channel and the valley floor is almost entirely taken up by the river.\n\nUsing Figure 3, give one reason why the boulders in the channel are angular.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one valid reason, e.g.:\n• they are close to the source so have not been transported far\n• there has been little attrition/abrasion to round them yet\n• they have recently fallen from the valley sides (weathering/mass movement).` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 4: an OS map extract (1:50 000, described for this practice question). A river enters the extract in grid square 8432 in the north-west and flows to grid square 8728 in the south-east. Spot heights fall from 410 m in square 8432 to 260 m in square 8728.\n\nUsing Figure 4, state the direction in which the river is flowing.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `1 mark: south-east (towards the south-east). Accept SE. Do not accept "east" or "south" alone.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 5 (data written for this practice question): fieldwork measurements of channel depth at five sites on a river in Yorkshire.\nSite 1 (1 km from source): 0.12 m\nSite 2 (8 km): 0.31 m\nSite 3 (17 km): 0.46 m\nSite 4 (29 km): 0.85 m\nSite 5 (41 km): 1.30 m\n\nSuggest one reason why the river channel becomes deeper downstream.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for one valid reason, e.g.:\n• tributaries join, so there is more water/greater discharge\n• more energy is available for vertical/lateral erosion of the bed\n• greater discharge needs a larger channel cross-section.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 6: a description of a photograph of a river bank on the outside of a meander bend on the River Severn. The bank is steep, about 2 m high, with exposed tree roots and soil slumping into fast-flowing water.\n\nUsing Figure 6, name the landform shown.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `1 mark: river cliff (accept bluff). Do not accept "meander" alone.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 7: a description of an OS map extract of part of the town of Banbury, Oxfordshire. The River Cherwell flows through the town. Much of the land close to the river is built up, with housing estates, an industrial estate and car parks on flat land shown by widely spaced contours.\n\nSuggest one reason why land use in Figure 7 may increase the risk of flooding.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for one valid reason, e.g.:\n• impermeable surfaces (tarmac, concrete, roofs) mean less infiltration / more surface run-off\n• drains carry water quickly to the river, shortening lag time\n• buildings on the floodplain are in the path of floodwater.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 2 (data written for this practice question): a storm hydrograph for a river in Cumbria. Rainfall peaks at 14 mm in the hour ending 09:00 on 3 December. Discharge rises from a base flow of 8 cumecs to a peak of 46 cumecs at 21:00 on 3 December, then falls back to 12 cumecs by 21:00 on 4 December.\n\nUsing Figure 2, calculate the lag time. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: time of peak discharge minus time of peak rainfall (21:00 − 09:00).\n1 mark for correct answer: 12 hours.\nUnits required for the second mark.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 8: an OS map extract (1:25 000, described for this practice question) of a river valley in the Lake District. A footbridge over the river is at grid reference 342 076. A waterfall is shown upstream at grid reference 334 091. On the map the straight-line distance between them measures 6.8 cm.\n\nUsing Figure 8, calculate the straight-line distance on the ground between the footbridge and the waterfall. Give your answer in km.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for using the scale correctly: 1 cm = 250 m (or 4 cm = 1 km), e.g. 6.8 × 250 = 1700 m.\n1 mark for the answer: 1.7 km (allow 1.6–1.8 km for measuring tolerance).` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 9: an OS map extract (1:50 000, described for this practice question) of Upper Teesdale. A spot height of 450 m is marked on the valley side at grid reference 862 290. The river below, at grid reference 868 284, is at 330 m. The horizontal distance between the two points is 2.4 km.\n\nCalculate the average gradient between the two points. Give your answer as a ratio (1 : x). Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: vertical drop 450 − 330 = 120 m; horizontal distance 2.4 km = 2400 m; 2400 ÷ 120.\n1 mark for answer: 1 : 20.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 10: an OS map extract (1:50 000, described for this practice question). The symbol for a waterfall lies in grid square 8828. It is three-tenths of the way east across the square from easting 88 and seven-tenths of the way north from northing 28.\n\nUsing Figure 10, give the six-figure grid reference of the waterfall.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `2 marks: 883 287.\n1 mark if one of the two halves is correct (883 or 287) or if the numbers are correct but reversed (287 883).` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 11 (data written for this practice question): discharge of a river in Somerset.\nAverage discharge on 1 October: 12 m³/s\nPeak discharge during a storm on 24 October: 87 m³/s\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in discharge between 1 October and the peak on 24 October. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: (87 − 12) ÷ 12 × 100 or 75 ÷ 12 × 100.\n1 mark for answer: 625%.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 12 (data written for this practice question): a student measured surface velocity at one site on a stream in Derbyshire by timing a float over 10 m. Five results were: 0.42 m/s, 0.55 m/s, 0.38 m/s, 0.61 m/s, 0.49 m/s.\n\nCalculate the mean velocity at this site. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: (0.42 + 0.55 + 0.38 + 0.61 + 0.49) ÷ 5 = 2.45 ÷ 5.\n1 mark for answer: 0.49 m/s.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nIn the upper course of a river such as the River Tees, the river erodes its bed during periods of high discharge.\n\nOutline how hydraulic action erodes the bed and banks of a river.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• the force of the water hits the bed/banks (1); it forces air and water into cracks, compressing it and loosening/breaking off material (+1).\nNo credit for describing abrasion or solution.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 14 (data written for this practice question): channel width at four sites on a river in north Wales.\nSite A (3 km from source): 2.1 m\nSite B (12 km): 4.8 m\nSite C (24 km): 4.5 m\nSite D (38 km): 11.2 m\n\nUsing Figure 14, describe the changes in channel width downstream.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for the overall trend: width increases downstream.\n1 mark for use of data or an anomaly, e.g. it increases from 2.1 m to 11.2 m (more than five times wider); there is a slight decrease between Site B and Site C (4.8 m to 4.5 m); the largest increase is between C and D (6.7 m).` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 15: a description of a cross-section across a meander bend on the River Tees near Yarm. The outer bank is steep and the water there is about 2.5 m deep. The inner bank slopes gently and the water is about 0.4 m deep, with a bank of sand and gravel exposed above the water.\n\nExplain why deposition takes place on the inside of the meander bend.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• the water is shallower on the inside of the bend so there is more friction (1); the velocity is lower so the river has less energy and drops its load, forming a slip-off slope / point bar (+1).` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 16: a description of a photograph of the steep, narrow Valency valley above Boscastle, Cornwall, where a flash flood occurred on 16 August 2004 after intense rainfall.\n\nOutline one physical factor that increases the risk of flooding.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a factor, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• steep valley sides (1) so rainwater runs off quickly into the river, reducing lag time (+1)\n• intense/prolonged rainfall (1) so the ground becomes saturated and cannot infiltrate more water (+1)\n• impermeable rock such as clay or granite (1) so water flows over the surface rather than infiltrating (+1).\nOnly one factor credited.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 17: a description of a labelled cross-section of a river in its lower course. Raised banks about 1.5 m high run along both sides of the channel. They are made of coarse sediment closest to the river, and finer silt is spread across the flat floodplain beyond them. The river bed is slightly higher than the floodplain.\n\nUsing Figure 17, identify two features of the levees.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark each for any two from Figure 17:\n• raised banks along both sides of the channel\n• about 1.5 m high\n• made of coarser sediment near the river\n• finer sediment further away on the floodplain\n• the river bed is above the level of the floodplain.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 18: information about the Banbury flood alleviation scheme on the River Cherwell, completed in 2012. It includes a 2.9 km earth embankment that creates a flood storage area upstream of the town, a raised section of the A361 road, and a new pumping station.\n\nOutline one social benefit of the Banbury flood alleviation scheme.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a benefit, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• fewer homes flood (1) — several hundred homes are at lower risk, so residents avoid the stress and cost of evacuation and repairs (+1)\n• the raised A361 stays open in floods (1) so people can still travel to work/school/hospital (+1)\n• lower insurance premiums/easier to get insurance (1), giving peace of mind (+1).\nEconomic or environmental benefits only credited if linked to people.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 19: a description of a photograph of the upper Tees valley. The valley sides are steep and slope down to a narrow river channel. Spurs of higher land stick out alternately from each side of the valley, and the river winds around them.\n\nUsing Figure 19, describe the cross profile of the valley.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid descriptive point from the figure:\n• V-shaped\n• steep valley sides\n• narrow valley floor taken up by the river channel\n• interlocking spurs from alternate sides.\nExplanations of formation are not credited.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nIn parts of the River Severn catchment, woodland has been cleared to create farmland.\n\nSuggest how deforestation may increase the risk of flooding.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• less interception by leaves (1) so more rain reaches the ground quickly, shortening lag time (+1)\n• fewer roots so less infiltration/less water taken up by trees (1) so more surface run-off reaches the river (+1)\n• soil is eroded into the river (1), raising the river bed and reducing channel capacity (+1).` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 20: a description of a photograph of the Tees estuary at Teesmouth near Redcar at low tide. Wide areas of mud and sand are exposed either side of the channel at Seal Sands, with salt marsh plants growing on the higher parts.\n\nExplain why mudflats form in an estuary.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• when the tide comes in the river is slowed/meets the sea (1) so it loses energy and fine silt/mud is deposited (+1)\n• at slack water at high tide the water is still (1) so suspended sediment settles out, building up mudflats which are exposed at low tide (+1).` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 21: a description of a photograph of High Force waterfall on the River Tees. The river drops about 21 m over a band of hard dolerite rock (the Whin Sill) that lies on top of softer limestone, sandstone and shale. Below the waterfall a narrow, steep-sided gorge about 700 m long leads downstream.\n\nExplain how a waterfall and gorge are formed. Use one or more labelled diagrams to support your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true, markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear, sequenced explanation linking processes to features; may use labelled diagrams effectively.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic explanation or description; limited reference to processes.\nIndicative content:\n• river flows over a band of hard rock (e.g. dolerite) lying on softer rock\n• soft rock is eroded more quickly (hydraulic action, abrasion) forming a step\n• a plunge pool is eroded at the base by hydraulic action and abrasion\n• the soft rock is undercut, leaving an overhang of hard rock\n• the unsupported overhang collapses; the rocks are used to erode the plunge pool further\n• repeated undercutting and collapse makes the waterfall retreat upstream, leaving a steep-sided gorge (e.g. 700 m gorge below High Force).\nDiagrams can earn credit if labelled with processes.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 22: a description of a photograph of a curved, horseshoe-shaped lake on the floodplain of the River Tees near Yarm. It is separated from the present river channel by a strip of land about 50 m wide. The lake is partly filled with reeds.\n\nExplain how an ox-bow lake is formed. Use one or more labelled diagrams to support your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', diagram: true, markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear, sequenced explanation of erosion and deposition processes.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic explanation; may describe stages without processes.\nIndicative content:\n• fastest flow on the outside of bends causes lateral erosion (hydraulic action, abrasion)\n• the neck of the meander narrows as the outside bends erode towards each other\n• during a flood, the river cuts through the neck, taking the shortest/steepest route\n• deposition seals off the old meander loop from the new straight channel\n• the cut-off loop becomes an ox-bow lake, which may later fill with sediment and vegetation (a meander scar).` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 17: a description of a labelled cross-section of a river in its lower course. Raised banks about 1.5 m high run along both sides of the channel. They are made of coarse sediment closest to the river, and finer silt is spread across the flat floodplain beyond them.\n\nExplain how levees are formed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of the link between flooding, loss of energy and sorted deposition.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• when the river floods, water spreads over the floodplain\n• friction increases and velocity falls as water leaves the channel, so the river loses energy\n• the heaviest/coarsest material is deposited first, closest to the channel\n• finer material is carried further and deposited across the floodplain\n• repeated floods build up the banks into levees\n• between floods, deposition on the bed may raise the channel.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 23 (data written for this practice question): two storm hydrographs for the same 40 mm rainfall event.\nRiver A (forested upland catchment on permeable sandstone): lag time 18 hours; peak discharge 22 cumecs; gentle rising limb.\nRiver B (urban catchment on clay): lag time 5 hours; peak discharge 61 cumecs; steep rising limb.\n\nExplain why the hydrographs for River A and River B are different.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of factors affecting both hydrographs, using the data.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic explanation of one factor or of one river; little use of data.\nIndicative content:\n• River A: trees intercept rain and take up water; permeable sandstone allows infiltration and slow throughflow/groundwater flow, so lag time is long (18 h) and peak lower (22 cumecs)\n• River B: impermeable concrete/tarmac and clay soils cause rapid surface run-off; drains carry water quickly to the channel, so lag time is short (5 h) and peak high (61 cumecs)\n• steep rising limb on B shows rapid response; B peak almost three times A's.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 24: a description of a photograph taken looking downstream along the River Tees in Upper Teesdale. The river winds from side to side. Ridges of high land project into the valley alternately from the left and right, so the view downstream is blocked.\n\nExplain the formation of interlocking spurs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of vertical erosion and why the river winds around resistant areas.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic explanation or description.\nIndicative content:\n• in the upper course the river has a steep gradient and erodes mainly vertically (hydraulic action, abrasion)\n• the river is small/has limited energy to erode laterally, so it flows around areas of harder rock rather than through them\n• this produces a winding course with ridges (spurs) from alternate sides that interlock\n• weathering and mass movement of the valley sides help form the V-shaped valley between them.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 25: a description of a photograph of the lower course of a river in Lincolnshire. A wide, flat valley floor extends about 2 km either side of a meandering river. The fields are lush grassland with hedges, and there are lines of trees along the river banks.\n\nExplain how a floodplain is formed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation involving both erosion and deposition.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• meanders migrate across and downstream the valley floor through lateral erosion on the outside of bends\n• this widens the valley floor and removes interlocking spurs\n• deposition on the inside of bends (point bars) builds up flat land\n• when the river floods, water spreads out and loses energy, depositing fine silt/alluvium across the valley floor\n• repeated floods build up a thick, flat layer of alluvium.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 18: information about the Banbury flood alleviation scheme on the River Cherwell, completed in 2012. It includes a 2.9 km earth embankment that creates a flood storage area upstream of the town holding up to about 3 million m³ of water, a raised section of the A361 road, and a new pumping station. The town had flooded in 1998 and 2007.\n\nExplain how the Banbury scheme reduces the risk of flooding in the town.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of how two or more parts of the scheme work, with specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic explanation of one part, or list of features.\nIndicative content:\n• the embankment holds back floodwater upstream in the storage area, so less water flows through the town at the peak\n• water is released slowly once the flood peak has passed, lowering peak discharge through Banbury\n• the raised A361 keeps a key route open and acts as part of the barrier\n• the pumping station removes water that would otherwise pond behind defences\n• other defences (e.g. flood walls/earth banks near the railway and industrial area) protect property.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 20: a description of a photograph of the Tees estuary at Teesmouth near Redcar at low tide. Wide areas of mud and sand are exposed either side of the channel at Seal Sands, with salt marsh plants growing on the higher parts.\n\nExplain how deposition forms the landforms of an estuary such as the Tees estuary.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of tidal processes, deposition and resulting landforms.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• an estuary is the tidal mouth of a river where fresh and salt water mix\n• the incoming tide slows the river flow; at slack water velocity falls to almost zero\n• fine suspended sediment (silt/mud) settles out; salt water causes clay particles to clump (flocculation) and sink\n• mudflats build up and are exposed at low tide\n• vegetation colonises higher mudflats, trapping more sediment to form salt marsh.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 26: two opinions about managing flood risk in a market town.\nShop owner: "My shop has flooded twice in ten years. We need concrete flood walls and a dam upstream so it never happens again."\nWildlife Trust officer: "Planting trees in the catchment and letting fields flood upstream is cheaper and better for wildlife."\n\nAssess the benefits of hard and soft engineering for reducing the risk of river flooding. Use Figure 26 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed, balanced assessment of the benefits of both approaches; uses Figure 26 and own understanding; reaches a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear but less balanced discussion of benefits; some use of Figure 26.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points; little assessment.\nIndicative content:\n• hard: dams/reservoirs control discharge and may provide HEP/water supply; flood walls/embankments give high, immediate protection to valuable property (shop owner's view); straightening speeds water away\n• but hard engineering is expensive, can fail in extreme events, and moves flooding downstream; visually intrusive\n• soft: afforestation increases interception and lag time; planned flooding/washlands store water; river restoration creates habitats (officer's view); cheaper, sustainable\n• but soft takes years (trees) and requires land use change; may not protect a town centre on its own\n• judgement: often a combination is most effective, e.g. Banbury combined a storage area with habitat creation.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\n\nUsing a named example of a UK river valley, explain how erosion and deposition have created the main landforms along its course.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation of erosional and depositional landforms, with accurate located detail for the named valley.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of some landforms; some place detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic, generic points.\nIndicative content (River Tees):\n• upper course (source on Cross Fell, about 890 m): vertical erosion forms V-shaped valley and interlocking spurs\n• High Force: about 21 m drop over hard Whin Sill dolerite above softer limestone/shale; undercutting and retreat formed a gorge about 700 m long; Low Force downstream\n• middle course: lateral erosion and deposition form meanders, e.g. near Barnard Castle and Yarm\n• lower course: meanders, ox-bow lakes and levees on a wide floodplain near Stockton-on-Tees/Yarm\n• estuary at Teesmouth: deposition of mud forms mudflats (Seal Sands) and salt marsh.\nCredit any appropriate named UK river.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 27 (data written for this practice question): factors recorded after a flood in a town in West Yorkshire.\n• 95 mm of rain fell in 36 hours after a wet month, so soils were saturated.\n• The catchment has steep valley sides on impermeable gritstone.\n• Moorland above the town has been drained and burned for grouse shooting.\n• New housing has been built on the floodplain since 1990.\n\n'Physical factors are more important than human factors in causing river floods.' Do you agree? Use Figure 27 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed, balanced evaluation of physical and human factors; uses Figure 27; clear supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear discussion; may be unbalanced; some use of Figure 27.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points; limited evaluation.\nIndicative content:\n• physical: prolonged/intense rain and saturated soils cause rapid overland flow; steep slopes shorten lag time; impermeable gritstone limits infiltration\n• human: moorland drainage/burning reduces vegetation and water storage, speeding run-off; urbanisation adds impermeable surfaces and drains; building on the floodplain increases the impact\n• evaluation: physical factors trigger the flood, but human factors increase its magnitude and impact; relative importance varies by place\n• credit any reasoned judgement.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\n\nUsing an example of a flood management scheme in the UK, evaluate how successful it has been.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed evaluation of successes and limitations, with accurate specific detail; supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear but less detailed discussion; some evaluation.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic description of the scheme.\nIndicative content (Banbury, River Cherwell):\n• why needed: floods in 1998 and 2007; 1998 damage estimated at about £12.5 million; railway and roads closed\n• features: 2.9 km earth embankment creating a flood storage area (about 3 million m³); raised A361; pumping station; earth banks/walls; completed 2012, cost about £18.5 million\n• successes: several hundred homes and businesses at lower risk; key routes kept open; new habitats (ponds, hedgerows) created; storage area is a green space\n• limitations: high cost; designed to a set standard so could be overtopped in an extreme event; farmland in the storage area floods\n• credit other UK schemes, e.g. Boscastle (channel widened and deepened, car park raised, new bridge after the 2004 flood).` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 28: an OS map extract (1:50 000, described for this practice question) of part of a river valley in County Durham. In the west of the extract, in grid squares 9228 and 9328, contours are very close together on both sides of a narrow river channel, and the words "Waterfall" and "Gorge" are printed beside the river. In the east, in squares 9827 and 9927, the river makes large loops across a valley floor about 1.5 km wide between the 100 m and 110 m contours; a curved blue lake lies beside one loop.\n\nUsing Figure 28, describe and explain the differences in the river and its valley between the west and the east of the map extract.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed description using map evidence (grid references, contours, symbols) and clear explanation of processes.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear description and some explanation; some map evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic description, little explanation or evidence.\nIndicative content:\n• west (9228/9328): close contours show steep valley sides / narrow V-shaped valley or gorge; waterfall shows hard rock band; explained by vertical erosion, steep gradient, retreat of a waterfall\n• east (9827/9927): wide, flat floodplain (1.5 km, little height change 100–110 m); large meanders; curved lake = ox-bow lake\n• explained by lateral erosion and deposition, greater discharge, gentle gradient, flooding depositing alluvium\n• comparison: valley widens and gradient decreases eastwards (downstream).` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 29: a description of a photograph of a stretch of the River Quaggy in south-east London after a river restoration project. Concrete channel walls have been removed; the river now flows in a natural winding channel through a park, with reeds, a gently sloping grassed area that is allowed to flood, and footpaths.\n\nAssess the extent to which soft engineering strategies such as those in Figure 29 are sustainable ways of managing flood risk.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed assessment of environmental, social and economic sustainability; uses Figure 29; supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear discussion; some assessment.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points.\nIndicative content:\n• river restoration: natural channel and meanders slow flow; park floods safely, storing water; reeds and habitats increase biodiversity; amenity for local people\n• other soft strategies: afforestation, planning/zoning to keep development off the floodplain, flood warnings and preparation\n• sustainable because low maintenance, work with natural processes, do not move the problem downstream\n• limitations: need space (scarce in cities); may not protect against very large floods; land given up to flooding; trees take years to grow\n• judgement on extent.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 1 (data written for this practice question): a long profile of a river in northern England. The source is at 620 m above sea level. The height falls to 300 m in the first 10 km, to 100 m by 30 km, and to 20 m by 60 km. The river reaches the sea at 75 km from the source.\n\nExplain how the long profile and cross profile of a river change from source to mouth. Use Figure 1 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation of changes in both profiles, linked to processes; uses Figure 1 data.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of both profiles or detailed explanation of one.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic description.\nIndicative content:\n• long profile: steep near the source (320 m drop in first 10 km), becoming gentle towards the mouth (concave profile)\n• upper course: vertical erosion; narrow, steep V-shaped valley; small, shallow channel\n• middle course: more lateral erosion; wider valley with gentler sides; channel wider and deeper as tributaries add discharge\n• lower course: wide, flat floodplain; wide, deep channel; deposition dominant\n• explanation: gradient falls as river nears base level (sea level); energy is used for lateral erosion and transport rather than vertical erosion.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 30 (data written for this practice question): results of fieldwork on sediment along a river in Yorkshire.\nSite 1 (2 km from source): mean pebble length 21 cm; mostly angular\nSite 3 (20 km): mean pebble length 9 cm; sub-angular\nSite 5 (45 km): mean pebble length 3 cm; mostly rounded\n\nExplain how fluvial processes change the size and shape of sediment downstream. Use Figure 30 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation of erosion and transportation processes linked to the data.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of some processes with some data use.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points.\nIndicative content:\n• near the source large, angular material comes from weathering and mass movement on steep valley sides\n• attrition: pebbles knock against each other, becoming smaller and more rounded (21 cm angular to 3 cm rounded)\n• abrasion: load scrapes the bed and banks, also wearing the load down\n• transport: traction/saltation of larger load upstream; finer load carried in suspension downstream\n• deposition: larger material dropped first where velocity falls, so finer material reaches the lower course\n• solution: soluble rock dissolved and carried in solution.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 31: information about Boscastle, Cornwall. On 16 August 2004 a flash flood swept through the village after heavy thunderstorms over the steep, narrow valleys of the Rivers Valency and Jordan. About 75 cars were washed into the sea and over 100 people were rescued by helicopter; no one was killed. Afterwards, the channel was widened and deepened, the car park was raised and a low bridge was replaced by a wider one.\n\nAssess the extent to which the physical geography of the area caused the Boscastle flood. Use Figure 31 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed assessment of physical and human factors; uses Figure 31; supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear discussion of factors; some use of Figure 31.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points.\nIndicative content:\n• physical: very intense rainfall in a few hours; steep, narrow valleys give rapid run-off and short lag time; confluence of two rivers in the village; relatively impermeable rocks/thin soils\n• human: village built on the valley floor close to the rivers; low bridge trapped debris and acted as a dam; car park in the floodplain; channel too narrow for flood flows\n• the post-flood scheme addressed human factors (widened channel, raised car park, new bridge), suggesting they increased the impact\n• judgement: physical factors caused the flood; human factors made its effects worse.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 32: two photographs described. Photograph A: a straightened, concrete-lined river channel through a town centre in Greater Manchester. Photograph B: a river in Shropshire where an old field drain has been blocked and a meander restored, with water spilling onto a wet meadow.\n\nDiscuss the advantages and disadvantages of channel straightening and river restoration as flood management strategies. Use Figure 32 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed, balanced discussion of both strategies; uses Figure 32.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear discussion; may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points.\nIndicative content:\n• straightening (A): removes meanders so water flows away faster; less flooding locally; useful in confined urban areas\n• disadvantages: speeds water downstream, increasing flood risk there; concrete is ugly and destroys habitats; costly maintenance; may increase erosion downstream\n• restoration (B): restored meanders and wetlands slow flow and store water, increasing lag time; creates habitats; cheap; low maintenance\n• disadvantages: farmland lost to flooding; needs space; may not protect high-value urban land on its own.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\n\nTo what extent are the costs of a river flood management scheme in the UK outweighed by its benefits? Use an example in your answer.\n[9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): thorough evaluation of social, economic and environmental costs and benefits; detailed, accurate use of a named scheme; well-supported judgement on "to what extent".\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear discussion with some evaluation; some specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic description of a scheme; little evaluation.\nIndicative content (Banbury, River Cherwell):\n• background: floods in 1998 and 2007; 1998 damage about £12.5 million; rail line and roads closed\n• costs: about £18.5 million; farmland in the 2.9 km embankment storage area floods; construction disruption; visual change\n• benefits: several hundred homes and around 70 businesses protected; A361 raised so access maintained; lower insurance costs; new habitats (ponds, hedgerows, wildflower areas); reduced stress\n• evaluation: benefits over the scheme's life exceed cost (avoided damage); but design standard can be exceeded; some environmental change\n• credit other schemes, e.g. Boscastle, Leeds, Jubilee River (Maidenhead).\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance (3): consistently accurate spelling, punctuation and grammar; wide range of specialist terms used precisely.\nIntermediate (2): considerable accuracy; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold (1): reasonable accuracy; limited range of specialist terms.\n0: no relevant answer or errors severely hinder meaning.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 33 (data written for this practice question): a flood risk map of a town in Oxfordshire. About 1200 properties lie in the zone with a 1 in 100 chance of flooding each year. The town centre is built close to the river. Upstream there are 15 km² of farmland and woodland in the catchment.\n\n'Hard engineering is the best way to protect UK towns from river flooding.' Do you agree with this statement? Use Figure 33 and your own understanding.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): thorough, balanced evaluation of hard and soft engineering, applied to Figure 33; well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear discussion, some application to Figure 33; some evaluation.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic points; limited reference to Figure 33.\nIndicative content:\n• hard: dams/reservoirs regulate flow; embankments/flood walls give high, predictable protection to 1200 properties in a dense town centre; flood relief channels divert peak flow; benefits can be costed against damage avoided\n• but hard engineering is costly, needs maintenance, may move flooding downstream, can fail when design standard is exceeded, and damages habitats\n• soft: afforestation and wetland restoration in the 15 km² upstream catchment increase lag time; planned washlands store water; floodplain zoning stops new building; flood warnings reduce impacts\n• but soft takes time, needs land and may be insufficient for an existing town centre\n• judgement: "best" may be a combined approach, e.g. Banbury storage area plus habitat creation.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\n\nUsing a named example of a UK river valley, evaluate the importance of geology in the formation of its landforms.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): thorough evaluation of geology compared with other factors (gradient, discharge, processes); detailed located examples; well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear explanation with some evaluation and place detail.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic, generic points.\nIndicative content (River Tees):\n• geology important: High Force forms where the river crosses hard Whin Sill dolerite above softer limestone/shale; differential erosion gives a 21 m drop and a 700 m gorge; Low Force also on resistant bands; interlocking spurs form where the river winds round harder rock\n• other factors: steep gradient in the upper course drives vertical erosion; increasing discharge downstream drives lateral erosion forming meanders near Yarm; flooding deposits alluvium to build levees and floodplain; tides and flocculation create mudflats at Teesmouth\n• evaluation: geology is crucial for waterfalls/gorges, less so for lower-course landforms which depend on energy, flow and deposition\n• human influence (e.g. Tees Barrage, channel straightening near Stockton) may be credited.` },
      { q: `Section C — River landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 34 (data written for this practice question): storm hydrographs for the same river in north-west England after similar 50 mm storms in 1975 and 2020. In 1975, lag time was 16 hours and peak discharge 38 cumecs. By 2020, after a new housing estate and a ring road had been built in the catchment, lag time was 7 hours and peak discharge 72 cumecs.\n\n'Human activity is now the main reason for increasing flood risk in the UK.' Evaluate this statement. Use Figure 34 and your own understanding.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): thorough evaluation of human and physical factors, uses Figure 34 data accurately (e.g. lag time fell by 9 hours; peak discharge almost doubled, +34 cumecs or about 89%); well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear discussion with some evaluation and use of data.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic points; little use of data.\nIndicative content:\n• human: urbanisation — impermeable surfaces and drains shorten lag time and raise peak (Figure 34); building on floodplains increases exposure; deforestation and moorland drainage; farming practices compacting soil\n• climate change (partly human): more intense rainfall and wetter winters in the UK\n• physical: prolonged/intense rainfall, saturated ground, steep relief, impermeable rock, snowmelt\n• management: better defences and warnings may reduce risk\n• judgement on "main reason".` }
    ]
  },
  '1.9': {
    name: 'Glacial Landscapes in the UK',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 1: a photograph taken from the summit of Helvellyn in the Lake District. A narrow, knife-edged rocky ridge with very steep sides runs eastwards from the summit. On each side of the ridge there is a deep, steep-sided armchair-shaped hollow; the hollow to the north contains a small circular lake (Red Tarn).\n\nUsing Figure 1, name the type of landform formed by the narrow rocky ridge.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `1 mark: arête.\nDo not accept: ridge / pyramidal peak / corrie.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\n\nWhich one of the following is a landform formed by glacial deposition?\nA  Arête\nB  Drumlin\nC  Corrie\nD  Truncated spur`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark: B (drumlin).` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 2: an OS map extract at 1:25 000 of part of a glaciated upland area (map extract written for this practice question). Eastings 32 to 36 run west to east and northings 14 to 18 run south to north. A summit with the spot height 842 m lies in grid square 3415. Measured within that square, the summit is seven-tenths of the way east from easting 34 and three-tenths of the way north from northing 15.\n\nUsing Figure 2, give the six-figure grid reference of the summit with the spot height 842 m.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark: 347153.\nDo not accept the four-figure reference 3415 on its own.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 3: an OS map extract at 1:25 000 of a glaciated valley (map extract written for this practice question). A small corrie lake (tarn) lies in grid square 2108. A long, narrow ribbon lake lies in grid square 2310 and stretches further north-east. A stream flows out of the tarn and down to the ribbon lake.\n\nUsing Figure 3, give the compass direction of the ribbon lake from the corrie lake.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark: north-east (NE).` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 4: a photograph of the Norber erratics near Austwick in the Yorkshire Dales. Large, dark grey, rounded boulders of Silurian sandstone (greywacke) sit on top of a pale grey pavement of Carboniferous limestone. There are no outcrops of the dark rock nearby.\n\nUsing Figure 4, give one reason why the boulders are evidence that ice once covered this area.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one of:\n• The boulders are a different rock type from the bedrock they rest on, so they must have been carried/transported there (by ice).\n• They are too large to have been moved by rivers/wind, so only a glacier could have transported them.\n• The dark rock does not outcrop nearby, so they have been moved from elsewhere.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nThe Lake District National Park, in Cumbria, is a glaciated upland area of the UK. Tourism is its largest industry.\n\nGive one economic activity, other than tourism, that takes place in a glaciated upland area of the UK.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one of: hill sheep farming (e.g. Herdwick sheep); forestry (e.g. Grizedale or Whinlatter Forest); quarrying / slate mining (e.g. Honister); water supply / reservoirs (e.g. Thirlmere supplying Manchester); renewable energy (e.g. small hydro-electric schemes).\nDo not accept: tourism or any tourist activity (e.g. hiking, boating).` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 5: an OS map extract at 1:25 000 of Great Langdale in the Lake District (map extract written for this practice question). On the map, the distance along the flat valley floor from the Old Dungeon Ghyll car park to the New Dungeon Ghyll car park measures 5.2 cm.\n\nUsing Figure 5, calculate the actual distance between the two car parks. Give your answer in km.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: 5.2 × 25 000 = 130 000 cm, or 5.2 × 250 m = 1300 m (1 cm = 250 m).\n1 mark for correct answer: 1.3 km.\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks. Allow 1 mark for 1300 m (correct value, wrong unit).` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 6: an OS map extract at 1:25 000 of a corrie (map extract written for this practice question). The contour at the top of the corrie backwall is 950 m. The contour at the edge of the corrie lake is 700 m. The horizontal distance between these two points, measured on the map, is 500 m.\n\nUsing Figure 6, calculate the average gradient of the corrie backwall. Give your answer as a ratio (1 in …).`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for the vertical drop: 950 − 700 = 250 m, and dividing horizontal distance by it: 500 ÷ 250 = 2.\n1 mark for correct answer: 1 in 2.\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks. Allow 1:2.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 7: a photograph of the backwall of a corrie in the Lake District. The upper rock face is jagged and cracked. Below it, a steep slope of loose, angular rock fragments (scree) stretches down towards the corrie floor.\n\nUsing Figure 7, describe how freeze-thaw weathering produces the angular rock fragments.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `2 × 1 marks, or 1 × 2 for a developed point:\n• Water enters cracks/joints in the rock during the day (or when temperatures are above 0 °C) (1).\n• At night/when the temperature falls below 0 °C, the water freezes and expands by about 9% (1), putting pressure on the rock and widening the crack (d).\n• Repeated freezing and thawing eventually breaks off angular fragments, which fall to form scree (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 8: a photograph of a valley floor in Snowdonia. The bare rock surface has been scratched with long, parallel grooves (striations), and on its downstream side the rock is jagged and broken.\n\nOutline how a glacier erodes rock by plucking.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `2 × 1 marks, or 1 × 2 for a developed point:\n• Meltwater at the base of the glacier seeps into cracks in the bedrock and freezes onto the rock (1).\n• As the glacier moves forward, it pulls/tears away loose pieces of rock that are frozen to it (1), leaving a jagged surface (d).\nDo not credit descriptions of abrasion.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 9: a photograph of Sourmilk Gill near Buttermere in the Lake District. A stream flows out of a small upland valley high on the side of the main valley and falls in a series of waterfalls down the steep valley side to Buttermere lake on the flat floor of the main valley, several hundred metres below.\n\nUsing Figure 9, describe the features of the hanging valley.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `2 × 1 marks:\n• The small valley is high above the floor of the main valley (1).\n• Its mouth/end is cut off by the steep side of the main valley (1).\n• A stream/waterfall drops from it down the steep side to the main valley floor (1).\n• It is a tributary valley that joins the main valley at a much higher level (1).\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 10: a description of drumlins in the Vale of Eden, Cumbria. Many smooth, oval-shaped hills, each about 300–500 m long and 20–40 m high, lie in groups across the valley floor. Their long axes all point in the same north-west to south-east direction. Each hill has a steeper, blunt end facing north-west and a gently sloping, tapered end facing south-east.\n\nUsing Figure 10, describe the shape and orientation of the drumlins.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for shape: smooth / oval / egg-shaped / elongated hills with a steep (blunt) end and a gentle tapered end, about 300–500 m long.\n1 mark for orientation: long axes all aligned north-west to south-east / in the same direction (parallel to the former ice flow).` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 11: visitors to the Lake District National Park (data written for this practice question). 2013: 15.8 million visitors. 2019: 18.4 million visitors.\n\nUsing Figure 11, calculate the percentage increase in visitors between 2013 and 2019. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: (18.4 − 15.8) ÷ 15.8 × 100, or 2.6 ÷ 15.8 × 100.\n1 mark for correct answer: 16.5%.\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks. Allow 16.46% for 1 mark only (not to one decimal place).` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nGlaciers in the UK during the last ice age carried huge amounts of rock across the landscape, some of it hundreds of kilometres from where it was eroded.\n\nOutline one way in which a glacier transports material.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for the method, + 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• Bulldozing (1): the snout of the glacier pushes loose material in front of it as the ice advances (d).\n• Supraglacial transport (1): rock that falls onto the surface of the glacier, e.g. from freeze-thaw on valley sides, is carried along on top of the ice (d).\n• Englacial / subglacial transport (1): material is carried within or frozen into the base of the ice and moves with the glacier as it flows downhill under gravity (d).\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 12: a photograph of a valley glacier. Along each edge of the glacier there is a dark line of rock debris, and above the glacier the valley sides are steep, bare and cracked.\n\nExplain why lateral moraine forms along the sides of a valley glacier.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 × 1 marks, or 1 × 2 for a developed point:\n• Freeze-thaw weathering breaks rock off the steep valley sides above the glacier (1), which falls onto the edges of the ice (d).\n• The glacier also erodes the valley sides by abrasion/plucking, and this material is carried along the edges of the ice (1).\n• When the ice melts, the debris is deposited as ridges along the valley sides (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 13: two opinions about land use in Great Langdale, Lake District.\nHill farmer: "Walkers leave gates open, their dogs worry my sheep and cars block the lanes so I cannot move my tractor."\nVisitor: "I come here to walk the fells. It is a national park, so everyone should be able to enjoy it."\n\nUsing Figure 13, outline one land-use conflict in a glaciated upland area.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying the conflict, + 1 for development, e.g.:\n• Farming vs tourism (1): visitors leave gates open and dogs disturb/worry sheep, which costs farmers money (d).\n• Traffic vs farming (1): parked cars block narrow lanes so farm vehicles cannot get through (d).\nAllow other valid conflicts linked to Figure 13 (e.g. footpath access vs grazing land).` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nLake Windermere in the Lake District is England's largest natural lake. It is about 17 km long and is used by power boats, ferries, sailors, swimmers and anglers.\n\nOutline one strategy used to manage the impacts of tourism at Lake Windermere.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a strategy, + 1 for development of how it reduces an impact, e.g.:\n• A 10 mph speed limit across the whole lake (introduced in 2005) (1) reduces noise, wash that erodes the shoreline and conflict between power boats and quieter users (d).\n• All powered boats must be registered with the National Park Authority (1), so rule-breakers can be traced and fined (d).\n• Lake wardens/rangers patrol the lake (1) to enforce the speed limit and help users (d).\n• Zoning of the lake (1) keeps swimmers and sailors away from faster boats (d).\n• Park-and-ride / lake ferries and buses (1) reduce traffic in Bowness and Ambleside (d).` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nYou have carried out fieldwork on a moraine in a glaciated valley. You measured the long axis of ten stones (data written for this practice question):\n12 cm, 31 cm, 8 cm, 45 cm, 19 cm, 23 cm, 6 cm, 27 cm, 15 cm, 38 cm.\n\nCalculate the median and the range of the long-axis lengths.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark: median = 21 cm (ordered: 6, 8, 12, 15, 19, 23, 27, 31, 38, 45; (19 + 23) ÷ 2).\n1 mark: range = 39 cm (45 − 6).` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 14: a description of corries in the Lake District. Of 30 corries surveyed, 22 face north, north-east or east, and only 3 face south or south-west (data written for this practice question).\n\nSuggest one reason why most corries in the Lake District face north to east.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, + 1 for development, e.g.:\n• North/north-east facing slopes are in shade and receive less sunlight (1), so snow lasts longer and builds up into ice (d).\n• The prevailing winds in the UK are south-westerly (1), so snow is blown over the summits and collects on the sheltered north-east facing slopes (d).` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 15: a photograph of Red Tarn below Helvellyn in the Lake District. A small, round lake lies in a deep, armchair-shaped hollow with a very steep, rocky backwall on three sides and a lip of rock at the front.\n\nExplain the formation of a corrie. Use one or more diagrams (described in words) to support your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO1/AO2 clear, sequenced explanation of the processes, e.g. snow accumulates in a north-facing hollow and compacts to névé/firn and then ice; the ice moves in a rotational (circular) motion; plucking steepens the backwall and freeze-thaw weathering supplies debris; abrasion deepens the hollow floor; less erosion at the front leaves a rock lip; after the ice melts water collects as a tarn (e.g. Red Tarn). Diagrams, if used, are labelled and add to the explanation.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic understanding, e.g. lists processes (plucking, abrasion) with little link to how the shape of the corrie forms, or describes the features only.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 16: a photograph of Yr Wyddfa (Snowdon) in Snowdonia. The summit is a sharp, pointed peak. Several narrow rocky ridges radiate from it, and between the ridges there are deep hollows, some containing small lakes.\n\nExplain how arêtes and pyramidal peaks are formed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of both landforms, e.g. an arête forms when two corries erode back-to-back (or side by side), plucking and freeze-thaw steepening their backwalls until only a narrow knife-edged ridge is left between them (e.g. Striding Edge, Helvellyn); a pyramidal peak forms when three or more corries erode around a single mountain, leaving a sharp peak with steep faces and arêtes radiating from it (e.g. Snowdon).\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic explanation, e.g. "corries erode back" with little detail of the processes, or only one landform explained.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 17: a description of Lake Windermere. It is a long, narrow lake, about 17 km long and up to 1.5 km wide, lying on the floor of a wide, flat-floored valley in the south of the Lake District.\n\nExplain how a ribbon lake is formed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation linking process to landform, e.g. a glacier moving along a valley erodes unevenly; where the rock is softer/weaker or where the ice is thicker (e.g. where tributary glaciers join) there is more abrasion and plucking, deepening a section of the valley floor into a rock basin; after the ice melts, the hollow fills with water from rain and rivers; in some cases terminal moraine blocks the end of the valley and dams the water. Named example (e.g. Windermere, Wastwater, Buttermere) used.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic understanding, e.g. "the glacier eroded a hollow that filled with water".\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 18: a description of the Nant Ffrancon valley in Snowdonia. The valley is straight with a wide, flat floor. Its sides are very steep and end in sharp, cliff-like edges where ridges from the surrounding mountains have been cut off. Small streams drop into the valley from smaller valleys high on each side.\n\nExplain the formation of truncated spurs and hanging valleys.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of both landforms, e.g. the river valley had interlocking spurs before glaciation; a glacier is too large and powerful to flow around them, so it erodes straight through them by abrasion and plucking, cutting off the ends to leave steep truncated spurs; smaller tributary glaciers in side valleys had less ice and eroded their valleys less deeply than the main glacier, so after the ice melted the tributary valleys were left "hanging" high above the main valley floor, often with waterfalls.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic understanding of one or both landforms, little reference to process.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 19: a photograph of a drumlin field in the Ribble valley, Lancashire. Dozens of smooth, egg-shaped hills cover the valley floor, all lying in the same direction. Fields and farms sit on and between them.\n\nExplain the formation of drumlins.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation, e.g. drumlins form under a moving glacier or ice sheet in lowland areas; the ice becomes overloaded with sediment (till) and deposits it, often around an obstacle or where friction with the ground is high; the moving ice then moulds/streamlines the till into an elongated hill, with a blunt steep stoss end facing up-ice and a tapered lee end pointing in the direction of ice flow; they occur in groups called drumlin fields ("basket of eggs" topography).\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic understanding, e.g. "glacier deposits material in hills" with little reference to the shape or ice flow.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 20: a description of a glacial valley in the Lake District. A ridge of mixed, unsorted rocks and clay runs across the valley floor near its lower end. Similar ridges run along the lower valley sides, and an uneven layer of the same material covers parts of the valley floor.\n\nExplain how different types of moraine are formed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of at least two types, e.g. terminal moraine forms at the snout where the glacier stops advancing and dumps material as a ridge across the valley; lateral moraine forms from debris along the glacier edges (from freeze-thaw on valley sides) deposited along the valley sides when the ice melts; medial moraine forms where two lateral moraines join when glaciers meet; ground moraine is till deposited under the glacier as it melts/retreats. Explanation includes why material is unsorted (all sizes dumped together when ice melts).\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic understanding, e.g. one type described or types named with limited explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 21: facts about the Lake District National Park. It was designated in 1951, covers about 2,360 km² and became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2017. It contains England's highest mountain, Scafell Pike (978 m), and England's largest natural lake, Windermere. It lies within a few hours' drive of several large cities.\n\nExplain why a glaciated upland area such as the Lake District attracts large numbers of tourists.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation linking attractions to reasons for visiting, e.g. dramatic glacial scenery (corries, arêtes, ribbon lakes) attracts sightseers and photographers; mountains such as Scafell Pike and Helvellyn attract walkers and climbers; ribbon lakes such as Windermere offer water sports, cruises and sailing; national park/World Heritage status attracts publicity and visitors; good access via the M6 and railway to Windermere from large cities (e.g. Manchester, Liverpool) makes day trips possible; cultural attractions (e.g. Wordsworth, Beatrix Potter).\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic list of attractions with little explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 22: a photograph of a popular path up a mountain in the Lake District. The path has become a wide scar of bare soil and loose stones, several metres across, with gullies where rainwater has run down it.\n\nExplain how footpath erosion in a glaciated upland area can be managed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of strategies and how they work, e.g. stone pitching (laying large stones as steps) gives a hard, durable surface; drainage channels/cross-drains divert rainwater off the path so it does not form gullies; re-seeding and fencing off damaged areas lets vegetation recover; signs direct walkers to stay on the path; named schemes such as Fix the Fells (National Trust and National Park Authority, with volunteers) repair upland paths.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic understanding, e.g. lists strategies with little explanation of how they reduce erosion.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 23: a photograph of Great Langdale in the Lake District. The valley has a wide, flat floor and steep, straight sides. At the head of the valley, jagged peaks rise above steep hollows with scree slopes below them. A waterfall falls from a small valley high on one side.\n\nExplain how processes of glacial erosion and weathering have shaped the landscape shown in Figure 23.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 = 2, AO2 = 4.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of processes (freeze-thaw, plucking, abrasion, rotational and downhill movement) clearly linked to the landforms in Figure 23 (glacial trough, corries, peaks/arêtes, scree, hanging valley); explanation is well sequenced and uses geographical terms accurately.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some processes, with some links to landforms in Figure 23.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic understanding; processes named or landforms described with little explanation or link to the figure.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content: valley widened and deepened by abrasion and plucking from a former V-shape to a U-shaped trough; flat floor from abrasion and later deposition; corries at the head formed by rotational movement, plucking of backwalls; freeze-thaw on exposed peaks gives jagged summits and angular scree; tributary glaciers eroding less leave hanging valleys with waterfalls.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 24: an OS map extract at 1:25 000 of a glaciated upland area (map extract written for this practice question). Contours are packed tightly together in a horseshoe shape around a small lake at 600 m in grid square 4521. From the 870 m summit north of the lake, a narrow ridge runs east, with closely spaced contours on both sides. To the south, a straight valley about 1 km wide has widely spaced contours on its floor at about 150 m and closely spaced contours on its sides. A long, narrow lake lies on the valley floor. A stream drops steeply down the valley side from a small valley at 400 m.\n\nUsing Figure 24, suggest how the landforms shown were formed.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `AO2 = 3, AO4 = 3.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: accurate interpretation of the map evidence to identify several landforms (corrie with tarn, arête, glacial trough, ribbon lake, hanging valley) and detailed, well-linked suggestions of how each formed; uses map evidence (contour spacing, heights, grid reference).\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear identification of some landforms with some explanation of formation; some use of map evidence.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic identification of landforms or processes with little use of the map.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content: horseshoe of tight contours around a tarn = corrie (snow accumulation, rotational movement, plucking, abrasion, rock lip); narrow ridge with steep sides = arête between corries; flat-floored, steep-sided valley = glacial trough eroded by abrasion/plucking; long narrow lake = ribbon lake in overdeepened rock basin; stream falling from a high side valley = hanging valley left by a smaller tributary glacier.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nDuring the last ice age, ice moved from the Lake District mountains out across the lowlands of Cumbria and Lancashire, leaving moraines, drumlins and erratics.\n\nExplain how glaciers move, transport and deposit material, and the landforms that result. Use one or more named examples in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of movement (rotational slip, basal sliding on meltwater, downhill under gravity), transport (bulldozing, carried on, within and beneath the ice) and deposition (when ice melts or is overloaded; unsorted till), clearly linked to landforms (erratics, drumlins, terminal/lateral/medial/ground moraine) with named examples (e.g. Norber erratics, Vale of Eden or Ribble valley drumlins).\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some processes linked to at least one landform; examples may be general.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic understanding; processes or landforms listed with little explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 25: facts about tourism in the Lake District National Park (data written for this practice question). About 18 million visits a year; visitors spend about £1.5 billion a year; about 15,000 jobs depend on tourism; about 83% of visitors arrive by car; around one in five homes in some villages are second or holiday homes; several popular mountain paths need repair each year.\n\n'Tourism brings more benefits than problems to a glaciated upland area.' Do you agree? Use Figure 25 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough evaluation weighing benefits (spending, jobs, support for services, money for conservation) against problems (traffic congestion and pollution, footpath erosion, second homes raising house prices, seasonal/low-paid jobs); detailed use of Figure 25 and own knowledge; a clear, justified conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear consideration of benefits and problems with some use of Figure 25; conclusion may be partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic, one-sided or list-like answer; little use of the figure.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nA conclusion either way can access full marks if supported.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 26: management at Lake Windermere.\n• A 10 mph speed limit has applied to all boats on the lake since 2005.\n• Powered boats must be registered with the National Park Authority.\n• Rangers patrol the lake by boat.\n• Lake ferries link Bowness, Ambleside and the western shore, reducing car journeys.\n• Water companies are investing to reduce sewage and phosphate entering the lake, which can cause algal blooms.\n\nAssess the effectiveness of strategies used to manage tourism at Lake Windermere. Use Figure 26 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of how well several strategies work, with balance (e.g. speed limit reduced noise, shoreline erosion and conflict with sailors/swimmers but reduced water-skiing businesses and visitor spend; registration and patrols help enforcement but cannot cover the whole lake at all times; ferries reduce traffic but car use remains high; water-quality investment is long-term and algal blooms still occur in hot summers); clear judgement about overall effectiveness.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear description of strategies with some assessment of effectiveness.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of strategies with little or no assessment.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 27: two opinions about second homes in a Lake District village.\nLocal teenager: "Most houses in the village are holiday homes. They are empty all winter and house prices are far too high for me to stay here."\nHoliday-home owner: "We spend money in the village shops and pubs and we pay local builders and cleaners."\n\nAssess the impact of second homes on communities in a glaciated upland area. Use Figure 27 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed, balanced assessment of positive impacts (spending in shops and pubs, work for local trades, maintenance of old buildings) and negative impacts (higher house prices push out young local people; houses empty out of season; closure of schools, bus services and shops; loss of community); uses Figure 27 and own understanding; reaches a justified judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear consideration of some impacts with some use of the figure; judgement may be limited.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic, one-sided points.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 28: economic activities in the Lake District.\n• Honister Slate Mine, near Buttermere, quarries green slate and runs mine tours.\n• Grizedale Forest is a large conifer plantation managed by Forestry England, used for timber and also for mountain biking and walking.\n• Hill farms graze hardy Herdwick sheep on the high fells.\n\nAssess the economic and environmental impacts of economic activities other than tourism in a glaciated upland area. Use Figure 28 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed, balanced assessment of economic impacts (jobs in remote areas, income from slate/timber, farms keeping traditional landscape, diversification into tourism) and environmental impacts (quarry scars, noise and dust, heavy lorries on narrow roads; conifer plantations reduce biodiversity but store carbon and reduce run-off; overgrazing by sheep vs maintenance of the open fell landscape); uses Figure 28 and own understanding; judgement reached.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear consideration of some economic and environmental impacts.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points; may only cover one type of impact.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nFigure 29: traffic in the Lake District (data written for this practice question). On a busy summer weekend about 30,000 cars enter the National Park. Narrow roads through Ambleside and along Windermere become congested. Strategies include: park-and-ride from Windermere railway station; car-park charges; extra summer bus services, including open-top buses; promoting the "Cross Lakes" boat and bus link.\n\nAssess the effectiveness of strategies used to reduce traffic congestion in a glaciated upland area. Use Figure 29 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of several strategies with balance (e.g. park-and-ride and buses take cars off narrow roads but are used mainly by day trippers and run less often out of season; car-park charges raise money but can push parking onto verges and lanes; boat links are popular but weather-dependent; most visitors still arrive by car); clear judgement on overall effectiveness.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of strategies with some assessment.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of strategies.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nYou have carried out fieldwork on a moraine ridge in a glaciated valley in the Lake District. Your aim was to find out whether stones in the moraine became more rounded down-valley. At each of three sites you chose stones by picking up the ones that were easiest to reach, measured their long axis with a ruler, and judged their roundness by eye using a roundness chart.\n\nEvaluate the reliability of your data collection methods.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO3 = 6.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed evaluation of strengths and weaknesses of the methods with clear links to reliability, e.g. choosing the easiest stones is biased (larger/surface stones picked more often) – random or systematic sampling (e.g. along a tape at fixed intervals) would be fairer; measuring long axis with a ruler is quick but awkward on irregular stones – callipers would be more accurate; judging roundness by eye with a chart is subjective – the same person should judge all stones or a group should agree; only three sites and a small sample size reduce reliability; repeating the survey would allow results to be checked. Clear judgement on overall reliability.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear evaluation of some aspects of the method with some suggested improvements.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic comments on the method, e.g. "it was not accurate", with little explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nGlaciated upland areas of the UK are used by farmers, foresters, quarry companies, water companies, conservation groups, local residents and millions of visitors.\n\nTo what extent can land-use conflicts in a glaciated upland area of the UK be successfully managed? Use a named example in your answer. [9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed knowledge of a named glaciated upland area (e.g. Lake District) and several land-use conflicts (tourism vs farming; tourism vs conservation; residents vs second-home owners; water supply/reservoirs vs access; quarrying vs scenery); thorough evaluation of management (National Park planning controls, Fix the Fells path repairs, Windermere 10 mph speed limit, park-and-ride/buses, codes of conduct, zoning) showing which conflicts are managed well and which remain; well-supported conclusion on "to what extent".\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear knowledge of conflicts and management, with some evaluation; the example is used but may lack detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic, generic points; little evaluation; example may be missing or only named.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nSPaG (3 marks): High 3 – spelling and punctuation consistently accurate, grammar controls meaning, wide range of specialist terms used precisely; Intermediate 2 – considerable accuracy, general control of meaning, good range of specialist terms; Threshold 1 – reasonable accuracy, reasonable range of specialist terms; 0 – does not reach threshold or no answer.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nThe ice sheets and glaciers of the last ice age left very different landscapes in upland areas such as the Lake District and Snowdonia and in lowland areas such as the Vale of Eden.\n\n'Glacial erosion has had a greater effect than glacial deposition on the landscape of the UK.' Do you agree? Use named examples in your answer. [9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed understanding of erosional processes (abrasion, plucking, freeze-thaw) and landforms (corries, arêtes, pyramidal peaks, glacial troughs, truncated spurs, hanging valleys, ribbon lakes) and of transportation/deposition landforms (erratics, drumlins, moraines); named examples (e.g. Red Tarn, Striding Edge, Snowdon, Great Langdale, Nant Ffrancon, Windermere, Norber erratics, Vale of Eden drumlins); thorough evaluation, e.g. erosion dominates the upland scenery while deposition shapes large lowland areas and influences farming/settlement; a well-supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear understanding of both erosion and deposition with some examples and some evaluation.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic, mainly descriptive answer; may cover only erosion or deposition; little evaluation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nGlaciated upland areas such as the Lake District attract millions of visitors each year, putting pressure on paths, roads, lakes and villages.\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of strategies used to manage tourism in a glaciated upland area of the UK. Use a named example in your answer. [9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed knowledge of the impacts of tourism in a named area (e.g. Lake District: footpath erosion, traffic congestion, pollution and conflict on Windermere, honeypot sites such as Bowness and Keswick) and of specific strategies (Fix the Fells stone pitching and drainage; 10 mph speed limit and boat registration on Windermere; park-and-ride, buses and lake ferries; car-park charges; visitor centres and education); thorough evaluation of how well each works and its drawbacks; a well-supported overall judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear knowledge of some strategies with some evaluation; the example is used but may lack detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic, generic description of strategies with little evaluation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` },
      { q: `Section C — Glacial landscapes in the UK.\nThe steep slopes, thin soils, high rainfall and dramatic scenery of glaciated upland areas create both opportunities and difficulties for the people who live and work there.\n\nDiscuss how the physical landscape of a glaciated upland area of the UK creates both opportunities and conflicts for economic activities. Use a named example in your answer. [9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `AO1 = 3, AO2 = 3, AO3 = 3.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed discussion of a named area (e.g. Lake District) showing how landforms and conditions create opportunities (corries, arêtes and ribbon lakes attract tourism; steep wet fells suit hill sheep farming and forestry; ribbon lakes and high rainfall suit water supply, e.g. Thirlmere for Manchester; hard rocks suit slate quarrying at Honister) and conflicts (farming vs walkers, quarrying vs scenery and conservation, reservoirs vs access and landscape, forestry vs biodiversity, tourism vs local residents); well-balanced, with a supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear discussion of some opportunities and conflicts linked to the landscape; example used but may lack detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic points, often generic or one-sided; little link to the physical landscape.\n0 marks: no relevant content.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.1': {
    name: 'Urban Issues in LICs and NEEs',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 1 (data written for this practice question): percentage of the population living in urban areas.\nAfrica: 1950 14%, 2020 43%\nAsia: 1950 18%, 2020 51%\nEurope: 1950 52%, 2020 75%\nLatin America and the Caribbean: 1950 41%, 2020 81%\nNorth America: 1950 64%, 2020 83%\n\nUsing Figure 1, identify the region with the highest percentage of its population living in urban areas in 2020.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark for: North America (83%).\nNo credit for Latin America and the Caribbean.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 1 (data written for this practice question): percentage of the population living in urban areas.\nAfrica: 1950 14%, 2020 43%\nAsia: 1950 18%, 2020 51%\nEurope: 1950 52%, 2020 75%\nLatin America and the Caribbean: 1950 41%, 2020 81%\nNorth America: 1950 64%, 2020 83%\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the increase in the percentage of Asia's population living in urban areas between 1950 and 2020.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for: 33 percentage points (51 − 18 = 33).\nAccept 33%.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 2: a world map showing the 33 megacities (cities of more than 10 million people) in 2018, as identified by the United Nations. 20 are in Asia, 6 in Latin America, 3 in Africa (Cairo, Lagos and Kinshasa), 2 in Europe (Moscow and Paris) and 2 in North America (New York and Los Angeles).\n\nUsing Figure 2, calculate the percentage of the world's megacities that were in Asia in 2018. Give your answer to the nearest whole number.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for: 61% (20 ÷ 33 × 100 = 60.6%).\nAccept 60.6% but do not accept 60%.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 3: a map of south-east Brazil. Brasília, the capital, lies inland on the central plateau. Rio de Janeiro lies about 930 km away on the Atlantic coast, on the western side of the entrance to Guanabara Bay. São Paulo lies about 360 km south-west of Rio de Janeiro.\n\nUsing Figure 3, give the direction of Rio de Janeiro from Brasília.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for: south-east (SE).\nAccept south-south-east. No credit for south or east alone.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 4: a photograph of Rocinha, Rio de Janeiro's largest favela. Thousands of brick and concrete houses, some of them three or four storeys high, are packed together on a steep hillside, with narrow alleys between them and tangles of electricity cables overhead.\n\nSuggest one reason why favelas such as Rocinha were built on steep hillsides.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for any one valid reason, e.g.\n• the land was unused/unwanted because it was too steep for formal building\n• the land was not owned/guarded, so people could occupy it illegally\n• the hillsides were the only land left that was close to jobs in the city centre/wealthy areas.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nIn Lagos, Nigeria, a large share of the city's growth is now caused by natural increase rather than by migration.\n\nGive one reason why rates of natural increase are high in cities in LICs and NEEs.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for any one, e.g.\n• many migrants are young adults of child-bearing age, so the birth rate is high\n• improved access to health care in cities lowers the death rate\n• better access to clean water/vaccinations means fewer infant deaths\n• cultural/religious reasons for large families.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 5: a photograph of part of a favela in Rio de Janeiro after the Favela Bairro project. A newly paved and widened street has street lights and a drainage channel; a small health centre and a children's day-care centre stand beside it.\n\nUsing Figure 5, give one way the Favela Bairro project improved the area.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for any one feature visible in the figure, e.g.\n• paved/widened roads (easier access for vehicles/emergency services)\n• street lighting (safer at night)\n• drainage channels (reduces flooding/landslide risk)\n• a health centre\n• a day-care centre.\nNo credit for features not shown in Figure 5.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 1 (data written for this practice question): percentage of the population living in urban areas.\nAfrica: 1950 14%, 2020 43%\nAsia: 1950 18%, 2020 51%\nEurope: 1950 52%, 2020 75%\nLatin America and the Caribbean: 1950 41%, 2020 81%\nNorth America: 1950 64%, 2020 83%\n\nUsing Figure 1, compare the urbanisation of Africa with that of Europe between 1950 and 2020.`, marks: 2, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Point marked. 1 mark for each valid comparison; use of data is needed for 2 marks.\n• Europe is more urbanised than Africa in both years (75% compared with 43% in 2020)\n• Africa's urban percentage has increased faster/by more (29 percentage points compared with 23 for Europe)\n• Africa's urban percentage has roughly tripled whereas Europe's has increased by less than half.\nComparative words (whereas, more than, faster) are needed; two separate descriptions max 1.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nDharavi in Mumbai, India, is one of the largest slums in Asia. About 1 000 000 people live there in an area of about 2.1 km².\n\nCalculate the population density of Dharavi. Give your answer to the nearest thousand people per km². Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: 1 000 000 ÷ 2.1 (= 476 190.5).\n1 mark for the answer: 476 000 people per km².\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks. Allow 1 mark for 476 190 or 476 191 (not rounded as asked).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 6 (data written for this practice question): the estimated population of Lagos, Nigeria — 1990: 4.8 million; 2020: 14.4 million.\n\nUsing Figure 6, calculate the percentage increase in the population of Lagos between 1990 and 2020. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: (14.4 − 4.8) ÷ 4.8 × 100, or 9.6 ÷ 4.8 × 100.\n1 mark for the answer: 200%.\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 7: a quotation from a young man who moved from a village in north-east Brazil to Rio de Janeiro.\n"Our land was dry for three years in a row and the crops failed. There was no secondary school and the nearest clinic was a day's walk away."\n\nUsing Figure 7, outline one push factor that caused this person to leave his village.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a push factor identified from the figure; 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Drought/crop failure (1) meant the family could not grow enough food or earn an income from farming (1).\n• Lack of services such as schools/health care (1) so he could not get an education or treatment when ill (1).\nNo credit for pull factors.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nRio de Janeiro, Brazil, has attracted millions of migrants from rural areas since the 1950s.\n\nOutline one pull factor that attracts migrants to a city such as Rio de Janeiro.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid pull factor; 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• More job opportunities (1), e.g. in the port, oil industry, construction or tourism, which pay more than farming (1).\n• Better access to services (1), such as schools, hospitals and clean water, improving quality of life (1).\n• Perceived better quality of life/'bright lights' (1) as friends and family who have already moved send back stories of success (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 8 (data written for this practice question): a line graph of the world's urban population. In 1950 about 0.8 billion people lived in urban areas, 70% of them in HICs. By 2020 about 4.4 billion lived in urban areas, 75% of them in LICs and NEEs.\n\nOutline one reason why the rate of urbanisation is fastest in LICs and NEEs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid reason; 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Large numbers of people are moving from rural areas to cities (1) because of a lack of jobs and services in the countryside/mechanisation of farming (1).\n• High rates of natural increase in cities (1) because migrants are mostly young and have children (1).\n• Industrialisation/economic development in NEEs (1) creates many factory jobs in cities (1).\n• HICs are already highly urbanised (1), so there is less scope for their urban percentage to rise (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 9 (based on Brazilian census figures, rounded): population of the city of Rio de Janeiro — 1950: 2.4 million; 1970: 4.3 million; 1990: 5.5 million; 2010: 6.3 million; 2022: 6.2 million.\n\nUsing Figure 9, describe the change in the population of Rio de Janeiro between 1950 and 2022.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Point marked. 1 mark for an overall trend; 1 mark for use of data or recognition of a change in rate.\n• The population increased overall, from 2.4 million to 6.2 million (1)\n• Growth was fastest between 1950 and 1970 (an increase of 1.9 million) (1)\n• The rate of growth slowed after 1970 / the population fell slightly between 2010 and 2022 (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nRio de Janeiro is Brazil's second largest city. It hosted the final of the 2014 FIFA World Cup and the 2016 Olympic Games, and is the headquarters of the state oil company Petrobras.\n\nOutline one reason why Rio de Janeiro is important internationally.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid reason; 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Major tourist destination (1) — attractions such as Christ the Redeemer, Sugarloaf Mountain and Copacabana beach attract large numbers of international visitors (1).\n• Hosted global sporting events (1) such as the 2014 World Cup final and the 2016 Olympics, raising its global profile (1).\n• Important port/oil industry (1) — offshore oil is exported and Petrobras attracts foreign investment (1).\n• Its landscape is a UNESCO World Heritage Site (1) recognised for its cultural/natural value (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nSince the 1990s, the city authorities in Rio de Janeiro have built new schools in favelas and started programmes to give adults evening classes.\n\nOutline one social opportunity that urban growth has created for people in a city in an LIC or NEE.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid social opportunity; 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Better access to education (1) so young people gain qualifications and can get better-paid jobs (1).\n• Better access to health care (1), e.g. new clinics/family health teams, increasing life expectancy (1).\n• Access to clean water and electricity (1), reducing water-borne disease (1).\nNo credit for economic opportunities unless linked to a social benefit.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nRio de Janeiro has a growing economy based on services, manufacturing and the offshore oil industry.\n\nOutline one economic opportunity that urban growth has created for people in a city in an LIC or NEE.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid economic opportunity; 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• More formal jobs in manufacturing/services (1) which give a regular wage and taxes for the city (1).\n• Jobs in the oil and gas industry/port (1), attracting investment from transnational companies (1).\n• Growth of tourism (1) creating jobs in hotels, restaurants and as guides, including favela tours (1).\n• Opportunities in the informal economy (1), e.g. street selling, giving an income to people without qualifications (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 10: a photograph of the shoreline of Guanabara Bay, Rio de Janeiro. Grey, foamy water surrounds plastic bottles and rubbish caught in mangroves; a pipe discharges dark water into the bay.\n\nUsing Figure 10, outline one environmental challenge caused by the rapid growth of Rio de Janeiro.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a challenge linked to the figure; 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Water pollution from untreated sewage (1) — many homes in favelas are not connected to the sewerage system, so waste flows into the bay, harming fish and spreading disease (1).\n• Waste disposal/litter (1) — rubbish is not collected from many favelas so it is dumped and washed into the bay, damaging mangrove habitats (1).\n• Industrial pollution (1) from factories/the port and oil refinery around the bay (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nIn 2008 Rio de Janeiro began setting up Pacifying Police Units (UPPs) in some favelas that had been controlled by drug gangs.\n\nOutline one way that a city in an LIC or NEE has tried to reduce crime.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid strategy; 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Pacifying Police Units (1) — police took control of favelas from drug gangs and then stayed permanently to patrol the community (1).\n• Improving street lighting/access roads (1) so police and emergency services can enter and residents feel safer (1).\n• Providing jobs/education for young people (1) so they are less likely to join gangs (1).\nCredit a named city other than Rio de Janeiro.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 11: a photograph of Makoko, an informal settlement on Lagos Lagoon, Nigeria. Wooden houses with corrugated metal roofs stand on stilts above dark water; people move between houses in narrow canoes and there is no road access.\n\nUsing Figure 11, identify two features of the housing in Makoko.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark for each feature shown in the figure (max 2), e.g.\n• built on stilts over water\n• made of wood/scrap materials\n• corrugated metal roofs\n• built close together/high density\n• no road access — reached only by canoe.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nAbout half of the workers in Lagos are employed in the informal economy, for example as street traders, motorbike-taxi riders and waste pickers.\n\nOutline one disadvantage of working in the informal economy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid disadvantage; 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Income is low and irregular (1) so workers cannot plan or save and may not afford rent/school fees (1).\n• No job security, sick pay or pension (1) so illness or old age leads to poverty (1).\n• Work can be dangerous/unregulated (1), e.g. waste pickers handle toxic materials without protection (1).\n• Workers pay no tax (1), so the city has less money to spend on services (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nRio de Janeiro has some of the worst traffic congestion in South America. For the 2016 Olympics the city extended Metro Line 4 to Barra da Tijuca and built Bus Rapid Transit (BRT) routes.\n\nOutline one way that a city in an LIC or NEE has tried to reduce traffic congestion.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid strategy; 1 further mark for development.\ne.g.\n• Extending the metro (1) so more people can travel quickly without using cars, e.g. Line 4 to Barra da Tijuca (1).\n• Bus Rapid Transit lanes (1) which let buses avoid traffic jams, making public transport faster (1).\n• Coastal roads closed to cars on Sundays/toll roads/new expressways (1) to reduce the number of vehicles at peak times (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 12 (data written for this practice question): sources of population growth in a city in an LIC.\n1980–2000: migration 60%, natural increase 40%.\n2000–2020: migration 35%, natural increase 65%.\n\nExplain how natural increase contributes to the growth of cities in LICs and NEEs. Use Figure 12 and your own understanding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of why natural increase is high, with links to migration and use of Figure 12.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points, e.g. "more births than deaths", little explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• Natural increase = births exceed deaths; Figure 12 shows it has risen from 40% to 65% of growth.\n• Most rural–urban migrants are young adults, so once in the city they have children — earlier migration causes later natural increase.\n• Better health care, clean water and vaccinations in cities reduce infant mortality and raise life expectancy, so death rates fall.\n• Birth rates stay high because of cultural expectations, limited access to contraception and children being able to work.\n• Each generation adds to the population, so the city grows even if migration slows.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 2: a world map showing the 33 megacities (cities of more than 10 million people) in 2018. In 1970 there were only 2 megacities (Tokyo and New York); most of the newer megacities, such as Delhi, Dhaka, Lagos and São Paulo, are in LICs and NEEs.\n\nExplain why the number of megacities in LICs and NEEs has increased.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of two or more causes with linked development.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple statements of causes.\nIndicative content:\n• Rural–urban migration: push factors (poverty, drought, conflict, lack of services) and pull factors (jobs, services) bring millions to the largest cities.\n• Natural increase: young migrant populations have high birth rates while death rates fall.\n• Industrialisation and investment by TNCs concentrate jobs in a few large cities, e.g. factories and offices in Delhi or Dhaka.\n• Primate cities/capitals receive most government investment, attracting more people.\n• Cities merge with surrounding settlements as they sprawl outwards.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 13: a photograph of Rio de Janeiro. On the right, high-rise luxury apartments line Ipanema beach; on the left, the hillside favela of Vidigal rises steeply above them, only a few hundred metres away.\n\nUsing Figure 13, explain why squatter settlements often develop close to wealthy areas in cities in LICs and NEEs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation linked to the figure, with developed reasons.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple reasons, little use of figure.\nIndicative content:\n• Wealthy areas provide jobs — e.g. as cleaners, cooks, security guards, builders — so living nearby saves time and transport costs.\n• Steep hillside land next to wealthy areas is unsuitable for formal building, so it was left empty and could be occupied.\n• Migrants could not afford formal housing, so they built their own homes on unused land.\n• Opportunities for informal work, e.g. selling to tourists on the beach.\n• Once a few homes were built, others followed friends and relatives (chain migration).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nThe Port of Rio de Janeiro and the nearby offshore oilfields have attracted large investments since the 2000s.\n\nUsing a case study of a major city in an LIC or NEE, explain how urban growth has created economic opportunities.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of economic opportunities with specific case-study detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: general statements, little place detail.\nIndicative content (Rio de Janeiro):\n• Rio is one of Brazil's main industrial and service centres — jobs in shipbuilding, chemicals, finance and retail.\n• The offshore oil industry (Petrobras headquarters in the city) brings well-paid jobs and foreign investment.\n• Tourism provides jobs in hotels and transport; events such as the 2016 Olympics brought construction jobs.\n• A large workforce and market attract further businesses (multiplier effect), increasing tax revenue to fund services.\n• The informal economy gives an income to those without formal qualifications.\nCredit other valid case studies, e.g. Lagos, Mumbai.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 14: a photograph of a street in a favela in Rio de Janeiro. A pile of rubbish bags lies beside an open drain; a single water pipe runs along the wall to a tap shared by several houses.\n\nExplain the challenges of providing clean water and waste disposal in squatter settlements in cities in LICs and NEEs. Use Figure 14 and your own understanding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of challenges with use of figure and developed consequences.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple description of the problems.\nIndicative content:\n• Settlements were built illegally without planning, so there are no water mains or sewers; Figure 14 shows a single shared tap and an open drain.\n• Narrow, steep alleys make it hard for collection lorries or engineers to reach homes, so rubbish piles up.\n• Rapid growth means the city cannot keep up with demand; authorities may not want to invest in illegal areas.\n• Consequences: water-borne diseases (cholera, diarrhoea), blocked drains causing flooding and landslides on steep slopes, rubbish attracting rats.\n• Residents may tap illegally into supplies, which can contaminate water.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nMigrants have arrived in Rio de Janeiro from rural areas of Brazil, especially the poorer north-east, and from other countries.\n\nUsing a case study of a major city in an LIC or NEE, explain how migration has affected the growth and character of the city.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of effects on growth and on character, with case-study detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple statements.\nIndicative content (Rio de Janeiro):\n• Rural–urban migration from the north-east (drought, poverty) has caused rapid population growth, with the metropolitan area now over 12 million people.\n• Many migrants could not afford formal housing, so favelas grew on hillsides and the edges of the city; about one in five residents lives in a favela.\n• The city has spread outwards — e.g. growth towards the west zone and Barra da Tijuca.\n• International migration (historic Portuguese, enslaved Africans, more recent migrants from South America and Africa) created a multicultural city reflected in food, music (samba) and festivals such as Carnival.\nCredit other valid case studies.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nIn many favelas, residents have been given cement, bricks and training so that they can improve their own homes. These are known as self-help schemes.\n\nExplain how self-help schemes can improve the quality of life of the urban poor in LICs and NEEs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of several benefits with developed links to quality of life.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple statements.\nIndicative content:\n• Residents replace wood and scrap materials with brick/concrete, making homes safer and more durable.\n• Low cost for the authorities, so more homes can be improved with limited money.\n• Residents learn building skills which can lead to paid work.\n• Communities stay together rather than being moved to distant estates, so people keep jobs and social networks.\n• A sense of ownership and pride; adding water, electricity and sanitation improves health.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 15: a photograph of the Complexo do Alemão cable car in Rio de Janeiro, which opened in 2011. Gondolas pass over a dense hillside favela between six stations along a 3.5 km route.\n\nExplain how improving transport can help to reduce the challenges faced by people living in squatter settlements. Use Figure 15 and your own understanding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation with use of figure and developed benefits.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple benefits.\nIndicative content:\n• The cable car links hilltop communities to the railway/city centre, cutting a long, steep walk to minutes.\n• Easier access to jobs elsewhere in the city reduces unemployment and raises incomes.\n• Better access to schools, hospitals and shops.\n• Less isolation of the community; can attract tourists and investment.\n• Evaluation: the cable car closed in 2016 because of funding problems, showing schemes must be maintained to have lasting benefits.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 12 (data written for this practice question): sources of population growth in a city in an LIC.\n1980–2000: migration 60%, natural increase 40%.\n2000–2020: migration 35%, natural increase 65%.\n\n'Rural–urban migration is the main cause of urban growth in LICs and NEEs.'\nDo you agree? Use Figure 12 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed understanding of both causes; thorough use of Figure 12; a clear, supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear understanding of causes; some use of the figure; judgement may be partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points; limited use of the figure; little or no judgement.\nIndicative content:\n• Agree: in 1980–2000 migration caused 60% of growth; push factors (rural poverty, mechanisation, drought, conflict) and pull factors (jobs, services, perceived better life) explain mass movement.\n• Disagree: by 2000–2020 natural increase caused 65% of growth; young migrants have children; improved health care lowers death rates.\n• Link: migration causes later natural increase, so the two are connected.\n• Varies by city/stage: e.g. in Lagos natural increase is now very important; recently industrialising cities may still grow mostly by migration.\n• Credit a judgement that it depends on the time period.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 16 (data written for this practice question): access to services in two areas of Rio de Janeiro.\nRocinha (favela): homes connected to sewerage 45%; homes with legal electricity 60%; adults who completed secondary school 25%.\nBarra da Tijuca (wealthy suburb): homes connected to sewerage 98%; homes with legal electricity 100%; adults who completed secondary school 80%.\n\nUsing Figure 16 and a case study of a major city in an LIC or NEE, explain the social challenges caused by rapid urban growth.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of several social challenges, with thorough use of figure and case-study detail.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation, some use of figure or case study.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of challenges.\nIndicative content:\n• Sanitation: only 45% of Rocinha homes have sewerage compared with 98% in Barra, leading to disease.\n• Energy: illegal connections (40% of homes) cause fires and power cuts.\n• Education: only 25% completed secondary school — children leave to work; shortage of teachers and school places.\n• Health: shortage of clinics; high infant mortality; water-borne disease.\n• Crime and drug gangs in favelas; violence affects daily life.\n• Inequality and social segregation between rich and poor areas.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nThe Favela Bairro project in Rio de Janeiro began in the mid-1990s. It aimed to upgrade existing favelas rather than demolish them, and reached about 250 000 people in over 70 favelas.\n\nEvaluate how successful an urban planning scheme in an LIC or NEE has been in improving the lives of the urban poor.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed knowledge of a named scheme; a balanced evaluation with a supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear knowledge; some evaluation.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description; little or no evaluation.\nIndicative content (Favela Bairro):\n• Successes: paved roads and walkways; mains water and sewerage; street lighting; day-care centres, health centres and sports facilities; families moved from areas at risk of landslides; residents could stay in their community; land rights offered to some residents; property values rose.\n• Limitations: high cost and limited coverage — many favelas were not included; maintenance has been poor in some areas; rising rents can push out the poorest; drug gangs remain in some communities; follow-up scheme (Morar Carioca) was not completed.\n• Conclusion: largely successful at improving infrastructure, less successful at reducing poverty and crime.\nCredit other valid schemes, e.g. in Lagos or Mumbai.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 17: a fact file about Rio de Janeiro. Population about 6.2 million (2022 census), over 12 million in the metropolitan area. Brazil's capital until 1960. Major port on Guanabara Bay. Headquarters of Petrobras. Christ the Redeemer statue was named one of the New Seven Wonders of the World in 2007. Hosted the 2016 Olympic Games.\n\nAssess the importance of a major city in an LIC or NEE at regional, national and international scales. Use Figure 17 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment across all three scales, thorough use of figure, supported judgement about the significance.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear points at two or more scales, some use of figure.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points, often listed from the figure.\nIndicative content:\n• Regional: main centre for services (hospitals, universities, shopping) for south-east Brazil; employs people from surrounding towns.\n• National: second largest city; former capital; major contributor to Brazil's economy through oil, the port, finance and media; Petrobras headquarters.\n• International: tourism (Christ the Redeemer, Sugarloaf Mountain, Carnival); hosted the 2014 World Cup final and 2016 Olympics; port exports oil and iron ore; attracts foreign investment.\n• Judgement, e.g. its international role is growing but its national economic role is most important.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 10: a photograph of the shoreline of Guanabara Bay, Rio de Janeiro. Grey, foamy water surrounds plastic bottles and rubbish caught in mangroves; a pipe discharges dark water into the bay. A caption states that the city promised before the 2016 Olympics to treat most of the sewage entering the bay, but this target was not met.\n\nUsing Figure 10 and a case study of a major city in an LIC or NEE, discuss the environmental challenges of rapid urban growth.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed discussion of several environmental challenges, with causes, effects and management; thorough use of figure and case study.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear discussion, some use of figure or case study.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic list of problems.\nIndicative content (Rio de Janeiro):\n• Water pollution: untreated sewage from favelas and industry enters Guanabara Bay; damages fishing and mangroves; health risks; clean-up targets missed.\n• Waste disposal: rubbish not collected from steep, narrow favela streets is dumped or burned; Jardim Gramacho landfill closed in 2012.\n• Air pollution: traffic congestion and industry; smog in valleys.\n• Landslide risk increased by building on steep slopes and deforestation.\n• Management: new sewage treatment works, eco-barriers in rivers, recycling schemes, metro/BRT to cut car use.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 18: two opinions about squatter settlements.\nCity official: "Squatter settlements are illegal, unsafe and a source of crime. They should be cleared and the residents rehoused."\nCommunity leader: "Our favela is where people first find a foothold in the city. We built our homes ourselves and we have shops, churches and a strong community."\n\n'Squatter settlements are a solution, not a problem.' Do you agree? Use Figure 18 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed, balanced discussion using both opinions and named examples; clear, supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear points for one or both sides; some use of figure.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points; little judgement.\nIndicative content:\n• Solution: cheap housing near jobs for migrants who could not otherwise afford to live in the city; self-built homes improve over time; strong community networks; informal businesses (e.g. Dharavi's recycling and leather industries).\n• Problem: built illegally on hazardous land (landslides, flooding); poor sanitation and disease; fires; crime and gangs; residents have no legal land rights.\n• Clearance can destroy livelihoods and communities (e.g. demolitions in Makoko, Lagos, 2012).\n• Judgement: e.g. upgrading schemes such as Favela Bairro show settlements can become a solution if supported.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 11: a photograph of Makoko, an informal settlement on Lagos Lagoon, Nigeria. Wooden houses with corrugated metal roofs stand on stilts above dark water; people move between houses in narrow canoes and there is no road access. Rubbish floats beneath the houses.\n\nSuggest how the quality of life of people living in the area shown in Figure 11 could be improved. Use Figure 11 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed suggestions, closely linked to problems visible in the figure, with developed explanation of how they help.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear suggestions with some links to the figure.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic suggestions, little explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• Waste: rubbish collection by boat/community clean-ups to reduce disease.\n• Water and sanitation: piped clean water, floating/composting toilets, so water-borne disease falls.\n• Housing: stronger stilt houses or floating buildings (e.g. the Makoko Floating School design) suited to the lagoon.\n• Energy: solar panels to provide safe electricity.\n• Services: schools and clinics accessible by canoe; skills training.\n• Legal land rights so residents are not evicted and are willing to invest in their homes.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 15: a photograph of the Complexo do Alemão cable car in Rio de Janeiro, which opened in 2011. Gondolas pass over a dense hillside favela between six stations. A caption explains that the cable car stopped running in 2016 after funding problems.\n\nAssess the success of attempts to improve transport in a major city in an LIC or NEE. Use Figure 15 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of named transport schemes, balanced, with a supported judgement; good use of figure.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear points about success or failure; some use of figure.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of schemes.\nIndicative content (Rio de Janeiro):\n• Cable car cut journeys from hilltop communities to the railway to a few minutes; free rides for residents; but closed in 2016, leaving residents without the service.\n• Metro Line 4 to Barra da Tijuca and BRT lanes reduced journey times for many commuters; but routes mainly serve wealthier areas and some BRT stations have since closed or been damaged.\n• Traffic congestion and air pollution remain serious.\n• Judgement: partly successful; benefits depend on long-term funding and maintenance.\nCredit other cities, e.g. Lagos BRT and Blue Line rail.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 19 (data written for this practice question): employment in a city in an NEE.\nFormal economy: 55% of workers; average monthly income US$420.\nInformal economy: 45% of workers; average monthly income US$150.\n\n'The informal economy brings more benefits than problems to cities in LICs and NEEs.' Do you agree? Use Figure 19 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed, balanced discussion with thorough use of figure data and a clear, supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear points, some use of data.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points; little judgement.\nIndicative content:\n• Benefits: employs 45% of workers who might otherwise have no income; easy to enter without qualifications; provides cheap goods and services (street food, transport, recycling); develops entrepreneurial skills; can lead to formal businesses.\n• Problems: average income only US$150 a month — less than half (about 36%) of formal workers' income; no job security, sick pay or pensions; often unsafe work; no taxes paid so the city has less to spend on services; can involve child labour.\n• Judgement: e.g. essential in the short term, but cities need more formal jobs.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 20: a photograph of a hillside favela in Rio de Janeiro after heavy rain in April 2010. Several houses have collapsed and a scar of bare mud runs down the slope; rescue workers dig through rubble.\n\nUsing Figure 20 and your own understanding, explain why people living in squatter settlements are especially vulnerable to natural hazards, and how this risk can be reduced.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of causes of vulnerability and of strategies, with good use of figure.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of vulnerability or strategies, some use of figure.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points.\nIndicative content:\n• Homes built on steep, unstable slopes or floodplains, because this was the only free land.\n• Vegetation removed, and no drainage, so heavy rain saturates soil and causes landslides (Figure 20).\n• Poor-quality, self-built houses collapse easily; high density means many people are affected.\n• Narrow streets make rescue difficult; residents have little money or insurance to recover.\n• Reducing risk: moving families from the most dangerous areas (as in Favela Bairro), drainage channels and retaining walls, replanting slopes, rain-gauge warning sirens, building regulations and stronger materials.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\n\nTo what extent has urban growth created opportunities for people living in a major city in an LIC or NEE? Use a case study in your answer.\n[9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Levels of response (AO2 understanding, AO3 evaluation).\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed, well-supported case-study knowledge of social and economic opportunities; thorough evaluation weighing opportunities against limits; clear, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear knowledge with some case-study detail; some evaluation; partial conclusion.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic, generic points; little or no evaluation.\n0: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content (Rio de Janeiro):\n• Social: better access to schools and health care than in rural areas; family health teams in favelas; access to clean water and electricity has expanded; Favela Bairro improved services.\n• Economic: jobs in the oil industry (Petrobras), port, manufacturing, finance and tourism; events such as the 2016 Olympics created construction jobs; informal economy provides income.\n• Limits: opportunities are unequal — many favela residents still lack sewerage and legal electricity; informal work is low paid; high unemployment among young people; crime restricts opportunities.\n• Conclusion: e.g. growth has created many opportunities, but the urban poor benefit less than wealthier residents.\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance 3: spelling and punctuation consistently accurate; grammar controls meaning; wide range of specialist terms used precisely.\nIntermediate performance 2: considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance 1: reasonable accuracy; no serious obstruction of meaning; limited range of specialist terms.\n0: no relevant answer or errors severely obstruct meaning.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 5: a photograph of part of a favela in Rio de Janeiro after the Favela Bairro project. A newly paved and widened street has street lights and a drainage channel; a small health centre and a children's day-care centre stand beside it.\n\nUsing Figure 5 and an example you have studied, evaluate the effectiveness of an urban planning scheme in improving the quality of life of the urban poor in an LIC or NEE.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed knowledge of a named scheme and good use of Figure 5; balanced evaluation of successes and limitations; substantiated judgement about effectiveness.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear knowledge; some evaluation; some use of figure.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic description; little or no evaluation.\n0: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content (Favela Bairro, Rio de Janeiro):\n• Aims: upgrade rather than demolish favelas; integrate them into the city.\n• Successes: paved roads, lighting and drainage (Figure 5) improve access and safety and reduce landslide/flood risk; water and sewerage connections improve health; health and day-care centres let parents work; families moved from hazardous areas; adult education and training; property values rose; residents could stay in their community.\n• Limitations: reached only part of the favela population; some services poorly maintained; higher rents and bills can force out the poorest; crime and gangs remain; follow-up scheme Morar Carioca was not completed.\n• Judgement: effective at improving physical infrastructure; less effective in tackling poverty, unemployment and crime.\nCredit other schemes, e.g. Lagos or Dharavi redevelopment.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\n\n'The challenges caused by rapid urban growth in a major city in an LIC or NEE are greater than the opportunities.'\nDo you agree? Use a case study in your answer.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed case-study knowledge of both challenges and opportunities; thorough evaluation; clear, well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear knowledge of challenges and/or opportunities; some evaluation and a judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic points; generic; little or no judgement.\n0: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content (Rio de Janeiro):\n• Challenges: favelas — about one in five residents; lack of sewerage and clean water; shortage of school places and clinics; unemployment and low-paid informal work; crime and drug gangs; traffic congestion; water pollution in Guanabara Bay; waste disposal; landslide risk on steep slopes.\n• Opportunities: jobs in oil, port, services and tourism; better access to education and health care than in rural areas; investment from global events; schemes such as Favela Bairro, UPPs, Metro Line 4 and BRT.\n• Judgement: e.g. challenges are serious and affect the poorest most, but opportunities are why migrants keep arriving; balance depends on who you are and where you live in the city.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 1 (data written for this practice question): percentage of the population living in urban areas.\nAfrica: 1950 14%, 2020 43%\nAsia: 1950 18%, 2020 51%\nEurope: 1950 52%, 2020 75%\nLatin America and the Caribbean: 1950 41%, 2020 81%\nNorth America: 1950 64%, 2020 83%\n\nUsing Figure 1 and a case study of a major city in an LIC or NEE, assess the extent to which rapid urbanisation can be managed successfully.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Levels of response.\nLevel 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: detailed understanding of the scale of urbanisation using Figure 1; detailed case-study knowledge of management strategies; thorough assessment with a substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear understanding; some use of data and case study; some assessment.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: basic points; little use of figure; little or no assessment.\n0: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Scale: Africa and Asia are urbanising fastest (Africa 14% to 43%, Asia 18% to 51%), so cities must provide services for huge numbers each year; Latin America already 81% urban.\n• Causes: rural–urban migration and natural increase make growth hard to plan for.\n• Management in Rio de Janeiro: Favela Bairro upgrades; UPP policing; health care via family health teams; Metro Line 4 and BRT; sewage treatment and eco-barriers for Guanabara Bay; schools and adult education.\n• Limits: cost and funding problems (cable car closure in 2016); targets missed (bay clean-up); many favelas not upgraded; crime returned to some pacified areas.\n• Conclusion: management can improve conditions, but the pace of growth and limited funding mean success is only partial.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.2': {
    name: 'Urban Change in the UK',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 1 (data written for this practice question): estimated populations of five UK cities — Birmingham 1,140,000; Leeds 810,000; Bristol 470,000; Cardiff 360,000; Aberdeen 230,000.\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the range in population between the five cities.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark: 910,000 (1,140,000 − 230,000).\nNo mark for the working alone without the correct answer.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 2 (data written for this practice question): a UK city has a population of 472,000 and covers an area of 110 km².\n\nCalculate the population density of the city. Give your answer to the nearest whole number of people per km².`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark: 4291 people per km² (472,000 ÷ 110 = 4290.9).\nAccept 4290 or 4291 people per km².` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 3: a choropleth map of UK population density. The darkest shading (over 1,000 people per km²) covers Greater London, the West Midlands, Greater Manchester, Merseyside, West Yorkshire, Tyneside and the Central Belt of Scotland around Glasgow and Edinburgh. The lightest shading (under 50 people per km²) covers the Scottish Highlands and Islands, central Wales and the northern Pennines.\n\nUsing Figure 3, name one area of the UK with a population density of under 50 people per km².`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `1 mark for any one of: the Scottish Highlands (and Islands); central Wales / mid-Wales; the northern Pennines.\nDo not accept a whole country, e.g. "Scotland".` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nBradley Stoke is a large housing development built from the late 1980s on the northern edge of Bristol, close to junctions on the M4 and M5 motorways.\n\nSuggest one reason why many people who live in Bradley Stoke work in Bristol.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for one valid reason, e.g.\n• good motorway/road links make commuting into the city quick\n• there are more (and better-paid) jobs in the city than in the settlement\n• Bradley Stoke is mainly housing, with few large employers\n• people want a quieter/cheaper home but keep a city job.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 4 (data written for this practice question): household waste recycled in a UK city — 62,000 tonnes in 2010 and 90,000 tonnes in 2020.\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in waste recycled between 2010 and 2020. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark: 45.2% ((90,000 − 62,000) ÷ 62,000 × 100 = 45.16).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 5: a photograph of an inner-city site in a UK city. Behind a metal fence are the remains of a red-brick factory with broken windows and a collapsed roof. Weeds grow through cracked concrete, and an old railway siding runs along one edge of the site.\n\nUsing Figure 5, give one piece of evidence that this is a brownfield site.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one piece of evidence from the figure, e.g.\n• the remains of an old factory / previous buildings on the site\n• cracked concrete / hard surfacing from earlier use\n• the old railway siding (former industrial use).\nAnswers must refer to evidence of previous development.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 6: a newspaper headline — "City centre streets to be lined with 30,000 new trees, green roofs on bus shelters".\n\nGive one environmental benefit of urban greening.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one valid benefit, e.g.\n• trees absorb carbon dioxide / air pollutants, improving air quality\n• shade reduces temperatures / the urban heat island effect\n• vegetation intercepts rainfall, reducing surface run-off and flood risk\n• creates habitats / increases biodiversity.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 3: a choropleth map of UK population density. The darkest shading (over 1,000 people per km²) covers Greater London, the West Midlands, Greater Manchester, Merseyside, West Yorkshire, Tyneside and the Central Belt of Scotland around Glasgow and Edinburgh. The lightest shading (under 50 people per km²) covers the Scottish Highlands and Islands, central Wales and the northern Pennines.\n\nUsing Figure 3, describe the distribution of population in the UK.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid descriptive point, up to 2 marks, e.g.\n• uneven distribution\n• highest densities in major conurbations, e.g. London, West Midlands, Greater Manchester\n• the south-east of England is densely populated\n• the Central Belt is the most densely populated part of Scotland\n• lowest densities in upland/remote areas, e.g. the Scottish Highlands, central Wales\n• 2nd mark can be for use of data from the key.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nAbout one in eight people in the UK live in London, and the south-east of England is the most densely populated region of the country.\n\nOutline one reason why the south-east of England is densely populated.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, +1 for development, e.g.\n• London is the capital (1) so it has many jobs in finance, government and services, attracting migrants (1)\n• lowland, flat land with a mild climate (1) which is easy to build on and farm (1)\n• close to mainland Europe (1) so good for trade/ports/Channel Tunnel and businesses locate there (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nThe population of London was 8.17 million in the 2011 census and 8.80 million in the 2021 census.\n\nCalculate the percentage change in London's population between 2011 and 2021. Show your working and give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: (8.80 − 8.17) ÷ 8.17 × 100\n1 mark for the answer: 7.7% (increase).\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 7 (data written for this practice question): a line graph of the number of people moving into a UK city from overseas each year. 2004: 2,100; 2006: 4,800; 2008: 5,600; 2010: 4,900; 2012: 5,200; 2014: 6,400; 2016: 7,100.\n\nUsing Figure 7, describe the changes in international migration into the city between 2004 and 2016.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point, up to 2, e.g.\n• overall increase, from 2,100 to 7,100 (more than tripled)\n• rapid rise 2004–2008 (to 5,600)\n• fall/dip in 2010 (to 4,900)\n• steady rise again from 2010 to 2016.\nData must be used to support at least one point for full marks.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nIn many UK cities, more than a quarter of residents were born outside the UK, and streets such as Stapleton Road in Bristol have shops and restaurants run by people from many different countries.\n\nOutline one way that international migration has changed the cultural mix of a UK city.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a change, +1 for development, e.g.\n• new places of worship (1) such as mosques, gurdwaras and temples, reflecting a range of religions (1)\n• many languages are spoken (1) so schools and services provide translation/support (1)\n• cultural events/festivals (1) e.g. St Pauls Carnival in Bristol celebrates African-Caribbean culture (1)\n• a variety of food/shops (1) adding to the vibrancy of local high streets (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nBristol's Harbourside is a former dockland area that now contains the M Shed museum, the Arnolfini arts centre, the science centre We The Curious, bars, restaurants and boat trips around the Floating Harbour.\n\nOutline one recreation or entertainment opportunity created by urban change in a UK city.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for an opportunity, +1 for development (named city detail credited), e.g.\n• old docks redeveloped into a leisure area (1) — Bristol Harbourside has museums, restaurants and festivals such as the Harbour Festival, attracting visitors and residents (1)\n• new sports/entertainment venues (1) e.g. Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park, London, has stadiums, swimming and parkland open to the public (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 8: a photograph of a MetroBus stop in Bristol — a bus lane separated from other traffic, a ticket machine on the platform, and a bicycle rack beside the shelter.\n\nUsing Figure 8, outline one way that an integrated transport system can reduce traffic congestion.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, +1 for development, e.g.\n• separate bus lanes (1) mean buses are faster than cars, encouraging people to leave their cars at home (1)\n• cycle racks at stops (1) allow people to combine cycling and public transport, reducing short car journeys (1)\n• buying tickets before boarding (1) speeds up journeys, making public transport more attractive (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFrom the 1970s, many factories and warehouses in inner-city areas of UK cities such as Manchester, Bristol and Glasgow closed.\n\nOutline one reason for the dereliction of inner-city areas in UK cities.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, +1 for development, e.g.\n• deindustrialisation (1) — factories closed due to cheaper competition from overseas, leaving buildings empty (1)\n• docks closed (1) because larger container ships could not reach them, so trade moved to deep-water ports such as Avonmouth (1)\n• businesses moved to out-of-town sites (1) where land is cheaper and access by road is easier (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 9 (data written for this practice question): indicators for two wards of a UK city.\nWard A (inner city): life expectancy 74 years; unemployment 9%; adults with no qualifications 31%.\nWard B (suburb): life expectancy 83 years; unemployment 2%; adults with no qualifications 8%.\n\nUsing Figure 9, compare the levels of deprivation in Ward A and Ward B.`, marks: 2, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `1 mark per valid comparative point, up to 2, e.g.\n• Ward A is more deprived than Ward B\n• life expectancy is 9 years lower in Ward A\n• unemployment is 4.5 times higher / 7 percentage points higher in Ward A\n• Ward A has almost four times the share of adults with no qualifications (31% compared with 8%).\nAnswers must compare; two separate descriptions without comparison = max 1.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nMany new housing estates on the edge of UK cities are built on greenfield sites (land that has not been built on before).\n\nGive two reasons why developers often prefer to build on greenfield sites.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid reason, up to 2, e.g.\n• cheaper — no need to clear old buildings or decontaminate land\n• land is often cheaper on the edge of the city\n• more space for larger houses and gardens, which sell well\n• easier access from main roads/motorways\n• more attractive, pleasant surroundings for buyers.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 10: a photograph of the rural–urban fringe near Bristol — a large out-of-town shopping centre and car parks on one side of a dual carriageway, with new houses being built on farmland on the other side.\n\nUsing Figure 10, outline one problem caused by urban sprawl.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a problem, +1 for development, e.g.\n• loss of farmland/greenfield land (1) reducing habitats and countryside for recreation (1)\n• more traffic (1) as people drive to the shopping centre and new houses, causing congestion and air pollution (1)\n• out-of-town shops (1) draw shoppers away from the city centre, causing shops to close there (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 11: a leaflet from a water company serving a UK city — "Fit a water butt, take shorter showers, and ask for a free water meter."\n\nOutline one way that water can be conserved in a sustainable city.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, +1 for development, e.g.\n• water butts collect rainwater (1) which can be used to water gardens instead of treated mains water (1)\n• water meters (1) make people pay for what they use, so they use less (1)\n• dual-flush toilets/low-flow showers (1) reduce the water used per household (1)\n• grey water recycling (1) reuses water from baths and sinks for flushing toilets (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nThe Beddington Zero Energy Development (BedZED) in Sutton, south London, was completed in 2002. Its homes face south, have thick insulation and solar panels, and have tall, colourful wind cowls on the roofs for ventilation.\n\nOutline one way that energy can be conserved in urban homes.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, +1 for development, e.g.\n• thick insulation/triple glazing (1) reduces heat loss, so less energy is needed for heating (1)\n• south-facing windows (1) make use of solar gain, warming homes naturally (1)\n• solar panels (1) generate renewable electricity on site, reducing use of fossil fuels (1)\n• natural ventilation through wind cowls (1) avoids the need for electric fans/air conditioning (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nYou have carried out fieldwork to investigate traffic in a UK city centre. Figure 12 (data written for this practice question): the number of vehicles counted in 10 minutes at five sites — 1,240; 980; 1,515; 860; 1,105.\n\nUsing Figure 12, calculate the mean number of vehicles counted per site. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: (1,240 + 980 + 1,515 + 860 + 1,105) ÷ 5 = 5,700 ÷ 5\n1 mark for the answer: 1,140 vehicles.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nBristol is in south-west England, on the River Avon close to the Severn Estuary. It has a port at Avonmouth, an international airport, and is close to where the M4 and M5 motorways meet.\n\nOutline one reason why Bristol is important nationally or internationally.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, +1 for development, e.g.\n• port at Avonmouth/Royal Portbury (1) handles imports such as cars and goods traded with the rest of the world (1)\n• aerospace industry (1) — firms such as Airbus and Rolls-Royce at Filton export around the world (1)\n• universities (1) attract students and researchers from across the UK and abroad (1)\n• transport hub (1) — motorways and rail link it to London, Wales and the rest of England (1).\nCredit equivalent points for another named UK city.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 26: a photograph of a commuter village near a UK city on a weekday afternoon — a closed village shop with a "To let" sign, a bus stop with a timetable showing two buses a day, and rows of new detached houses with empty driveways.\n\nUsing Figure 26, outline one challenge faced by commuter settlements.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a challenge, +1 for development, e.g.\n• local services close (1) — the shop shut because commuters shop in the city, so older/non-driving residents lose access (1)\n• poor public transport (1) — only two buses a day, so people depend on cars, causing congestion at rush hour (1)\n• the village is empty in the day (1), so it can lose its sense of community (a "dormitory" settlement) (1)\n• new houses raise house prices (1), so young local people cannot afford to stay (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 13: a map of the UK's ten largest cities. Most are in lowland areas, many lie on major rivers or estuaries (London on the Thames, Glasgow on the Clyde, Newcastle on the Tyne, Liverpool on the Mersey), and several lie on or near former coalfields in northern England and the Midlands.\n\nUsing Figure 13 and your own understanding, explain why the population of the UK is unevenly distributed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point, with extra marks for development, up to 4.\n• Cities grew on rivers/estuaries (1) for trade and ports, e.g. Liverpool, so jobs attracted people (1).\n• Coalfields led to industrial cities (1) in the North and Midlands during the Industrial Revolution, drawing in workers (1).\n• Lowland areas are easier to build on and farm (1) whereas uplands such as the Highlands are steep, cold and wet with few jobs, so are sparsely populated (1).\n• London as the capital attracts businesses and migrants (1), concentrating population in the south-east (1).\nMax 3 if no use of Figure 13.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nThe population of a major UK city has grown over the last 30 years. Many of the new residents moved from other parts of the UK, and others arrived from countries such as Poland, Somalia, India and Jamaica.\n\nUsing a case study of a major city in the UK, explain how national and international migration have affected the growth and character of the city.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Point-marked: 1 mark for a valid point, extra marks for development and case-study detail, up to 4.\n• Young adults move in from other parts of the UK for university or jobs (1) increasing the population and creating demand for housing (1).\n• International migrants (1) — e.g. Bristol has large Somali and Polish communities — adding to the cultural mix through shops, food and places of worship (1).\n• Migrants are often young (1), so higher birth rates add to natural increase and the growth of the city (1).\nMax 2 for generic answers with no named city.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 14 (data written for this practice question): employment in a major UK city — 1981: manufacturing 32%, services 64%, other 4%; 2021: manufacturing 9%, services 88%, other 3%.\n\nUsing Figure 14 and a case study of a major UK city, explain how urban change has created employment opportunities.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point with development, up to 4.\n• Shift from manufacturing (32% to 9%) to services (64% to 88%) (1).\n• New jobs in finance, retail, tourism and the creative/digital industries (1), e.g. TV and media firms in Bristol or MediaCity in Salford (1).\n• Regeneration projects such as Bristol Temple Quarter or Cabot Circus create construction and retail jobs (1).\n• Universities and high-tech/aerospace firms (Airbus, Rolls-Royce at Filton) provide skilled, well-paid jobs (1).\nMax 3 if no use of Figure 14.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 15: two photographs of Bristol's Broadmead area. Photograph A (2003) shows a run-down 1960s shopping precinct with empty units and a large surface car park. Photograph B (2010) shows the Cabot Circus shopping centre, opened in 2008, with a glass roof, new shops and restaurants, and a multi-storey car park.\n\nUsing Figure 15, explain how urban regeneration has improved the area.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point with development, up to 4.\n• Empty units replaced by new shops (1) attracting shoppers and creating jobs (1).\n• The glass roof/modern buildings improve the appearance of the area (1), making the city centre more attractive to visitors and investors (1).\n• Restaurants add evening use (1) so the area is busier and feels safer (1).\n• Multi-storey car park frees land previously used for surface parking (1).\nMax 2 if no reference to the photographs.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 16: a map of transport schemes in London — the Congestion Charge zone in central London (introduced 2003), the Ultra Low Emission Zone, Santander Cycles docking stations, and the Elizabeth line crossing the city from east to west.\n\nUsing Figure 16, explain how UK cities are trying to reduce traffic congestion.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point with development, up to 4.\n• Congestion Charge makes drivers pay to enter central London (1), discouraging car journeys so traffic falls (1).\n• ULEZ charges older, more polluting vehicles (1), encouraging people to switch to cleaner vehicles or public transport (1).\n• Bike-hire schemes (1) provide an alternative to the car for short trips (1).\n• New rail lines such as the Elizabeth line (1) increase capacity and speed of public transport, reducing road use (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 9 (data written for this practice question): indicators for two wards of a UK city.\nWard A (inner city): life expectancy 74 years; unemployment 9%; adults with no qualifications 31%.\nWard B (suburb): life expectancy 83 years; unemployment 2%; adults with no qualifications 8%.\n\nUsing Figure 9 and your own understanding, explain why levels of deprivation vary within a UK city.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point with development, up to 4.\n• Inner-city areas lost jobs when factories closed (1), so unemployment is higher (9% v 2%) (1).\n• Poor-quality, cheaper housing concentrates low-income households (1), while wealthier people moved to the suburbs (1).\n• Lower qualifications (31% with none) limit access to well-paid jobs (1), creating a cycle of deprivation (1).\n• Poorer diets, overcrowding and pollution reduce health (1), explaining lower life expectancy (1).\nMax 3 if no use of the data.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nUrban regeneration is the investment in the revival of old, urban areas by improving housing, services and the environment.\n\nUsing an example of an urban regeneration project in the UK, explain why the area needed regeneration.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point with development, up to 4, credit specific detail.\n• The Lower Lea Valley in east London (site of the 2012 Olympic Park) had derelict industrial land and contaminated soil (1), so few businesses invested (1).\n• High unemployment and deprivation in boroughs such as Newham (1) meant residents had poor job prospects (1).\n• Poor transport/housing and polluted waterways (1) made the area unattractive (1).\nOr equivalent for Bristol Temple Quarter (derelict railway land near Temple Meads), Salford Quays/MediaCity (closed docks), London Docklands etc.\nMax 2 with no named project.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 4 (data written for this practice question): household waste recycled in a UK city — 62,000 tonnes in 2010 and 90,000 tonnes in 2020. The council now collects food waste weekly, provides separate boxes for glass, paper and cans, and generates electricity from waste that cannot be recycled.\n\nExplain how waste recycling helps to make urban living more sustainable.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point with development, up to 4.\n• Less waste goes to landfill (1), so less land is used and less methane is released (1).\n• Recycling materials such as glass and metals saves raw resources (1) and uses less energy than making new products (1).\n• Food waste can be composted or used to make biogas (1), providing renewable energy (1).\n• Energy-from-waste plants generate electricity (1) reducing use of fossil fuels (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nVillages such as Keynsham, between Bristol and Bath, have grown rapidly since the 1960s, and many residents travel into a nearby city to work each day.\n\nExplain why commuter settlements have grown around UK cities.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point with development, up to 4.\n• Improved transport — car ownership, motorways and rail links (1) — make it possible to live further from work (1).\n• Houses are often cheaper/larger with gardens (1) than in the city (1).\n• People want a quieter, safer, less polluted environment (1), especially families with children (1).\n• Planners have allowed new estates to be built (1) to meet the demand for housing that the city cannot supply (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nUsing a case study of a major city in the UK, explain why the city is important regionally, nationally and internationally.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO1 shows detailed knowledge of the city. AO2 shows thorough understanding of its importance at more than one scale, with explanation linked to specific place detail.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO1 shows clear knowledge. AO2 shows some understanding, with some explanation at more than one scale.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO1 shows limited knowledge. AO2 basic understanding; points are mostly generic.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content (Bristol):\n• Regional: largest city in the south-west; centre for shopping (Cabot Circus, Broadmead), hospitals, universities and jobs.\n• National: on the M4/M5 and main rail lines to London; aerospace (Airbus, Rolls-Royce, GKN at Filton); media and creative industries; financial services.\n• International: ports at Avonmouth and Royal Portbury; Bristol Airport; universities attract international students; European Green Capital 2015; exports of aerospace products.\nAccept London, Manchester or another major UK city with equivalent detail. Max Level 2 if only one scale is addressed.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 17: a photograph of St Pauls Carnival in Bristol, with crowds watching a procession of dancers in bright costumes, food stalls and sound systems along the streets.\n\nUsing Figure 17 and a case study of a major city in the UK, explain how urban change has created social opportunities.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough explanation of a range of social opportunities with specific case-study detail; good use of Figure 17.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some opportunities with some case-study detail and some use of the figure.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic, mainly descriptive points; little case-study detail.\nIndicative content:\n• Cultural mix — migration has brought festivals (St Pauls Carnival), food, music and places of worship; many languages spoken.\n• Recreation and entertainment — Harbourside museums (M Shed), theatres (Bristol Old Vic), music venues, sports grounds, Harbour Festival.\n• Urban greening — parks such as Castle Park and the Ashton Court Estate, community gardens and cycle paths.\n• Integrated transport — MetroBus, cycle routes, rail; better access to services.\n• Regeneration of old dockland gives new public spaces.\nMax Level 2 without use of the figure. Max Level 1 if not linked to a named city.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 18 (data written for this practice question): changes at the rural–urban fringe of a UK city, 1990–2020 — farmland lost to housing: 1,200 hectares; new out-of-town retail parks: 3; car journeys per day on the main ring road: 28,000 rising to 46,000; new homes built: 14,000.\n\nAssess the challenges created by urban sprawl on the rural–urban fringe. Use Figure 18 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO3 detailed assessment of challenges, weighing their significance; AO2 thorough understanding; effective use of Figure 18.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of challenges with some assessment and some use of data.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic, list-like identification of challenges; little or no data.\nIndicative content:\n• Loss of 1,200 ha of farmland — habitats lost, less land for food, countryside further from residents.\n• Traffic increased by 18,000 journeys/day (about 64%) — congestion, air pollution, need for new roads.\n• Out-of-town retail parks draw trade from the city centre — empty shops, decline of the CBD.\n• 14,000 homes increase demand for services (schools, GPs) that may not be built in time.\n• Commuter settlements can become "dormitory" places; locals priced out by rising house prices.\n• Assessment: e.g. environmental loss may be most serious as it is permanent; counter-point — new homes are needed to meet demand.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nUsing an example of an urban regeneration project in the UK, evaluate the effectiveness of the project.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO3 well-balanced evaluation reaching a supported judgement; AO1 detailed knowledge of a named project.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear evaluation of some successes and/or limitations with some detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of the project; little evaluation.\nIndicative content (e.g. Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park, Stratford, London):\n• Successes: derelict, contaminated land cleaned up; new homes in East Village; Westfield Stratford City (opened 2011) created thousands of jobs; improved transport (Stratford station, DLR, Elizabeth line); new parkland and sports venues open to the public.\n• Limitations: rising house prices and rents may price out existing low-income residents; many jobs went to people from outside the area; costs were very high.\n• Judgement, e.g. effective for the environment and transport, less so for reducing local deprivation.\nAccept Bristol Temple Quarter, Bristol Harbourside, Salford Quays/MediaCity, London Docklands, etc. Max Level 1 if no named example.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 19: two opinions about urban greening in a UK city.\nCouncil officer: "New parks, street trees and green roofs make the city cooler, reduce flooding and improve people's health."\nLocal business owner: "Green spaces are nice, but the money would be better spent on housing and jobs in the poorest areas."\n\n'Urban greening brings more benefits than costs to UK cities.' Do you agree? Use Figure 19 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO3 well-developed argument considering both views, with a clear, supported conclusion; effective use of Figure 19.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear argument with some reference to both views and some use of the figure.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points, mostly one-sided.\nIndicative content:\n• Benefits: shade and evaporation reduce the urban heat island; vegetation reduces run-off and flooding; trees absorb pollutants and CO₂; space for exercise improves physical and mental health; habitats and biodiversity; makes areas more attractive to investors.\n• Costs: land could be used for much-needed housing; maintenance costs; green space may be unevenly distributed, with fewer parks in deprived areas; can raise house prices (green gentrification).\n• Named example detail credited, e.g. Bristol as European Green Capital 2015; Castle Park; Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park.\nCredit any conclusion that is supported.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 20 (data written for this practice question): transport in a UK city before and after a new bus rapid-transit system opened.\nPeak-time journey time by bus from the airport road to the city centre: 45 minutes before, 28 minutes after.\nShare of commuters travelling by car: 58% before, 52% after.\nCost of the scheme: £230 million.\n\nAssess the effectiveness of transport strategies in reducing traffic problems in UK cities. Use Figure 20 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO3 detailed assessment with a clear judgement; effective use of data; AO1 accurate knowledge of strategies.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of how strategies work with some assessment and some data.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of strategies.\nIndicative content:\n• Data: bus journey time fell by 17 minutes (about 38%); car share fell by only 6 percentage points — most commuters still drive; high cost.\n• Other strategies: bus lanes, park and ride, cycle lanes and bike hire, Clean Air Zones (Bristol, launched 2022), the London Congestion Charge (2003) and ULEZ, Metrolink trams in Manchester, residents' parking zones.\n• Effectiveness depends on cost, reliability and coverage; charging schemes can be unpopular and affect low-income drivers; congestion may move to other routes.\n• Judgement: partly effective — improves public transport but does not remove congestion without measures that discourage car use.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nIn 2015 Bristol became the first UK city to be named European Green Capital.\n\nUsing a case study of a major city in the UK, explain how the city is trying to become more sustainable.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough explanation of a range of strategies (environmental, social, economic) with specific case-study detail.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some strategies with some detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic list of strategies; generic.\nIndicative content (Bristol):\n• Water conservation — water meters, rainwater harvesting, efficient appliances.\n• Energy conservation — insulation programmes, solar panels on homes and public buildings, district heating networks.\n• Waste recycling — kerbside collections of food waste and recyclables, reducing landfill.\n• Green space — parks, Ashton Court Estate, urban greening, community gardens.\n• Traffic management — MetroBus, cycle routes (e.g. Bristol and Bath Railway Path), Clean Air Zone, park and ride, rail improvements.\n• Brownfield regeneration (Temple Quarter, Harbourside) reduces sprawl.\nMax Level 1 if not linked to a named city.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 21: a table comparing two possible sites for 2,000 new homes near a UK city.\nSite X (brownfield, inner city): former railway yard; land must be decontaminated; close to the city centre and a railway station; existing water, sewerage and electricity supplies.\nSite Y (greenfield, rural–urban fringe): farmland; flat and easy to build on; next to a motorway junction; no existing services; local residents oppose the scheme.\n\nDiscuss the advantages and disadvantages of building new housing on brownfield and greenfield sites. Use Figure 21 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: well-developed discussion of advantages and disadvantages of both types of site; effective use of Figure 21.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear discussion of some advantages and disadvantages; some use of the figure; may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points, mainly lifted from the figure.\nIndicative content:\n• Brownfield advantages: reuses derelict land, regenerates the inner city; services already in place; close to jobs and public transport, reducing car use; protects countryside.\n• Brownfield disadvantages: decontamination is costly; sites can be small/awkward; may be less attractive to buyers; higher land costs.\n• Greenfield advantages: cheaper and quicker to build; space for gardens; good road access.\n• Greenfield disadvantages: loss of farmland and habitats; new roads and services needed; encourages car commuting and sprawl; local opposition.\nMax Level 2 if only one type of site is discussed.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nIn some parts of Bristol, such as Filwood in the south of the city, average life expectancy is several years lower than in wealthy suburbs such as Clifton.\n\nUsing a case study of a major city in the UK, explain how urban change has created social and economic challenges.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough explanation of a range of challenges linked to causes, with specific case-study detail.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some challenges with some detail.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic identification of challenges.\nIndicative content:\n• Urban deprivation — closure of industries left high unemployment in some areas (e.g. Filwood, Lawrence Hill).\n• Inequalities in housing — high house prices and rents; overcrowding; homelessness.\n• Inequalities in education — lower attainment and fewer qualifications in deprived wards.\n• Inequalities in health — lower life expectancy, higher rates of illness in poorer areas.\n• Inequalities in employment — skills mismatch between old manual jobs and new service/high-tech jobs.\n• Cycle of deprivation; crime and social exclusion.\nMax Level 1 if not linked to a named city.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 22: a photograph of a high street in a UK city with a Polish delicatessen, a Somali café, an Indian restaurant, a mosque and a church next to each other. A sign outside a primary school says "Welcome" in eight languages.\n\nUsing Figure 22 and a case study of a major city in the UK, discuss the impacts of migration on the city.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: well-developed discussion of a range of positive and negative impacts with case-study detail; effective use of Figure 22.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear discussion of some impacts; some detail and some use of the figure.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic points; one-sided or generic.\nIndicative content:\n• Population growth and a younger age structure; higher birth rates.\n• Cultural mix — food, shops, festivals (St Pauls Carnival), places of worship, music.\n• Economic — migrants fill job shortages (e.g. health service, construction, hospitality); start businesses that revive high streets.\n• Pressure on housing, school places and health services; need for language support.\n• Possible tensions or segregation in some areas.\nMax Level 2 without use of the figure.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 23: a photograph of a derelict waterfront in a UK city. Old warehouses have boarded-up windows and graffiti, there is litter and an abandoned shopping trolley in the water, and a "Site for sale" sign is attached to the fence.\n\nUsing Figure 23 and your own understanding, explain the environmental challenges created by urban change in UK cities.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough explanation of a range of environmental challenges with clear causes; effective use of Figure 23; named example detail.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some challenges; some use of the figure.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of the figure.\nIndicative content:\n• Dereliction — closure of docks and industry left empty buildings and land; graffiti and vandalism; reduces investment and lowers quality of life.\n• Contaminated land and water pollution, litter in waterways.\n• Building on brownfield and greenfield sites — brownfield needs decontamination; greenfield development removes countryside.\n• Waste disposal — large volumes of household waste; landfill, fly-tipping.\n• Air pollution and congestion from traffic.\n• Urban sprawl on the rural–urban fringe.\nMax Level 2 without use of the figure.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 24: features of the Beddington Zero Energy Development (BedZED), Sutton, south London — homes built from recycled and locally sourced materials; thick insulation; south-facing conservatories; solar panels; rainwater stored for flushing toilets; car-sharing club and few parking spaces; close to railway and tram stations.\n\nAssess the extent to which developments like this can make urban living more sustainable. Use Figure 24 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO3 detailed assessment with a supported judgement on "extent"; effective use of Figure 24.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of how features help sustainability with some assessment.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: basic description of features.\nIndicative content:\n• Energy conservation: insulation and solar gain reduce heating demand; solar panels provide renewable energy.\n• Water conservation: rainwater for toilets reduces mains use.\n• Materials: recycled/local materials reduce transport emissions and waste.\n• Traffic: car club, few parking spaces and nearby rail/tram reduce car use.\n• Limitations: small scale (around 100 homes) compared with city demand; higher building costs; some technology has not worked as well as planned; most people live in older homes that are harder to retrofit.\n• Judgement: shows what is possible, but limited impact unless applied widely.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\n'Urban regeneration projects bring more benefits than problems to UK cities.'\n\nUsing an example of an urban regeneration project in the UK, to what extent do you agree with this statement?\n[9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO1 detailed knowledge of a named regeneration project. AO2 thorough understanding of its social, economic and environmental effects. AO3 well-balanced evaluation of benefits and problems, reaching a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: AO1 clear knowledge with some place detail. AO2 clear understanding. AO3 some evaluation; may be unbalanced; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: AO1 limited knowledge. AO2 basic understanding. AO3 limited evaluation; generic statements.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content (e.g. Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park, Stratford):\n• Benefits: derelict, contaminated industrial land reclaimed; new homes (East Village), schools and health centre; Westfield Stratford City and later offices/universities brought jobs; transport upgrades; parkland, wetlands and sports venues.\n• Problems: rising house prices and rents; gentrification may displace existing residents; many jobs taken by people from outside the local boroughs; high public cost; some "affordable" housing not affordable for local people.\n• Judgement, e.g. benefits for the environment and economy are clear, but the extent to which local deprived residents gained is less certain.\nAccept Bristol Temple Quarter, Harbourside, Salford Quays/MediaCity, London Docklands, etc.\nSPaG (3 marks)\nHigh performance 3: spelling and punctuation with consistent accuracy; consistent control of meaning; wide range of specialist terms used precisely.\nIntermediate performance 2: considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance 1: reasonable accuracy; reasonable control of meaning; limited specialist terms.\n0: no answer, or errors severely hinder meaning.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nUsing a case study of a major city in the UK, to what extent has urban change created more opportunities than challenges?\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO1 detailed case-study knowledge. AO2 thorough understanding of opportunities and challenges. AO3 well-balanced evaluation leading to a substantiated judgement on "extent".\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: AO1 clear knowledge. AO2 clear understanding. AO3 some evaluation; may focus mainly on one side.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: AO1 limited knowledge. AO2 basic understanding. AO3 limited or no evaluation.\nIndicative content (Bristol):\n• Opportunities — social and economic: cultural mix from migration (St Pauls Carnival, Stapleton Road); recreation and entertainment (Harbourside, M Shed, theatres, sport); employment in aerospace, finance, media, universities; integrated transport (MetroBus, rail, cycle routes); urban greening (European Green Capital 2015, parks).\n• Challenges — social and economic: deprivation and inequalities in housing, education, health and employment (e.g. Filwood v Clifton); high house prices.\n• Challenges — environmental: dereliction; building on brownfield and greenfield sites; waste disposal; urban sprawl (Bradley Stoke, Cribbs Causeway) and commuter settlements; traffic congestion and air pollution.\n• Judgement, e.g. opportunities are significant but are unevenly shared across the city.\nMax Level 1 if no named city.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nUsing a case study of a major city in the UK, evaluate the effectiveness of strategies used to make urban living more sustainable.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO1 detailed knowledge of a range of strategies in a named city. AO2 thorough understanding of how they improve sustainability. AO3 well-developed evaluation of effectiveness and limitations, reaching a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: AO1 clear knowledge. AO2 clear understanding. AO3 some evaluation; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: AO1 limited knowledge. AO2 basic understanding. AO3 limited evaluation; mainly descriptive.\nIndicative content:\n• Water conservation — meters, efficient appliances, rainwater harvesting; effective at household scale but relies on behaviour change.\n• Energy conservation — insulation schemes, solar panels, district heating; reduces emissions but high initial costs.\n• Waste recycling — kerbside food waste and recycling collections; recycling rates have risen, but contamination and limited markets for recyclables remain.\n• Creating green space — parks, urban greening, community gardens; benefits health and flood risk; may be unevenly distributed.\n• Traffic management — MetroBus, cycle routes, park and ride, Clean Air Zone (Bristol, 2022), Congestion Charge/ULEZ (London); reduce pollution, but congestion persists and charges can be unpopular.\n• Judgement, e.g. strategies are making progress (Bristol European Green Capital 2015) but car dependence and inequality limit how sustainable the city is.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\nFigure 25 (data written for this practice question): traffic in a UK city, 2012 and 2022.\nAverage rush-hour speed in the city centre: 14 km/h in 2012, 13 km/h in 2022.\nDaily bus passengers: 120,000 in 2012, 150,000 in 2022.\nDays per year when air pollution exceeded safe limits at the busiest junction: 61 in 2012, 23 in 2022.\nKilometres of cycle lanes: 90 km in 2012, 210 km in 2022.\n\n'Urban transport strategies in UK cities have been successful in reducing traffic problems.' Use Figure 25 and your own understanding. To what extent do you agree?\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO1 detailed knowledge of transport strategies, with named examples. AO2 thorough understanding of how they affect traffic problems. AO3 effective use of Figure 25 to support a balanced, substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: AO1 clear knowledge. AO2 clear understanding. AO3 some evaluation with some data use.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: AO1 limited knowledge. AO2 basic understanding. AO3 limited evaluation; little or no data.\nIndicative content:\n• Successes from data: bus passengers up 30,000 (25%); cycle lanes more than doubled (+120 km); pollution exceedance days down from 61 to 23 (about 62% fall).\n• Limits from data: rush-hour speed fell from 14 to 13 km/h — congestion has not improved.\n• Strategies: bus lanes and bus rapid transit (Bristol MetroBus), trams (Manchester Metrolink), park and ride, bike-hire schemes, Clean Air Zones, the London Congestion Charge and ULEZ, Elizabeth line.\n• Reasons for limited success: population and car ownership growth; car dependence in suburbs and commuter settlements; cost and unpopularity of charges.\n• Judgement, e.g. successful in reducing pollution and increasing sustainable travel, but less successful in reducing congestion.` },
      { q: `Section A — Urban issues and challenges.\n'Migration has been the most important cause of change in UK cities.'\n\nUsing a case study of a major city in the UK, to what extent do you agree with this statement?\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO1 detailed case-study knowledge. AO2 thorough understanding of the role of migration and other causes of change. AO3 well-balanced evaluation reaching a substantiated judgement on the importance of migration.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: AO1 clear knowledge. AO2 clear understanding. AO3 some evaluation; may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: AO1 limited knowledge. AO2 basic understanding. AO3 limited evaluation.\nIndicative content:\n• Migration as a cause: national migration of students and workers; international migration (e.g. Caribbean, South Asian, Somali, Polish communities in Bristol) — growth of population, younger age structure, cultural mix, demand for housing and services.\n• Other causes: deindustrialisation and the closure of docks/factories (dereliction, deprivation); growth of service, finance, creative and high-tech industries; regeneration projects (Harbourside, Temple Quarter, Cabot Circus); transport improvements enabling suburbanisation and commuter settlements; government planning and sustainability policy.\n• Links: migrants fill jobs created by economic change; regeneration attracts new residents.\n• Judgement, e.g. migration is central to population growth and the cultural mix, but economic change explains the location of dereliction, deprivation and regeneration.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.3': {
    name: 'The Changing Economic World',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 1 (data written for this practice question): a table of gross national income (GNI) per head, US$, for five countries. UK 49,000; Brazil 9,000; Nigeria 2,100; Malawi 640; Niger 590.\n\nUsing Figure 1, identify the country with the lowest GNI per head.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Niger (1).\nNo other answer.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 2: a population pyramid for Niger. The base is very wide: about 9% of males and 9% of females are aged 0–4. Each age group above is clearly smaller than the one below it, so the sides slope steeply inwards. Fewer than 2% of each sex are aged 65 and over.\n\nWhich one of the following stages of the Demographic Transition Model (DTM) best matches the population structure shown in Figure 2?\nA  Stage 1\nB  Stage 2\nC  Stage 4\nD  Stage 5\nShade one circle only.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B — Stage 2 (1).\n(High birth rate gives the wide base; falling death rate but short life expectancy gives the narrow top.)` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 3: a list of four development indicators.\nA  Gross national income (GNI) per head\nB  Human Development Index (HDI)\nC  Infant mortality rate\nD  Literacy rate\n\nWhich one of the indicators in Figure 3 combines life expectancy, education and income into a single value between 0 and 1?\nShade one circle only.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `B — Human Development Index (HDI) (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 4 (data written for this practice question): birth and death rates for an LIC in 2020. Birth rate 44 per 1,000 people per year; death rate 11 per 1,000 people per year.\n\nUsing Figure 4, calculate the natural increase rate for this country. Give your answer per 1,000 people per year.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `33 (per 1,000 per year) (1).\nWorking: 44 − 11 = 33. Accept 3.3%.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 5 (data written for this practice question): a pie chart of employment in the UK by sector in 2021. Primary 1%; secondary 16%; tertiary 81%; quaternary shown as part of tertiary; remaining 2% labelled 'other'.\n\nUsing Figure 5, state the percentage of people in the UK employed in the tertiary sector in 2021.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `81% (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 6: a photograph of a beach near Montego Bay, Jamaica. White sand and clear turquoise sea are in the foreground; a large hotel complex with swimming pools stands behind palm trees; a cruise ship is moored offshore.\n\nUsing Figure 6, give one reason why tourists are attracted to Jamaica.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one reason from the photograph, e.g.:\n• sandy beaches (1)\n• clear / warm-looking sea for swimming or snorkelling (1)\n• hotels / resorts with pools (1)\n• cruise ships visit / easy to reach by cruise (1)\n• palm trees suggest a hot, tropical climate (1).\nMax 1.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 7: an outline map of West Africa showing Nigeria and the four countries that share a land border with it. One country lies to the west, one to the north, one to the north-east and one to the east. The Gulf of Guinea lies to the south.\n\nName the country that borders Nigeria to the west.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Benin (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 8 (data written for this practice question): manufacturing employment in Wales — 1971: 330,000; 2021: 150,000. A photograph shows the closed Tower Colliery headgear in the South Wales valleys.\n\nGive one reason why many jobs in primary and secondary industry have been lost in the UK since the 1970s.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one reason, e.g.:\n• cheaper coal / steel / manufactured goods imported from abroad (globalisation) (1)\n• mechanisation / automation replaced workers (1)\n• coal seams became exhausted or too expensive to mine (1)\n• government policy, e.g. closure of loss-making mines or privatisation (1)\n• lower labour costs in NEEs, e.g. China (1).\nMax 1.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 9 (data written for this practice question): GNI per head (US$) for five countries. UK 49,000; Brazil 9,000; Nigeria 2,100; Malawi 640; Niger 590.\n\nUsing Figure 9, calculate the range of GNI per head. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `49,000 − 590 (1) = US$48,410 (1).\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 9a (data written for this practice question): GNI per head (US$). UK 49,000; Malawi 640.\n\nUsing Figure 9a, calculate how many times greater GNI per head is in the UK than in Malawi. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `49,000 ÷ 640 (1)\n= 76.6 times greater (1).\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 10 (data written for this practice question): adult literacy rates (%) for five countries. Nigeria 63; Jamaica 89; Malawi 68; Niger 38; India 76.\n\nUsing Figure 10, calculate the mean adult literacy rate for the five countries. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `63 + 89 + 68 + 38 + 76 = 334 (1)\n334 ÷ 5 = 66.8% (1).\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks. Accept 67%.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 11 (data written for this practice question): stop-over and cruise visitor arrivals to Jamaica. 2010: 2.8 million; 2019: 4.2 million.\n\nUsing Figure 11, calculate the percentage change in visitor arrivals to Jamaica between 2010 and 2019. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(4.2 − 2.8) ÷ 2.8 × 100 (1)\n= 50% increase (1).\nAccept +50%. Correct answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 12 (data written for this practice question): employment in manufacturing in the UK. 1981: 6.0 million; 2021: 2.6 million.\n\nUsing Figure 12, calculate the percentage change in manufacturing employment in the UK between 1981 and 2021. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(2.6 − 6.0) ÷ 6.0 × 100 (1)\n= −56.7% (a 56.7% decrease) (1).\nAccept 56.7% decrease. Correct answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 13: GNI per head for Qatar is one of the highest in the world, but many migrant construction workers there earn low wages and live in crowded accommodation.\n\nOutline one limitation of using GNI per head as a measure of development.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point.\n• It is an average so it hides inequality within a country (1) — a rich minority can raise the figure while many people, such as migrant workers, remain poor (1).\n• It measures only income, not quality of life (1) — it does not show access to healthcare, education or clean water (1).\n• It does not include the informal economy (1) — so income in LICs may be underestimated (1).\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 14 (data written for this practice question): two countries with similar GNI per head (about US$12,000). Country X: life expectancy 79 years, HDI 0.85. Country Y: life expectancy 64 years, HDI 0.70.\n\nUsing Figure 14, suggest why HDI may be a better measure of development than GNI per head.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• HDI combines several indicators (income, life expectancy, education) (1) so it shows differences that GNI hides — X and Y have the same GNI but X has a much higher life expectancy and HDI (1).\n• HDI includes social as well as economic measures (1) so it gives a fuller picture of quality of life (1).\nMax 2. Data use from Figure 14 can develop the point.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 15: a graph of the Demographic Transition Model. In Stage 1 the death rate fluctuates at a high level (about 35 per 1,000). It falls steeply through Stage 2 and continues to fall more slowly in Stage 3, reaching about 10 per 1,000. It stays low in Stages 4 and 5, rising slightly in Stage 5.\n\nUsing Figure 15, describe the changes in death rate shown by the Demographic Transition Model.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid descriptive point:\n• high and fluctuating in Stage 1 (1)\n• falls rapidly in Stage 2 (1)\n• continues to fall but more slowly in Stage 3 (1)\n• low / steady in Stage 4 (1)\n• rises slightly in Stage 5 (1)\n• use of data, e.g. from about 35 to about 10 per 1,000 (1).\nMax 2. Do not credit explanation.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 16: a map of Africa highlighting 16 landlocked countries, including Chad, Niger, Mali and Malawi. Several of these countries also lie in the Sahel, where annual rainfall is low and unreliable.\n\nOutline one physical cause of uneven development.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• Being landlocked (1) makes trade more expensive because goods must cross other countries to reach ports (1).\n• Low / unreliable rainfall or drought (1) reduces crop yields, so farmers earn little and food shortages occur (1).\n• Frequent natural hazards, e.g. tropical storms or earthquakes (1), destroy infrastructure so money is spent on rebuilding rather than development (1).\n• Few raw materials (1) so little income from exports (1).\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 17: an extract from a history textbook. 'By 1914, European powers controlled almost all of Africa. Colonial borders were drawn with little regard for ethnic groups, and colonies were run mainly to supply raw materials such as cocoa, rubber and minerals to Europe.'\n\nOutline one way in which colonialism has contributed to uneven development.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• Colonies were made to export raw materials (1), so they did not develop manufacturing and still depend on low-value primary exports (1).\n• Borders were drawn artificially (1), which has led to conflict between groups that slows development (1).\n• Wealth / resources were taken to Europe (1), so the colonial powers became richer while colonies stayed poor (1).\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 18 (data written for this practice question): a bar graph of the number of people born in Nigeria and living in the UK. 2001: 88,000; 2011: 191,000; 2021: 271,000.\n\nOutline one way in which uneven development leads to international migration.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• People move from poorer to richer countries for higher wages / more jobs (1), sending remittances home to support their families (1).\n• Lack of services such as healthcare or universities in LICs/NEEs (1) encourages people to move to HICs for a better quality of life (1).\n• Conflict or poverty in some areas (1) forces people to move as refugees or economic migrants (1).\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 19: a newspaper extract. 'The Grameen Bank in Bangladesh, founded by Muhammad Yunus, lends small sums to people too poor to borrow from ordinary banks. Most borrowers are women, who use the loans to buy a sewing machine, a cow or stock for a small shop.'\n\nUsing Figure 19, outline how microfinance can help to reduce the development gap.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• Small loans allow people to start their own businesses (1), which earn an income so families can afford food, healthcare and school fees (1).\n• It gives women access to money (1), increasing their independence and status (1).\n• Loans are repaid and re-lent (1), so the benefits spread to more people over time (1).\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 20: a photograph of a hand-operated water pump in a village in rural Malawi. Women are filling plastic containers. A caption reads: 'Installed by a charity; local people were trained to repair it using spare parts available in the nearest town.'\n\nUsing Figure 20, suggest why intermediate technology is suitable for many rural areas in LICs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• It is cheap to build and run (1), so communities with little money can afford it (1).\n• Local people can maintain / repair it (1), so it keeps working without outside experts (1).\n• It does not need electricity or fuel (1), which is often unavailable in rural areas (1).\n• It provides clean water (1), reducing disease and saving time spent walking to collect water (1).\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 21: a cocoa farmer in Ghana belongs to a Fairtrade co-operative. The co-operative is paid at least the Fairtrade Minimum Price for its cocoa, plus a Fairtrade Premium which members vote on how to spend.\n\nUsing Figure 21, outline one way in which fair trade can benefit farmers in LICs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• A minimum price is guaranteed (1), so farmers have a reliable income even when world prices fall (1).\n• The Premium is spent on community projects (1), such as schools, clean water or health clinics (1).\n• Co-operatives give farmers more bargaining power (1), so they receive a fairer share of the final price (1).\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 22: in 2005, as part of the Heavily Indebted Poor Countries (HIPC) initiative, Zambia had most of its foreign debt cancelled. Before this, Zambia was spending more on debt repayments than on health care.\n\nOutline one advantage of debt relief for an LIC.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• Money that was used for repayments can be spent on services (1), e.g. Zambia was able to spend more on health and education such as free primary care (1).\n• It reduces the burden of interest payments (1), allowing the government to invest in infrastructure that encourages economic growth (1).\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 23 (data written for this practice question): two pie charts of Nigeria's GDP by sector. 1990: agriculture 32%, industry 41%, services 27%. 2022: agriculture 24%, industry 31%, services 45%.\n\nUsing Figure 23, describe how Nigeria's industrial structure changed between 1990 and 2022.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid descriptive point:\n• Services increased from 27% to 45% / became the largest sector (1)\n• Industry decreased from 41% to 31% (1)\n• Agriculture decreased from 32% to 24% (1)\n• Overall shift from primary and secondary towards tertiary (1).\nMax 2. At least one figure needed for full marks.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 24: the Shell Petroleum Development Company operated onshore oil fields in the Niger Delta for decades, in a joint venture with the Nigerian National Petroleum Corporation. Crude oil provides most of Nigeria's export earnings.\n\nOutline one way in which a transnational corporation (TNC) such as Shell has benefited Nigeria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point; 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• It creates jobs (1), both directly and in supporting industries, increasing incomes / the multiplier effect (1).\n• The government receives taxes and royalties (1), which can be spent on services and infrastructure (1).\n• It brings investment and new technology / skills (1), training Nigerian workers (1).\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 25: Cambridge Science Park, founded by Trinity College in 1970, lies on the northern edge of Cambridge next to the A14. It has more than 100 companies working in biotechnology, computing and research, set in landscaped grounds with lakes and footpaths.\n\nUsing Figure 25, identify two features that attract companies to a science park.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark per feature, e.g.:\n• close to a university (for research links and graduates) (1)\n• good road access via the A14 (1)\n• other high-tech companies nearby to share ideas (1)\n• attractive, landscaped environment (1)\n• edge-of-city location with space to expand (1).\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 26 (data written for this practice question): birth rates in Bangladesh. 1970: 47 per 1,000; 1995: 30 per 1,000; 2020: 18 per 1,000. Over the same period the percentage of girls attending secondary school rose sharply.\n\nExplain why birth rates fall as a country moves through Stage 3 of the Demographic Transition Model.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation with developed reasons; may use Figure 26.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic explanation, mainly simple statements.\nIndicative content:\n• falling infant mortality means parents need fewer children to ensure some survive\n• better education, especially for girls, leads to later marriage and careers\n• increased access to contraception and family planning\n• move from farming to urban jobs means children are less needed as farm labour and cost more to raise\n• changing attitudes / status of women\n• use of data, e.g. birth rate fell from 47 to 18 per 1,000 as school attendance rose.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 27 (data written for this practice question): the price of a 1 kg bag of coffee in a UK supermarket is £10. The coffee farmer in Uganda receives about 50p; the rest goes to exporters, shippers, roasters, retailers and in taxes.\n\nExplain how international trade can cause uneven development.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of how trade causes uneven development, with developed points.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• LICs often export low-value primary products while HICs export high-value manufactured goods and services\n• most of the profit is made in processing and retailing in HICs (e.g. 50p of £10 = 5% to the farmer)\n• world prices of commodities fluctuate, so incomes in LICs are unreliable\n• trade blocs and tariffs in HICs make it harder for LICs to sell processed goods\n• poor infrastructure / landlocked position raises the cost of trading.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 28 (data written for this practice question): life expectancy and doctors per 10,000 people. UK: 81 years, 32 doctors. Nigeria: 53 years, 4 doctors. Malawi: 63 years, 0.5 doctors.\n\nUsing Figure 28 and your own understanding, explain how uneven development affects people's health.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation linking development to health, using Figure 28.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic explanation or description of data only.\nIndicative content:\n• poorer countries can afford fewer doctors and hospitals (e.g. Malawi 0.5 per 10,000 compared with UK 32), so diseases go untreated\n• lack of clean water and sanitation spreads diseases such as cholera\n• poor diet / malnutrition lowers resistance to disease\n• life expectancy is lower (Nigeria 53 compared with 81 in UK); infant mortality is higher\n• wealthier countries can afford vaccinations, but face more diseases of affluence (e.g. heart disease).` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 29: a leaflet from an aid charity. 'With funding from the UK government and the Global Fund, millions of insecticide-treated bed nets have been distributed across Nigeria to protect families from malaria-carrying mosquitoes.'\n\nUsing a case study of an LIC or NEE, explain how international aid can help the country to develop.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of how aid helps, with specific detail from a named LIC/NEE.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic explanation, generic.\nIndicative content (Nigeria):\n• bed nets reduce malaria, lowering infant mortality and days lost from work and school\n• healthier people can work and earn more, helping the economy\n• aid funds education and training, increasing skills\n• aid for infrastructure (roads, power, water) supports business growth\n• credit other valid named examples (e.g. aid for polio vaccination in Nigeria).` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 30: Nigeria is a member of the African Union, the Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS), OPEC and the Commonwealth. In recent decades China has become a major trading partner and has funded new railways, including the Lagos–Ibadan line.\n\nUsing a case study of an LIC or NEE, explain how the country's political and trading relationships with the wider world have changed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of changing relationships with specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points, little detail.\nIndicative content (Nigeria):\n• former British colony; independence in 1960, trade once dominated by the UK\n• trade has broadened to include China, India, the USA and EU countries\n• China invests in infrastructure in exchange for access to oil and markets\n• membership of ECOWAS encourages free trade with neighbouring West African countries\n• OPEC membership links Nigeria to world oil markets; dependence on oil exports means income varies with oil prices\n• political stability since the return to civilian rule in 1999 has encouraged foreign investment.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 31: a photograph of creeks near Bodo in the Niger Delta after oil spills from a pipeline in 2008–2009. Black oil coats the water surface and the roots of dead mangrove trees. Gas flares burn in the distance.\n\nUsing Figure 31 and your own understanding, explain the environmental impacts of economic development in an LIC or NEE.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of environmental impacts, with specific detail from a named example.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic explanation or simple list.\nIndicative content (Nigeria):\n• oil spills pollute water and soils, killing mangroves and fish, so fishing and farming communities lose their livelihoods\n• gas flaring releases greenhouse gases and pollutants, causing acid rain and breathing problems\n• Shell agreed to pay £55 million compensation to the Bodo community in 2015 for the spills\n• rapid urban and industrial growth (e.g. Lagos) causes air and water pollution and waste problems\n• deforestation for farming and commercial logging.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 32: a timeline of UK economic policy. 1980s — state-owned industries such as British Steel and British Coal privatised; many loss-making coal mines closed. 2015 — Kellingley, the last deep coal mine in Britain, closes.\n\nExplain how government policies have contributed to economic change in the UK.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of how policies changed the UK economy.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points.\nIndicative content:\n• privatisation and removal of subsidies led to closure of uncompetitive mines and factories, causing deindustrialisation\n• deregulation of the financial sector helped London grow as a global financial centre\n• Enterprise Zones and grants attracted new industries and foreign investment to declining areas\n• support for research and higher education encouraged the growth of quaternary industries\n• free trade policies exposed UK industry to cheaper imports from abroad.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 33: Torr Works is a large limestone quarry near Shepton Mallet in Somerset. Much of the stone leaves by rail rather than road. Plans for when quarrying ends include flooding the quarry to create a lake surrounded by wildlife habitats.\n\nUsing Figure 33, explain how a modern industrial development can be more environmentally sustainable.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation with specific detail of how impacts are reduced.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points.\nIndicative content:\n• transporting stone by rail reduces lorry traffic, so less air pollution, congestion and CO₂ emissions\n• restoration to a lake and wildlife habitats creates new ecosystems and leisure space after use\n• landscaping / screening banks and tree planting reduce visual impact and noise\n• careful blasting times and dust controls reduce disturbance to local people\n• credit other valid sustainable features (e.g. renewable energy at a factory).` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 34 (data written for this practice question): population of the Outer Hebrides, Scotland. 1951: 35,600; 2022: 26,100. A pie chart shows that 27% of residents are aged 65 or over, compared with 19% for Scotland as a whole.\n\nUsing Figure 34 and your own understanding, explain the social and economic changes in a rural area of the UK where the population is declining.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of both social and economic changes, with specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points or description of data only.\nIndicative content:\n• population fell by about 27% (35,600 to 26,100) as young people leave for education and jobs on the mainland\n• ageing population: schools close or merge, demand rises for health and care services\n• fewer workers so businesses close; jobs in fishing, crofting and traditional industries such as Harris Tweed have declined\n• services such as shops and bus routes become unviable, increasing isolation\n• tourism and renewable energy offer some new jobs; second homes can raise house prices.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 35: undersea fibre-optic cables come ashore at Bude in Cornwall, linking the UK's internet to North America and Europe. Heathrow is one of the world's busiest international airports.\n\nExplain how the UK is linked to the wider world.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of a range of links with examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points.\nIndicative content:\n• electronic communication: undersea cables, e.g. at Bude, make the UK a hub for global data and finance\n• transport: Heathrow and other airports, ports such as London Gateway and Felixstowe, the Channel Tunnel\n• trade: major trading partners include the EU and USA; trade agreements since leaving the EU in 2020\n• culture: films, music, TV and sport (e.g. the Premier League) are exported worldwide\n• political links: Commonwealth, UN Security Council, NATO.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 36 (data written for this practice question): development indicators for three countries.\nCountry | GNI per head (US$) | Life expectancy (years) | Literacy rate (%) | Infant mortality (per 1,000 live births)\nUK | 49,000 | 81 | 99 | 4\nBrazil | 9,000 | 73 | 94 | 13\nNigeria | 2,100 | 53 | 63 | 70\n\nAssess the usefulness of different measures of development. Use Figure 36 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed assessment of strengths and limitations of several measures; good use of Figure 36; clear judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): some assessment; some use of the figure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic statements about measures; little assessment.\nIndicative content:\n• GNI per head is easy to compare but is an average that hides inequality and ignores the informal economy\n• social measures (life expectancy, literacy, infant mortality) show quality of life; infant mortality is a sensitive indicator of healthcare and clean water (70 per 1,000 in Nigeria compared with 4 in UK)\n• single indicators can mislead; data may be out of date or unreliable in LICs\n• composite measures such as HDI are more useful as they combine indicators\n• in Figure 36 all indicators rank the countries in the same order, which suggests they are linked\n• judgement: a range of measures together gives the most accurate picture.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 37: population pyramids for Niger and the UK. Niger: very wide base (about 9% of each sex aged 0–4), steeply narrowing sides and very few people aged over 65. UK: narrow base (about 3% of each sex aged 0–4), straight sides up to age 60 and a wide top with many people over 65, more women than men at the oldest ages.\n\nUsing Figure 37, explain how the Demographic Transition Model can be linked to a country's level of development.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation linking DTM stages to development; good use of both pyramids.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation with some links; some use of Figure 37.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points; little linking.\nIndicative content:\n• Niger is in Stage 2: high birth rate (wide base) due to lack of contraception, need for child labour and high infant mortality; death rate falling but life expectancy low (narrow top) due to limited healthcare\n• the UK is in Stage 4: low birth rate (narrow base) due to family planning, careers for women and cost of children; low death rate and high life expectancy (wide top) due to good healthcare\n• as countries develop, healthcare improves first (death rate falls), then social change lowers the birth rate\n• the DTM can help predict future needs, e.g. Niger needs schools and jobs; the UK needs care for an ageing population\n• limitation: the model is based on European experience and may not fit all countries.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 38: three statements.\nA: 'Many of the world's poorest countries are landlocked or suffer frequent droughts.'\nB: 'Countries that export raw materials such as cocoa earn far less than those that export manufactured goods.'\nC: 'Former colonies often inherited weak economies based on a few primary products.'\n\nExplain how physical, economic and historical factors cause uneven development. Use Figure 38 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation of all three types of cause with clear chains of reasoning.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of at least two types of cause.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points; may restate Figure 38.\nIndicative content:\n• physical: landlocked countries face high transport costs; droughts reduce harvests; natural hazards destroy infrastructure; poor soils; diseases such as malaria\n• economic: dependence on low-value primary exports; fluctuating commodity prices; unfair trade and tariffs; debt repayments; corruption\n• historical: colonialism took resources and left economies dependent on primary exports; artificial borders led to conflict; conflict destroys infrastructure and deters investment\n• factors interact, e.g. a landlocked former colony dependent on one crop.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 39: a news report. 'Fighting in South Sudan since 2013 has forced more than 2 million people to flee to neighbouring countries. Meanwhile, remittances from family members working in HICs make up a large share of income in many LICs.'\n\nUsing Figure 39 and your own understanding, explain the consequences of uneven development.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation of a range of consequences (wealth, health, migration) with clear linkages.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of some consequences.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points.\nIndicative content:\n• disparities in wealth: large gap in income between and within countries\n• health: lower life expectancy and higher infant mortality in poorer countries\n• international migration: economic migrants move to HICs for work; refugees flee conflict and poverty (e.g. South Sudan)\n• remittances sent home support families but may make countries dependent\n• loss of skilled workers ('brain drain') from LICs; pressure on services in receiving areas\n• links: conflict both causes and is caused by poverty.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 40 (data written for this practice question): tourism in Jamaica.\n• Visitor arrivals rose from 2.8 million (2010) to 4.2 million (2019).\n• Tourism employs about 1 in 4 Jamaican workers directly or indirectly.\n• Many visitors stay in foreign-owned all-inclusive resorts.\n• Arrivals fell by about 70% in 2020 during the COVID-19 pandemic.\n\nTo what extent does tourism help to reduce the development gap? Use Figure 40 and an example of an LIC or NEE.`, marks: 6, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed evaluation of benefits and limitations with specific use of Figure 40 and the example; clear supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): some evaluation; some specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points; little evaluation.\nIndicative content (Jamaica):\n• benefits: jobs in hotels, restaurants and transport; foreign currency earnings; taxes fund infrastructure such as roads and airports; the multiplier effect supports farmers and craft workers\n• limitations: leakage of profits from foreign-owned all-inclusive resorts; seasonal or low-paid jobs; dependence on one industry makes the economy vulnerable to shocks (70% fall in 2020), hurricanes and recession in HICs\n• environmental damage, e.g. to coral reefs, could reduce future income\n• judgement: tourism helps if profits stay in the country and benefits are widely spread.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 41: two opinions about aid.\nCharity worker: 'Aid saves lives. Vaccines and bed nets have cut child deaths across Africa.'\nEconomist: 'Some aid is tied — it must be spent on goods from the donor country — and some never reaches the people who need it.'\n\nAssess the advantages and disadvantages of international aid as a strategy to reduce the development gap. Use Figure 41 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed, balanced assessment with examples; clear judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): some advantages and disadvantages; some assessment.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points; one-sided.\nIndicative content:\n• advantages: emergency aid saves lives after disasters; development aid funds health, education and infrastructure; can be targeted at the poorest; NGO projects work with communities\n• disadvantages: tied aid benefits donor businesses; corruption means aid may not reach people; dependency; large top-down projects may not meet local needs; conditions attached\n• judgement: small-scale, bottom-up long-term aid is often more effective than large top-down projects.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 42: three strategies used to reduce the development gap.\nStrategy 1 — Fair trade: farmers receive a guaranteed minimum price and a social premium.\nStrategy 2 — Microfinance: small loans for people who cannot borrow from banks.\nStrategy 3 — Debt relief: cancelling some of a country's debt so money can be spent on services.\n\nWhich strategy do you think would be most effective in reducing the development gap? Use Figure 42 and your own understanding to justify your choice.`, marks: 6, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed justification of chosen strategy with clear reasons, examples and comparison with at least one other strategy.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear justification; some detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic reasons; little justification.\nNo marks for the choice alone. Indicative content:\n• fair trade: stable income, premium funds schools and clean water (e.g. cocoa co-operatives in Ghana); but only a small share of world trade, higher price may reduce demand, certification costs\n• microfinance: helps individuals start businesses, empowers women (Grameen Bank, Bangladesh); but small scale, risk of debt for borrowers, high interest from some lenders\n• debt relief: frees government money for health and education across the whole country (e.g. Zambia, HIPC); but needs good governance, conditions attached, risk of new borrowing\n• credit any choice that is well justified.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 43: a fact file on Nigeria. Population over 200 million — the largest in Africa. In 2014 it became Africa's largest economy after its GDP was recalculated. Major oil exporter. Lagos is one of Africa's largest cities. 'Nollywood' is one of the world's biggest film industries by number of films.\n\nUsing Figure 43 and your own understanding, explain why a named LIC or NEE is important regionally and globally.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation of both regional and global importance with specific detail.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of some importance; may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points; may copy Figure 43.\nIndicative content (Nigeria):\n• regional: largest population and one of the largest economies in Africa; leading member of ECOWAS and the African Union; provides peacekeeping troops; major market for neighbouring countries\n• global: oil exports to many countries; OPEC member; growing manufacturing and services attract TNCs; Nollywood and music spread Nigerian culture worldwide; member of the Commonwealth and UN\n• location in West Africa on the Gulf of Guinea gives access to Atlantic shipping routes.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 44: Unilever has operated in Nigeria since 1923, making soap, tea and food products in factories near Lagos. Shell has extracted oil in the Niger Delta for decades, where oil spills and gas flaring have damaged fishing and farming.\n\nAssess the advantages and disadvantages of transnational corporations (TNCs) for an LIC or NEE. Use Figure 44 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed, balanced assessment using named TNCs; clear judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): some advantages and disadvantages with some detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points; one-sided.\nIndicative content (Nigeria):\n• advantages: jobs and training (e.g. Unilever factories); tax revenue for the government; investment in infrastructure; community projects; multiplier effect for local suppliers\n• disadvantages: profits go overseas to the TNC's home country; pollution from oil spills and gas flaring (Niger Delta); low wages and poor conditions in some cases; TNCs may leave if costs rise; links to corruption and local conflict\n• judgement: benefits depend on regulation by the government and how revenue is used.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 45 (data written for this practice question): a table of UK employment by sector.\nYear | Primary (%) | Secondary (%) | Tertiary (%) | Quaternary (%)\n1971 | 4 | 37 | 54 | 5\n2021 | 1 | 16 | 70 | 13\n\nUsing Figure 45, explain how the UK is moving towards a post-industrial economy.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation of causes and features of the post-industrial economy with good use of the data.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation; some use of Figure 45.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic description of data or simple points.\nIndicative content:\n• secondary employment fell from 37% to 16% due to deindustrialisation: globalisation, cheaper imports, mechanisation\n• tertiary rose from 54% to 70%: growth of retail, finance, tourism and public services\n• quaternary more than doubled from 5% to 13%: growth of IT, research and development, science parks (e.g. Cambridge Science Park)\n• development of information technology allows services to be provided from anywhere\n• London as a global financial centre; growth of business parks on the edge of cities.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 46 (data written for this practice question): population of South Cambridgeshire. 2001: 130,000; 2021: 162,000. Northstowe, a new town for about 10,000 homes, is being built on a former RAF base. Average house prices in South Cambridgeshire are well above the UK average.\n\nUsing Figure 46 and your own understanding, explain the social and economic changes in a rural area of the UK where the population is growing.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation of both social and economic changes with specific detail.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation; some detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points.\nIndicative content:\n• population rose by about 25% (130,000 to 162,000) as people move out from Cambridge and London (counter-urbanisation) and to work in high-tech jobs\n• house prices rise, so young local people cannot afford to stay; new estates and new towns such as Northstowe\n• more traffic and congestion from commuters; pressure on roads, e.g. the A14 upgrade\n• schools and services under pressure but some services kept open by higher demand\n• villages may become dormitory settlements, quiet during the day; tensions between newcomers and established residents\n• economic: growth of science and business parks provides jobs; farmland lost to housing.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 47 (data written for this practice question): median weekly pay for full-time workers. London £860; South East £720; North East £590; Wales £600. A list of strategies: the Northern Powerhouse; Enterprise Zones; devolved powers to elected mayors, e.g. Greater Manchester.\n\nAssess the success of strategies used to reduce the north–south divide in the UK. Use Figure 47 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed assessment of named strategies, using Figure 47; clear judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): some assessment of strategies; some detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic description of strategies or data.\nIndicative content:\n• Figure 47 shows the divide persists: London pay is £270 a week more than the North East\n• Northern Powerhouse: investment in transport, science and culture in northern cities; some growth (e.g. MediaCityUK, Salford) but criticised for slow delivery\n• Enterprise Zones: tax breaks and simpler planning attracted firms, but some jobs moved from nearby areas rather than being new\n• devolution lets regions decide their own priorities, e.g. Greater Manchester transport\n• transport projects such as HS2 were cut back (northern leg cancelled in 2023), limiting benefits for the north\n• judgement: some success in major cities, but the gap remains large.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 48: London Gateway is a deep-water port on the Thames estuary in Thurrock, Essex, that opened in 2013. It can handle some of the largest container ships in the world, and has a large logistics park alongside it with road and rail links.\n\nEvaluate the importance of improvements to transport infrastructure for the UK economy. Use Figure 48 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed evaluation of benefits and costs of named improvements; clear judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): some evaluation with some detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points.\nIndicative content:\n• ports: London Gateway allows the largest ships to dock, reduces transport costs and creates jobs in logistics\n• rail: HS2 (London–Birmingham) increases capacity and cuts journey times, but costs have risen greatly and parts were cancelled\n• roads: motorway and A-road upgrades (e.g. A14) reduce congestion and help firms\n• airports: plans for a third runway at Heathrow would boost trade and tourism but increase noise and CO₂ emissions\n• costs: high construction costs, loss of homes, habitats and countryside\n• judgement: infrastructure is vital for trade and rebalancing the economy but must be weighed against environmental costs.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 49: four strategies used to reduce the development gap — tourism (e.g. Jamaica), fair trade (e.g. cocoa farmers in Ghana), microfinance (e.g. the Grameen Bank in Bangladesh) and intermediate technology (e.g. hand pumps in rural Malawi).\n\nTo what extent can the development gap be reduced? Use Figure 49 and examples in your answer.\n[9 marks + 3 SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): detailed, well-developed evaluation of a range of strategies with specific examples; balanced; substantiated judgement on 'to what extent'.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear discussion of some strategies with some examples; some evaluation; partial judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic description of strategies; little evaluation or examples.\nIndicative content:\n• tourism brings jobs and foreign currency (Jamaica) but leakage and vulnerability to shocks\n• fair trade guarantees prices and funds community projects, but covers a small share of trade\n• microfinance helps individuals start businesses, especially women, but is small-scale\n• intermediate technology is cheap and sustainable, improving health and farming, but local in impact\n• aid, debt relief and investment (FDI, industrial development) work at a national scale but depend on governance\n• the gap is widening or narrowing at different rates: some NEEs (e.g. China, India) have grown quickly, while many LICs lag behind\n• judgement: the gap can be reduced if several strategies are combined, but strategies often treat symptoms rather than causes such as unfair trade.\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh (3): accurate spelling and punctuation; grammar used with consistent control; wide range of specialist terms used precisely.\nIntermediate (2): considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold (1): reasonable accuracy; no serious obstruction to meaning; limited range of specialist terms.\nNo marks (0): no response, or response does not reach threshold.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 50 (data written for this practice question): Nigeria, 1990 and 2020.\nIndicator | 1990 | 2020\nGNI per head (US$) | 560 | 2,000\nLife expectancy (years) | 46 | 53\nInfant mortality (per 1,000 live births) | 125 | 72\nPopulation with access to electricity (%) | 27 | 55\nPeople living on under US$2.15 a day (%) | 57 | 31\n\nTo what extent has economic development improved the quality of life for people in an LIC or NEE? Use Figure 50 and a case study in your answer.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): detailed evaluation of improvements and limitations, with thorough use of Figure 50 and specific case-study detail; substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear discussion with some use of data and case study; partial judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic points; limited use of data; generic.\nIndicative content (Nigeria):\n• improvements: GNI per head more than tripled (560 to 2,000); infant mortality fell from 125 to 72; electricity access doubled; poverty fell from 57% to 31%\n• growth of jobs in services, manufacturing and the oil industry; more money for schools, healthcare and infrastructure\n• limitations: life expectancy rose only 7 years and is still low; 31% still live in extreme poverty; wealth concentrated among an elite and in the south / Lagos, while the north is poorer\n• oil wealth has been linked to corruption; pollution in the Niger Delta has harmed health and livelihoods; conflict (e.g. Boko Haram in the north-east) limits progress; rapid population growth spreads gains thinly\n• judgement: quality of life has improved overall but unevenly, and many people have not benefited.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 51: two photographs from Nigeria. Photograph A: a modern container port and oil terminal with large tankers. Photograph B: a fisherman in a canoe in the Niger Delta surrounded by oil-covered water and dead mangroves.\n\n'The environmental costs of economic development in LICs and NEEs are greater than the economic benefits.' Do you agree? Use Figure 51 and a case study of an LIC or NEE in your answer.`, marks: 9, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): detailed, well-balanced evaluation of environmental costs and economic benefits, with specific case-study detail; substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear discussion of both sides with some detail; partial judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic points; one-sided or generic.\nIndicative content (Nigeria):\n• economic benefits: oil provides most export earnings and much government revenue; growth of manufacturing and services; jobs in TNCs such as Shell and Unilever; investment in ports, roads and railways\n• environmental costs: oil spills (e.g. Bodo, 2008–2009) destroy mangroves and fisheries; gas flaring causes air pollution and acid rain; rapid growth of Lagos causes waste, traffic and air pollution; deforestation\n• environmental damage also has economic costs: lost fishing and farming income, health costs, clean-up costs\n• benefits are unevenly shared while costs fall on poorer communities in the Delta\n• attempts to manage impacts: compensation (£55 million to the Bodo community in 2015), clean-up projects, laws to reduce gas flaring\n• judgement: either view credited if supported with evidence.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 52 (data written for this practice question): regional data for the UK.\nRegion | Median weekly pay (£) | Life expectancy, men (years) | Population change 2011–2021 (%)\nLondon | 860 | 80 | +8\nSouth East | 720 | 81 | +8\nNorth East | 590 | 77 | +2\nWales | 600 | 78 | +1\n\n'The north–south divide in the UK is too large to be reduced.' Do you agree? Use Figure 52 and your own understanding.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): detailed evaluation of the extent of the divide and of strategies to reduce it, with good use of Figure 52; substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear discussion of the divide and some strategies; some use of data; partial judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic points; little evaluation.\nIndicative content:\n• Figure 52: London pay is £270 a week higher than the North East; life expectancy 3–4 years lower in the North East and Wales; slower population growth in the north and Wales\n• causes: deindustrialisation hit the north and Wales hardest; growth of finance and quaternary industries concentrated in London and the South East\n• strategies: Northern Powerhouse, Enterprise Zones, devolution to elected mayors, transport investment, moving government and BBC jobs to the north (e.g. MediaCityUK, Salford), freeports\n• evidence of success in some northern cities (Manchester, Leeds) but the gap remains; some strategies cut back (HS2 northern leg cancelled in 2023)\n• the divide is not simple: there are poor areas in the south and rich areas in the north\n• judgement: either view credited if supported.` },
      { q: `Section B — The changing economic world.\nFigure 53: two descriptions of UK places. Kellingley, North Yorkshire: the last deep coal mine in Britain closed here in 2015 with the loss of about 450 jobs. Cambridge: the 'Cambridge cluster' of science parks and high-tech firms employs tens of thousands of people in research and technology.\n\nTo what extent have changes in the UK economy brought benefits to people and the environment? Use Figure 53 and your own understanding.`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–9 marks): detailed evaluation of the effects of economic change on both people and the environment, using specific examples; substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear discussion with some examples; partial judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic points; little evaluation.\nIndicative content:\n• deindustrialisation: job losses and unemployment in former mining and manufacturing areas (Kellingley), social problems and out-migration of young people\n• benefits: new jobs in services and quaternary industries (Cambridge cluster), higher wages, cleaner working conditions\n• benefits are unevenly shared: growth concentrated in London and the South East, widening the north–south divide; pressure on housing in growth areas\n• environment: closure of heavy industry has reduced air and water pollution; brownfield sites regenerated; but science and business parks and new housing take greenfield land, and commuting increases traffic\n• modern industry can be sustainable, e.g. Torr Works quarry restoration\n• judgement: overall benefits for the national economy and environment, but costs for some communities.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.4': {
    name: 'Resource Management',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 1: a table of the share of UK electricity generation by source (data written for this practice question).\nSource | 2012 (%) | 2022 (%)\nCoal | 39 | 2\nGas | 28 | 38\nNuclear | 19 | 15\nRenewables (wind, solar, hydro, bioenergy) | 11 | 40\nOther | 3 | 5\n\nUsing Figure 1, identify the source of electricity that decreased the most between 2012 and 2022.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark: coal (fell from 39% to 2%).\nNo credit for a figure on its own.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 2: a table of energy consumption per person in six countries (data written for this practice question).\nCountry | Energy consumption per person (gigajoules, GJ)\nCanada | 380\nUSA | 280\nChina | 115\nUK | 110\nIndia | 27\nNigeria | 9\n\nUsing Figure 2, calculate the range of energy consumption per person.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark: 371 GJ (380 − 9).\nUnits not required for the mark.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 3: a pie chart showing where the food eaten in the UK comes from (data written for this practice question). UK 60%; European Union 25%; Africa 4%; Asia 4%; South America 4%; North America 2%; rest of the world 1%.\n\nUsing Figure 3, calculate the percentage of food eaten in the UK that is imported.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark: 40% (100 − 60, or the sum of the non-UK sectors).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 4: a table comparing the carbon footprint of some foods sold in a UK supermarket in December (data written for this practice question).\nFood | Where grown | Transport to UK shop | Distance (km) | Carbon footprint (kg CO₂e per kg of food)\nGreen beans | Kenya | air freight | 6,800 | 12.0\nGreen beans | Lincolnshire, UK (summer crop, frozen) | lorry | 150 | 0.4\nTomatoes | Somerset, UK (heated greenhouse) | lorry | 200 | 2.8\nTomatoes | southern Spain (unheated polytunnel) | lorry | 2,000 | 1.0\n\nUsing Figure 4, calculate how many times greater the carbon footprint of Kenyan green beans is than that of UK green beans.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark: 30 times (12.0 ÷ 0.4).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nTotal energy use in the UK has fallen since the early 2000s, even though the population has grown.\n\nGive one reason why energy use in the UK has fallen.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one valid reason, e.g.:\n• more energy-efficient appliances, lighting (LED bulbs) and vehicles\n• better insulation of homes (loft/cavity wall insulation, double glazing)\n• decline of energy-intensive heavy industry such as steel making\n• high energy prices encouraging people to use less\n• government targets/campaigns to reduce energy use and carbon emissions.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 5: a photograph of a UK supermarket shelf with a sign reading "Organic — grown without artificial fertilisers or pesticides". The organic carrots on the shelf cost about twice as much as the non-organic carrots next to them.\n\nGive one reason why some UK consumers choose to buy organic food.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one valid reason, e.g.:\n• they believe it is healthier/has no pesticide residues\n• it is better for the environment, e.g. less chemical run-off into rivers, more wildlife\n• concern for animal welfare (organic livestock standards)\n• they think it tastes better\n• they can afford the higher price/rising incomes.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 1: a table of the share of UK electricity generation by source (data written for this practice question).\nSource | 2012 (%) | 2022 (%)\nCoal | 39 | 2\nGas | 28 | 38\nNuclear | 19 | 15\nRenewables (wind, solar, hydro, bioenergy) | 11 | 40\nOther | 3 | 5\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the percentage change in the share of electricity generated by renewables between 2012 and 2022.\nShow your working. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: (40 − 11) ÷ 11 × 100\n1 mark for the answer: 263.6% (increase)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks. Accept +263.6%.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 2: a table of energy consumption per person in six countries (data written for this practice question).\nCountry | Energy consumption per person (gigajoules, GJ)\nCanada | 380\nUSA | 280\nChina | 115\nUK | 110\nIndia | 27\nNigeria | 9\n\nUsing Figure 2, calculate the median energy consumption per person.\nShow your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: values in order (9, 27, 110, 115, 280, 380) and the middle two identified/added: (110 + 115) ÷ 2\n1 mark for the answer: 112.5 GJ\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 6: a photograph taken in a rural area of the Sahel, Africa. Two girls are carrying large yellow plastic containers of water along a dusty track. A caption says: "Many girls here spend three hours a day collecting water instead of going to school."\n\nUsing Figure 6, outline one way that a lack of safe water affects social well-being.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid effect, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• girls miss school because they spend hours collecting water (1), so they gain fewer qualifications and are trapped in poverty (1)\n• unsafe water carries diseases such as cholera/diarrhoea (1), which causes illness and deaths, especially of young children (1)\n• time spent collecting water (1) reduces time to work or farm, lowering family income/food supply (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 7: a map of water supply and demand in the UK. Scotland, Wales, north-west England and the Lake District receive over 1,500 mm of rain a year and have low population densities; they are shaded "water surplus". London and south-east England receive under 700 mm a year and have population densities over 400 people per km²; they are shaded "water deficit" (serious water stress).\n\nUsing Figure 7, outline one reason why south-east England has a water deficit.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, +1 for development or use of Figure 7, e.g.:\n• low rainfall — under 700 mm a year (1), so rivers and aquifers are refilled slowly (1)\n• high population density — over 400 people per km² (1), so demand for water from homes and businesses is very high (1)\n• demand from intensive farming/irrigation in the south-east (1) uses water in the driest months (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nThe Environment Agency monitors the quality of rivers in England. In recent years only about 14% of England's rivers have been judged to be in good ecological health.\n\nOutline one way that water quality is managed in the UK.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a strategy, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• the Environment Agency tests rivers and fines companies that pollute (1), discouraging illegal discharges (1)\n• water companies treat sewage and drinking water (1), removing harmful bacteria and chemicals before water is supplied/returned to rivers (1)\n• Nitrate Vulnerable Zones limit when farmers can spread fertiliser (1), reducing run-off of nitrates into rivers (1)\n• water companies must invest in storm overflows/sewers (1) to reduce untreated sewage entering rivers (1)\n• buffer strips of vegetation along riverbanks (1) trap soil and chemicals before they reach the water (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 8: a table showing the number of times sewage was released from storm overflows into rivers and seas in England (rounded, data written for this practice question).\nYear | Number of releases\n2021 | 372,500\n2022 | 301,100\n2023 | 464,100\n\nUsing Figure 8, calculate the percentage increase in releases between 2022 and 2023.\nShow your working. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: (464,100 − 301,100) ÷ 301,100 × 100\n1 mark for the answer: 54.1%\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 3: a pie chart showing where the food eaten in the UK comes from (data written for this practice question). UK 60%; European Union 25%; Africa 4%; Asia 4%; South America 4%; North America 2%; rest of the world 1%.\n\nOutline one reason why the UK imports so much of its food.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• the UK climate is too cool for some crops (1), so tropical foods such as bananas, coffee and rice must be imported (1)\n• consumers want seasonal foods all year round (1), e.g. strawberries in winter grown in Spain or Morocco (1)\n• food can be produced more cheaply abroad (1) because of lower labour costs, so supermarkets import it (1)\n• a more ethnically diverse population (1) increases demand for a wider range of foods from around the world (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 9: a table showing where strawberries sold by a UK supermarket come from in different months (data written for this practice question).\nMonths | Main source | % of strawberries grown in the UK\nJanuary–March | Spain, Egypt, Morocco | 0\nApril–May | Spain, UK | 40\nJune–August | UK | 100\nSeptember–October | UK, Netherlands | 75\nNovember–December | Egypt, Morocco | 0\n\nUsing Figure 9, describe how the source of strawberries changes during the year.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point, or 1 for a point plus 1 for use of data, e.g.:\n• all strawberries are UK-grown in summer (June–August, 100%) (1)\n• in winter (November–March) none are UK-grown; they are imported from Spain, Egypt and Morocco (1)\n• spring and autumn are transition periods with a mix of UK and imported fruit (40% and 75%) (1).\nMaximum 1 mark if no data or months used.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 10: a photograph of farmland in the Lincolnshire Fens. A huge flat field of lettuces stretches to the horizon with no hedgerows. Large tractors and a packing lorry with the logo of a food-processing company stand next to rows of irrigation pipes.\n\nUsing Figure 10, outline one characteristic of agribusiness.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a characteristic, +1 for development or use of Figure 10, e.g.:\n• very large fields with hedgerows removed (1) so large machines can be used efficiently (1)\n• high use of machinery/technology such as irrigation (1), replacing farm workers and raising yields (1)\n• the farm is run like a big business linked to food processing and supermarkets (1), e.g. the food-processing lorry shows produce goes straight for packing (1)\n• monoculture — one crop grown over a large area (1) for economies of scale (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nOil and gas have been extracted from the North Sea off the coast of Scotland since the 1970s, from platforms up to 200 km offshore.\n\nOutline one environmental issue of exploiting oil and gas from the North Sea.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for an issue, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• risk of oil spills from platforms or pipelines (1), which coat seabirds and harm marine ecosystems (1)\n• burning the oil and gas releases carbon dioxide (1), contributing to the enhanced greenhouse effect and climate change (1)\n• decommissioning old platforms (1) can leave waste/toxic material on the sea bed (1)\n• noise from seismic surveys and drilling (1) disturbs marine mammals such as whales and dolphins (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 11: a line graph of UK North Sea oil production, 1985–2022 (data written for this practice question). Production was 128 million tonnes in 1985, rose to a peak of 137 million tonnes in 1999, then fell to 85 million tonnes in 2005, 63 million tonnes in 2010, 45 million tonnes in 2015 and 37 million tonnes in 2022.\n\nUsing Figure 11, describe the trend in UK North Sea oil production between 1985 and 2022.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for the overall trend, +1 for use of data, e.g.:\n• production rose slightly to a peak in 1999 (137 million tonnes) (1), then declined steadily to 37 million tonnes by 2022 (1)\n• overall production fell by about 73% from the 1999 peak (1)\nMaximum 1 mark without data.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nThe Dogger Bank Wind Farm is being built in the North Sea, over 130 km off the coast of north-east England. When complete it is planned to be the largest offshore wind farm in the world.\n\nOutline one economic issue of developing offshore wind energy in the UK.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for an issue, +1 for development, e.g.:\n• very high construction costs (1) because turbines must be built far out at sea and connected by undersea cables (1)\n• creates jobs in manufacturing, construction and maintenance (1), e.g. in ports such as Hull and Teesside (1)\n• wind is intermittent (1), so back-up electricity (e.g. gas) is still needed, adding to costs (1)\n• reduces reliance on imported gas (1), protecting the UK from rises in world energy prices (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nYou have carried out fieldwork in a supermarket to investigate food miles. You recorded the country of origin of 40 fresh fruit and vegetable products and calculated the distance each had travelled to the UK.\n\nSuggest one appropriate way to present your food miles data. Justify your choice.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for an appropriate technique, +1 for justification, e.g.:\n• a flow-line map with lines from each country to the UK (1), with line width proportional to the number of products, showing where food comes from and how far (1)\n• a bar graph/histogram of products grouped by distance travelled (1), making it easy to compare how many travelled short and long distances (1)\n• a choropleth/located proportional-symbols map of countries (1) to show the pattern of sources across the world (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 1: a table of the share of UK electricity generation by source (data written for this practice question).\nSource | 2012 (%) | 2022 (%)\nCoal | 39 | 2\nGas | 28 | 38\nNuclear | 19 | 15\nRenewables (wind, solar, hydro, bioenergy) | 11 | 40\nOther | 3 | 5\n\nExplain why the use of coal to generate electricity in the UK has declined.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation with developed reasons and links.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple reasons with little development.\nIndicative content:\n• coal is the most polluting fuel; the UK has legally binding targets to cut carbon emissions (Climate Change Act 2008, net zero by 2050), so coal power stations were phased out — the last, Ratcliffe-on-Soar, closed in 2024\n• carbon pricing/taxes made coal electricity more expensive than gas\n• most UK deep coal mines had closed because reserves were difficult and expensive to extract, so coal had to be imported\n• gas from the North Sea and imports was cheaper and cleaner\n• falling costs and government subsidies made renewables such as wind competitive\n• coal burning also causes acid rain and air pollution, harming health.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nThe average person in the UK uses about 140 litres of water a day. Water companies forecast that demand will continue to increase.\n\nExplain why the demand for water in the UK is increasing.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation with developed reasons.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple reasons.\nIndicative content:\n• population growth, especially in London and the south-east, increases total demand\n• more, smaller households (more people living alone) use more water per person\n• rising living standards — more showers, dishwashers, washing machines, garden watering, swimming pools\n• industry and energy production need large amounts of water, e.g. cooling in power stations\n• farmers need more water for irrigation, especially for high-value crops in East Anglia\n• climate change is bringing hotter, drier summers, increasing demand when supply is lowest.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 12: a photograph of a river flowing through farmland in Herefordshire. The water is green and cloudy with thick algae near the banks. Fields of crops and poultry sheds slope down to the river with no hedges or vegetation along the riverbank.\n\nUsing Figure 12, explain how farming can reduce water quality in UK rivers.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of processes, with use of Figure 12.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points about pollution.\nIndicative content:\n• fertilisers (nitrates and phosphates) wash off fields into the river when it rains; this causes eutrophication — algae grow rapidly (the green water in Figure 12), use up oxygen when they decay and fish die\n• manure from poultry sheds is rich in phosphates and can run off into the river\n• pesticides washed into rivers poison insects and fish\n• no vegetation along the riverbank means soil is eroded into the river, making the water cloudy and smothering habitats\n• polluted water needs more expensive treatment before it can be used for drinking.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 9: a table showing where strawberries sold by a UK supermarket come from in different months (data written for this practice question).\nMonths | Main source | % of strawberries grown in the UK\nJanuary–March | Spain, Egypt, Morocco | 0\nApril–May | Spain, UK | 40\nJune–August | UK | 100\nSeptember–October | UK, Netherlands | 75\nNovember–December | Egypt, Morocco | 0\n\nExplain why there is a growing demand in the UK for food out of season.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation with developed reasons.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple reasons.\nIndicative content:\n• rising incomes mean people can afford to buy high-value foods such as berries and asparagus all year round\n• supermarkets have global supply chains and stock imported fresh food all year, creating demand\n• cheap air freight and refrigerated transport allow perishable foods to reach the UK quickly\n• consumers are more health-conscious and want fresh fruit and vegetables every day\n• travel abroad and a more diverse population have increased demand for exotic and varied foods\n• growing seasons in countries such as Spain, Morocco and Kenya are opposite to/longer than the UK's.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nIn 2022 fossil fuels (oil, gas and coal) still supplied around three-quarters of the UK's total energy needs, including transport fuel and heating.\n\nExplain why the UK still relies on fossil fuels for much of its energy.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation with developed reasons.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple reasons.\nIndicative content:\n• most homes are heated by gas boilers and replacing them with heat pumps is expensive\n• most vehicles still use petrol or diesel; electric vehicles are only a small share of cars\n• gas power stations provide reliable electricity when wind and solar output is low (intermittency)\n• existing infrastructure (gas pipelines, refineries, power stations) is already built\n• the UK still has North Sea oil and gas reserves, providing energy security, jobs and tax revenue\n• industry such as chemicals and steel needs high temperatures that are hard to supply from renewables.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 7: a map of water supply and demand in the UK. Scotland, Wales, north-west England and the Lake District receive over 1,500 mm of rain a year and have low population densities; they are shaded "water surplus". London and south-east England receive under 700 mm a year and have population densities over 400 people per km²; they are shaded "water deficit" (serious water stress). Arrows show existing transfers, such as from the Elan Valley in Wales to Birmingham and from Kielder Water in Northumberland to the rivers Wear and Tees.\n\nUsing Figure 7, explain why water transfer schemes are used in the UK.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation using Figure 7.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points.\nIndicative content:\n• there is a mismatch between supply and demand: most rain falls in the north and west (over 1,500 mm) where few people live, while demand is highest in the south-east (over 400 people per km², under 700 mm rain)\n• areas of surplus have upland valleys suitable for reservoirs, impermeable rock and high rainfall\n• transfers move water from surplus areas to deficit areas through pipelines, aqueducts and rivers, e.g. Elan Valley to Birmingham\n• this provides a reliable supply for homes and industry in cities and reduces the risk of hosepipe bans and drought\n• demand in the south-east is forecast to grow with population and climate change.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nAbout 2.2 billion people in the world do not have access to safely managed drinking water, and around 700 million people do not have electricity.\n\nExplain why a reliable supply of energy is important for economic and social well-being.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation covering economic and/or social links.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points.\nIndicative content:\n• economic: energy powers factories, machinery and transport, allowing industry to grow and create jobs and income\n• economic: energy for irrigation pumps and food processing increases farm output\n• social: electric lighting allows children to study after dark, improving education\n• social: hospitals and clinics need electricity for equipment and refrigerating vaccines, improving health\n• social: clean cooking fuels replace wood/charcoal fires, reducing indoor air pollution and time spent collecting fuel\n• a lack of energy keeps people in poverty and widens global inequality.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 4: a table comparing the carbon footprint of some foods sold in a UK supermarket in December (data written for this practice question).\nFood | Where grown | Transport to UK shop | Distance (km) | Carbon footprint (kg CO₂e per kg of food)\nGreen beans | Kenya | air freight | 6,800 | 12.0\nGreen beans | Lincolnshire, UK (summer crop, frozen) | lorry | 150 | 0.4\nTomatoes | Somerset, UK (heated greenhouse) | lorry | 200 | 2.8\nTomatoes | southern Spain (unheated polytunnel) | lorry | 2,000 | 1.0\n\nExplain how the carbon footprint of the food eaten in the UK could be reduced.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of strategies with reasons, may use Figure 4.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple strategies.\nIndicative content:\n• buy seasonal food — Figure 4 shows UK tomatoes from heated greenhouses in winter have a higher footprint (2.8) than Spanish tomatoes (1.0), so the energy used to grow food matters, not only distance\n• buy local food to reduce food miles and fuel burnt in transport\n• avoid air-freighted food — Kenyan beans have 30 times the footprint of UK beans\n• grow your own food, e.g. allotments\n• eat less meat, especially beef and lamb, which have high emissions from livestock\n• reduce food waste so less energy is used producing food that is thrown away\n• farmers and greenhouses can use renewable energy.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 13: a world map of water stress. Most of North Africa, the Middle East and parts of South Asia are shaded "extremely high water stress". Most of northern Europe, Canada, Russia and the Amazon Basin are shaded "low water stress". A second map shows calorie intake per person: over 3,400 kcal per day in North America and Europe, under 2,200 kcal per day in parts of sub-Saharan Africa.\n\nUsing Figure 13 and your own understanding, discuss why there are global inequalities in the supply and consumption of resources.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: thorough discussion of reasons for inequality in both supply and consumption, using Figure 13 and own understanding; well-developed links.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear discussion of some reasons; some use of Figure 13.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points; limited use of Figure 13.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• supply: physical factors — climate and rainfall (deserts of North Africa/the Middle East have low rainfall and high evaporation); soils and growing seasons; geology determines where fossil fuels are found\n• supply: human factors — wealth and technology to extract, store and treat resources (dams, desalination, pipelines), conflict and poor infrastructure\n• consumption: HICs consume far more food, water and energy per person due to higher incomes, industry, car ownership and lifestyles (over 3,400 kcal per day)\n• LICs consume less because of poverty, poor access and population growth outstripping supply (under 2,200 kcal per day)\n• NEEs such as China and India are rapidly increasing consumption as they industrialise\n• trade allows wealthy countries to import resources, so consumption does not match local supply.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 6: a photograph taken in a rural area of the Sahel, Africa. Two girls are carrying large yellow plastic containers of water along a dusty track. A caption says: "Many girls here spend three hours a day collecting water instead of going to school."\n\nAssess the importance of food, water and energy to economic and social well-being. Use Figure 6 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of the importance of all three resources to economic and social well-being; uses Figure 6; reaches a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of the importance of some resources; some assessment.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple statements about the importance of resources.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• food: a balanced diet is needed for health and energy to work; malnutrition causes stunting, illness and reduced productivity\n• water: safe water prevents disease (cholera, diarrhoea); water is needed for farming and industry; Figure 6 shows time collecting water stops girls attending school\n• energy: needed for industry, transport and jobs; electricity improves education, health care and communications\n• the resources are interlinked — water is needed to grow food and generate energy; energy is needed to pump and treat water\n• judgement may consider which is most fundamental (e.g. water, as life and food production depend on it) or that all three must be secure together.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 14: information about the Elan Valley water transfer scheme. Reservoirs in the Elan Valley, mid-Wales, were opened in 1904. Water flows 118 km to Birmingham by gravity along the Elan Aqueduct, with no pumping needed. The scheme supplies around 300 million litres of water a day. About 100 people were moved from their homes when the valley was flooded. Today the reservoirs attract visitors for walking, cycling and birdwatching.\n\nAssess the advantages and disadvantages of water transfer schemes in the UK. Use Figure 14 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed, balanced assessment of advantages and disadvantages; uses Figure 14; reaches a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of advantages and/or disadvantages; some use of Figure 14.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\nAdvantages:\n• provides a reliable supply to areas of deficit, e.g. 300 million litres a day to Birmingham\n• gravity flow means low running costs and low energy use\n• reservoirs bring tourism and recreation income, and can generate hydroelectric power\nDisadvantages:\n• flooding valleys destroys homes, farmland and habitats (about 100 people moved)\n• very high construction costs; pipelines and pumping for other schemes use energy\n• water from one river can harm ecosystems in another (different temperature/chemistry, spread of species)\n• political issues, e.g. Welsh water supplying English cities\n• alternatives such as reducing leakage, water meters and desalination may be more sustainable.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 4: a table comparing the carbon footprint of some foods sold in a UK supermarket in December (data written for this practice question).\nFood | Where grown | Transport to UK shop | Distance (km) | Carbon footprint (kg CO₂e per kg of food)\nGreen beans | Kenya | air freight | 6,800 | 12.0\nGreen beans | Lincolnshire, UK (summer crop, frozen) | lorry | 150 | 0.4\nTomatoes | Somerset, UK (heated greenhouse) | lorry | 200 | 2.8\nTomatoes | southern Spain (unheated polytunnel) | lorry | 2,000 | 1.0\n\n'Buying food grown in the UK is always better for the environment than buying imported food.'\nDo you agree? Use Figure 4 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed evaluation of both sides; well-supported use of Figure 4; clear, justified conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear points for and/or against; some use of Figure 4; may have a simple conclusion.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points; little use of Figure 4.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\nAgree:\n• fewer food miles means less fuel burnt and less CO₂ — UK beans (0.4) compared with Kenyan air-freighted beans (12.0), 30 times higher\n• local food supports UK farmers and needs less packaging and refrigeration\nDisagree:\n• UK tomatoes grown in heated greenhouses in December (2.8) have nearly three times the footprint of Spanish tomatoes (1.0) despite travelling a tenth of the distance\n• transport mode matters more than distance — sea and road freight produce far less CO₂ than air\n• intensive UK farming can pollute rivers and remove hedgerows\n• buying from countries such as Kenya supports jobs and development there\nA conclusion might be that it depends on season and transport method — buying seasonal food is more important than buying local.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 10: a photograph of farmland in the Lincolnshire Fens. A huge flat field of lettuces stretches to the horizon with no hedgerows. Large tractors and a packing lorry with the logo of a food-processing company stand next to rows of irrigation pipes.\n\nAssess the impacts of the growth of agribusiness on the UK. Use Figure 10 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of positive and negative impacts (economic, social, environmental); uses Figure 10; reaches a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some impacts; some use of Figure 10.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\nPositive:\n• high yields and economies of scale produce cheap food for consumers and reduce reliance on imports\n• links to processing companies create jobs in packing and transport\n• efficient use of technology and irrigation\nNegative:\n• removal of hedgerows reduces wildlife habitats and increases soil erosion by wind on flat land such as the Fens\n• heavy use of fertilisers and pesticides pollutes rivers and causes eutrophication\n• irrigation adds to water demand in the driest part of the UK\n• machinery replaces farm workers; small family farms cannot compete\n• supermarkets' buying power can push down prices paid to farmers.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 15: two opinions about shale gas (fracking) in the UK.\nEnergy company spokesperson: "Shale gas under northern England could provide gas for decades, create thousands of jobs and reduce our reliance on imported gas."\nResident near Preston, Lancashire: "Fracking at a site near here caused small earthquakes in 2018 and 2019. It could pollute groundwater and keeps us burning fossil fuels."\n\n'The UK should allow fracking for shale gas.' Do you agree? Use Figure 15 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed evaluation of economic and environmental arguments on both sides; well-supported use of Figure 15; clear, justified conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear points for and/or against; some use of Figure 15.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\nFor:\n• energy security — less reliance on imported gas as North Sea production falls\n• jobs and investment in areas such as Lancashire; tax revenue\n• gas produces less CO₂ than coal and can back up renewables\nAgainst:\n• earth tremors at Preston New Road led the government to pause fracking in England in 2019\n• risk of groundwater contamination from fracking fluids; large amounts of water needed\n• lorry traffic, noise and visual impact for local communities\n• methane leaks and continued fossil-fuel use conflict with net-zero targets\n• investment could go to renewables instead\nCredit any justified conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 1: a table of the share of UK electricity generation by source (data written for this practice question).\nSource | 2012 (%) | 2022 (%)\nCoal | 39 | 2\nGas | 28 | 38\nNuclear | 19 | 15\nRenewables (wind, solar, hydro, bioenergy) | 11 | 40\nOther | 3 | 5\n\nUsing Figure 1 and your own understanding, explain how and why the UK's energy mix is changing.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed explanation of how and why the mix is changing; accurate use of Figure 1 data; well-developed reasons.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear description of changes with some explanation.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple description or reasons.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\nHow:\n• coal has almost disappeared (39% to 2%); renewables have grown from 11% to 40%; gas increased; nuclear declined slightly\nWhy:\n• climate change targets (net zero by 2050) and carbon taxes made coal uneconomic\n• falling cost of wind and solar; subsidies and auctions; large offshore wind farms such as Hornsea and Dogger Bank\n• North Sea oil and gas production is declining, so the UK increasingly imports gas, raising energy security concerns\n• ageing nuclear power stations are closing; new ones such as Hinkley Point C take many years to build\n• public concern about air pollution and the environment.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 8: a table showing the number of times sewage was released from storm overflows into rivers and seas in England (rounded, data written for this practice question).\nYear | Number of releases\n2021 | 372,500\n2022 | 301,100\n2023 | 464,100\n\nAssess how effectively water quality and pollution are being managed in the UK. Use Figure 8 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of strategies and their effectiveness; uses Figure 8; reaches a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some strategies with some assessment.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points about pollution or management.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• strategies: Environment Agency monitoring, fines and prosecutions; water treatment works; Nitrate Vulnerable Zones; buffer strips; water company investment in sewers and storm tanks (e.g. the Thames Tideway Tunnel in London)\n• effective: drinking water in the UK is of very high quality; some rivers such as the Thames have recovered since the 1950s, with fish and seals returning\n• less effective: Figure 8 shows releases rose by about 54% from 2022 to 2023; old Victorian combined sewers overflow in heavy rain; farm run-off still causes eutrophication; few rivers are in good ecological health\n• judgement — e.g. drinking water is well managed but river and coastal water quality is not.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 16: information about Hinkley Point C nuclear power station, being built on the Somerset coast next to the older Hinkley Point B. It will have two reactors with a capacity of 3.2 gigawatts, enough to supply about 7% of the UK's electricity. Thousands of people have worked on the construction site. Its completion date has been delayed and its cost has risen by billions of pounds. A guaranteed price for its electricity was agreed with the government.\n\nAssess the economic and environmental issues of developing nuclear power in the UK. Use Figure 16 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: detailed assessment of both economic and environmental issues; uses Figure 16; reaches a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: clear explanation of some issues.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: simple points.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\nEconomic:\n• thousands of construction jobs and contracts for local businesses in Somerset\n• very high and rising costs, delays; the guaranteed price may mean higher bills for consumers\n• reliable baseload electricity (7% of UK supply) improves energy security and reduces gas imports\n• decommissioning costs at the end of its life are very high\nEnvironmental:\n• low-carbon electricity helps meet net-zero targets\n• radioactive waste must be stored safely for thousands of years\n• risk of accidents, although rare\n• construction affects coastal habitats; cooling water intakes can kill fish in the Severn Estuary\nCredit a judgement that weighs low carbon/reliability against cost and waste.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 17: a table of UK electricity generation from renewable sources (terawatt hours, TWh) (data written for this practice question).\nYear | Wind | Solar | Bioenergy | Hydro | Total renewables\n2010 | 10 | 0 | 12 | 4 | 26\n2016 | 37 | 10 | 30 | 5 | 82\n2022 | 80 | 13 | 34 | 6 | 133\nA note under the table states: "On calm, cloudy winter evenings, wind and solar can supply less than 10% of UK electricity demand."\n\n'Renewable energy can meet the UK's future demand for energy.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use Figure 17 and your own understanding.\n(9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG)`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: thorough evaluation of the potential and limitations of renewable energy; detailed, accurate use of Figure 17 and own understanding; substantiated conclusion about the extent of agreement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear explanation of points for and/or against; some use of Figure 17; a conclusion that is partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: simple points; limited use of Figure 17; little or no judgement.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\nAgree:\n• renewable output has risen about fivefold, from 26 TWh in 2010 to 133 TWh in 2022, led by wind (10 to 80 TWh)\n• the UK has excellent wind resources, especially offshore (shallow North Sea, e.g. Dogger Bank, Hornsea); also tidal and wave potential\n• costs of wind and solar have fallen; government targets support growth\n• renewables are low carbon and reduce reliance on imported gas\nDisagree:\n• intermittency — calm, cloudy winter evenings (under 10% of demand) need back-up from gas, nuclear or storage\n• demand will rise as transport (electric vehicles) and heating (heat pumps) are electrified\n• energy storage (batteries, pumped storage such as Dinorwig) and grid upgrades are expensive\n• opposition to onshore wind and solar farms on visual or land-use grounds; bioenergy may rely on imported wood pellets\n• renewables currently supply electricity, but oil and gas still dominate transport and heating\nConclusion may argue renewables can meet much but not all demand without nuclear, storage and reduced demand.\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance (3): spelling and punctuation with consistent accuracy; rules of grammar used with effective control of meaning; wide range of specialist terms used adeptly and with precision.\nIntermediate performance (2): spelling and punctuation with considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms used appropriately.\nThreshold performance (1): spelling and punctuation with reasonable accuracy; reasonable control of meaning; limited range of specialist terms.\nNo marks (0): no relevant response or below threshold.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 3: a pie chart showing where the food eaten in the UK comes from (data written for this practice question). UK 60%; European Union 25%; Africa 4%; Asia 4%; South America 4%; North America 2%; rest of the world 1%.\nA newspaper headline reads: "Organic, local and seasonal — shoppers turn away from air-freighted food."\n\n'The UK's changing demand for food has created more problems than opportunities.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use Figure 3 and your own understanding.\n(9 marks)`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: thorough evaluation of problems and opportunities arising from the UK's changing demand for food (imports, high-value/out-of-season foods, organic, local and seasonal food, agribusiness); detailed use of Figure 3; substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear explanation of some problems and/or opportunities; some use of Figure 3; partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: simple points; little judgement.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\nProblems:\n• around 40% of food is imported, increasing food miles and the carbon footprint of the UK diet\n• dependence on imports reduces food security (e.g. price rises, poor harvests, supply disruption)\n• air-freighted high-value foods have a very high carbon footprint\n• growth of agribusiness to produce cheap food harms wildlife, soils and rivers\n• exporting countries may use scarce water and land to grow food for UK consumers\nOpportunities:\n• year-round choice of fresh, healthy food for consumers\n• trade supports jobs and incomes in LICs and NEEs, e.g. Kenyan horticulture\n• growing demand for organic, local and seasonal food creates markets for farm shops, farmers' markets and organic farmers and reduces chemical use\n• agribusiness keeps food affordable and increases UK production\nConclusion should weigh environmental costs against economic and social benefits.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 7: a map of water supply and demand in the UK. Scotland, Wales, north-west England and the Lake District receive over 1,500 mm of rain a year and have low population densities; they are shaded "water surplus". London and south-east England receive under 700 mm a year and have population densities over 400 people per km²; they are shaded "water deficit" (serious water stress). Arrows show existing transfers, such as from the Elan Valley in Wales to Birmingham and from Kielder Water in Northumberland to the rivers Wear and Tees.\nA water company report states: "About one-fifth of the water we put into our pipes is lost through leaks."\n\n'Water transfer schemes are the best way to meet the UK's water deficit.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use Figure 7 and your own understanding.\n(9 marks)`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: thorough evaluation of water transfer against other approaches; detailed use of Figure 7 and own understanding (e.g. Elan Valley, Kielder); substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear explanation of points for and/or against; some use of Figure 7; partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: simple points; little judgement.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\nFor transfer:\n• moves water from surplus areas (over 1,500 mm rain, low density) to deficit areas (under 700 mm, over 400 people per km²)\n• schemes such as the Elan Valley (gravity-fed to Birmingham) and Kielder have supplied water reliably for decades\n• can supply large volumes; Kielder was built with spare capacity\nAgainst / alternatives:\n• high construction costs; pumping uses energy; flooding valleys displaces people and habitats\n• transfers can harm river ecosystems and cause political disagreement between regions\n• reducing leakage (about one-fifth of water lost) could save large volumes more cheaply\n• water meters, efficient appliances, recycling grey water and public awareness campaigns reduce demand\n• desalination (e.g. the Beckton plant in London) and new reservoirs in the south-east increase local supply\nConclusion may argue transfers are useful but managing demand and fixing leaks is more sustainable.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management.\nFigure 11: a line graph of UK North Sea oil production, 1985–2022 (data written for this practice question). Production was 128 million tonnes in 1985, rose to a peak of 137 million tonnes in 1999, then fell to 85 million tonnes in 2005, 63 million tonnes in 2010, 45 million tonnes in 2015 and 37 million tonnes in 2022.\nFigure 18: a photograph of a line of onshore wind turbines on a moorland ridge in the Scottish Borders, with a farmhouse in the valley below.\n\nEvaluate the economic and environmental issues associated with the exploitation of energy sources in the UK. Use Figures 11 and 18 and your own understanding.\n(9 marks)`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: thorough evaluation of economic and environmental issues for both fossil fuels and renewable/nuclear sources; detailed use of Figures 11 and 18; substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: clear explanation of some economic and/or environmental issues; some use of the figures; partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: simple points; little judgement.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\nFossil fuels (Figure 11):\n• economic — North Sea oil and gas have provided jobs (e.g. Aberdeen) and tax revenue, but production has fallen by about 73% since the 1999 peak, so jobs are being lost and imports are rising\n• environmental — burning fossil fuels releases CO₂; risk of oil spills; decommissioning platforms; fracking causes tremors and groundwater concerns\nRenewables (Figure 18):\n• economic — wind is cheap to run once built and creates jobs in manufacturing and maintenance, but has high set-up costs and needs back-up; landowners and communities may receive payments\n• environmental — low carbon, but turbines change the landscape (visual pollution on moorland), noise for nearby homes and risk to birds; access roads damage peat\n• nuclear — low carbon and reliable but costly and produces radioactive waste\nConclusion may weigh short-term economic benefits of fossil fuels against long-term environmental costs, and the trade-offs of renewables.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.5': {
    name: 'Food',
    questions: [
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 1 (data written for this practice question): average daily calorie intake per person, 2020. USA 3,780 kcal; UK 3,400 kcal; Brazil 3,250 kcal; India 2,580 kcal; Ethiopia 2,330 kcal; Madagascar 2,050 kcal. The NHS recommends about 2,500 kcal a day for an adult man and 2,000 kcal for an adult woman.\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the range in daily calorie intake. Give your answer in kcal.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark (AO4).\n• 1,730 kcal (3,780 − 2,050) (1).\nNo other answer is acceptable.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 2: a choropleth world map of average daily calorie intake per person. The darkest shading (more than 3,200 kcal) covers North America, most of Europe, Russia, Australia and parts of South America. The lightest shading (less than 2,500 kcal) is concentrated in sub-Saharan Africa, with smaller areas in South Asia (e.g. Afghanistan) and Haiti and Bolivia in the Americas.\n\nUsing Figure 2, name the continent with the largest number of countries with a daily calorie intake below 2,500 kcal.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `1 mark (AO4).\n• Africa (1).\nAllow sub-Saharan Africa.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nIn 1980 the average person in China ate about 15 kg of meat a year. By 2020 this had risen to over 60 kg a year as incomes rose rapidly.\n\nGive one reason why economic development leads to an increase in food consumption.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark (AO1). One from:\n• People have higher disposable incomes so can afford more food / more expensive food (1).\n• Diets change to include more meat and dairy, which need more crops to feed animals (1).\n• Growth of supermarkets, fast food and processed food makes food more available all year round (1).\n• People demand a wider variety of imported and out-of-season food (1).\nDo not accept 'population growth' — it is not a result of economic development.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 3: a description of Thanet Earth, Kent. Seven large glasshouses grow tomatoes, peppers and cucumbers in rows of troughs with no soil. The roof of each glasshouse collects rainwater, which is stored in on-site reservoirs. An on-site power plant burns natural gas to make electricity, heat and carbon dioxide. Bumblebee hives sit between the rows of plants.\n\nUsing Figure 3, identify one way in which Thanet Earth reduces its use of resources.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark (AO4). One from:\n• Rainwater is collected from the roofs and stored in reservoirs (so less mains/river water is used) (1).\n• The power plant's heat and CO₂ are reused in the glasshouses (combined heat and power) (1).\n• Crops are grown without soil (hydroponics), so water and nutrients are recycled (1).\n• Bumblebees pollinate the plants instead of using labour or machinery (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 4 (data written for this practice question): a student's fieldwork survey of 'food miles' in a UK supermarket. Distance travelled by eight products (km): carrots 120; green beans (Kenya) 9,600; tomatoes (Spain) 1,750; oranges (Morocco) 2,100; apples 85; lamb (New Zealand) 10,800; potatoes 350; mangoes (Peru) 6,900.\n\nUsing Figure 4, calculate the median distance travelled. Give your answer in km.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark (AO4).\n• 1,925 km (1).\nWorking: ordered values 85, 120, 350, 1,750, 2,100, 6,900, 9,600, 10,800; median = (1,750 + 2,100) ÷ 2 = 1,925.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nBetween 2019 and 2021 swarms of desert locusts spread across East Africa. Kenya had its worst outbreak for 70 years, and Ethiopia and Somalia their worst for 25 years.\n\nSuggest one reason why a locust swarm reduces food supply.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark (AO2). One from:\n• Locusts eat crops/pasture, destroying harvests (1).\n• They eat grazing land so livestock go hungry / die, reducing meat and milk (1).\n• Farmers lose seed for the next season, so future harvests fall (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 5 (data written for this practice question): global meat production was 180 million tonnes in 1990 and 337 million tonnes in 2020.\n\nUsing Figure 5, calculate the percentage increase in global meat production between 1990 and 2020. Show your working. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2 marks (AO4).\n• Correct working: (337 − 180) ÷ 180 × 100 (1).\n• 87.2% (1).\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks. Allow 87% for 1 mark only (not to one decimal place).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 2: a choropleth world map of average daily calorie intake per person. The darkest shading (more than 3,200 kcal) covers North America, most of Europe, Russia, Australia and parts of South America. The lightest shading (less than 2,500 kcal) is concentrated in sub-Saharan Africa, with smaller areas in South Asia (e.g. Afghanistan) and Haiti and Bolivia in the Americas.\n\nUsing Figure 2, describe the global distribution of areas of food deficit.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `2 marks (AO4). 1 mark per valid descriptive point, or 1 + 1 for a developed point.\n• Most areas of low calorie intake/food deficit are in sub-Saharan Africa (1).\n• Some are in South Asia, e.g. Afghanistan (1).\n• Smaller, isolated areas occur in the Americas, e.g. Haiti and Bolivia (1).\n• Mostly in LICs / the global south; very few in the northern hemisphere's HICs (1).\nUse of map evidence (named regions/countries) needed for 2 marks. Do not credit explanation.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nIn 2022 the Horn of Africa (Kenya, Ethiopia and Somalia) suffered a fifth failed rainy season in a row. Millions of livestock died and harvests failed.\n\nOutline one way that climate can affect food supply.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `2 marks (AO1). 1 mark for a simple point, 2 for a developed point.\n• Low or unreliable rainfall/drought (1) means crops wilt and fail and pasture dies, so less food is produced (1).\n• Extreme weather such as floods or tropical storms (1) destroys crops and washes away fertile soil (1).\n• Temperatures that are too high or too low / short growing season (1) limit which crops can grow and reduce yields (1).\nAllow positive effects, e.g. a long, warm growing season allows two harvests a year (1 + 1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 6: a photograph of a farm in Israel. Thin black plastic pipes run along the base of each row of crops, with small drips of water falling onto the soil next to each plant. The soil between the rows is dry.\n\nUsing Figure 6, outline one advantage of drip irrigation compared with flooding fields with water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `2 marks (AO3/AO4). 1 + 1 for a developed point.\n• Water goes straight to the roots of each plant (1) so less is lost by evaporation/run-off, saving water in a dry area (1).\n• The soil between the rows stays dry (1) so fewer weeds grow / less water is wasted (1).\n• Less waterlogging (1) reducing the risk of salinisation of the soil (1).\nMust link to Figure 6 or be a clear comparison with flood irrigation for 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nYemen has been affected by civil war since 2014. By 2023 more than 17 million people, over half the population, were food insecure.\n\nOutline one way that conflict reduces food supply.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `2 marks (AO1). 1 + 1 for a developed point.\n• Farmland is damaged or mined / farmers flee (1) so crops are not planted or harvested (1).\n• Ports, roads and markets are blocked or destroyed (1) so food imports and aid cannot reach people (1).\n• Food and seed stores, irrigation systems or livestock are looted or destroyed (1) so future production falls (1).\n• Money is spent on fighting rather than agriculture (1), so food prices rise beyond what people can afford (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 7: a photograph inside a hydroponic glasshouse. Lettuce plants sit in plastic channels. Their roots hang in a thin film of water that flows along the channel. A pipe labelled 'nutrient solution' feeds the channels, and there is no soil anywhere.\n\nUsing Figure 7, explain how hydroponics can increase food supply.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 marks (AO2/AO3). 1 + 1 for a developed point.\n• Plants get exactly the nutrients they need from the solution (1) so they grow faster and give higher yields (1).\n• No soil is needed (1) so food can be grown where soil is poor, or indoors/in cities (1).\n• Water is recycled through the channels (1) so it can be used in water-stressed areas (1).\n• Controlled indoor conditions (1) allow crops to be grown all year round / with fewer pests (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nIn Burkina Faso, farmers dig small 'zai' pits, about 20 cm deep, and fill them with manure before planting seeds. Lines of stones are laid along the contours of fields.\n\nOutline how this appropriate technology can increase food supply.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `2 marks (AO1/AO2). 1 + 1 for a developed point.\n• Zai pits collect and hold rainwater around the roots (1) so crops survive dry spells and yields increase (1).\n• The manure adds nutrients/organic matter (1) improving fertility of degraded soil (1).\n• Stone lines slow surface run-off (1) so more water infiltrates and soil is not washed away (1).\n• Cheap, uses local materials and skills (1) so poor farmers can afford it and maintain it themselves (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 8: a photograph from northern Ethiopia showing a hillside with no trees, bare dry soil and deep gullies cut into the slope. A herd of goats is grazing on the few remaining plants.\n\nUsing Figure 8, suggest how food insecurity can lead to soil erosion.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `2 marks (AO2/AO3). 1 + 1 for a developed point.\n• Hungry people overgraze land with goats/livestock (1) removing the vegetation that binds the soil, so rain washes it away forming gullies (1).\n• Trees are cut down for fuel or to clear more farmland (1) exposing the soil to wind and rain (1).\n• Land is overcultivated with no fallow period (1) so soil loses structure and nutrients and erodes more easily (1).\nReference to Figure 8 (goats, bare slopes, gullies) needed for 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 9: a label on a pack of fish in a UK supermarket. It shows a blue tick logo with the words 'Certified sustainable seafood — MSC' and 'Caught by line, North-East Atlantic'.\n\nOutline how buying fish like this can help move towards a sustainable food supply.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `2 marks (AO2). 1 + 1 for a developed point.\n• Certified fisheries follow catch limits/quotas (1) so fish stocks are not overfished and can recover for the future (1).\n• Line-caught fishing has less bycatch than trawling (1) so other species and the sea bed are less damaged (1).\n• Consumers' choices put pressure on retailers (1) so more fisheries adopt sustainable methods (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nAfter the collapse of the Soviet Union in the early 1990s, Cuba lost most of its imported fuel, fertiliser and food. In Havana, thousands of small organic gardens called 'organopónicos' were set up on empty land within the city.\n\nOutline one way that urban farming can make food supply more sustainable.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `2 marks (AO1/AO2). 1 + 1 for a developed point.\n• Food is grown close to where it is eaten (1) so food miles, transport fuel and emissions are reduced (1).\n• Vacant/derelict land is reused (1) so no extra countryside is cleared for farming (1).\n• Organic methods such as compost from city waste are used (1) so less chemical fertiliser is needed and waste is recycled (1).\n• Fresh, affordable food is available locally (1) improving food security for poorer urban residents (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nA UK supermarket sells strawberries all year round. In winter they are flown in from Egypt or Morocco; in June and July they come from farms in Kent.\n\nOutline how eating seasonal food can contribute to a sustainable food supply.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `2 marks (AO2). 1 + 1 for a developed point.\n• Seasonal food can be grown locally (1) so it travels fewer food miles / avoids air freight, reducing greenhouse gas emissions (1).\n• It does not need heated glasshouses out of season (1) so less energy is used (1).\n• It supports local farmers (1) keeping money in the local economy and reducing reliance on imports (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nGolden Rice has been genetically modified to contain beta-carotene, which the body converts to vitamin A. It was approved for growing in the Philippines in 2021.\n\nOutline one disadvantage of using biotechnology, such as GM crops, to increase food supply.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `2 marks (AO1). 1 + 1 for a developed point.\n• GM seed is expensive and often patented by large companies (1) so poorer farmers may get into debt / become dependent on buying new seed (1).\n• Concern that GM genes may spread to wild plants (1) reducing biodiversity / creating 'superweeds' (1).\n• Some consumers and countries do not trust GM food (1) so it may be hard to sell or export (1).\n• Growing one GM variety over large areas (monoculture) (1) makes crops vulnerable to a new pest or disease (1).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 10 (data written for this practice question): UK households threw away 6.6 million tonnes of food in one year. Of this, 4.5 million tonnes was food that could have been eaten.\n\nUsing Figure 10, calculate the percentage of household food waste that could have been eaten. Show your working. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2 marks (AO4).\n• Correct working: 4.5 ÷ 6.6 × 100 (1).\n• 68.2% (1).\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 11: a climate graph for Lodwar, north-west Kenya. Temperatures are 28–31 °C all year. Total annual rainfall is about 200 mm, falling mostly in April and November, with several months receiving less than 5 mm. An annotation reads: 'River Turkwel — flow falling as more water is taken upstream'.\n\nExplain how water stress can affect food supply. Use Figure 11 and your own understanding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `4 marks (AO2 2, AO3 2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of how water stress affects food supply, with developed chains of reasoning and good use of Figure 11.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; simple points that are not developed, or little use of Figure 11.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Very low rainfall (about 200 mm a year) and high temperatures (28–31 °C) mean high evaporation, so there is little soil moisture for crops.\n• Rain falls in only two short seasons, so crops can only be grown for part of the year; if one season fails, harvests fail.\n• Pasture dries up, so livestock die or produce less milk and meat — important where people are pastoralists.\n• Falling river flow means less water for irrigation; competition with upstream users can cause conflict.\n• Farmers may over-abstract groundwater, lowering water tables and causing salinisation, so land becomes less productive.\n• Lack of water raises food prices and increases reliance on food aid.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 12 (data written for this practice question): world population and world food consumption. World population rose from 5.3 billion in 1990 to 7.8 billion in 2020. Over the same period global meat consumption almost doubled and the number of people in middle-income households rose by about 2 billion, mainly in Asia.\n\nExplain why global food consumption is increasing. Use Figure 12 and your own understanding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `4 marks (AO1 2, AO3 2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of at least two reasons, both developed, using Figure 12.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; reasons identified but not developed.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Population growth — 2.5 billion more people between 1990 and 2020 means more mouths to feed, so total food demand rises.\n• Economic development — rising incomes in NEEs such as China and India mean people can afford more food and more varied diets.\n• Diets shift towards meat and dairy; it takes several kilograms of grain to produce 1 kg of beef, so demand for crops rises faster than population.\n• Urbanisation and supermarkets increase demand for processed food and imported, out-of-season produce.\n• Rising demand for biofuels competes with food crops (credit as a related point).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nIn Malawi, about 70% of people live below the international poverty line. Most are smallholder farmers who grow maize on less than one hectare of land.\n\nExplain how poverty can lead to food insecurity.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `4 marks (AO1 2, AO2 2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear, developed explanation showing how poverty limits both the production of food and access to it.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points, mostly undeveloped.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Poor farmers cannot afford fertiliser, improved seed, irrigation or machinery, so yields stay low.\n• Small plots produce little surplus, so families cannot store food for bad years or sell crops to buy other food.\n• When harvests fail, poor households cannot afford to buy food when prices rise — food is available in markets but not accessible.\n• Poverty forces families to sell livestock or eat seed stock, reducing future production (a cycle of poverty).\n• Poor countries have limited money for storage, roads and transport, so food is lost after harvest and cannot reach deficit areas.\n• Undernutrition reduces people's ability to work, keeping them poor.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 3: a description of Thanet Earth, Kent. Seven large glasshouses grow tomatoes, peppers and cucumbers in rows of troughs with no soil. The roof of each glasshouse collects rainwater, which is stored in on-site reservoirs. An on-site power plant burns natural gas to make electricity, heat and carbon dioxide. Bumblebee hives sit between the rows of plants.\n\nUsing Figure 3, explain the advantages of a large-scale agricultural development such as Thanet Earth.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `4 marks (AO2 2, AO3 2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of a range of advantages, developed and linked to evidence from Figure 3.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation of advantages with limited use of the figure.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Hydroponics and controlled heat and light give high yields of salad crops all year round, reducing UK reliance on imports.\n• Produce reaches UK supermarkets quickly, reducing food miles compared with imports from Spain or the Netherlands.\n• Rainwater harvesting reduces demand on local water supplies in the dry south-east of England.\n• Combined heat and power — heat and CO₂ from the power plant are reused to warm glasshouses and boost plant growth; surplus electricity can go to the National Grid.\n• Bumblebees for pollination and biological pest control reduce the need for pesticides.\n• Creates several hundred local jobs in Thanet, an area with relatively high unemployment.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nIn India, Bt cotton — genetically modified to resist bollworm — was first grown in 2002. Scientists are now developing drought-tolerant varieties of maize for farmers in sub-Saharan Africa.\n\nExplain how the new green revolution and biotechnology can increase food supply.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `4 marks (AO1 2, AO2 2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear, developed explanation of how biotechnology/the new green revolution increases food supply, with examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points with little development.\n\nIndicative content:\n• GM crops can be made resistant to pests (e.g. Bt cotton/Bt crops), so less of the harvest is lost and less pesticide is needed.\n• Drought- or salt-tolerant varieties allow crops to grow in water-stressed or marginal land, increasing the area farmed.\n• Higher-yielding varieties produce more food per hectare without clearing more land.\n• Biofortified crops (e.g. Golden Rice, vitamin A) tackle undernutrition, not just quantity of food.\n• The new green revolution aims to be more sustainable than the first — using biotechnology together with local knowledge and appropriate technology so poorer farmers benefit.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 13: a photograph of an organic farm in Devon. Hedgerows border small fields; one field contains clover, another has cattle grazing, and a sign at the gate reads 'Soil Association Certified Organic — no artificial fertilisers or pesticides'.\n\nUsing Figure 13, explain how organic farming can contribute to a sustainable food supply.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `4 marks (AO2 2, AO3 2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of how organic farming is sustainable, with developed points linked to Figure 13.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points with limited development or use of the figure.\n\nIndicative content:\n• No artificial fertilisers or pesticides, so rivers are not polluted by nitrate run-off and wildlife (e.g. bees) is protected.\n• Clover fixes nitrogen and crop rotation with grazing cattle (manure) keeps soil fertile naturally, so the land can be farmed in the long term.\n• Hedgerows provide habitats for predators of pests and reduce soil erosion by wind.\n• Less energy is used than in producing chemical fertilisers, reducing carbon emissions.\n• Organic food often sells for a higher price, supporting smaller farms; but yields can be lower (credit balanced comment).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 14: a sketch of a permaculture garden in Malawi. Fruit trees form a top layer; beneath them are banana plants, then beans, pumpkins and herbs. Swales (shallow ditches along the contour) catch rainwater, compost heaps sit beside the house, and chickens move between the plots.\n\nUsing Figure 14, explain how permaculture can increase sustainable food supply.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `4 marks (AO2 2, AO3 2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear, developed explanation using evidence from Figure 14.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points, little development or reference to the figure.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Layers of plants (trees, bananas, beans, pumpkins) make full use of space and light, so more food is produced from a small plot.\n• Mixing many crops (polyculture) gives a varied diet all year and reduces risk — if one crop fails, others survive.\n• Swales trap rainwater and increase infiltration, reducing the impact of dry spells and soil erosion.\n• Compost and chicken manure recycle nutrients so no bought fertiliser is needed; beans fix nitrogen.\n• Works with natural systems and local resources, so it can continue in the long term at low cost.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nIn 2007–08 world prices of wheat, rice and maize rose sharply. There were food riots in more than 30 countries, including Haiti and Egypt. Rising bread prices were also one cause of protests across North Africa in 2011.\n\nExplain why food insecurity can lead to rising prices and social unrest.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `4 marks (AO1 2, AO2 2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear, developed explanation linking food shortages to prices and to unrest.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points with limited links.\n\nIndicative content:\n• When supply falls below demand (e.g. after droughts or because of biofuel demand), food prices rise.\n• The poorest households spend a large share of their income on food, so price rises push them into hunger.\n• Countries that rely on imported grain (e.g. Egypt) are hit hard when world prices rise.\n• Anger at governments for failing to provide affordable food leads to protests, riots and in some cases political change.\n• Unrest disrupts farming and markets, making food insecurity worse (a cycle).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 1 (data written for this practice question): average daily calorie intake per person, 2020. USA 3,780 kcal; UK 3,400 kcal; Brazil 3,250 kcal; India 2,580 kcal; Ethiopia 2,330 kcal; Madagascar 2,050 kcal. The NHS recommends about 2,500 kcal a day for an adult man and 2,000 kcal for an adult woman.\n\nSuggest reasons for the differences in daily calorie intake shown in Figure 1. Use Figure 1 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `6 marks (AO2 3, AO3 3).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough, well-developed reasons for the differences, covering physical and human factors; accurate and detailed use of Figure 1.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear reasons with some development; some use of Figure 1.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic, undeveloped reasons; little or no use of Figure 1.\n\nIndicative content:\n• HICs (USA, UK) have intake well above recommended levels — high incomes, mechanised farming, ability to import food from around the world, processed and fast food.\n• NEEs (Brazil, India) — rising incomes and agricultural development (e.g. green revolution in India) have increased intake, but inequality means many still go hungry.\n• LICs (Ethiopia, Madagascar) are close to or below 2,500 kcal — poverty means people cannot afford enough food; farmers lack fertiliser, irrigation and technology.\n• Physical factors — drought, unreliable rainfall and soil erosion in Ethiopia; tropical cyclones in Madagascar destroy crops.\n• Conflict and political instability disrupt supply in some LICs.\n• Use of data, e.g. USA intake (3,780) is 1,730 kcal more than Madagascar (2,050).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 15: two descriptions of irrigation. A — The Indus valley, Pakistan: canals take water from the River Indus to irrigate about 18 million hectares of wheat, rice and cotton, much of which would be desert without it. B — The Aral Sea, Central Asia: water from the Amu Darya and Syr Darya rivers was diverted to grow cotton from the 1960s. The sea has since lost most of its area and the surrounding soils have become salty.\n\nAssess the effectiveness of irrigation as a strategy to increase food supply. Use Figure 15 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `6 marks (AO2 3, AO3 3).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough assessment weighing benefits against problems, with a clear, supported judgement and detailed use of Figure 15.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear assessment with some balance; some use of Figure 15; judgement may be implied.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points about irrigation; little assessment or use of the figure.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Effective — turns dry land into productive farmland (Indus: c. 18 million ha); allows more than one crop a year and more reliable yields in dry seasons.\n• Supports large populations and exports, and reduces risk from unreliable rainfall.\n• Less effective — can over-use rivers and groundwater, causing environmental disasters (Aral Sea shrinkage, collapse of fishing industry).\n• Salinisation and waterlogging reduce soil fertility over time, so yields eventually fall.\n• Large schemes are costly; can cause conflict between upstream and downstream users.\n• Effectiveness depends on method — drip irrigation is far more efficient than flood/canal irrigation.\n• Judgement, e.g. effective in the short term but only sustainable if water use is managed.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 16: a description of a vertical farm in Newark, New Jersey, USA. Leafy greens grow in stacked trays under LED lights in a former steel mill. Their roots hang in the air and are sprayed with a nutrient mist. The company claims it uses about 95% less water than field farming and needs no pesticides.\n\n'Hydroponics and aeroponics are the best way to increase food supply in the future.' Do you agree? Use Figure 16 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `6 marks (AO2 3, AO3 3).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough evaluation of the view with a well-supported conclusion; detailed use of Figure 16.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear evaluation with some balance and some use of Figure 16.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points; little evaluation.\n\nIndicative content:\n• For — very high yields per square metre by stacking trays; year-round production regardless of climate.\n• Uses far less water (claimed 95% less) — valuable where water stress limits farming.\n• No soil or pesticides; can be built in cities on derelict sites (former steel mill), cutting food miles.\n• Against — high energy use for LED lights and pumps; expensive to build, so unlikely in LICs where food insecurity is greatest.\n• Mainly suited to leafy greens and salad, not staple crops such as wheat, rice or maize that supply most calories.\n• Several vertical farms have struggled financially, so long-term viability is uncertain.\n• Other strategies — appropriate technology, irrigation, the new green revolution — may help more people.\n• Conclusion should be supported.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 17: a newspaper summary of East Africa, 2019–2022. 'Four rainy seasons failed in a row. Desert locusts destroyed crops and pasture. Conflict in parts of Somalia and Ethiopia stopped farmers reaching their fields. Food prices rose after 2022 because of a fall in grain exports from Ukraine.'\n\nUsing Figure 17, suggest how physical and human factors can combine to cause food insecurity.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `6 marks (AO2 3, AO3 3).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough suggestions showing clear links between physical and human factors; detailed use of Figure 17.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear suggestions covering both physical and human factors, with some links and use of Figure 17.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points about individual factors; little linkage.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Physical — repeated drought reduced crop yields and killed livestock; locust swarms destroyed what crops and pasture remained.\n• Human — conflict prevented planting and harvesting and blocked aid and markets.\n• Global trade — reduced grain exports from Ukraine raised import prices beyond the reach of poor households.\n• Poverty means families cannot store food or buy it when prices rise, so physical hazards have a larger impact.\n• Combined — drought plus conflict means people cannot migrate to find pasture safely; locust control is impossible in conflict zones.\n• Each factor makes the others worse, causing undernutrition and in places famine.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nUsing an example of a large-scale agricultural development, evaluate its advantages and disadvantages.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `6 marks (AO1 2, AO2 2, AO3 2).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed knowledge of the example; thorough evaluation of advantages and disadvantages with a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear knowledge of the example; some evaluation of both sides.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic, generic points; little place-specific detail or evaluation.\nMaximum Level 2 if no named example.\n\nIndicative content (Thanet Earth, Kent):\n• Advantages — high-yield hydroponic salad crops all year; reduced imports and food miles; rainwater harvesting; CHP plant reuses heat and CO₂ and exports electricity; biological pest control and bumblebee pollination; several hundred jobs in a deprived coastal area.\n• Disadvantages — built on greenfield farmland; large visual impact and light pollution at night; relies on natural gas, a fossil fuel; many jobs are low-paid/seasonal and some filled by migrant workers; produce goes to large supermarkets rather than local shops; small independent growers face competition.\n• Evaluation — e.g. economically successful and more efficient than imported produce, but not fully sustainable while dependent on gas.\nCredit other valid examples (e.g. large irrigation schemes, Almería plastic greenhouses in Spain).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nUsing an example of a local scheme in an LIC or NEE, explain how it has increased sustainable supplies of food.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `6 marks (AO1 3, AO2 3).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed, accurate knowledge of the named scheme; thorough explanation of how it increases food supply AND why it is sustainable.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear knowledge of the scheme; some explanation of food supply and/or sustainability.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic, generic points with little place detail.\nMaximum Level 2 if no named example.\n\nIndicative content (e.g. Makueni County, Kenya):\n• A semi-arid area with unreliable rainfall and frequent crop failure.\n• Sand dams built across seasonal rivers by local community groups store water in sand, reducing evaporation and providing water through the dry season.\n• Terracing reduces soil erosion and holds rainwater on slopes.\n• Drought-tolerant crops (e.g. sorghum, cowpeas, mango trees) and better seed give more reliable harvests.\n• Training in farming methods; surplus crops sold for income.\n• Sustainable because it uses local labour and materials, is cheap to maintain, works with the environment and is run by the community.\nCredit other valid schemes, e.g. zai pits and stone lines in Burkina Faso; agroforestry in Mali.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 18 (data written for this practice question): annual cost and number of farmers helped by three strategies in one district of Tanzania. Treadle pumps: £4,000, 400 farmers. Rainwater harvesting tanks: £9,000, 300 farmers. Diesel-powered irrigation scheme: £60,000, 250 farmers.\n\nAssess the extent to which appropriate technology can increase food supply in LICs. Use Figure 18 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `6 marks (AO2 3, AO3 3).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough assessment with a clear, supported judgement; detailed use of Figure 18 (including data manipulation).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear assessment with some balance and some use of Figure 18.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points; little assessment or use of data.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Treadle pumps help the most farmers (400) at the lowest cost — £10 per farmer, compared with £30 for tanks and £240 for the diesel scheme.\n• Appropriate technology is cheap, uses local materials and skills and is easy to repair, so it lasts and suits poor rural communities.\n• It does not depend on imported fuel, so running costs are low and it is more sustainable.\n• Increases yields by providing water in the dry season, allowing a second crop and a surplus to sell.\n• Limits — small scale; cannot overcome conflict, poverty or national-scale drought; hand-powered methods need labour; may not feed fast-growing urban populations.\n• Larger schemes or the new green revolution may be needed alongside it.\n• Judgement, e.g. effective at improving local food security but only part of the solution.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 19: three statements from UK residents. A: 'I buy MSC-certified fish and eat meat only twice a week.' B: 'I plan my meals so I throw away hardly any food.' C: 'I buy fruit and vegetables from a local farm shop, only when they are in season.'\n\nDiscuss how individuals can help move towards a sustainable food supply. Use Figure 19 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `6 marks (AO2 3, AO3 3).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough discussion of a range of actions, with developed explanation of why each is sustainable and some evaluation of limits; detailed use of Figure 19.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear discussion of some actions with some development and use of Figure 19.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points, little development.\n\nIndicative content:\n• A — fish from sustainable sources protects stocks for the future; eating less meat reduces land, water and grain used to feed livestock and cuts methane emissions.\n• B — reducing food waste means less food has to be grown, transported and sent to landfill, where it produces methane.\n• C — local, seasonal food reduces food miles and the need for heated glasshouses and air freight; supports local farmers.\n• Other actions — buying organic, growing own food/allotments, joining urban farming or community schemes.\n• Limits — sustainable and local food can be more expensive; individuals' impact is small unless many people change; some foods cannot be grown in the UK.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 20 (data written for this practice question): change in food insecurity in an LIC. Percentage of the population undernourished: 2000 — 32%; 2010 — 26%; 2020 — 29%. An annotation for 2015–2020 reads: 'Three droughts; rapid population growth; maize price doubled.'\n\nUsing Figure 20, explain the impacts of food insecurity on people and the environment.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `6 marks (AO2 3, AO3 3).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough, well-developed explanation of a range of social, economic and environmental impacts; accurate use of Figure 20.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of some impacts with some development and use of Figure 20.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic list of impacts; little explanation.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Undernutrition — 29% of the population in 2020; leads to stunting in children, weakened immune systems and higher death rates from disease.\n• Famine in the worst years — mass starvation and migration to camps or cities.\n• Rising prices — maize price doubled, so the poorest cannot afford food; may cause social unrest.\n• Lower productivity — hungry people cannot work or attend school well, keeping families in poverty.\n• Soil erosion — pressure to farm marginal land, overgraze and cut trees exposes soil, reducing future yields (a downward spiral).\n• Use of data, e.g. improvement from 32% to 26% (2000–2010) reversed after droughts, rising to 29%.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\n'The new green revolution and biotechnology are the most effective way to increase global food supply.'\n\nTo what extent do you agree with this statement? [9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `9 marks (AO1 3, AO2 3, AO3 3) + 3 SPaG.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Detailed knowledge of the new green revolution/biotechnology and other strategies; thorough, well-balanced evaluation leading to a substantiated conclusion; well-chosen examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Clear knowledge; evaluation with some balance; conclusion partly supported; some examples.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Basic knowledge; limited or one-sided evaluation; little use of examples.\n\nIndicative content:\n• For — GM crops resistant to pests (Bt cotton, India), drought and salt tolerance, higher yields from existing land; biofortification (Golden Rice, Philippines).\n• The new green revolution aims to combine science with sustainable methods and local knowledge, avoiding some failures of the first green revolution.\n• Against — expensive seed and patents can cause debt; risks of monoculture and loss of biodiversity; public opposition to GM.\n• It does not address underlying causes such as poverty, conflict and unequal distribution — the world already produces enough calories for everyone.\n• Alternatives — irrigation, hydroponics/aeroponics, appropriate technology (zai pits, treadle pumps), reducing waste, sustainable diets.\n• Effectiveness varies: HICs/NEEs with money and infrastructure benefit most; small farmers in LICs may gain more from appropriate technology.\n• Conclusion weighing the statement.\n\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance (3): spelling and punctuation consistently accurate; rules of grammar used with effective control of meaning; wide range of specialist terms used precisely.\nIntermediate performance (2): considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance (1): reasonable accuracy; no serious errors of meaning; limited range of specialist terms.\nNo marks (0): no relevant response, or writing does not reach threshold.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\n'Large-scale agricultural developments do more harm than good.'\n\nUsing an example, do you agree with this statement?`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `9 marks (AO1 3, AO2 3, AO3 3).\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Detailed, accurate knowledge of the named development; thorough evaluation of benefits and harm (economic, social, environmental); clear, substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Clear knowledge of the example; some evaluation of both sides; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Basic, mostly generic points; little evaluation.\nMaximum Level 2 if no named example.\n\nIndicative content (e.g. Thanet Earth, Kent):\n• Good — large output of salad crops all year, reducing UK imports; hydroponics recycles water and nutrients; rainwater harvesting; CHP reuses heat and CO₂, surplus power to the grid; biological pest control; several hundred jobs in a deprived area; lower food miles than imports.\n• Harm — loss of greenfield farmland and habitats; visual intrusion and light pollution; dependence on natural gas, a fossil fuel; low-paid work; benefits mostly go to large supermarkets and investors; competition for small local growers.\n• Comparison with other examples (e.g. Almería plastic greenhouses — water over-abstraction and plastic waste) may support evaluation.\n• Judgement depends on viewpoint and scale: e.g. more good than harm economically but environmental sustainability depends on energy source.` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nUsing an example of a local scheme in an LIC or NEE, evaluate how successful it has been in increasing sustainable supplies of food. [9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `9 marks (AO1 3, AO2 3, AO3 3)\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Detailed, accurate knowledge of the scheme; thorough evaluation of successes and limitations for food supply and sustainability; substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Clear knowledge; some evaluation of success; conclusion partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Basic knowledge, mostly generic; little evaluation.\nMaximum Level 2 if no named example.\n\nIndicative content (e.g. Makueni County, Kenya):\n• Background — semi-arid; rainfall unreliable; frequent crop failure and reliance on food aid.\n• Successes — sand dams store water through the dry season for people, livestock and irrigation; terracing reduces soil erosion; drought-tolerant crops and fruit trees give more reliable harvests and surplus for sale; less time spent collecting water, so more time for farming and school.\n• Sustainability — community-built using local materials and labour; low maintenance; does not damage the environment; raises groundwater locally.\n• Limitations — depends on some rainfall to recharge dams; relies on NGO funding and training to start; benefits limited to communities near suitable rivers; cannot solve wider poverty or national drought.\n• Judgement, e.g. successful at a local scale and sustainable, but needs to be part of a wider strategy.\nCredit other valid schemes (e.g. Burkina Faso zai pits; Mali agroforestry).` },
      { q: `Paper 2, Section C — Food.\nFigure 21: two opinions. Farmer in Niger: 'The rains are getting less reliable every year. When the harvest fails there is nothing we can do.' Aid worker: 'There is enough food in the world. People go hungry because of poverty, conflict and the way food is shared out.'\n\nTo what extent is food insecurity caused by human factors rather than physical factors? Use Figure 21 and your own understanding.`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `9 marks (AO1 3, AO2 3, AO3 3).\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Detailed knowledge of physical and human factors; thorough evaluation of their relative importance with linkage between them; substantiated conclusion; good use of Figure 21 and examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Clear knowledge of both types of factor; some evaluation; conclusion partly supported; some use of Figure 21.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Basic knowledge; mostly descriptive or one-sided.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Physical — drought and unreliable rainfall (Sahel/Niger; Horn of Africa 2020–22); extreme weather (floods, tropical cyclones); pests and disease (desert locusts 2019–21); poor soils; climate change increasing unpredictability.\n• Human — poverty limits access to seed, fertiliser, irrigation and purchased food; conflict (Yemen, South Sudan) destroys farms and blocks supply; lack of technology, storage and transport; global trade and prices (2007–08 price spike); population growth; overgrazing and deforestation causing soil erosion.\n• Interaction — physical hazards become food crises mainly where poverty and weak government reduce resilience; HICs suffer droughts without famine.\n• World production exceeds the calories needed for everyone, supporting the aid worker's view that distribution and access matter most.\n• Judgement, e.g. physical factors often trigger shortages but human factors largely decide who goes hungry.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.6': {
    name: 'Water',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 1: a world map of water surplus and deficit. Areas of water surplus (shaded blue) cover most of Canada, northern South America around the Amazon basin, central Africa around the Congo basin, northern Europe and most of Russia. Areas of water deficit (shaded orange) cover North Africa, the Middle East, central Asia, most of inland Australia, the south-west USA, northern Mexico and northern China.\n\nUsing Figure 1, name one continent that has large areas of water surplus.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `1 mark for one of:\n• North America (Canada)\n• South America\n• Europe (northern Europe)\n• Asia (Russia / Siberia)\n• Africa (central Africa)\nDo not credit a country on its own (e.g. 'Canada') unless the continent is also given; do not credit Australia.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 2: a table of global water withdrawals by sector (approximate figures).\nAgriculture (mainly irrigation): 69%\nIndustry (including energy production): 19%\nDomestic (households and municipal supply): 12%\n\nUsing Figure 2, calculate how many times greater the share of water used by agriculture is than the share used for domestic supply. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `69 ÷ 12 = 5.75 → 5.8 times (1)\nAccept 5.75. Do not accept 6 or 5.7.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nBetween 2000 and 2022 average household water use per person rose in many cities in NEEs such as Mumbai and São Paulo as incomes increased.\n\nGive one reason why water consumption increases as a country develops economically.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one valid reason, e.g.:\n• more homes have piped water / flush toilets / showers / washing machines\n• growth of manufacturing industry, which uses large amounts of water\n• more energy production (e.g. cooling power stations)\n• richer diets with more meat / more irrigated farming to feed the population\n• more leisure uses (e.g. swimming pools, golf courses)` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 3: a photograph of the Buriganga River in Dhaka, Bangladesh. The water is dark grey and covered in floating rubbish. Pipes from tanneries and houses empty untreated waste straight into the river, and people are washing clothes at the water's edge.\n\nUsing Figure 3, name one waterborne disease that people may catch from using this water.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `1 mark for one of: cholera; typhoid; dysentery; diarrhoeal disease (diarrhoea); hepatitis A; giardiasis.\nDo not accept malaria (spread by mosquitoes, not by drinking water).` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 4: a table of renewable fresh water available per person per year (data written for this practice question).\nCountry W: 15,200 m³\nCountry X: 1,450 m³\nCountry Y: 820 m³\nCountry Z: 3,100 m³\nA country is said to have water stress when it has less than 1,700 m³ per person per year, and water scarcity when it has less than 1,000 m³ per person per year.\n\nUsing Figure 4, identify the country that has water stress but not water scarcity.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Country X (1,450 m³ is below 1,700 m³ but above 1,000 m³) (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 5: a cross-section through part of south-east England. A layer of chalk dips beneath London, with layers of clay above and below it. Rain falls on the chalk hills (the Chilterns and the North Downs) on either side, and boreholes in London reach down into the chalk.\n\nSuggest one reason why the chalk stores large amounts of groundwater.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for one of:\n• chalk is porous / permeable so water can soak into it and be stored in the pores and cracks\n• the clay above and below is impermeable so water is trapped in the chalk (it forms an aquifer)\n• rain falling on the exposed chalk hills recharges it` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 6: information about the Thames Water desalination plant at Beckton, east London. It opened in 2010 and can treat up to 150 million litres of brackish water from the tidal River Thames a day. It is used mainly during droughts because it is expensive to run.\n\nUsing Figure 6, give one reason why the plant is used mainly during droughts.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one of:\n• it is expensive to run / costs more than other sources\n• it uses a lot of energy (electricity) to remove the salt\n• other supplies (reservoirs, rivers, groundwater) are cheaper and enough at other times` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 1: a world map of water surplus and deficit. Areas of water surplus (shaded blue) cover most of Canada, northern South America around the Amazon basin, central Africa around the Congo basin, northern Europe and most of Russia. Areas of water deficit (shaded orange) cover North Africa, the Middle East, central Asia, most of inland Australia, the south-west USA, northern Mexico and northern China.\n\nUsing Figure 1, describe the distribution of areas of water deficit.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid descriptive point, e.g.:\n• mainly found between about 15° and 40° north and south of the Equator / in the subtropics (1)\n• a large continuous belt across North Africa and the Middle East into central Asia (1)\n• found in the interior of continents, e.g. inland Australia (1)\n• in western parts of continents such as the south-west USA (1)\n• use of named examples from the map, e.g. Sahara, Arabian Peninsula, northern China (1)\nMax 1 mark for a list of places without a pattern.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 7: global water withdrawals, 1900–2010 (data written for this practice question).\n1900: 600 km³ per year\n1950: 1,400 km³ per year\n2010: 3,900 km³ per year\n\nUsing Figure 7, calculate the percentage increase in global water withdrawals between 1900 and 2010. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `3,900 − 600 = 3,300 (1)\n3,300 ÷ 600 × 100 = 550% (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with no working.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nIn the Sahel region of Africa, south of the Sahara, most rain falls in a short wet season of three to four months, and in some years the rains fail completely.\n\nOutline one way that climate affects the availability of water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• low rainfall means there is little surface water (1) so rivers and wells may dry up / there is little water to recharge groundwater (1)\n• high temperatures increase evaporation (1) so water is lost from reservoirs, rivers and soils (1)\n• rainfall is unreliable / seasonal (1) so there are long dry periods or droughts when supplies run out (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nBeneath the North China Plain, millions of wells pump groundwater for irrigation and for cities such as Beijing. In parts of the plain the water table has fallen by tens of metres since the 1970s and the ground surface has sunk.\n\nOutline how over-abstraction reduces the availability of water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for development, e.g.:\n• water is taken out of an aquifer faster than it is replaced by rainfall (1) so the water table falls and wells run dry / must be drilled deeper (1)\n• rivers are drained by taking too much water (1) so less flows downstream for other users (1)\n• near the coast, falling groundwater lets salt water seep into the aquifer (1) making the water unusable (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nIn rural Ethiopia many villages are not connected to a piped water supply. Women and children often walk for several hours a day to collect water from rivers and unprotected wells.\n\nOutline one way that poverty can lead to water insecurity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• poor households cannot afford to pay for piped water / to buy water from vendors (1) so they use unsafe sources such as rivers (1)\n• poor countries / local governments lack money to build infrastructure (1) such as pipes, treatment works and wells (1)\n• people cannot afford pumps or storage tanks (1) so they cannot reach groundwater or store rain for the dry season (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nThe River Ganges in India receives large amounts of untreated sewage from cities and chemical waste from industries such as the leather tanneries in Kanpur.\n\nOutline how pollution can reduce the availability of water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for development, e.g.:\n• sewage and chemicals make water unsafe to drink (1) so although water is present it cannot be used without expensive treatment (1)\n• polluted water cannot be used for irrigation or industry (1) reducing the usable supply (1)\n• pollution of groundwater (e.g. by fertilisers or arsenic) (1) means wells must be abandoned (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nIn June 2019 the four main reservoirs supplying Chennai, a city of about 10 million people in south India, almost ran dry after poor monsoon rains. Some IT companies asked staff to work from home and some hotels and restaurants closed for part of the day.\n\nOutline one way that water insecurity can affect industrial output.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• factories need water for cooling, cleaning and processing (1) so a shortage forces them to reduce production or close (1)\n• businesses must pay more to buy water from tankers (1) which raises costs and reduces profits / output (1)\n• hydroelectric and thermal power stations need water (1) so electricity shortages cut industrial output (1)\nCredit use of the Chennai example.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nEthiopia has built the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam on the Blue Nile. Downstream, Egypt depends on the River Nile for most of its fresh water.\n\nSuggest one reason why the dam has led to tension between Ethiopia and Egypt.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for a developed point, e.g.:\n• Egypt fears that less water will flow downstream (1), especially while the reservoir is filling or during droughts, threatening its farming and water supply (1)\n• the countries have not agreed how fast to fill the reservoir / how much water each should get (1) because there is no binding agreement on sharing the river (1)\n• the river crosses international borders (1) so one country's actions upstream affect people in another (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nThe Colorado River in the USA is diverted along canals and aqueducts, such as the All-American Canal, to farms in the Imperial Valley and to cities in southern California.\n\nOutline how diverting supplies can increase the supply of water to an area.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for development, e.g.:\n• water is taken from a river where it is plentiful (1) and carried by canals or pipes to an area with a deficit (1)\n• water can be moved to where demand is high, e.g. dry farmland or growing cities (1), allowing irrigation / meeting urban demand (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 8: a photograph of a large dam across a river valley in a mountainous area. Behind the concrete dam wall is a long reservoir filling the valley. Large pipes lead from the dam towards a city in the lowlands.\n\nUsing Figure 8, give two ways in which dams and reservoirs increase water supply.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid way, up to 2 marks, e.g.:\n• water is stored in wet periods for use in dry periods / droughts (1)\n• the reservoir provides a reliable supply for a city (1)\n• water can be released for irrigation of farmland (1)\n• the reservoir can feed water transfer schemes / pipelines to other areas (1)\n• regulating the river flow keeps water available downstream all year (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 9: information about China's water (approximate figures). Northern China has about 45% of China's population and about 60% of its farmland, but only about 20% of its water resources. Southern China, including the Yangtze basin, has about 80% of the water.\n\nUsing Figure 9, give two pieces of evidence that northern China needs a water transfer scheme.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for each piece of evidence from Figure 9, up to 2 marks:\n• northern China has only about 20% of China's water (1)\n• but about 45% of the population (1)\n• and about 60% of the farmland, which needs irrigation (1)\n• southern China has a surplus (about 80% of the water) that could be transferred (1)\nAllow 1 mark for a comparison, e.g. the north has over twice as much of the population as its share of water.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 10: a leaflet from a UK water company. It shows a dual-flush toilet, a shower timer, a water butt connected to a drainpipe and a water meter.\n\nUsing Figure 10, outline two ways that households can conserve water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid way, up to 2 marks, e.g.:\n• using a dual-flush toilet so less water is used for each flush (1)\n• taking shorter showers / using a shower timer (1)\n• collecting rainwater in a water butt to water the garden (1)\n• having a water meter so people pay for what they use and waste less (1)\n• fixing leaks / turning off taps while cleaning teeth (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nBedZED is an eco-village in Sutton, south London. Water from its baths, showers and sinks is collected and treated on site, then used to flush toilets.\n\nOutline how using grey water reduces the demand for fresh water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for development, e.g.:\n• waste water from sinks, baths and washing machines is reused (1) for uses that do not need drinking-quality water, such as flushing toilets or watering gardens (1)\n• less treated mains water is needed (1) because toilet flushing is a large share of household use (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nThe Ogallala aquifer lies beneath the Great Plains of the USA. Farmers pump water from it to irrigate crops such as maize. In parts of Kansas and Texas the water level has fallen so much that some wells have dried up.\n\nSuggest two ways that groundwater can be managed more sustainably.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid way, up to 2 marks, e.g.:\n• limit the amount each user can pump / issue licences or quotas (1)\n• monitor water levels so abstraction does not exceed recharge (1)\n• use more efficient irrigation such as drip irrigation (1)\n• grow crops that need less water (1)\n• artificially recharge the aquifer, e.g. with treated waste water or flood water (1)\n• protect the aquifer from pollution by controlling fertiliser and chemical use (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nSingapore treats used water with advanced membranes and ultraviolet light to make 'NEWater'. NEWater can meet up to about 40% of Singapore's current water demand.\n\nOutline how recycling water can help to increase water supply.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point, 2 marks for development, e.g.:\n• waste water is treated so it can be used again (1) so the same water is used many times instead of new supplies being needed (1)\n• it gives a reliable supply that does not depend on rainfall (1), important for a country with few natural sources such as Singapore (1)\n• recycled water can be used by industry / for cooling (1) leaving fresh water for drinking (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nThe world's population reached 7 billion in 2011 and 8 billion in 2022. Over the same period many countries, including China and India, experienced rapid economic development.\n\nExplain why global water consumption is increasing.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Shows clear understanding of the reasons for increasing water consumption. Explanation is developed, linking population growth and economic development to increased demand.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Shows limited understanding. Reasons are stated or listed with little explanation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• rising population means more people need water for drinking, cooking and sanitation\n• more food is needed, so more irrigation (agriculture uses about 70% of withdrawals)\n• economic development increases industrial use, e.g. manufacturing, mining, cooling power stations\n• rising incomes mean more homes have piped water, flush toilets, showers and washing machines\n• changing diets with more meat need more water per calorie\n• urbanisation increases demand for piped supplies and leisure uses\n• climate change and hotter summers increase demand for irrigation and cooling` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nThe Nubian Sandstone Aquifer lies beneath the Sahara in Egypt, Libya, Sudan and Chad. Libya pumps water from it to coastal cities through the 'Great Man-Made River' pipelines. In other parts of the world, areas of granite or clay have very little groundwater.\n\nExplain how geology affects the availability of water.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of how rock type affects water availability, with developed explanation of both surface water and groundwater.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Limited understanding; simple statements about rock type.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• permeable and porous rocks (sandstone, chalk, limestone) allow water to soak in and be stored as groundwater in aquifers\n• aquifers can supply water even in deserts, e.g. the Nubian Sandstone Aquifer, although this water is often 'fossil' water that is not being replaced\n• impermeable rocks (granite, clay) do not store water, so there is little groundwater, but rain runs off into rivers, making them suitable for reservoirs\n• in limestone areas water may disappear underground, leaving little surface water\n• aquifers sandwiched between impermeable layers can be reached by boreholes / may form artesian wells` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nSince 2016 Yemen, a country affected by war, has had one of the largest cholera outbreaks ever recorded, with more than two million suspected cases. Many water treatment works and pipes have been damaged.\n\nExplain how water insecurity can lead to waterborne disease.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of the links between water insecurity, pollution and disease; explanation is developed and may use the example.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Limited understanding; simple statements.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• without safe piped supplies people drink from rivers, ponds and unprotected wells\n• these sources are polluted by untreated sewage containing bacteria such as those causing cholera and typhoid\n• lack of sanitation means human waste contaminates water sources\n• damaged or absent water treatment works (as in Yemen) mean water is not cleaned\n• storing water in open containers allows contamination and breeding of mosquitoes\n• shortages mean less water for washing hands, so diseases spread more easily\n• diarrhoeal diseases cause dehydration and death, especially in children` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nSaudi Arabia and the United Arab Emirates are among the largest users of desalination in the world. Both countries are oil-rich, have very low rainfall and have few rivers.\n\nSuggest why desalination is widely used in wealthy countries in the Middle East.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding with developed reasons linking physical and economic factors to the use of desalination.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Limited understanding; reasons stated with little development.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• very low rainfall and high evaporation mean few rivers and little surface water\n• groundwater is limited / being over-used, and much is non-renewable fossil water\n• long coastlines give easy access to unlimited sea water (Persian Gulf, Red Sea)\n• desalination is expensive to build and run, which wealthy countries can afford\n• it needs large amounts of energy; oil and gas are cheap and plentiful in these countries\n• rapid population growth and urbanisation (e.g. Dubai) increase demand that cannot be met in other ways` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nThe Wakel River Basin is in the arid south of Rajasthan, India. Rainfall is low and unreliable, and in the past many men migrated to cities in the dry season to find work.\n\nExplain how a local scheme in the Wakel River Basin has increased sustainable supplies of water.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO1 Accurate knowledge of the scheme. AO2 Clear explanation of how the methods increase supply and why they are sustainable.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO1 Limited knowledge. AO2 Simple statements about the scheme.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• taankas: underground tanks that collect and store rainwater from roofs and the ground for use in the dry season\n• johads (check dams): small earth dams that hold back monsoon run-off so it soaks into the ground and recharges groundwater, raising the level in wells\n• pats: channels that divert water from streams to fields for irrigation\n• anicuts: small dams across streams that store water and recharge groundwater\n• sustainable because they use simple, low-cost technology and local materials that people can build and repair themselves\n• communities are involved in planning and managing the water\n• more water for farming means less seasonal migration and better food security\nAllow any other appropriate local scheme in an LIC or NEE.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nThe Three Gorges Dam on the Yangtze River in China was completed in 2006. Its reservoir is about 600 km long and over one million people had to be moved from towns and villages that were flooded.\n\nExplain the disadvantages of building large dams and reservoirs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of social, economic and environmental disadvantages, with developed explanation.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Limited understanding; disadvantages listed.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• people are displaced as land is flooded; loss of homes, farmland and communities (over one million at Three Gorges)\n• very expensive to build, often needing large loans\n• habitats are flooded and fish migration is blocked\n• sediment is trapped behind the dam, so the reservoir silts up and downstream farmland and deltas receive less fertile silt\n• evaporation from large reservoirs wastes water in hot climates\n• reduced flow downstream can affect other users or countries\n• heritage sites and archaeological sites may be lost\n• the weight of water may trigger earthquakes / landslides on the reservoir sides` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nSingapore has few natural sources of water. As well as collecting rainwater in reservoirs and importing water from Malaysia, it recycles waste water to make 'NEWater' and has built desalination plants.\n\nExplain how recycling water can help a country to manage its water supply sustainably.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear, developed explanation of how recycling increases supply and why it is sustainable; may use Singapore.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Limited understanding; simple statements.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• waste water is treated (e.g. membranes, ultraviolet light) so it can be reused\n• it reduces the need to take water from rivers, reservoirs or aquifers, leaving these for the future\n• it is a reliable supply that does not depend on rainfall or droughts\n• it reduces dependence on imported water (Singapore and Malaysia)\n• recycled water can be used for industry, cooling or irrigation, saving drinking-quality water\n• less waste water is released into rivers and the sea, reducing pollution\n• it uses less energy than desalinating sea water` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nIn the North China Plain, groundwater has been pumped faster than it is replaced for many decades, causing the water table to fall and the land to subside. Since 2014 water from the South–North Water Transfer Project has allowed some wells to be closed.\n\nExplain how groundwater can be managed so that it is used sustainably.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear, developed explanation of strategies and how they keep abstraction within recharge.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Limited understanding; strategies listed.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• monitoring water levels and recharge rates so that abstraction does not exceed recharge\n• licences, quotas or charges for pumping groundwater; closing illegal wells\n• replacing groundwater with other sources (e.g. transferred water, as in the North China Plain) so the aquifer can recover\n• artificial recharge, e.g. pumping treated water or storing flood water in the ground\n• more efficient irrigation (drip irrigation) so less groundwater is needed\n• protecting the aquifer from pollution by controlling fertiliser, pesticide and industrial waste\n• involving local communities in managing wells` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 11: information about the Cape Town water crisis, South Africa (dam levels are data written for this practice question).\nLevel of the city's main dams in February: 2014 95%, 2015 72%, 2016 55%, 2017 35%, 2018 25%.\nIn early 2018 the city warned of 'Day Zero', when taps would be turned off. Residents were limited to 50 litres per person per day. Farmers in the Western Cape had their water allocations cut and some tourists cancelled holidays.\n\nSuggest how water insecurity affects people and the economy. Use Figure 11 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Shows thorough application of knowledge to suggest a range of impacts on people and the economy. AO3 Uses evidence from Figure 11 effectively, including the data, to support the answer.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Shows reasonable application of knowledge to suggest impacts. AO3 Uses some evidence from Figure 11.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Limited application; impacts are simple or listed. AO3 Little or no use of Figure 11.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• dam levels fell from 95% to 25% between 2014 and 2018, a fall of 70 percentage points\n• people: limited to 50 litres per day, so less water for washing, cooking and sanitation; risk of disease if hygiene falls; queues at collection points; stress\n• the poorest, in informal settlements, may suffer most as they cannot afford to buy water or tanks\n• economy: farmers' allocations cut, so lower crop yields, job losses for farm workers, higher food prices\n• tourism falls as visitors cancel, reducing income for hotels and restaurants\n• businesses face higher costs (buying water, drilling boreholes) and lower output\n• positive responses: people used grey water and rainwater tanks; demand fell sharply` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 12: a table about water availability in four countries (approximate figures).\nCanada: high rainfall, large lakes and rivers; high income; water surplus.\nEgypt: very low rainfall; depends on the River Nile; NEE; water stress.\nAustralia: low, unreliable rainfall inland; high evaporation; high income; parts in deficit.\nDemocratic Republic of Congo: high rainfall, large River Congo; LIC; many people lack safe water.\n\nUsing Figure 12 and your own understanding, explain how both physical and human factors affect the availability of water.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Thorough understanding of both physical and human factors, with well-developed explanation. AO3 Effective use of Figure 12, e.g. recognising that DR Congo has plenty of water but people still lack safe water.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Reasonable understanding of factors, some explanation; may be unbalanced. AO3 Some use of Figure 12.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Limited understanding; factors listed. AO3 Little use of the figure.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\nPhysical:\n• climate: high rainfall gives surplus (Canada, DR Congo); low or unreliable rainfall and high evaporation give deficit (Egypt, inland Australia)\n• geology: permeable rocks form aquifers; impermeable rocks give surface run-off for reservoirs\nHuman:\n• poverty: DR Congo has a surplus but many lack safe water as people and government cannot afford infrastructure\n• limited infrastructure: lack of pipes, treatment works and wells\n• pollution of rivers and groundwater reduces usable water\n• over-abstraction of rivers and aquifers lowers availability\n• wealth allows countries such as Australia to build dams, transfers and desalination plants\nCredit recognition that economic water scarcity differs from physical water scarcity.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nAbout 2 billion people in the world do not have safely managed drinking water. Most of them live in LICs in sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia.\n\n'Poverty is the main cause of water insecurity.' Do you agree with this statement? Use named examples in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO1 Detailed knowledge of the causes of water insecurity with named examples. AO3 Thorough evaluation, weighing poverty against other factors, reaching a well-supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO1 Clear knowledge; some use of examples. AO3 Some evaluation; conclusion may be partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO1 Limited knowledge. AO3 Little or no evaluation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\nAgree:\n• poor countries / households cannot afford pipes, treatment, wells or pumps (e.g. rural Ethiopia)\n• countries with plenty of water such as DR Congo still have many people without safe water — economic scarcity\n• wealthy dry countries (Saudi Arabia, UAE, Israel) overcome physical shortages by desalination\n• poor people in cities pay more for water from vendors\nDisagree / other factors:\n• climate: low, unreliable rainfall and drought (Sahel; Cape Town 2018 affected a middle-income city)\n• over-abstraction: North China Plain, Ogallala aquifer in the USA (a rich country)\n• pollution of rivers such as the Ganges\n• conflict damaging infrastructure (Yemen)\n• rising demand from population growth\nA conclusion may argue that physical factors create shortages but poverty decides whether people can overcome them.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 13: two views about the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam (GERD) on the Blue Nile.\nEthiopian government official: "The dam will provide electricity for millions of Ethiopians who have none. The Nile begins in our country and we have the right to use it."\nEgyptian farmer: "Without the Nile our fields would be desert. If less water reaches Egypt, we will not be able to grow our crops."\n\nExplain how water insecurity can lead to conflict. Use Figure 13 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Thorough, well-developed explanation of how competition for water leads to tension and conflict at different scales. AO3 Effective use of Figure 13.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear explanation with some development. AO3 Some use of Figure 13.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Simple statements. AO3 Little use of Figure 13.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• many rivers cross international borders, so upstream countries control what flows downstream\n• Ethiopia wants water for hydroelectric power and development; Egypt depends on the Nile for farming and drinking water\n• filling a reservoir or taking water upstream reduces flow downstream, especially in droughts\n• growing populations and climate change increase competition for limited water\n• lack of agreements on sharing water causes disputes; treaties such as the Indus Waters Treaty (India and Pakistan) can reduce conflict\n• conflict can also be local, e.g. between farmers and herders over wells, or between farmers and cities\n• water infrastructure may be targeted in wars` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 14: information about desalination.\n• The Sorek plant in Israel is one of the largest seawater desalination plants in the world. Desalination now supplies most of Israel's domestic water.\n• Desalination needs large amounts of energy, much of which comes from burning fossil fuels.\n• For every litre of fresh water produced, more than a litre of very salty brine is released, often back into the sea.\n\nEvaluate the use of desalination to increase water supply. Use Figure 14 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Thorough understanding of desalination. AO3 Balanced evaluation of advantages and disadvantages using Figure 14 effectively, with a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding. AO3 Some evaluation; may be one-sided or the judgement may be weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Limited understanding. AO3 Little evaluation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\nAdvantages:\n• unlimited supply of sea water for coastal countries; does not depend on rainfall\n• reliable in droughts (e.g. Beckton plant, London; Israel)\n• has given Israel water security, so it can reduce use of the Sea of Galilee and aquifers\n• technology is improving, so costs are falling\nDisadvantages:\n• high energy use and cost, so often unaffordable for LICs\n• burning fossil fuels releases greenhouse gases, adding to climate change\n• brine and chemicals discharged can harm marine ecosystems\n• only practical near the coast; inland areas need pumping, adding cost\nJudgement: effective for wealthy coastal countries but not a sustainable solution for all, unless powered by renewable energy.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 15: information about the Lesotho Highlands Water Project.\nLesotho is a small, mountainous LIC with high rainfall in its highlands. Phase 1 of the project, including the Katse Dam (completed in 1997) and the Mohale Dam (completed in 2002), transfers water through tunnels to the Vaal River, which supplies Gauteng province in South Africa, including Johannesburg. Lesotho receives royalty payments from South Africa and the Muela hydroelectric power station generates electricity for Lesotho. Thousands of people were moved from their land.\n\nAssess the advantages and disadvantages of the project for Lesotho. Use Figure 15 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Thorough understanding of the impacts of the scheme. AO3 Balanced assessment that weighs advantages against disadvantages and reaches a supported judgement; Figure 15 used effectively.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding. AO3 Some assessment; may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Limited understanding. AO3 Little assessment.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\nAdvantages for Lesotho:\n• royalties from South Africa are an important source of income for the government\n• Muela hydroelectric power reduces the need to import electricity\n• jobs during construction; new roads into the highlands\n• some improvement in local water supply\nDisadvantages for Lesotho:\n• thousands of people displaced; loss of farmland and grazing land, compensation often seen as inadequate\n• flooding of valleys damaged ecosystems; reduced river flows downstream harm fisheries and wetlands\n• benefits mainly go to South Africa (Gauteng) while many people in Lesotho still lack safe water\n• corruption in awarding contracts was found during construction\n• money may not reach the rural poor\nJudgement on overall balance, e.g. benefits at national scale but costs borne by local communities.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 16: information about drought and farming (data written for this practice question).\nIn a region of the Sahel, the maize harvest was 1.8 tonnes per hectare in a year of normal rainfall but only 0.6 tonnes per hectare in a drought year. The number of cattle owned by herders fell by a third during the same drought.\n\nDiscuss the impacts of water insecurity on food production. Use Figure 16 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Thorough discussion of a range of impacts with well-developed links between water and food. AO3 Effective use of Figure 16 data (e.g. yield fell by 1.2 t/ha, a 67% fall).\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear discussion with some development. AO3 Some use of Figure 16.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Simple statements. AO3 Little use of Figure 16.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• yields fell from 1.8 to 0.6 t/ha — a fall of 1.2 t/ha, about two-thirds (67%)\n• crops fail without enough water; irrigation is needed for about 40% of the world's food (allow approximate figures)\n• livestock die or must be sold cheaply when pasture and water holes dry up\n• food shortages lead to malnutrition and famine, e.g. Somalia 2011\n• food prices rise, affecting the poor in cities\n• farmers lose income, leading to debt and migration to cities\n• over-irrigation in dry areas can cause salinisation, reducing future yields\n• water insecurity also limits processing and food hygiene` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 17: four strategies used to manage water sustainably.\nA: a hosepipe ban and water meters in south-east England.\nB: quotas on pumping from the Ogallala aquifer in Kansas, USA.\nC: NEWater recycling plants in Singapore.\nD: grey water systems in new homes at BedZED, London.\n\nAssess the effectiveness of strategies to manage water sustainably. Use Figure 17 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Thorough understanding of a range of sustainable strategies. AO3 Detailed assessment of how effective they are, with a supported judgement; Figure 17 used effectively.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding. AO3 Some assessment; may be descriptive in places.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Limited understanding. AO3 Little assessment; strategies described.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• conservation (A): metering encourages people to use less; hosepipe bans cut peak demand quickly; but relies on public behaviour and bans are temporary\n• groundwater management (B): quotas keep abstraction closer to recharge, but are hard to enforce and may reduce farm incomes; the water table may take decades to recover\n• recycling (C): reliable supply independent of rainfall; very effective in Singapore; but expensive and some people dislike drinking recycled water\n• grey water (D): reduces mains demand for toilet flushing and gardens; but costly to fit in existing homes and needs maintenance\n• overall: most effective when combined and when people support them; cheaper and less damaging than large dams or transfers` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nMany rural areas in LICs and NEEs have seasonal rainfall, with a short monsoon season followed by months with almost no rain.\n\nUsing a named example of a local scheme in an LIC or NEE, explain how it has increased sustainable supplies of water.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO1 Detailed, accurate knowledge of a named local scheme. AO2 Thorough explanation of how the methods increase supply and why they are sustainable.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO1 Clear knowledge of a scheme, some detail. AO2 Clear explanation, partly developed.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO1 Limited knowledge; scheme may be generic or unnamed. AO2 Simple statements.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content (Wakel River Basin, Rajasthan, India):\n• arid area with low, unreliable rainfall, falling groundwater and seasonal migration to cities\n• taankas store rainwater for the dry season\n• johads / check dams and anicuts hold back monsoon run-off, letting it soak in and recharge groundwater so wells hold water for longer\n• pats divert stream water to irrigate fields\n• low-cost, simple technology built and maintained by local people with local materials\n• communities share and manage the water, so the scheme lasts\n• results: more irrigated land, better harvests, less migration, less time spent collecting water\nCredit other valid schemes, e.g. hand pumps and rainwater harvesting by an NGO in a named country.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 18: two views about building a large new dam in an LIC.\nGovernment minister: "The dam will store water for our growing cities, irrigate farmland and produce cheap hydroelectric power."\nCampaign group: "Thousands of villagers will lose their land. Small-scale schemes such as rainwater harvesting would help the poorest people more."\n\nTo what extent are dams and reservoirs the best way to increase water supply? Use Figure 18 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Thorough understanding of dams and alternative strategies. AO3 Well-balanced evaluation that reaches a clear, supported judgement 'to what extent'; Figure 18 used effectively.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding. AO3 Some evaluation; judgement may be partial.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Limited understanding. AO3 Little evaluation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\nFor dams:\n• store large volumes of water for dry seasons and droughts; reliable supply for cities and irrigation\n• multi-purpose: hydroelectric power, flood control (e.g. Three Gorges Dam)\nAgainst:\n• displacement of people (over one million at Three Gorges), high cost, debt\n• environmental damage: flooded habitats, trapped sediment, evaporation losses\n• benefits may go to cities and industry, not the rural poor\nAlternatives:\n• water transfers, desalination, groundwater, recycling, conservation\n• small-scale schemes (e.g. Wakel River Basin) are cheaper, sustainable and help rural communities\nJudgement may depend on the scale of demand, wealth of the country and the physical setting.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 19: information about India (approximate figures).\nPopulation: 2000 about 1.06 billion; 2022 about 1.42 billion.\nGDP per person: 2000 about US$440; 2022 about US$2,400.\nUrban population: 2000 about 28%; 2022 about 36%.\n\nExplain why the demand for water is increasing in NEEs such as India. Use Figure 19 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Thorough, well-developed explanation linking population growth, economic development and urbanisation to water demand. AO3 Effective use of Figure 19 data.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear explanation with some development. AO3 Some use of Figure 19.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Simple statements. AO3 Little use of Figure 19.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• population grew by about 360 million (about 34%), so more water is needed for drinking, cooking and sanitation\n• more food is needed, increasing irrigation (agriculture is the largest user in India)\n• GDP per person rose more than five times, so people can afford piped water, flush toilets, washing machines and showers\n• industrial growth (manufacturing, IT, textiles, power stations) uses large amounts of water\n• urbanisation from 28% to 36%: city dwellers use more water per person than rural dwellers; demand for piped supplies grows\n• changing diets (more meat and dairy) and leisure uses increase demand\n• hotter summers linked to climate change increase demand for cooling and irrigation` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 20: a map of the South–North Water Transfer Project, China. The Eastern Route takes water from the lower Yangtze River near Yangzhou and follows the Grand Canal north towards Shandong and Tianjin. The Central Route takes water from the Danjiangkou Reservoir on the Han River (a tributary of the Yangtze) about 1,260 km north to Beijing and Tianjin; it opened in 2014. A Western Route in the Tibetan Plateau is planned but not built. Labels show: 'Danjiangkou Dam raised, hundreds of thousands of people relocated' and 'North China Plain: groundwater over-abstraction and land subsidence'.\n\nTo what extent does a large-scale water transfer scheme bring both advantages and disadvantages? Use Figure 20 and an example you have studied.\n[9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO1 Detailed knowledge of a named large-scale water transfer scheme. AO2 Thorough understanding of its advantages and disadvantages for different places and people. AO3 Detailed, balanced evaluation leading to a substantiated judgement 'to what extent'; Figure 20 used effectively.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: AO1 Clear knowledge of a scheme. AO2 Clear understanding of advantages and disadvantages. AO3 Some evaluation; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: AO1 Limited knowledge; scheme may be unnamed. AO2 Limited understanding. AO3 Little evaluation; mostly description.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content (South–North Water Transfer Project):\nAdvantages:\n• moves water from the water-rich south (Yangtze) to the dry, densely populated north\n• the Central Route now provides a large share of Beijing's piped water\n• reduces groundwater pumping on the North China Plain, slowing the fall of the water table and subsidence\n• supports industry and farming in the north; improves drinking water quality in some cities\nDisadvantages:\n• very high cost (tens of billions of US dollars)\n• hundreds of thousands of people relocated when the Danjiangkou Dam was raised and the reservoir enlarged\n• lower flow in the Han River downstream, concentrating pollution and harming wetlands\n• Eastern Route water was badly polluted and needed costly treatment\n• evaporation losses; large energy use for pumping on the Eastern Route\n• may encourage continued high water use in the north rather than conservation\nAllow other schemes, e.g. Lesotho Highlands Water Project.\n\nSPaG:\nHigh performance (3 marks): spelling and punctuation with consistent accuracy; rules of grammar used with effective control of meaning; wide range of specialist terms used adeptly and with precision.\nIntermediate performance (2 marks): spelling and punctuation with considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms used appropriately.\nThreshold performance (1 mark): spelling and punctuation with reasonable accuracy; rules of grammar used with some control of meaning; limited range of specialist terms used appropriately.\nNo marks awarded (0): no response, response not related to the question, or errors severely hinder meaning.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 21: two approaches to increasing water supply.\nApproach 1: large-scale engineering — mega-dams, inter-basin water transfers and desalination plants.\nApproach 2: sustainable strategies — water conservation, groundwater management, recycling and grey water, and small local schemes such as rainwater harvesting.\n\n'Sustainable strategies are more effective than large-scale engineering in meeting future water needs.' Do you agree with this statement? Use Figure 21 and named examples to justify your decision.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO1 Detailed knowledge of both approaches with named examples. AO2 Thorough understanding of how each meets water needs. AO3 Detailed evaluation that reaches a well-justified decision; Figure 21 used effectively.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: AO1 Clear knowledge, some examples. AO2 Clear understanding. AO3 Some evaluation and a partly justified decision.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: AO1 Limited knowledge. AO2 Limited understanding. AO3 Little evaluation; decision unsupported.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\nLarge-scale engineering:\n• can supply very large volumes to big cities and industry (South–North Water Transfer to Beijing; Israel's desalination; Lesotho Highlands supplying Gauteng)\n• but very expensive, high energy use, displaces people, damages ecosystems; may not reach the rural poor; may encourage waste\nSustainable strategies:\n• conservation and metering cut demand (e.g. Cape Town reduced use dramatically in 2018)\n• recycling gives a reliable supply (Singapore NEWater)\n• groundwater management protects aquifers for the future (North China Plain, Ogallala)\n• local schemes are cheap, community-run and help the poorest (Wakel River Basin)\n• but may not be enough for megacities or very dry countries; rely on behaviour change\nJustification may argue for a combination: sustainable strategies first, engineering where demand cannot otherwise be met.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 22: four photographs showing impacts of water insecurity.\nPhoto A: children collecting water from a polluted stream in Malawi.\nPhoto B: a field of withered maize in Zimbabwe during a drought.\nPhoto C: a textile factory in Tamil Nadu, India, closed because of water shortages.\nPhoto D: protesters in a city demanding a fair share of river water.\n\n'Disease is the most serious impact of water insecurity.' To what extent do you agree? Use Figure 22 and your own understanding.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO1 Detailed knowledge of the impacts of water insecurity. AO2 Thorough understanding of how they affect people, the economy and relations between groups. AO3 Detailed evaluation of their relative seriousness, reaching a substantiated judgement; Figure 22 used effectively.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: AO1 Clear knowledge. AO2 Clear understanding. AO3 Some evaluation; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: AO1 Limited knowledge. AO2 Limited understanding. AO3 Little evaluation.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content:\n• disease (A): drinking polluted water spreads cholera, typhoid and diarrhoeal disease; diarrhoea kills hundreds of thousands of children each year; outbreak in Yemen; illness stops children attending school and adults working\n• food production (B): drought causes crop failure and livestock deaths, leading to malnutrition and famine (e.g. Somalia 2011); rising food prices\n• industrial output (C): factories and power stations need water; shortages cause closures and job losses (Chennai 2019)\n• conflict (D): competition between countries (GERD on the Nile) or groups within countries; can threaten stability\n• impacts are linked: food shortages weaken people so disease is worse; loss of income reduces money for safe water\n• seriousness varies by place: disease and food most serious in LICs; industrial impacts more significant in NEEs and HICs\nA judgement may argue that disease is most serious because it causes death directly, or that the impacts are interlinked.` },
      { q: `Section C — Water.\nFigure 23: comments from people in a village in the Wakel River Basin, Rajasthan, India.\nFarmer: "Since the johads were built, our wells hold water for more months of the year and we can grow a second crop."\nWoman: "The taanka by our house means I spend less time walking to fetch water."\nVillage elder: "In a year when the monsoon fails, there is still not enough water for everyone."\n\nUsing a case study of a local scheme in an LIC or NEE, evaluate how successful it has been in increasing sustainable supplies of water.\n[9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO1 Detailed, accurate knowledge of a named local scheme. AO2 Thorough understanding of how it increases supply sustainably. AO3 Detailed evaluation of its successes and limitations, reaching a substantiated judgement; Figure 23 used effectively.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: AO1 Clear knowledge. AO2 Clear understanding. AO3 Some evaluation; judgement partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: AO1 Limited knowledge; scheme may be generic. AO2 Limited understanding. AO3 Little evaluation; description only.\n0 marks: nothing worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative content (Wakel River Basin):\nSuccesses:\n• johads, anicuts and check dams trap monsoon run-off and recharge groundwater, so wells hold water longer\n• taankas store rainwater near homes, saving time (especially for women and girls) and improving hygiene\n• pats divert water to fields; more irrigation allows a second crop and higher yields\n• less seasonal migration to cities; better food security and incomes\n• sustainable: low-cost, appropriate technology, local materials, community managed and maintained\nLimitations:\n• still depends on the monsoon; in drought years supplies are not enough\n• small scale, so benefits are local; may need repeated maintenance\n• stored water may need treatment to be safe to drink\n• relies on NGO/government support and community co-operation\nJudgement on overall success, e.g. successful for its scale and cost but not a complete solution.\nCredit other valid local schemes in an LIC or NEE.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.7': {
    name: 'Energy',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 1 (data written for this practice question): energy consumption per person, 2022 (gigajoules, GJ). Qatar 690; Canada 360; USA 280; UK 105; India 25; Ethiopia 4.\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the range in energy consumption per person. Give your answer in GJ.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for 686 (GJ).\n(690 − 4 = 686.) Do not credit an answer without the correct figure.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nSome energy sources are renewable and others are non-renewable.\n\nWhich one of the following is a renewable source of energy? Shade one circle only.\nA  Coal\nB  Geothermal\nC  Natural gas\nD  Uranium`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark for B (Geothermal).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 2 (data written for this practice question): a world choropleth map showing energy surplus and deficit. Dark shading (large energy surplus) covers Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Kuwait, Russia, Norway, Canada and Australia. Mid shading (small surplus) covers Nigeria, Algeria and Colombia. Light shading (energy deficit) covers Japan, South Korea, most of western Europe including the UK, Germany and France, and India.\n\nUsing Figure 2, name one country in Asia that has an energy deficit.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark for Japan or South Korea or India.\nDo not credit Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Kuwait or Russia (surplus).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 3: a photograph of the Trans-Alaska Pipeline, which carries oil about 1,280 km from Prudhoe Bay on the Arctic coast to the port of Valdez. Across long sections the pipe is raised several metres above the frozen ground on steel supports, with gaps beneath it.\n\nSuggest one reason why much of the pipeline is raised above the ground.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for any one valid reason, e.g.:\n• so that heat from the warm oil does not melt the permafrost (which would make the ground unstable / the pipe sink or break)\n• to allow animals such as caribou to migrate underneath\n• to allow the pipe to move during earthquakes / with temperature change.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nIn 2019 fracking for shale gas at Preston New Road in Lancashire was stopped. The UK government then introduced a moratorium (temporary ban) on fracking in England.\n\nGive one reason why fracking at Preston New Road was stopped.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for one valid reason, e.g.:\n• it caused earth tremors / induced seismicity (a magnitude 2.9 tremor in August 2019)\n• the risk of earthquakes could not be accurately predicted\n• strong local opposition / protests.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nChambamontera is a small, remote farming community in the Andes mountains of northern Peru, many hours' travel from the nearest town.\n\nSuggest one reason why remote communities like Chambamontera were not connected to the national electricity grid.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for one valid reason, e.g.:\n• steep, mountainous terrain makes building power lines difficult\n• it is too expensive to extend the grid to a small number of scattered houses\n• low incomes mean the electricity company would not make a profit\n• poor roads / remoteness make access difficult.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 4 (data written for this practice question): global primary energy consumption was 360 exajoules (EJ) in 1990 and 604 EJ in 2022.\n\nUsing Figure 4, calculate the percentage increase in global energy consumption between 1990 and 2022. Give your answer to one decimal place. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2 marks for 67.8%.\n1 mark for correct working: (604 − 360) ÷ 360 × 100 or 244 ÷ 360 × 100.\nAllow 1 mark for 67.7% or 68% (rounding error).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 2 (data written for this practice question): a world choropleth map showing energy surplus and deficit. Dark shading (large energy surplus) covers Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Kuwait, Russia, Norway, Canada and Australia. Mid shading (small surplus) covers Nigeria, Algeria and Colombia. Light shading (energy deficit) covers Japan, South Korea, most of western Europe including the UK, Germany and France, and India.\n\nUsing Figure 2, describe the global distribution of countries with a large energy surplus.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid descriptive point, up to 2 marks, e.g.:\n• concentrated in the Middle East (Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Kuwait)\n• found in large countries with low population density, e.g. Russia, Canada, Australia\n• many are in the northern hemisphere / high latitudes (Russia, Canada, Norway)\n• use of a named country or region from the map as evidence can earn the second mark.\nNo credit for explanation.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 5 (data written for this practice question): two divided bars showing sources of UK electricity.\n2010: gas 46%, coal 28%, nuclear 16%, renewables 7%, other 3%.\n2023: gas 34%, coal 1%, nuclear 14%, renewables 47%, other 4%.\n\nUsing Figure 5, compare the sources of UK electricity in 2010 and 2023.`, marks: 2, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `1 mark per valid comparative point, up to 2 marks, e.g.:\n• renewables increased greatly, from 7% to 47% (an increase of 40 percentage points)\n• coal fell from 28% to 1% / almost disappeared\n• gas was the largest source in 2010 but renewables were the largest in 2023\n• gas fell (46% to 34%) but is still important; nuclear fell slightly (16% to 14%).\nPoints must be comparative; simply listing figures for one year is not credited.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 5 (data written for this practice question): the share of UK electricity generated from coal fell from 28% in 2010 to 1% in 2023. The last UK coal-fired power station, Ratcliffe-on-Soar, closed in 2024.\n\nSuggest one reason why the share of UK electricity generated from coal has fallen.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid reason, plus 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• government targets to cut carbon emissions (1) because burning coal releases more CO₂ than any other fuel, adding to climate change (d)\n• carbon taxes made coal power more expensive (1) so gas and renewables became cheaper to generate (d)\n• UK coal reserves are expensive to mine / most deep mines closed (1) so coal had to be imported (d)\n• rapid growth of cheaper offshore wind (1) replaced coal power stations as they reached the end of their life (d).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nIceland lies on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and has many active volcanoes and fast-flowing glacial rivers. Almost all of its electricity comes from geothermal power and hydroelectric power (HEP).\n\nOutline one way that physical factors can affect the supply of energy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid point, plus 1 mark for development/example, e.g.:\n• geology determines where fossil fuels are found (1), e.g. oil and gas only exist in sedimentary basins such as the North Sea (d)\n• tectonic activity allows geothermal energy (1) as hot rocks near the surface heat water, as in Iceland (d)\n• relief and high rainfall allow HEP (1) because steep valleys can be dammed and rivers flow quickly, e.g. Norway (d)\n• climate affects renewables (1), e.g. sunny deserts suit solar and windy coasts suit wind turbines (d).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nIn 2022, after Russia invaded Ukraine, Russian gas supplies to many European countries were cut. The wholesale price of gas in Europe rose to several times its 2021 level.\n\nOutline one way that political factors can affect the supply of energy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid point, plus 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• conflict can cut supplies (1), e.g. Russia reduced gas exports to Europe in 2022 so prices rose sharply (d)\n• countries may use energy as a political weapon (1) by restricting exports to countries that oppose them (d)\n• governments can ban or restrict a source (1), e.g. the UK moratorium on fracking from 2019 (d)\n• political instability in producing countries (1) can stop production and investment, e.g. in parts of the Middle East (d).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nShale gas is trapped in rock with very low permeability. Until the 2000s it was too difficult to extract. Since then the USA has become one of the world's largest gas producers.\n\nOutline one way that technology has increased the supply of energy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid point, plus 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• horizontal drilling and hydraulic fracturing (fracking) (1) mean gas can be released from shale rock that was previously unusable (d)\n• deep-water drilling platforms (1) allow oil and gas to be extracted from deep oceans, e.g. the Gulf of Mexico (d)\n• improved, cheaper solar panels and larger wind turbines (1) generate more electricity per unit (d)\n• technology to cope with extreme cold (1) allows extraction in the Arctic, e.g. Alaska (d).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nWhen the world oil price fell sharply in 2014–2016, some oil fields in the North Sea were closed and plans for new fields were delayed.\n\nOutline how the cost of exploitation and production affects the supply of energy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid point, plus 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• reserves are only exploited if it is profitable (1), so when prices are low, expensive fields such as deep North Sea or Arctic fields are closed or not developed (d)\n• when prices rise (1) more expensive sources, e.g. tar sands or shale gas, become worth extracting so supply increases (d)\n• high start-up costs of nuclear power stations (1) can delay new supply for many years (d).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nYou have carried out fieldwork on household carbon footprints. Figure 6 (data written for this practice question) shows the results of a questionnaire for six households, in tonnes of CO₂ per year: 8.2, 11.6, 6.4, 14.0, 9.3, 7.5.\n\nUsing Figure 6, calculate the mean carbon footprint of the six households. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2 marks for 9.5 (tonnes of CO₂ per year).\n1 mark for correct working: 57.0 ÷ 6, or (8.2 + 11.6 + 6.4 + 14.0 + 9.3 + 7.5) ÷ 6.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 7 (data written for this practice question): a pie chart showing the carbon footprint of an average UK household. Transport (car and flights) 32%; home heating 26%; food 21%; electricity 11%; other goods and services 10%.\n\nUsing Figure 7, outline one way an individual could reduce their carbon footprint.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid way, plus 1 mark for development linked to Figure 7, e.g.:\n• walk, cycle or use public transport instead of the car (1), which cuts the largest part of the footprint, transport at 32% (d)\n• fly less / take holidays in the UK (1) as flights release large amounts of CO₂ per journey (d)\n• improve home insulation or turn the thermostat down (1) reducing heating, which is 26% (d)\n• eat local, seasonal food / less meat (1) reducing food miles and emissions from farming (d).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nBedZED in Sutton, south London, is a housing development completed in 2002. Homes face south, have very thick insulation and triple glazing, solar panels on the roofs and colourful wind cowls that ventilate the houses.\n\nOutline one way that the design of BedZED reduces energy use.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid feature, plus 1 mark for how it reduces energy use, e.g.:\n• thick insulation / triple glazing (1) keeps heat in so less energy is needed for heating (d)\n• south-facing windows (1) use passive solar gain to warm the homes naturally (d)\n• solar panels (1) generate electricity on site so less is taken from the grid (d)\n• wind cowls (1) provide natural ventilation instead of electric fans (d).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nHinkley Point C is a nuclear power station being built in Somerset. Construction began in 2017. It has been delayed several times and its estimated cost has risen to tens of billions of pounds.\n\nOutline one economic issue of using nuclear power to increase energy supply.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid issue, plus 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• very high construction costs (1), e.g. Hinkley Point C's cost has risen to tens of billions of pounds, which consumers pay for through bills (d)\n• delays mean no electricity is produced for many years (1) so the investment brings no return (d)\n• decommissioning and storing radioactive waste is very expensive (1) and continues for decades after the station closes (d)\nAllow a positive economic point, e.g. it creates thousands of construction jobs (1) boosting the local economy through the multiplier effect (d).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nIn Chambamontera, in the Andes of Peru, the charity Practical Action helped local people build a micro-hydro scheme. Water from a mountain stream is channelled downhill through a pipe to turn a small turbine.\n\nOutline one benefit of the micro-hydro scheme for the people of Chambamontera.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid benefit, plus 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• homes have electric light (1) so children can study in the evening / families no longer use smoky kerosene lamps (d)\n• electricity can power machinery (1), e.g. to process coffee, increasing farmers' incomes (d)\n• it is cheap to run (1) because the water is free and local people maintain the turbine themselves (d)\n• residents have radios, TV and phone charging (1) so they are better connected to the rest of Peru (d).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 4 (data written for this practice question): global primary energy consumption rose from 360 EJ in 1990 to 604 EJ in 2022. Over the same period world population grew from 5.3 billion to 8.0 billion, and the number of cars in China rose from under 10 million to over 300 million.\n\nExplain why global energy consumption is increasing.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Shows clear understanding of the reasons for rising consumption, with developed explanation of at least two factors, which may use Figure 4.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Shows basic understanding; reasons are listed or simply stated with little explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• economic development: as NEEs such as China and India industrialise, factories need more energy\n• rising incomes: more people buy cars, air conditioners, fridges and electronic devices\n• population growth: 2.7 billion more people since 1990 all need energy for cooking, heating and lighting\n• technology: more devices, data centres and the internet all use electricity\n• urbanisation increases demand for transport, lighting and building.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nA country has energy insecurity when it cannot be sure of a reliable and affordable supply of energy. Since 2022 many European countries have had high energy prices after gas supplies from Russia were cut.\n\nExplain the impacts of energy insecurity.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of at least two impacts, each explained (e.g. cause → effect chain).\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Basic understanding; impacts listed with little explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• exploration of difficult and environmentally sensitive areas (Arctic, deep oceans) increases the risk of pollution, e.g. oil spills\n• economic and environmental costs: high prices raise household bills and cause fuel poverty; industry costs rise so firms may close or move\n• food production: farmland may be used for biofuel crops instead of food, and fertiliser and machinery costs rise\n• industrial output falls if energy is rationed or too expensive\n• potential for conflict where demand exceeds supply, e.g. disputes over oil and gas in the South China Sea or the Arctic.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 2 (data written for this practice question): countries with a large energy surplus include Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Russia, Norway and Canada. Countries with an energy deficit include Japan, the UK, Germany and India.\n\nExplain why some countries have an energy surplus while others have an energy deficit.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of both supply and demand factors, with developed explanation and reference to examples.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Basic understanding; simple statements about resources with little explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• surplus countries have large reserves of fossil fuels due to their geology, e.g. oil in Saudi Arabia, gas in Qatar and Russia\n• some have small populations relative to their reserves, so they produce far more than they use (e.g. Qatar, Norway) and export the rest\n• deficit countries may have few reserves, e.g. Japan, or reserves that are running out / too expensive, e.g. the UK North Sea\n• deficit countries often have large populations and/or high industrial demand (Germany, Japan, India)\n• political choices, e.g. Germany closing its nuclear stations, can increase dependence on imports.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nIn 1989 the oil tanker Exxon Valdez ran aground in Prince William Sound, Alaska, spilling about 41 million litres of crude oil. Hundreds of thousands of seabirds and thousands of sea otters died.\n\nExplain the environmental issues of using fossil fuels to increase energy supply.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of a range of environmental issues across extraction, transport and use, with explanation.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Basic understanding; issues stated with little explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• burning fossil fuels releases CO₂, increasing the greenhouse effect and climate change\n• burning coal releases sulfur dioxide, causing acid rain that damages forests and lakes\n• oil spills during transport kill wildlife and pollute coasts, e.g. Exxon Valdez\n• extraction destroys habitats, e.g. open-cast coal mines, pipelines and roads across tundra\n• fracking may contaminate groundwater and trigger small earthquakes.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nHornsea, in the North Sea off the Yorkshire coast, is one of the world's largest offshore wind farms. Hundreds of turbines are linked by undersea cables to the National Grid on land.\n\nExplain the advantages and disadvantages of using wind power to increase energy supply.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of both advantages and disadvantages, each explained.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Basic understanding; advantages and/or disadvantages listed. An answer covering only advantages or only disadvantages is limited to Level 1.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\nAdvantages: renewable, will not run out; no CO₂ emitted when generating; offshore sites have stronger, more reliable winds and avoid visual impact on land; increases energy security by reducing gas imports; jobs in ports such as Hull.\nDisadvantages: intermittent, no electricity when the wind is too weak or too strong so back-up is needed; high construction and maintenance costs offshore; may harm birds and seabed habitats; onshore farms can be noisy and spoil views, leading to local opposition.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nChambamontera is a remote community in the Andes of northern Peru. Before the micro-hydro scheme, families used candles, kerosene lamps and wood fires. The scheme was built with local labour and is managed by a village committee; households pay a small monthly charge.\n\nExplain why the micro-hydro scheme at Chambamontera is an example of sustainable energy supply.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of how the scheme is sustainable, explaining environmental, economic and/or social aspects.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Basic understanding; simple statements, e.g. "it uses water".\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• renewable: uses the continuous flow of a mountain stream which will not run out\n• low environmental impact: no large dam or reservoir, so no flooding of land; no CO₂ or smoke\n• replaces kerosene and firewood, reducing deforestation and indoor air pollution\n• appropriate (intermediate) technology: simple to build and repair, so local people can maintain it without outside experts\n• affordable: low running costs; monthly fees pay for maintenance, so the scheme can continue long-term\n• community ownership and training give local people control and skills.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nOil was discovered at Prudhoe Bay on the north coast of Alaska in 1968. Today oil companies want to drill in the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge and in new fields such as the Willow project, which was approved in 2023.\n\nUsing a named example of an extraction site, explain the economic issues of extracting a fossil fuel.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of economic advantages and/or disadvantages, explained and supported by place-specific detail from a named example.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Basic understanding; generic points with little or no place detail.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content (Alaska; credit other valid examples, e.g. fracking in Lancashire):\n• jobs in drilling, the pipeline and services; oil revenue is a large share of the state's income\n• the Alaska Permanent Fund pays an annual dividend to residents from oil money\n• increases US energy security and reduces imports\n• very high costs of building and maintaining infrastructure in extreme cold and on permafrost, e.g. the Trans-Alaska Pipeline\n• output from Prudhoe Bay has declined, so jobs and revenue are falling\n• clean-up costs of spills, e.g. Exxon Valdez, and damage to fishing and tourism.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 8 (data written for this practice question): a UK energy company advertises "smart meters show you exactly how much energy you use, and cheaper electricity at night". The government also offers grants for loft insulation, heat pumps and electric car chargers.\n\nUsing Figure 8, explain how energy use can be reduced to create a more sustainable energy future.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2/AO3 Clear understanding of at least two ways of reducing energy use, explained and linked to Figure 8.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic understanding; strategies listed with little explanation.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• smart meters make people aware of their use, so they switch off devices and waste less\n• cheaper night-time electricity (demand reduction / time-of-use pricing) spreads demand so fewer power stations are needed at peak times\n• insulation reduces heat loss, so less gas is burned for heating\n• heat pumps are more efficient than gas boilers and can run on renewable electricity\n• electric cars are more efficient than petrol cars and, charged from renewables, cut emissions\n• individuals can also reduce their carbon footprint by using public transport, cycling or walking.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 9 (data written for this practice question): a fact file on energy insecurity in Europe, 2021–2023.\n• Average wholesale gas price: 2021 = 47 euros per MWh; 2022 = 130 euros per MWh; 2023 = 41 euros per MWh.\n• Some fertiliser factories in Germany and the UK temporarily stopped production.\n• Germany built new terminals to import liquefied natural gas (LNG) by ship from the USA and Qatar.\n• Governments paid billions to cap household energy bills.\n\nUsing Figure 9 and your own understanding, explain the impacts of energy insecurity.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Shows thorough geographical understanding of a range of impacts of energy insecurity. AO3 Makes detailed, relevant use of Figure 9 (e.g. quoting price changes) and develops chains of explanation.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Shows clear understanding of some impacts. AO3 Some use of Figure 9; explanation partly developed.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Basic understanding; impacts listed. AO3 Limited or no use of Figure 9.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• economic costs: gas price nearly trebled from 47 to 130 euros per MWh, raising household bills and causing fuel poverty\n• government spending on price caps diverts money from schools, hospitals etc.\n• industrial output falls: energy-intensive industries such as fertiliser production stopped\n• food production: fertiliser shortages raise food prices and may reduce yields\n• countries seek new supplies (LNG from Qatar and the USA), which is costly and may increase exploration of difficult areas\n• potential for conflict and political tension over supplies\n• environmental costs: some countries reopened coal power stations, raising emissions; but insecurity also sped up investment in renewables.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 10 (data written for this practice question): a fact file on Hinkley Point C nuclear power station, Somerset.\n• Planned capacity: 3,200 MW, enough for about 6 million homes (roughly 7% of UK electricity).\n• Construction began 2017; first electricity now expected in the 2030s.\n• Cost has risen from about £18 billion to tens of billions of pounds.\n• Around 10,000 people employed on site at the peak of construction.\n• Very low carbon emissions once running; radioactive waste must be stored for thousands of years.\n\nEvaluate the use of nuclear power as a strategy to increase energy supply. Use Figure 10 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Thorough understanding of the advantages and disadvantages of nuclear power. AO3 Detailed evaluation that weighs economic and environmental issues, uses Figure 10 effectively and reaches a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of some advantages and disadvantages. AO3 Some evaluation and use of Figure 10; judgement may be implied.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Basic understanding. AO3 Limited evaluation; list-like.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\nFor: large, reliable baseload supply (3,200 MW, about 7% of UK electricity) unlike intermittent wind and solar; very low carbon emissions; improves energy security by reducing gas imports; creates thousands of jobs (10,000 at peak) and boosts the local economy; long lifespan (about 60 years).\nAgainst: huge and rising cost and delays (from about £18 billion to tens of billions); consumers may pay higher prices; radioactive waste must be stored safely for thousands of years; risk of accidents (e.g. Fukushima 2011) and public fear; uranium is non-renewable and imported; long time before any electricity is produced.\nCredit a justified judgement either way, e.g. nuclear is useful alongside renewables to provide reliable low-carbon power, but is expensive and slow.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 11: two opinions about fracking in the UK.\nEnergy company spokesperson: "The Bowland Shale under Lancashire contains enough gas to supply the UK for decades. Fracking would create jobs, cut our dependence on imported gas and keep bills down."\nLocal resident near Preston New Road: "Fracking caused earthquakes here. It uses huge amounts of water and chemicals, brings lorries through our villages and it is still a fossil fuel that adds to climate change."\n\n'Fracking should be allowed to go ahead in the UK.' Do you agree? Use Figure 11 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Thorough understanding of the economic and environmental issues of fracking. AO3 Detailed evaluation of both opinions in Figure 11, with a well-supported conclusion that answers the question.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of some issues. AO3 Some evaluation; uses Figure 11; conclusion may be partial.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Basic understanding. AO3 Little evaluation; may simply repeat Figure 11.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\nAgree: large reserves in the Bowland Shale; improves energy security and reduces gas imports, important after the 2022 price rises; jobs and community payments; gas emits less CO₂ than coal and could be a "bridge" fuel; the USA's shale boom cut its gas prices.\nDisagree: induced earthquakes (magnitude 2.9 at Preston New Road in 2019) led to a moratorium; risk of groundwater contamination by chemicals; very large water use; lorry traffic, noise and visual impact in rural areas; methane leaks; delays the move to renewables and conflicts with net-zero targets; UK geology is more faulted than in the USA, and dense population means few suitable sites.\nCredit a supported decision either way, or a conditional view (e.g. only with strict regulation).` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nMany communities in LICs and NEEs have no access to reliable electricity. Small, local renewable schemes are one way of providing energy.\n\nUsing an example of a local renewable energy scheme in an LIC or NEE, explain how it provides sustainable supplies of energy.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO1 Demonstrates detailed knowledge of a named local scheme, with accurate place-specific detail. AO2 Thorough understanding of how the scheme is sustainable (environmental, economic and social), with well-developed explanation.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO1 Clear knowledge of an example with some detail. AO2 Clear understanding of some ways the scheme is sustainable.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO1 Limited or generic knowledge. AO2 Basic understanding; little explanation. An answer with no example is limited to Level 1.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content (Chambamontera micro-hydro, Peru; credit other valid local schemes, e.g. solar lanterns or biogas in an LIC):\n• remote Andean community not on the grid; Practical Action helped install a micro-hydro turbine using a local stream\n• renewable and low-impact: run-of-river, no large dam or reservoir, no emissions\n• replaces kerosene, candles and firewood, so less deforestation and cleaner indoor air\n• local people built the scheme and are trained to maintain it (appropriate technology), so it keeps running without outside help\n• households pay a small monthly fee, which covers repairs; loans helped with start-up costs\n• electricity supports lighting for study, radios/TV, and processing of coffee, raising incomes and quality of life.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 5 (data written for this practice question): sources of UK electricity.\n2010: gas 46%, coal 28%, nuclear 16%, renewables 7%, other 3%.\n2023: gas 34%, coal 1%, nuclear 14%, renewables 47%, other 4%.\nOn some calm, cold winter evenings in 2023, wind supplied less than 5% of UK electricity.\n\nAssess the extent to which renewable energy can meet the UK's energy needs. Use Figure 5 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Thorough understanding of the potential and limits of renewables. AO3 Detailed assessment using Figure 5 with data, reaching a well-supported judgement about the extent.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding. AO3 Some assessment and use of Figure 5; judgement may be implied.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Basic understanding. AO3 Limited use of Figure 5; little assessment.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• renewables rose from 7% to 47% in 13 years and are now the largest source, showing rapid growth is possible\n• UK has strong physical advantages: windy, shallow North Sea for offshore wind; tidal potential; some HEP in Scotland\n• renewables reduce emissions and imported gas, improving energy security\n• but wind and solar are intermittent (under 5% on calm winter evenings), so back-up from gas, nuclear, storage or interconnectors is still needed\n• gas still supplies about a third of electricity, and heating and transport still rely mainly on fossil fuels — electricity is only part of energy needs\n• costs of new grid connections and storage; local opposition to onshore wind and solar farms on farmland\n• judgement: renewables can meet a large and growing share, but not all needs at all times without storage and other sources.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 7 (data written for this practice question): the carbon footprint of an average UK household is about 13 tonnes of CO₂ per year. Transport (car and flights) 32%; home heating 26%; food 21%; electricity 11%; other goods and services 10%.\n\nUsing Figure 7 and your own understanding, discuss how individuals can help to move towards a more sustainable energy future.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Thorough understanding of ways individuals can reduce energy use and carbon footprints. AO3 Detailed discussion making effective use of Figure 7, considering how effective or realistic each action is.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of some actions. AO3 Some use of Figure 7 and some discussion.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Basic understanding; actions listed. AO3 Little use of Figure 7.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• transport is the largest share (32%): walk, cycle, car-share, use public transport, switch to an electric car, fly less\n• heating (26%): insulate lofts and walls, draught-proof, lower the thermostat, install a heat pump or solar panels\n• food (21%): eat local, seasonal food and less meat, reduce food waste\n• electricity (11%): switch off appliances, buy energy-efficient (A-rated) appliances and LED bulbs, choose a renewable tariff, use smart meters\n• discussion: some changes are cheap and easy; others (heat pumps, electric cars) are expensive so may not be affordable for all; individual action needs government support (grants, public transport); combined small changes can make a significant difference.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 12 (data written for this practice question):\n• Norway: steep mountains and high rainfall; about 90% of its electricity comes from HEP. It also exports large amounts of North Sea oil and gas.\n• Japan: few fossil fuel reserves; imports about 85% of its energy; after the Fukushima nuclear accident in 2011 most nuclear stations were shut down.\n• Chad: has oil reserves but few pipelines and power stations; fewer than 15% of people have electricity.\n\n'Physical factors are the most important influence on the supply of energy.' Do you agree? Use Figure 12 and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Thorough understanding of physical, cost, technological and political factors. AO3 Detailed evaluation using Figure 12, weighing physical factors against other factors, with a supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of some factors. AO3 Some evaluation and use of Figure 12.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Basic understanding. AO3 Little evaluation; may only describe Figure 12.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\nAgree: Norway's mountains and rainfall allow HEP; geology determines where oil and gas are found (North Sea); Japan's lack of reserves forces it to import; climate decides the potential of solar and wind.\nDisagree: cost of exploitation — reserves are only used if profitable; technology — e.g. fracking and deep-water drilling make new reserves accessible; Chad has oil but lacks money and technology to provide electricity; political factors — Japan's shutdown of nuclear power after Fukushima, sanctions, conflict (Russia 2022) and government policy (UK fracking moratorium) all change supply.\nCredit a judgement that physical factors set the potential but cost, technology and politics decide whether it is used.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nThe Trans-Alaska Pipeline crosses about 1,280 km of tundra, mountains and rivers from Prudhoe Bay to Valdez. Alaska is home to the Porcupine caribou herd, polar bears and important salmon fisheries.\n\nUsing a named example, evaluate the economic and environmental issues of extracting a fossil fuel.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO1 Detailed, accurate place-specific knowledge. AO2 Thorough understanding of economic and environmental issues. AO3 Evaluation weighs the issues and reaches a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO1 Clear knowledge with some place detail. AO2 Clear understanding of both economic and environmental issues. AO3 Some evaluation.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO1 Limited, generic knowledge. AO2 Basic understanding. AO3 Little or no evaluation. Answers covering only economic or only environmental issues are limited to Level 2.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content (Alaska; credit fracking in the UK or other valid examples):\nEconomic: thousands of jobs; oil taxes fund much of the state budget; Permanent Fund dividend paid to residents; US energy security; but extremely high costs of building and maintaining infrastructure in Arctic conditions; declining output reduces revenue; spill clean-up costs.\nEnvironmental: oil spills (Exxon Valdez 1989, about 41 million litres in Prince William Sound) killed seabirds, otters and fish; pipeline crosses permafrost — heat could melt it, so it is raised on supports; may disrupt caribou migration; roads and drilling pads fragment fragile tundra that recovers very slowly; burning the oil adds to climate change, which is warming the Arctic.\nJudgement e.g. economic benefits are large but short-term compared with long-lasting environmental damage in a fragile environment.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nThe Three Gorges Dam on the Yangtze River in China is the world's largest HEP station, with a capacity of 22,500 MW. Its reservoir is about 600 km long and over 1 million people had to be moved from their homes.\n\nAssess the economic and environmental issues of using renewable energy sources to increase energy supply. Use one or more examples in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: AO2 Thorough understanding of economic and environmental issues of renewables. AO3 Detailed assessment using examples, weighing benefits and problems, with a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of some issues. AO3 Some assessment and use of examples.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: AO2 Basic understanding; list-like. AO3 Little assessment.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• HEP (Three Gorges): reliable, large low-carbon supply; flood control and improved river navigation; but huge cost, over 1 million people displaced, farmland and heritage sites flooded, sediment trapped behind the dam, river ecosystems disrupted\n• wind: low running costs and no emissions, but intermittent and costly offshore; visual impact; bird deaths\n• solar: falling costs, suits sunny areas, small-scale use in LICs; but needs storage, large solar farms use farmland\n• biofuels/biomass: can be carbon-neutral, but crops compete with food production and may lead to deforestation\n• tidal/geothermal: reliable but limited to specific locations and expensive to build\n• judgement: environmental issues are usually smaller than for fossil fuels, but large schemes can have serious local impacts.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nKenya, an NEE in East Africa, needs much more electricity: fewer than half of rural households were connected to the grid in the 2010s. Figure 13 shows three options for increasing supply (details written for this practice question).\nOption 1 — Expand geothermal power at Olkaria in the Rift Valley: reliable 24-hour supply; very high drilling costs; near Hell's Gate National Park and Maasai land.\nOption 2 — Build a large coal-fired power station on the coast at Lamu: produces large amounts of cheap electricity quickly; coal must be imported; high CO₂ emissions; close to a World Heritage town and mangroves.\nOption 3 — Install solar mini-grids and solar home systems in remote rural counties: quick to install, villages manage them; supply only in daylight unless batteries are bought; smaller output.\n\nWhich option do you think would be the best way to increase Kenya's energy supply? Use Figure 13 and your own understanding to justify your decision. [9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO2 Shows thorough understanding of the economic, social and environmental issues of energy supply. AO3 Makes a well-justified decision, evaluating the chosen option and explaining why the other options are less suitable; uses Figure 13 in detail.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of the issues. AO3 Reasonable justification of the chosen option, with some reference to the others and to Figure 13.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: AO2 Basic understanding. AO3 Limited justification; may describe options from Figure 13 without evaluating them.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\nOption 1: renewable and low-carbon; reliable baseload (unlike solar/wind) using Kenya's tectonic advantage in the Rift Valley; reduces imports; but high cost and risk of drilling; conflict over land and displacement of Maasai; impacts on wildlife and tourism in Hell's Gate.\nOption 2: large output quickly supports industry; but imported coal reduces energy security and costs foreign currency; high CO₂ and air pollution; risk to mangroves, fishing and tourism at Lamu (the real licence was cancelled in 2019 after environmental objections — credit if mentioned).\nOption 3: reaches remote households that the grid cannot; community management is sustainable and bottom-up; improves lighting, education, phone charging and small businesses; but intermittent and smaller scale, batteries cost money, may not power large industry.\nAny option can reach Level 3 if well justified.\nSPaG (3 marks): High performance 3 — spelling and punctuation consistently accurate, rules of grammar used with effective control of meaning, wide range of specialist terms used precisely. Intermediate 2 — considerable accuracy, general control of meaning, good range of specialist terms. Threshold 1 — reasonable accuracy, does not seriously obstruct meaning, limited range of specialist terms. 0 — no relevant response or errors severely obstruct meaning.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nCountries are using many different strategies to increase their energy supply, including new nuclear power stations, offshore wind farms, fracking and oil drilling in the Arctic.\n\n'Strategies to increase energy supply always bring more environmental problems than economic benefits.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use one or more named examples in your answer. [9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO1 Detailed, accurate knowledge of named examples. AO2 Thorough understanding of environmental and economic issues of renewable and non-renewable strategies. AO3 Detailed evaluation weighing costs and benefits across strategies, leading to a substantiated conclusion about the extent.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: AO1 Clear knowledge with some named detail. AO2 Clear understanding. AO3 Some evaluation; conclusion may be partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: AO1 Limited knowledge; generic. AO2 Basic understanding. AO3 Little evaluation; one-sided.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• fossil fuels (e.g. Alaska oil): jobs, state revenue, energy security vs oil spills (Exxon Valdez), permafrost and tundra damage, emissions\n• fracking (e.g. Preston New Road, Lancashire): potential cheap gas and jobs vs earthquakes, water use, local opposition, emissions\n• nuclear (e.g. Hinkley Point C): low carbon and reliable vs huge cost, radioactive waste, accident risk\n• renewables (e.g. Hornsea offshore wind, Three Gorges HEP): low emissions and falling costs vs intermittency, habitat impacts, large-scale displacement for HEP\n• the statement says "always": renewables often bring economic benefits and fewer environmental problems; some non-renewables bring large economic benefits but serious environmental costs\n• credit discussion of short-term v long-term and local v global scale.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nSome fossil fuel reserves are found in fragile environments such as the Arctic tundra of Alaska, or under rural areas with dense populations, such as the Bowland Shale in Lancashire.\n\n'The benefits of extracting fossil fuels outweigh the costs.' Using a named example of an extraction site, to what extent do you agree? [9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO1 Detailed, accurate, place-specific knowledge of a named extraction site. AO2 Thorough understanding of economic, social and environmental benefits and costs. AO3 Detailed evaluation weighing benefits against costs, leading to a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: AO1 Clear knowledge with some place detail. AO2 Clear understanding of benefits and costs. AO3 Some evaluation with a partly supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: AO1 Limited or generic knowledge. AO2 Basic understanding. AO3 Limited evaluation; one-sided.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content (Alaska):\nBenefits: Prudhoe Bay is one of North America's largest oil fields; jobs; oil taxes fund much of Alaska's budget and the Permanent Fund dividend; improves US energy security; infrastructure (roads, airstrips) benefits remote communities.\nCosts: Exxon Valdez spill 1989 (about 41 million litres) damaged wildlife, fishing and tourism for years; pipeline built over permafrost and caribou migration routes; tundra recovers very slowly; Indigenous communities' traditional hunting may be affected; drilling in the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge or the Willow project is controversial; extra CO₂ adds to Arctic warming.\nIndicative content (fracking in the UK): benefits of domestic gas supply, jobs, community payments, lower imports; costs of tremors (2.9 in 2019), possible groundwater pollution, traffic and noise, protests, conflict with climate targets, leading to the moratorium.\nCredit a supported judgement in either direction.` },
      { q: `Section C — The challenge of resource management: Energy.\nFigure 14 (data written for this practice question): ways of moving towards a sustainable energy future.\n• An LED bulb uses about 80% less electricity than an old filament bulb.\n• A typical electric car produces about two-thirds less CO₂ over its lifetime than a petrol car when charged from the UK grid.\n• BedZED in south London uses insulation, solar panels and passive solar design to cut heating demand.\n• Average UK household carbon footprint: about 13 tonnes of CO₂ per year.\n\n'Energy-efficient technology is more important than changes in individual behaviour in creating a sustainable energy future.' Do you agree? Use Figure 14 and your own understanding. [9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: AO2 Thorough understanding of sustainable energy, efficient technology and individual energy use / carbon footprints. AO3 Detailed evaluation of both technology and behaviour, using Figure 14 and examples, with a well-supported conclusion.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: AO2 Clear understanding of both. AO3 Some evaluation and use of Figure 14; conclusion partly supported.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: AO2 Basic understanding. AO3 Limited evaluation; may list ideas from Figure 14.\n0 marks: no relevant content.\nIndicative content:\nTechnology: LED bulbs (80% less electricity), efficient appliances, heat pumps, insulation, smart meters, electric and hybrid cars, eco-homes such as BedZED; reduces energy use without people giving up comfort; but often expensive to buy, so needs grants; rebound effect (people use more because it is cheaper).\nBehaviour: individuals can cut carbon footprints (about 13 tonnes per household) by driving less, flying less, eating local food, turning heating down, switching off appliances; free and immediate; but relies on people choosing to change, and many changes are small or hard to sustain.\nLinks: technology works best with behaviour change (smart meters change habits); government policy, carbon pricing and renewable electricity also matter.\nCredit a justified judgement either way.` }
    ]
  },
  '3.1': {
    name: 'Issue Evaluation and Fieldwork',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): Easthaven is a village on a soft boulder clay coastline in north-east England. Figure 1 is a table of cliff retreat at Easthaven, 2014–2023 (metres per year): 2014 1.8; 2015 2.4; 2016 1.1; 2017 3.6; 2018 2.0; 2019 1.5; 2020 4.2; 2021 2.3; 2022 1.9; 2023 2.7.\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the mean annual rate of cliff retreat at Easthaven between 2014 and 2023. Give your answer in metres per year.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2.35 m per year (1 mark).\nWorking: total retreat 23.5 m ÷ 10 years = 2.35 m per year.\nAllow 2.4 m per year if rounded.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): At Easthaven, a village on a boulder clay coast in north-east England, the coast road that links the village to the nearest town is 47 m from the cliff edge. The mean rate of cliff retreat between 2014 and 2023 was 2.35 m per year.\n\nCalculate how many years it would take for the cliff edge to reach the coast road if the mean rate of retreat continues. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `20 years (2 marks).\n1 mark for correct working: 47 ÷ 2.35.\n1 mark for the correct answer: 20 years.\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): Figure 1 shows cliff retreat at Easthaven, a boulder clay coast in north-east England, 2014–2023 (metres per year): 1.8, 2.4, 1.1, 3.6, 2.0, 1.5, 4.2, 2.3, 1.9, 2.7. The highest values were in 2017 (3.6 m) and 2020 (4.2 m). Figure 2 notes that the winter of 2019–20 had a series of named storms and the wettest February on record.\n\nSuggest two reasons why the rate of cliff retreat varies from year to year.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid reason, up to 2 marks.\n• More frequent / more powerful storms in some winters give destructive waves and more hydraulic action and abrasion at the cliff foot.\n• Heavy or prolonged rainfall saturates the boulder clay, making it heavier and weaker so slumping / rotational slip is more likely (as in winter 2019–20).\n• Strong onshore winds with a long fetch in some years.\n• Storm surges / high spring tides allow waves to reach the cliff foot.\n• A previous large slump may protect the cliff foot for a year or so until the debris is removed.\n• Variations in beach width/sediment supply from year to year.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): Figure 3 is a photograph of the cliffs at Easthaven, north-east England. The cliffs are about 15 m high and made of brown boulder clay. Large slumped blocks lie at the cliff foot and there are cracks along the cliff top. The beach is narrow and waves reach the base of the cliff at high tide. Two kilometres to the north (updrift), the town of Northby is protected by groynes that hold a wide sandy beach. The dominant direction of longshore drift is north to south.\n\nUsing Figure 3 and your own understanding, explain why the cliffs at Easthaven are eroding rapidly.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation of physical and human factors, using evidence from Figure 3; clear links between processes and the rate of erosion.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic explanation; may list factors with limited use of the figure.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Boulder clay is soft, unconsolidated rock that is easily eroded by hydraulic action and abrasion.\n• Rainfall saturates the clay, which then slumps (cracks on the cliff top, slumped blocks at the foot).\n• The narrow beach gives little protection, so waves reach the cliff foot at high tide and remove slumped material quickly.\n• Groynes at Northby trap sediment moved by longshore drift from the north, so less sediment reaches Easthaven and its beach is starved.\n• Destructive waves with a long fetch from the North Sea.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): Option 1 for Easthaven, a village on an eroding boulder clay coast in north-east England, is to 'hold the line' by placing 600 m of rock armour along the cliff foot and building two rock groynes. Cost £9.5 million; expected life 50 years. Longshore drift moves sediment from north to south. To the south (downdrift) of Easthaven are 3 km of farmland, a small hamlet of 12 houses and a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) at a sand spit.\n\nAssess the possible effects of Option 1 on people and the environment to the south of Easthaven.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed assessment of effects on both people and the environment downdrift; makes a judgement on the significance of effects; uses evidence from the resource well.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear understanding of how the scheme could affect areas downdrift; some assessment.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic statements about effects with little assessment.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Groynes trap sediment moving south, so beaches downdrift become narrower (terminal groyne effect).\n• Narrower beaches mean less protection, so erosion speeds up south of the scheme; farmland lost; farmers lose income and may receive no compensation.\n• The hamlet of 12 houses could be at greater risk; property values fall; may cause conflict between communities.\n• Reduced sediment supply could starve the spit SSSI, harming habitats and the spit itself.\n• Rock armour is visually intrusive but does not directly affect areas to the south.\n• Judgement: effects may be serious because the scheme moves the problem along the coast; may be limited if the groynes are short or sediment is recharged.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): Easthaven is a village of 34 homes (total value £6.8 million) on a boulder clay coast in north-east England. The cliffs retreat at a mean of 2.35 m per year and the coast road is 47 m from the cliff edge. A caravan park with 120 pitches employs 25 local people. Three options are proposed.\nOption 1 — Hold the line: 600 m of rock armour and two rock groynes. Cost £9.5 million, 50-year life. Likely to increase erosion downdrift.\nOption 2 — Managed retreat: move the coast road 200 m inland, compensate the owners of the 12 homes closest to the cliff and help the caravan park 'roll back' inland. Cost £4.2 million.\nOption 3 — No active intervention: no money spent; homes and the road would be lost as the cliff retreats.\nViews: Resident — 'My family has lived here for 60 years. We should be protected like Northby.' Council officer — 'We cannot justify spending more than the value of the property we are protecting.' Natural England — 'Sediment from these cliffs feeds the protected spit to the south.'\n\nWhich option do you think would be the best for the future of Easthaven? Use evidence from the Resource Booklet and your own understanding to explain your decision. [9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: Detailed evaluation of the chosen option, with a well-justified decision supported by evidence from the Resource Booklet and own understanding; shows thorough understanding of the interrelationships between social, economic and environmental factors; considers the drawbacks of the chosen option and why other options were rejected.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: Clear evaluation with a reasoned decision; some use of evidence from the booklet; some consideration of other options.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: Basic statements about an option; limited use of evidence; decision may be asserted rather than justified.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content (any option can reach Level 3 if well justified):\n• Option 1: protects all 34 homes, the road and the caravan park jobs; but £9.5 million is more than the £6.8 million value of the homes, so fails a cost–benefit test; terminal groyne effect would increase erosion downdrift and starve the spit SSSI; rock armour is unattractive to tourists.\n• Option 2: cheaper (£4.2 million) and keeps the road open; caravan park and 25 jobs survive by rolling back; natural sediment supply to the spit continues; sustainable in the long term. But 12 families lose their homes and community ties; compensation may not reflect emotional value.\n• Option 3: no cost to the taxpayer and best for the natural system; but road reached in about 20 years (47 ÷ 2.35), cutting off the village; loss of homes and jobs with no compensation; social conflict.\n• Credit use of calculated data, the views given, and links to Shoreline Management Plan policies.\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance 3 marks: spelling and punctuation consistently accurate; rules of grammar used with effective control of meaning; wide range of specialist terms used precisely.\nIntermediate performance 2 marks: considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance 1 mark: reasonable accuracy; does not hinder meaning; limited specialist terms.\n0 marks: no relevant content or below threshold.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): A company has proposed a wind farm at Harlow Fell, an area of upland moorland in northern England. Figure 4 shows the results of a survey of 250 local residents: strongly support 38; support 62; neutral 45; oppose 70; strongly oppose 35.\n\nUsing Figure 4, calculate the percentage of residents who support or strongly support the wind farm.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `40% (1 mark).\nWorking: (38 + 62) ÷ 250 × 100 = 40%.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): The proposed Harlow Fell wind farm in northern England would have 14 turbines, each with a capacity of 3.6 MW. The company has promised a community benefit fund of £5,000 per MW of capacity each year, to be spent on local projects.\n\nCalculate the total amount of money that would be paid into the community benefit fund each year. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `£252,000 (2 marks).\n1 mark for working: 14 × 3.6 = 50.4 MW, then 50.4 × £5,000.\n1 mark for the correct answer: £252,000.\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): Figure 4 shows the results of a survey of 250 residents about a proposed wind farm at Harlow Fell, northern England: strongly support 38; support 62; neutral 45; oppose 70; strongly oppose 35.\n\nUsing Figure 4, describe the views of local residents about the proposed wind farm.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid descriptive point, up to 2 marks. Use of data is needed for 2 marks.\n• Opinion is divided / no clear majority either way.\n• Slightly more residents oppose (105, 42%) than support (100, 40%).\n• 'Oppose' is the single largest category (70).\n• Almost a fifth are neutral (45, 18%).\n• Fewer people hold strong views (38 strongly support, 35 strongly oppose) than moderate views.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): Figure 5 is a map of Harlow Fell, northern England. The proposed 14 turbines (each 150 m to blade tip) would be built on a ridge of blanket peat bog that is a breeding area for curlew. A new 6 km access track would be cut across the moor. The site is 4 km from the edge of a National Park and can be seen from a popular walking route. The nearest village, Harlow, has 1,800 residents; two farms and three holiday cottages are within 1 km of a turbine.\n\nUsing Figure 5, explain why some people might oppose the wind farm.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation of reasons for opposition, using specific evidence from Figure 5; points are developed.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic reasons, mostly listed, with limited use of the figure.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Visual impact: 150 m turbines on a ridge, visible from the walking route and close to a National Park, could reduce the attraction for tourists and walkers.\n• Holiday cottage owners near the turbines may lose bookings and income.\n• Noise/shadow flicker for the farms and cottages within 1 km.\n• Damage to blanket peat by the access track and foundations releases stored carbon and harms the habitat.\n• Risk to breeding curlew from disturbance and turbine blades.\n• Some may feel the benefits (electricity, profit) go to the company rather than the community.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): The proposed Harlow Fell wind farm in northern England would have a capacity of 50.4 MW, enough to supply about 40,000 homes, and would pay £252,000 a year into a community fund. It would create 120 construction jobs for 18 months and 6 permanent jobs. Figure 6 gives four views:\nLocal farmer: 'Rent from three turbines would double my income.'\nHoliday cottage owner: 'Visitors come here for the wild, empty landscape.'\nWildlife Trust: 'Building on deep peat will damage one of England's best curlew sites.'\nYoung resident: 'The fund could pay for the bus service and the new village hall. We need to do our bit to cut carbon emissions.'\n\nUsing Figure 6, suggest why there are conflicting views about the proposed wind farm.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed suggestions that link each viewpoint to the interests and values of the group; clear sense of why benefits and costs fall on different people; good use of the resource.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear suggestions of reasons for conflict; some use of the resource.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic points, may just repeat the views.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Benefits are uneven: the farmer gains rent; the young resident values services funded by the £252,000 a year; others gain little directly.\n• Costs are uneven: the cottage owner fears loss of tourist income because the landscape attracts visitors.\n• Different values: economic gain versus conserving landscape and wildlife (curlew, peat).\n• Scale: global/national benefit (low-carbon electricity for 40,000 homes, energy security) versus local costs.\n• Time: short-term construction jobs (120 for 18 months) compared with only 6 permanent jobs; peat damage may be long-term.\n• Peat stores carbon, so environmental groups argue the carbon saving may be reduced.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): The Harlow Fell wind farm (14 turbines, 50.4 MW) is proposed on upland peat moorland in northern England, 4 km from a National Park. It would supply about 40,000 homes and pay £252,000 a year to a community fund. A survey of 250 residents found 40% in support, 42% opposed and 18% neutral. The site is a breeding area for curlew. Figure 7 shows that the share of UK electricity from wind rose from about 2% in 2008 to about 29% in 2023.\n\n'The Harlow Fell wind farm should be given planning permission.' Do you agree? Use evidence from the Resource Booklet and your own understanding.`, marks: 6, command: 'Do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed, balanced evaluation with a clear, justified conclusion; thorough use of evidence from the booklet and own understanding of UK energy issues.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear evaluation with some justification; some use of evidence.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic points for or against; little justification.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• For: renewable, low-carbon electricity for 40,000 homes; helps UK meet net-zero targets and reduces reliance on imported gas; community fund of £252,000 a year can improve local services; continues the national trend in Figure 7.\n• Against: damage to peat bog releases carbon and harms curlew habitat; visual impact near a National Park and on tourism; local opinion is split, with slightly more opposed; few permanent jobs.\n• Conditions could reduce impacts (moving turbines off deep peat, habitat restoration, restricting construction outside the breeding season).\n• Credit a clear judgement that weighs the national need against local costs.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): Figure 9 shows the national electricity generation of an NEE where the Sarani Gorge hydroelectric dam is proposed. In 2015 the country generated 4,800 GWh. Once the dam is operating in 2030, generation is forecast to be 7,200 GWh.\n\nCalculate the percentage change in electricity generation between 2015 and 2030. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `+50% / an increase of 50% (2 marks).\n1 mark for working: (7,200 − 4,800) ÷ 4,800 × 100.\n1 mark for the correct answer: 50% increase.\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): Figure 10 describes the Sarani Gorge hydroelectric dam proposed in an NEE. Only 38% of rural households in the country have mains electricity, compared with 91% in cities. Factories in the capital lose an average of 9 hours of production each month to power cuts. The dam would also store water for irrigation of 45,000 hectares of farmland, and 4,000 construction jobs would be created.\n\nUsing Figure 10, explain how the dam could help the economic development of the country.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation of two or more ways the dam could aid development, with developed chains of reasoning and use of the figure.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic points with limited development.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Reliable electricity reduces power cuts, so factories increase output and attract foreign investment (TNCs).\n• Rural electrification (from 38%) allows small businesses, refrigeration, lighting for study, improving education and incomes.\n• Irrigation of 45,000 ha raises crop yields and allows a second harvest, increasing food security and farm exports.\n• 4,000 construction jobs give wages that are spent locally (multiplier effect).\n• Less need to import fuel, saving foreign currency.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): A Sarani Gorge hydroelectric dam is proposed in an NEE. Downstream of the dam site, 60,000 farmers grow rice on a floodplain that floods each year, and the river delivers sediment to a delta used by fishing communities.\n\nOutline one way the dam could affect people living downstream.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point; 2 marks for a developed point.\n• The dam traps sediment (1), so floodplain soils receive less silt and farmers must buy fertiliser / yields fall (1).\n• Flooding is controlled (1), so homes and crops are at less risk of damage (1).\n• Less sediment reaches the delta (1), so the coast erodes and fishing grounds/fish catches decline (1).\n• Regulated flow allows irrigation all year (1), so farmers can grow more than one crop (1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): The Sarani Gorge hydroelectric dam proposed in an NEE would cost US$2.8 billion, much of it a loan from a foreign bank. It would generate 1,200 MW, increase national electricity generation by 50% and irrigate 45,000 hectares. The reservoir (210 km²) would flood 26 villages, an area of forest and a sacred site, and 18,500 people would be resettled to land 40 km away. A local leader says: 'Our families have farmed this valley for generations. The new land is poor and far from the river.'\n\nTo what extent do the benefits of the Sarani Gorge dam outweigh the costs? Use evidence from the Resource Booklet.`, marks: 6, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed weighing of economic, social and environmental benefits and costs with a clear, supported judgement; considers who benefits and who loses and at what scale.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear discussion of benefits and costs; some judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic list of benefits or costs; little or no judgement.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Benefits: 50% more electricity; renewable, low-carbon energy; irrigation supports food supply; jobs; supports industrial growth; flood control.\n• Costs: 18,500 people resettled onto poorer land; loss of culture, sacred site and community; forest and river habitats lost; sediment trapping downstream; debt repayments on a foreign loan; methane from flooded vegetation.\n• Scale: benefits mostly national/urban; costs mostly local and borne by rural people with least power.\n• Judgement could depend on fair compensation, quality of resettlement land, and whether smaller schemes could deliver benefits with fewer costs.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): Castlebridge Docks is a former dockland ward in a UK city. Figure 11 shows the ward's population: 2001 — 6,240; 2021 — 4,680. Most dock jobs were lost in the 1980s and 1990s.\n\nCalculate the percentage change in the population of Castlebridge Docks between 2001 and 2021. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `−25% / a decrease of 25% (2 marks).\n1 mark for working: (4,680 − 6,240) ÷ 6,240 × 100.\n1 mark for the correct answer: −25% (must show it is a decrease).\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): Figure 12 compares Castlebridge Docks, a former dockland ward in a UK city, with the city average. Unemployment: 11.2% (city 4.8%). Households with no car: 52% (city 31%). Adults with no qualifications: 34% (city 19%). Area of derelict or vacant land: 48 hectares. The ward is in the most deprived 10% of areas in England.\n\nUsing Figure 12, explain how regeneration of the docks could reduce deprivation in Castlebridge.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation linking regeneration to specific indicators in Figure 12; developed points.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic points; limited use of the data.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Building on 48 ha of derelict land brings new businesses and jobs, reducing unemployment (11.2%) if local people are recruited.\n• Training/apprenticeship schemes or a new college could reduce the 34% with no qualifications.\n• Improved public transport (tram/bus links) helps the 52% with no car reach jobs across the city.\n• Clearing derelict land improves the environment, attracting further investment (multiplier effect) and reducing crime/vandalism.\n• New services (health centre, shops) improve quality of life.` },
      { q: `Section A — Issue evaluation. Resource Booklet (data written for this practice question): Castlebridge Docks is a former dockland ward in a UK city. Its population fell by 25% between 2001 and 2021; unemployment is 11.2% (city 4.8%) and 52% of households have no car. There are 48 hectares of derelict land beside the river. The city council must choose one plan.\nOption 1 — Waterfront apartments and marina: 1,400 luxury apartments, restaurants and a marina. Developer pays all costs. 900 jobs, mostly in hospitality. No affordable housing.\nOption 2 — Mixed community: 800 homes (40% affordable), a riverside park, a health centre and a training college. Cost to council £60 million. 500 jobs.\nOption 3 — Retail and leisure park: large stores, a cinema and 2,000 parking spaces. Developer pays. 1,200 jobs, many part-time. Expected to draw shoppers away from the city centre.\nViews: Long-term resident — 'Our children cannot afford to stay here.' Developer — 'Only high-value homes will make the area attractive to investors.' City-centre trader — 'Another out-of-town style retail park will empty our high street.'\n\nWhich option do you think would be the best for Castlebridge Docks? Use evidence from the Resource Booklet and your own understanding to explain your decision. [9 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 9, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: Detailed evaluation of the chosen option with a well-justified decision; thorough use of evidence from the booklet; considers social, economic and environmental effects and who gains and loses; explains why other options were rejected.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: Clear evaluation with a reasoned decision; some evidence; some reference to other options.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: Basic statements; decision asserted; little use of evidence.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content (any option can reach Level 3 if well justified):\n• Option 1: no cost to the council; high-value investment changes the image of the area; 900 jobs. But gentrification – no affordable homes, local residents priced out; jobs mostly low-paid hospitality; does little for the 52% with no car.\n• Option 2: affordable housing keeps families and may reverse the 25% population fall; training college tackles low qualifications; health centre and park improve quality of life and the environment; more sustainable. But costs the council £60 million and creates the fewest jobs (500).\n• Option 3: most jobs (1,200), no cost to the council; but many part-time; harms the city-centre high street; 2,000 parking spaces increase traffic and are of little use to residents without cars.\n• Credit reference to examples of UK regeneration and to sustainability.\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance 3 marks: spelling and punctuation consistently accurate; rules of grammar used with effective control of meaning; wide range of specialist terms used precisely.\nIntermediate performance 2 marks: considerable accuracy; general control of meaning; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance 1 mark: reasonable accuracy; does not hinder meaning; limited specialist terms.\n0 marks: no relevant content or below threshold.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. A group of students investigated how channel characteristics change downstream along a small river in the Lake District. They measured the river at six sites, one every 1 km along the river from a starting point near the source.\n\nIdentify the type of sampling the students used to choose their sites.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Systematic sampling (1 mark).` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. Figure 13 (data written for this practice question): a student counted pedestrians for 5 minutes at 10 sites along a transect from the centre of a market town, at 100 m intervals. Counts at sites 1–10: 112, 87, 64, 45, 51, 33, 28, 19, 22, 9.\n\nUsing Figure 13, calculate the median pedestrian count.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `39 (1 mark).\nWorking: in rank order 9, 19, 22, 28, 33, 45, 51, 64, 87, 112; median = (33 + 45) ÷ 2 = 39.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. Figure 14 (data written for this practice question): river data from six sites along a river in the Lake District. Distance downstream (km): 0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5, 5.5. Width (m): 1.2, 1.9, 2.4, 2.2, 3.6, 4.1. Mean depth (cm): 8, 12, 15, 21, 19, 27. Mean velocity (m/s): 0.21, 0.26, 0.30, 0.34, 0.39, 0.45.\n\nUsing Figure 14, identify the site at which river width does not fit the general trend.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Site 4 / 3.5 km downstream (width 2.2 m is narrower than at site 3) (1 mark).` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. Figure 15 (data written for this practice question): a student measured the long axis of 11 pebbles at one site on a beach in Dorset. Lengths (cm): 4.2, 5.1, 3.8, 6.4, 7.0, 4.9, 5.5, 8.3, 3.1, 5.8, 6.1.\n\nUsing Figure 15, calculate the median pebble length.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `5.5 cm (1 mark).\nWorking: in rank order 3.1, 3.8, 4.2, 4.9, 5.1, 5.5, 5.8, 6.1, 6.4, 7.0, 8.3; the 6th value is 5.5.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. Figure 14 (data written for this practice question): at site 6 on a river in the Lake District, the students recorded a width of 4.1 m, a mean depth of 0.27 m and a mean velocity of 0.45 m/s. Discharge = cross-sectional area × velocity.\n\nCalculate the discharge of the river at site 6. Give your answer in m³/s to two decimal places. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0.50 m³/s (2 marks).\n1 mark for working: cross-sectional area = 4.1 × 0.27 = 1.107 m², then 1.107 × 0.45.\n1 mark for the correct answer: 0.50 m³/s (allow 0.498).\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. Figure 15 (data written for this practice question): a student measured the long axis of 11 pebbles at one site on a beach in Dorset. Lengths (cm): 4.2, 5.1, 3.8, 6.4, 7.0, 4.9, 5.5, 8.3, 3.1, 5.8, 6.1.\n\nUsing Figure 15, calculate the interquartile range of pebble length. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2.2 cm (2 marks).\n1 mark for identifying the quartiles: in rank order 3.1, 3.8, 4.2, 4.9, 5.1, 5.5, 5.8, 6.1, 6.4, 7.0, 8.3; lower quartile = 3rd value = 4.2 cm; upper quartile = 9th value = 6.4 cm.\n1 mark for the correct answer: 6.4 − 4.2 = 2.2 cm.\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. Figure 14 (data written for this practice question): river data from six sites along a river in the Lake District. Distance downstream (km): 0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5, 5.5. Mean depth (cm): 8, 12, 15, 21, 19, 27.\n\nDraw a scatter graph of mean depth against distance downstream and add a line of best fit.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `1 mark for all six points plotted accurately, with distance downstream (km) on the x-axis and mean depth (cm) on the y-axis, both axes labelled with a suitable scale.\n1 mark for a straight line of best fit rising from left to right, with roughly equal numbers of points either side (passing close to about 8 cm at 0.5 km and about 26 cm at 5.5 km). Do not credit a line joining point to point.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. A student wanted to find out residents' views about a new bypass around a town in Shropshire. The town has three wards: Ward A with 4,000 adults, Ward B with 2,500 and Ward C with 1,500. The student used stratified sampling to choose 80 people to complete a questionnaire.\n\nOutline one advantage of using stratified sampling in this investigation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point; 2 marks for a developed point.\n• Each ward is represented in proportion to its population (1), e.g. 40 from A, 25 from B and 15 from C (1), so results are more representative of the whole town (1).\n• It avoids bias towards one ward (1), so differences in opinion between wards (e.g. those nearest the bypass) can be compared fairly (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. You have carried out fieldwork in a physical geography environment, for example a river, a coast or a slope.\n\nOutline one risk you faced while collecting your physical geography data and how you reduced this risk.`, marks: 2, command: 'Outline', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a relevant risk for the location; 1 mark for an appropriate way of reducing it.\n• Fast-flowing / deep water in a river (1); only entered at shallow sites below knee depth, in pairs, wearing wellingtons (1).\n• Being cut off by the incoming tide on a beach (1); checked tide times and worked on a falling tide (1).\n• Rock falls from cliffs (1); stayed away from the cliff foot (1).\n• Slipping on wet rocks (1); wore suitable footwear and avoided algae-covered rocks (1).\nRisk and method must match.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. You have carried out fieldwork in a human geography environment, for example a town centre, a rebranded area or a rural settlement.\n\nSuggest one limitation of the method you used to collect primary data in your human geography enquiry.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point; 2 marks for a developed point linked to the method used.\n• Pedestrian counts were taken for only 5 minutes at each site (1), so a short-term event such as a bus arriving could make the count unrepresentative (1).\n• Questionnaires were completed only by people willing to stop (1), so the sample may be biased, e.g. towards older people (1).\n• Environmental quality scores are subjective (1), so different students might give the same site different scores (1).\n• Data were collected on one weekday (1), so they may not reflect weekends or other seasons (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. You have carried out fieldwork in a physical geography environment.\n\nExplain why your choice of location was suitable for your physical geography enquiry.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation of why the named location was suitable, linked to the aim of the enquiry; detailed reference to the location.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic reasons, which may be generic (e.g. 'it was safe') with little link to the enquiry.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• The location allowed the question to be investigated, e.g. a river with accessible sites from upper to lower course to test changes downstream; a beach with groynes to test longshore drift.\n• It showed the features being studied, e.g. a sequence of dunes from embryo to grey dunes.\n• Safe access: shallow water, footpaths, public land, permission obtained.\n• Close enough to reach and study within the time available.\n• Secondary data available for the site, e.g. Environment Agency gauging station data, OS maps.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. You have carried out fieldwork in a physical geography environment.\n\nExplain the geographical theory or concept on which your physical geography enquiry was based.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear and accurate explanation of a relevant theory/concept, showing how it shaped the enquiry question or hypothesis.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic statement of a theory with limited explanation or link to the enquiry.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Rivers: the Bradshaw model – width, depth, velocity and discharge increase downstream while gradient and sediment size decrease, because tributaries add water and attrition reduces load size; led to a hypothesis such as 'Channel width increases with distance downstream'.\n• Coasts: longshore drift moves sediment in the direction of the prevailing wind, so sediment builds up on the updrift side of groynes; or the effectiveness of coastal management.\n• Sand dunes: plant succession – vegetation cover and species diversity increase inland as conditions become less harsh.\n• Credit accurate geographical terminology.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. A student compared the environmental quality of two areas of Sheffield: a regenerated riverside area and an older terraced-housing area. At each area she stood at one point on a Tuesday afternoon and scored five factors (litter, noise, graffiti, green space, building condition) on a bipolar scale from −2 to +2. She then added the scores to give a total for each area.\n\nSuggest how the reliability of her data collection could be improved.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear suggestions that are developed to show how each change would make the results more reliable.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic suggestions with little explanation.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Survey several sites in each area (e.g. systematic sites every 50 m) rather than a single point, so results represent the whole area.\n• Repeat the survey at different times / days (weekday and weekend, morning and evening) to reduce the effect of one-off conditions.\n• Have several students score each site and take the mean, reducing subjective bias.\n• Agree clear criteria/photographs for each score so scoring is consistent.\n• Add more factors, or support scores with photographs and noise-meter readings.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. Students investigated whether shoppers' perceptions of a high street in Lincoln had improved since it was redeveloped. They planned to stop 50 shoppers on a Saturday morning and ask them to complete a questionnaire with closed questions (e.g. 'Rate the cleanliness of the street from 1 to 5') and one open question ('What would you change about the street?').\n\nExplain the advantages and disadvantages of using a questionnaire for this investigation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation of both advantages and disadvantages, applied to this investigation.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic points, may cover only advantages or disadvantages, or be generic.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Advantages: gathers people's perceptions, which cannot be measured with equipment; closed questions give numerical data that can be totalled, averaged and graphed; open question gives detailed qualitative views and ideas the students had not thought of.\n• Disadvantages: people may refuse or rush answers; a Saturday morning sample may not represent weekday shoppers or older/younger groups; open answers are hard to compare and analyse; shoppers may not remember the street before redevelopment; question wording could lead people to particular answers.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. Figure 14 (data written for this practice question): river data from six sites along a river in the Lake District. Distance downstream (km): 0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5, 5.5. Width (m): 1.2, 1.9, 2.4, 2.2, 3.6, 4.1. Mean depth (cm): 8, 12, 15, 21, 19, 27. Mean velocity (m/s): 0.21, 0.26, 0.30, 0.34, 0.39, 0.45.\n\nUsing Figure 14 and your own understanding, analyse how the channel characteristics of the river change downstream.`, marks: 6, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed analysis using data accurately (including manipulation, e.g. increases or comparisons), recognising anomalies, and linking patterns to geographical theory/processes.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear description of patterns with some data and some explanation.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic description of a trend; little data or explanation.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• All three characteristics generally increase downstream: width from 1.2 m to 4.1 m (more than three times wider); depth from 8 cm to 27 cm; velocity from 0.21 to 0.45 m/s (more than doubles).\n• Velocity is the only variable that increases at every site; width falls at site 4 (2.2 m) and depth falls at site 5 (19 cm) – anomalies may reflect local features such as a riffle, bedrock or a meander, or measurement error.\n• Possible inverse link at site 4: narrower but deeper channel.\n• Explanation: tributaries add water so discharge rises; more lateral erosion widens the channel; smoother, more efficient channel with less friction allows faster flow, as in the Bradshaw model.\n• Only six sites over 5.5 km, so the trend should be treated with caution.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. Figure 13 (data written for this practice question): a student counted pedestrians for 5 minutes at 10 sites along a transect from the centre of a market town, at 100 m intervals (site 1 is at the market square). Counts at sites 1–10: 112, 87, 64, 45, 51, 33, 28, 19, 22, 9. Site 5 is next to a supermarket and site 9 is next to a bus stop. The counts were made between 10:00 and 11:30 on one Wednesday.\n\n'Pedestrian numbers decrease with distance from the town centre.' Assess how far the data in Figure 13 support this hypothesis.`, marks: 6, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed assessment that uses data accurately, identifies and explains anomalies, considers the reliability of the data, and reaches a clear judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear use of data to support the hypothesis, with some recognition of anomalies or limitations.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic statement that the data support or do not support the hypothesis.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Strong overall support: counts fall from 112 at site 1 to 9 at site 10; the first four sites alone account for more than half the total.\n• Steepest decline near the centre (112 to 64 over the first 200 m), reflecting the concentration of shops and services in the CBD.\n• Anomalies at site 5 (51 > 45) and site 9 (22 > 19), explained by the supermarket and the bus stop.\n• Reliability: single 5-minute counts on one weekday morning; results might differ on a Saturday or in the afternoon; only one transect.\n• Judgement: the hypothesis is largely supported, but more repeat counts and transects would be needed to be confident.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. You have carried out fieldwork in a human geography environment.\n\nEvaluate the effectiveness of your primary data collection methods in your human geography enquiry.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed evaluation of named methods, with strengths and limitations explained in the context of the enquiry and a judgement on how effective the methods were in answering the question.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear discussion of strengths and/or limitations of methods used, with some reference to the enquiry.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic description of methods with little evaluation.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Methods may include questionnaires, pedestrian or traffic counts, land-use mapping, environmental quality surveys, photographs, interviews.\n• Strengths: e.g. counts gave quantitative data that could be compared between sites; questionnaires gave views on the issue; field sketches/photos recorded evidence.\n• Limitations: sample size, timing and sampling strategy; subjectivity of EQS scores; group members recording differently; weather affecting counts.\n• Judgement on whether the data were accurate, reliable and enough to reach a valid conclusion, with suggested improvements.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. You have carried out fieldwork in a physical geography environment.\n\nTo what extent were your sampling methods appropriate for your physical geography enquiry?`, marks: 6, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed evaluation of the sampling method(s) actually used (random, systematic and/or stratified), explaining strengths and weaknesses in the context of the named enquiry and reaching a supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear explanation of the sampling method used with some evaluation.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic description of sampling with little evaluation.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Systematic sampling of river sites (e.g. every 1 km) covered the whole river evenly so trends downstream could be identified; but access points meant some sites were moved, so spacing was not exact.\n• Random sampling of pebbles (e.g. picking the pebble touching a toe/metre-rule point) reduced bias towards larger pebbles.\n• Stratified sampling of dune zones ensured each zone was represented.\n• Sample size: number of sites/pebbles/quadrats and whether this was enough to be representative.\n• Judgement and suggested improvements (more sites, repeats, a mix of methods).` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. A student is planning an investigation into how vegetation changes inland across the sand dunes at Studland, Dorset. Her plan: walk inland from the strandline for 200 m; place a 1 m² quadrat wherever the vegetation 'looks interesting'; at each quadrat estimate percentage plant cover and name the species; work alone on a single afternoon; present the results as a pie chart for each quadrat.\n\nEvaluate the student's plan.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 5–6 marks: Detailed evaluation of strengths and weaknesses across sampling, data collection, safety and presentation, with well-justified improvements and an overall judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 3–4 marks: Clear identification of strengths and weaknesses with some improvements.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–2 marks: Basic comments on the plan.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Strengths: a transect from the strandline inland matches the expected succession; quadrats and percentage cover are suitable methods; recording species allows diversity to be compared.\n• Weaknesses: choosing quadrats where vegetation 'looks interesting' is biased – use systematic sampling (e.g. every 10 m) or stratified sampling by dune zone; no measurement of other variables (e.g. soil pH, wind speed, sand depth) to help explain changes; single transect and one afternoon limit reliability; working alone is unsafe and makes recording harder; percentage cover estimates are subjective.\n• Presentation: pie charts for each quadrat make change inland hard to see – a kite diagram or line graph against distance would be better.\n• Also consider permission/trampling of a protected habitat.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. You have carried out two geographical enquiries, one in a physical environment and one in a human environment. Use either your physical or your human geography enquiry to answer this question.\n\n'The conclusions of my enquiry were reliable.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? [9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: Detailed evaluation of the reliability of the conclusions, drawing on specific evidence from the named enquiry across several stages (question, location, sampling, data collection, presentation, analysis); thorough understanding of reliability and accuracy; a well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: Clear evaluation with some specific detail from the enquiry; some judgement.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: Basic statements about the enquiry; generic points; little judgement.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• States the conclusion clearly (e.g. 'Width increased downstream, partly supporting the Bradshaw model').\n• Factors increasing reliability: appropriate sampling strategy; sufficient sample size; repeat measurements and use of means; standardised equipment and methods; agreement with secondary data or theory.\n• Factors reducing reliability: limited sites or time; measurement errors (e.g. float method for velocity, subjective EQS scores); unusual weather; anomalies; group members using methods differently; human error in recording.\n• Recognition that conclusions apply only to the place and time studied.\n• Judgement on the extent to which the conclusions can be trusted, with suggested improvements.` },
      { q: `Section B — Fieldwork. You have carried out fieldwork in a physical geography environment, for example a river, a coast or a slope.\n\n'The most important stage of a geographical enquiry is choosing a suitable question and location.' Using your physical geography enquiry, to what extent do you agree with this statement? [9 marks]`, marks: 9, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `Level 3 (Detailed) 7–9 marks: Detailed evaluation of the importance of the question and location compared with other stages of the enquiry, using specific evidence from the named physical enquiry; a well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (Clear) 4–6 marks: Clear discussion with some specific detail from the enquiry and some comparison with other stages.\nLevel 1 (Basic) 1–3 marks: Basic statements; generic points not linked to the enquiry.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• A clear, measurable question linked to theory (e.g. Bradshaw model, longshore drift) gave a focus for data collection and analysis.\n• Location: had to show the features being investigated (e.g. a river with accessible sites over a long enough distance), be safe and accessible, and be manageable in the time available.\n• Poor choices create problems later (e.g. too few accessible sites, or a stretch altered by human activity).\n• Other stages also matter: sampling and data collection determine accuracy and reliability; presentation and analysis determine whether patterns are identified; evaluation shows the limits of conclusions.\n• Judgement: the question and location are the foundation, but a good enquiry depends on all stages working together.` }
    ]
  }
};
